// The Modern World, part 7: food waste, electronic waste, plant-based eating, the four-day week, universal basic income and digital IDs.
ADDART({id:"foodwaste",t:"Food Waste",c:"Environment",i:"🍲",w:"Food waste",r:["foodsecurity","recycling","climchange","plasticwaste","rice"],
p:[["Wasted food","Around a fifth of the food available to shoppers worldwide is thrown away."],["Lost along the way","More food is lost between farms and stores."],["Simple fixes","Planning meals, storing food well and sharing leftovers reduce waste."]],
l:"Food waste is food that is fit to eat but is thrown away, while food loss refers to food that spoils or is discarded between harvest and the shops. The United Nations estimated that in 2022 households, restaurants and shops wasted more than a billion metric tons of food, about a fifth of the food available to consumers, while millions of people went hungry. Wasted food also wastes the water, land, energy and labor used to produce it, and rotting food in landfills releases methane, a powerful greenhouse gas. Reducing food waste is part of the Sustainable Development Goals, which aim to halve it by 2030. Governments, businesses and families are trying many solutions, from better storage and donation programs to composting.",
s:[["Where food is wasted","Most food waste happens in homes, followed by restaurants, canteens and shops. Households throw away leftovers, spoiled fruit and vegetables and food past its date. Restaurants prepare more than they sell, and shops discard items that look imperfect or reach their sell-by dates."],["Food lost before it reaches us","In many developing countries, more food is lost on farms and in transport than in kitchens. Poor roads, lack of refrigeration, pests and weak storage cause harvests to rot. In the Philippines, a large share of fruits and vegetables can spoil between farm and market because of heat and delays."],["Why it matters","Wasted food means wasted money for families, wasted resources for farmers and more greenhouse gas emissions. Scientists estimate that food loss and waste together cause a significant share of global emissions. At the same time, hundreds of millions of people do not have enough to eat."],["Reducing waste at home","Families can reduce waste by planning meals, buying only what they need, storing food properly, understanding date labels, freezing extra portions and turning leftovers into new meals. Many Filipino dishes already use leftovers creatively, such as sinangag, garlic fried rice made from day-old rice."]],
k:[["Food wasted by consumers (2022)","More than 1 billion metric tons"],["Share of consumer food wasted","About one-fifth"],["Main source","Households"],["UN goal","Halve food waste by 2030"],["Greenhouse gas from rotting food","Methane"]],
d:[["2011","A UN report estimates that about a third of food is lost or wasted"],["2015","The Sustainable Development Goals include halving food waste"],["2016","France requires large supermarkets to donate unsold food"],["2019","The UN declares 29 September the International Day of Awareness of Food Loss and Waste"],["2021","The UN Food Waste Index is first published"],["2024","The UN reports more than a billion metric tons of food wasted in 2022"]],
s2:[["Date labels","Many people throw away food because they misunderstand date labels. A use-by date is about safety, especially for meat and dairy, while a best-before date is about quality; food is often still safe to eat after its best-before date if stored well. Clearer labels could reduce waste."],["Food banks and donation","Food banks collect surplus food from shops, farms and restaurants and give it to people in need. In the Philippines, groups and community pantries have shared food, especially during the COVID-19 pandemic, when community pantries spread across the country in 2021. Laws in some countries encourage or require donations."],["Composting","Food scraps can be composted, turning them into fertilizer for gardens and farms instead of producing methane in landfills. Some Philippine cities and barangays run composting programs, and the Ecological Solid Waste Management Act of 2000 requires waste to be separated into biodegradable and other types."],["Ugly produce","Fruits and vegetables that are misshapen, scarred or the wrong size are often rejected by shops, even though they taste the same. Some companies now sell such ugly produce at lower prices, and campaigns encourage shoppers to accept imperfect food."],["Restaurants and buffets","Restaurants and buffets can produce large amounts of waste. Some charge diners for uneaten food, offer smaller portions or donate leftovers. Technology such as smart scales helps kitchens measure what they throw away and adjust how much they cook."],["Rice and respect for food","Many cultures teach respect for food. Filipino parents often remind children to finish every grain of rice on their plates, sometimes warning that wasted rice will make the rice cry. Such traditions reflect the hard work of farmers and the value of food in a country where rice is the staple."]],
sp:["The community pantries of 2021","In April 2021, during the COVID-19 lockdowns, a young woman set up a small bamboo cart on Maginhawa Street in Quezon City, filled with vegetables, rice and canned goods. A sign invited people to give what they could and take only what they needed. The idea spread quickly on social media, and within days hundreds of community pantries appeared across the Philippines, in cities, villages and parishes. They helped families struggling with lost incomes and gave farmers and shops a way to share surplus food. The movement became a symbol of the Filipino spirit of bayanihan, or community cooperation."],
g:[["Food waste","Edible food thrown away by shops, restaurants or households"],["Food loss","Food lost between harvest and retail"],["Compost","Decayed organic matter used as fertilizer"],["Best-before date","A label showing when food is at its best quality"]],
f:["About a fifth of consumer food is wasted.","Households waste the most food.","Rotting food in landfills releases methane."],
fun:"If food waste were a country, its greenhouse gas emissions would rank among the highest in the world."});

ADDART({id:"ewaste",t:"Electronic Waste",c:"Environment",i:"📱",w:"Electronic waste",r:["recycling","smartphone","plasticwaste","battery","comp"],
p:[["Old gadgets","Electronic waste, or e-waste, is discarded phones, computers, appliances and other electronics."],["Fastest-growing waste","E-waste is one of the fastest-growing kinds of waste in the world."],["Treasure and danger","It contains valuable metals but also toxic substances."]],
l:"Electronic waste, or e-waste, refers to discarded electrical and electronic devices, such as mobile phones, computers, televisions, refrigerators, batteries, chargers and toys. As people buy more devices and replace them more often, e-waste has become one of the fastest-growing types of waste in the world. A United Nations report estimated that 62 million metric tons were produced in 2022, and less than a quarter was properly collected and recycled. E-waste contains valuable materials such as gold, silver, copper and rare metals, but also hazardous substances such as lead, mercury and cadmium. When it is dumped or burned in the open, it can poison soil, water and people. Proper recycling, repair and longer-lasting products are key solutions.",
s:[["What is in e-waste","A smartphone contains dozens of elements, including copper wiring, gold and silver in circuits, cobalt and lithium in batteries and rare earth metals in speakers and motors. Older televisions and monitors contain lead in their glass, and some devices contain mercury, flame retardants and plastics."],["A growing mountain","The amount of e-waste is rising several times faster than the amount properly recycled. Reasons include falling prices of electronics, short product lifetimes, difficulty repairing devices and rapid changes in technology that make older gadgets obsolete."],["Health and environmental dangers","When e-waste is burned to recover copper or dumped in open sites, toxic chemicals can enter the air, soil and water. Informal recyclers, including children in some countries, may be exposed to lead, mercury and smoke, which can harm the brain, lungs and kidneys."],["Urban mining","Recovering metals from e-waste is sometimes called urban mining. A metric ton of old phones can contain more gold than a metric ton of gold ore. Proper recycling saves resources, reduces the need for new mining and creates jobs, but it requires safe facilities and good collection systems."]],
k:[["E-waste produced (2022)","About 62 million metric tons"],["Properly recycled","Less than a quarter"],["Valuable materials","Gold, silver, copper, rare metals"],["Hazardous materials","Lead, mercury, cadmium"],["Related idea","Right to repair"]],
d:[["1989","The Basel Convention on hazardous waste trade is adopted"],["2003","The European Union introduces rules on electronic waste"],["2014","The first Global E-waste Monitor is published"],["2021","Ghana clears the Agbogbloshie scrapyard, a major informal e-waste site"],["2022","The European Union agrees a common charger for phones and devices"],["2024","The UN reports 62 million metric tons of e-waste produced in 2022"]],
s2:[["Shipping e-waste abroad","For years, large amounts of used electronics from rich countries were shipped to developing countries in Africa and Asia, sometimes labeled as secondhand goods. Much of it ended up dumped or burned. The Basel Convention restricts trade in hazardous waste, and countries have tightened rules, though illegal shipments continue."],["E-waste in the Philippines","The Philippines generates growing amounts of e-waste as more people own phones, computers and appliances. Much is collected by informal junk shops, whose workers dismantle devices by hand. The government regulates hazardous waste under laws on toxic substances and has encouraged accredited recycling facilities and collection drives for old gadgets and batteries."],["The right to repair","Many devices are hard to repair because parts are glued together, spare parts are expensive or manufacturers restrict repair. The right to repair movement campaigns for laws requiring companies to provide parts, tools and manuals. The European Union and several US states have passed right-to-repair rules, while some manufacturers argue that such rules raise safety and security concerns."],["Batteries","Lithium-ion batteries in phones, laptops and electric bikes can catch fire if crushed or damaged in garbage trucks or recycling plants. They should be taken to special collection points. Recycling batteries also recovers valuable metals such as lithium, cobalt and nickel, which are needed for electric vehicles."],["Designing longer-lasting products","Experts encourage companies to design devices that last longer, can be easily repaired and upgraded, and can be recycled at the end of their life. The European Union’s decision to require a common USB-C charger aims to reduce the number of chargers thrown away."],["What you can do","People can reduce e-waste by keeping devices longer, repairing rather than replacing them, buying refurbished products, donating working devices, deleting personal data before passing them on and taking broken electronics and batteries to proper collection points instead of throwing them in household trash."]],
sp:["Medals from old phones","For the Tokyo 2020 Olympic and Paralympic Games, held in 2021, organizers made all of the gold, silver and bronze medals from metals recovered from old electronics. Over two years, people across Japan donated about 79,000 metric tons of used devices, including more than six million mobile phones, at collection boxes in shops and offices. Recyclers extracted gold, silver and copper from the circuits to produce about 5,000 medals. The project showed how valuable discarded electronics can be and encouraged people to recycle their old gadgets instead of leaving them in drawers or throwing them away."],
g:[["E-waste","Discarded electrical and electronic devices"],["Urban mining","Recovering valuable materials from waste such as electronics"],["Right to repair","The idea that people should be able to fix their own devices"],["Basel Convention","An international treaty controlling the movement of hazardous waste"]],
f:["About 62 million metric tons of e-waste were produced in 2022.","Old phones contain gold and silver.","Tokyo 2020 medals were made from old electronics."],
fun:"A metric ton of old mobile phones can contain more gold than a metric ton of gold ore from a mine."});

ADDART({id:"plantbased",t:"Plant-Based Eating",c:"Food and Health",i:"🥗",w:"Plant-based diet",r:["nutri","climchange","foodsecurity","obesity","rice"],
p:[["Eating more plants","Plant-based eating means building meals mainly around vegetables, fruits, grains, beans and nuts."],["Vegetarian and vegan","Some people avoid meat entirely, while others simply eat less of it."],["Why people choose it","Reasons include health, the environment, animal welfare, religion and cost."]],
l:"Plant-based eating refers to diets that are made up mostly or entirely of foods from plants, such as vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, nuts and seeds. It ranges from vegan diets, which exclude all animal products, and vegetarian diets, which exclude meat and fish but may include eggs and dairy, to flexitarian diets that simply include less meat. People choose plant-based eating for health, environmental, ethical, religious and economic reasons. In recent years, plant-based meat and milk alternatives have become widely available in supermarkets and restaurants. Nutrition experts generally agree that eating more plant foods is healthy, while debates continue over processed alternatives, nutrient needs and the role of meat in diets and economies.",
s:[["Kinds of plant-based diets","Vegans eat no animal products at all, including meat, fish, eggs, dairy and honey. Lacto-ovo vegetarians eat dairy and eggs but no meat or fish. Pescatarians eat fish but not other meat. Flexitarians eat mostly plant foods with occasional meat. Many traditional diets around the world are largely plant-based."],["Health","Diets rich in vegetables, fruits, whole grains and legumes are linked to lower risks of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and some cancers. People who avoid all animal products need to plan carefully to get enough protein, iron, calcium, vitamin B12 and other nutrients, sometimes using fortified foods or supplements."],["The environment","Producing meat, especially beef, generally uses more land and water and produces more greenhouse gases than producing plant foods. Scientists have suggested that eating more plants could reduce the environmental impact of food systems. Others note that grazing animals can use land unsuited to crops and support livelihoods."],["Plant-based alternatives","Companies now make burgers, sausages, chicken, milk and cheese from soy, peas, wheat, oats, coconut and other plants, designed to taste like animal products. Some consumers welcome them, while others prefer whole foods and question highly processed products. Scientists are also developing cultivated meat grown from animal cells."]],
k:[["No animal products","Vegan"],["No meat or fish","Vegetarian"],["Mostly plants, some meat","Flexitarian"],["Key nutrient for vegans","Vitamin B12"],["First cultivated meat approval","Singapore, 2020"]],
d:[["1847","The Vegetarian Society is founded in England"],["1944","The word vegan is coined by Donald Watson"],["2009","Beyond Meat is founded"],["2016","The Impossible Burger is launched"],["2019","A major scientific report proposes a planetary health diet rich in plants"],["2020","Singapore becomes the first country to approve the sale of cultivated meat"]],
s2:[["Plant foods in Filipino cuisine","Many Filipino dishes are built around vegetables, legumes and coconut, such as pinakbet, laing, ginataang gulay, monggo stew, tortang talong, ensaladang mangga and ginataang langka. Tofu, called tokwa or tahu, has long been part of the diet through Chinese influence. Some dishes include small amounts of meat or fish sauce for flavor, but can be adapted."],["Religion and tradition","Many religious traditions encourage plant-based eating. Hindus, Jains and many Buddhists follow vegetarian diets, and some Christian groups, such as Seventh-day Adventists, promote vegetarianism. Catholics traditionally avoid meat on certain days, such as Fridays during Lent, when many Filipino families eat fish and vegetable dishes."],["Cost and access","Beans, rice, vegetables and root crops are often cheaper than meat, making plant-based meals affordable. However, specialty products such as plant-based meat substitutes can be expensive, and fresh vegetables may be hard to find or costly in some places. Food prices shape what people can choose."],["The planetary health diet","In 2019 a group of scientists known as the EAT–Lancet Commission proposed a diet designed to be healthy for people and sustainable for the planet. It recommended mainly plant foods, with modest amounts of fish, poultry and dairy and small amounts of red meat. Supporters see it as a useful guide, while critics argue it may not fit all cultures, budgets or nutritional needs."],["Debates over meat","Livestock farming supports millions of farmers worldwide, including backyard hog and poultry raisers in the Philippines. Some people argue for reducing meat consumption for environmental reasons, while others defend meat as nutritious, culturally important and economically vital. Many experts suggest that the choice is personal and that balance and moderation are key."],["Getting enough nutrients","People eating fully plant-based diets can meet their needs with careful planning. Beans, lentils, tofu and nuts provide protein; leafy greens such as malunggay provide iron and calcium; and vitamin B12, found naturally mainly in animal foods, can come from fortified foods or supplements. Children, pregnant women and older people may need special advice."]],
sp:["The miracle tree in the backyard","In many Filipino backyards grows a slender tree with feathery leaves: malunggay, or moringa. Its small leaves are rich in vitamins A and C, calcium, iron and protein, and are added to tinola, monggo and many other dishes. Malunggay grows quickly, tolerates drought and needs little care, making it a valuable source of nutrition for families. It has been promoted in school feeding programs and is now sold worldwide as a powder in health food stores. For many Filipinos, though, it is simply a familiar leaf picked fresh from the yard for the day’s soup."],
g:[["Vegan","A diet without any animal products"],["Vegetarian","A diet without meat or fish"],["Flexitarian","A mainly plant-based diet that sometimes includes meat"],["Cultivated meat","Meat grown from animal cells in a laboratory"]],
f:["The word vegan was coined in 1944.","Many Filipino dishes are vegetable-based.","Malunggay is rich in nutrients."],
fun:"Singapore became the first country to allow cultivated chicken, grown from animal cells, to be sold to diners in 2020."});

ADDART({id:"fourdayweek",t:"The Four-Day Week",c:"Economics and Society",i:"📅",w:"Four-day week",r:["remotework","gigeconomy","automation","mentalyouth","labor"],
p:[["Working fewer days","A four-day week means working four days instead of five, usually for the same pay."],["Trials around the world","Companies and governments in several countries have tested it."],["Debated benefits","Supporters cite wellbeing and productivity; critics raise costs and practicality."]],
l:"The four-day week is a work arrangement in which employees work four days a week instead of the usual five. In the version most discussed today, workers keep their full pay while working fewer total hours, often described as 100 percent pay for 80 percent of the time in exchange for maintaining productivity. Another version, the compressed workweek, keeps the same total hours spread over four longer days. Trials in Iceland, the United Kingdom, Spain, Japan and elsewhere have reported benefits such as less stress and burnout with productivity maintained, and many participating companies kept the arrangement. Critics argue that it does not suit every job, may raise costs and that results from voluntary trials may not apply everywhere.",
s:[["Two kinds of four-day weeks","In a reduced-hours four-day week, staff work around 32 hours instead of 40 for the same pay. In a compressed workweek, they work the same 40 hours in four days of about ten hours each. Both give a three-day weekend, but they have different effects on workers and employers."],["How the five-day week began","For much of history people worked six or seven days a week. In the 20th century, labor movements and some employers, such as the car maker Henry Ford in 1926, adopted a five-day, 40-hour week, which became standard in many countries. Some argue that, with modern technology, it is time to shorten the week again."],["What trials found","In a large trial in the United Kingdom in 2022, involving about 60 companies, most firms reported that productivity held steady while employees reported less stress, burnout and sick leave, and the majority continued the four-day week afterward. Trials in Iceland between 2015 and 2019 involving public sector workers reported similar results."],["Concerns","Critics say a four-day week may be difficult in jobs requiring constant coverage, such as hospitals, shops, factories and emergency services, and may increase costs if extra staff are needed. Some workers find longer days in compressed weeks tiring. Economists note that voluntary trials may attract companies most likely to succeed."]],
k:[["Common formula","100% pay, 80% time, 100% output"],["Five-day week popularized","1920s and 1930s"],["Large UK trial","2022"],["Iceland trials","2015–2019"],["Alternative","Compressed workweek"]],
d:[["1926","Henry Ford adopts a five-day week in his factories"],["1938","The United States sets a 44-hour, later 40-hour, standard workweek"],["2015","Iceland begins trials of shorter working weeks"],["2019","Microsoft Japan reports productivity gains in a four-day week trial"],["2022","About 60 British companies take part in a large four-day week trial"],["2022","Belgium gives workers the right to request a compressed four-day week"]],
s2:[["Wellbeing and burnout","Supporters argue that a longer weekend gives workers more time to rest, exercise, spend time with family, care for children and older relatives, and pursue hobbies and learning. Trials have reported improvements in mental health, sleep and job satisfaction. Some companies say it helps them attract and keep staff."],["Productivity","Supporters argue that people often waste time in long meetings and on distractions, and that a shorter week encourages focus. Some trials reported that output stayed the same or improved. Skeptics say productivity gains can be hard to measure in many jobs and may fade over time."],["Work in the Philippines","The standard workweek in the Philippines is generally 48 hours over six days or 40 hours over five days, depending on the employer. The Department of Labor and Employment has allowed compressed workweeks by agreement between employers and workers, and the Civil Service Commission has allowed compressed schedules in some government offices, including during periods of high fuel prices. Long commutes in Metro Manila make shorter weeks attractive to many workers."],["Who benefits?","Office workers may find a four-day week easier to adopt than people in shift work, farming, retail or the informal economy, where most Filipinos and many workers worldwide earn their living. Some critics worry that the four-day week could widen gaps between different kinds of workers."],["Governments and the four-day week","Some governments have encouraged experiments. Belgium allows workers to request a compressed four-day week, and local governments in Japan, Spain and elsewhere have tested shorter weeks for public employees. Others remain cautious about economic effects."],["The future of work","The four-day week is part of wider changes in how people work, along with remote and hybrid work, flexible hours and automation. Economist John Maynard Keynes predicted in 1930 that his grandchildren’s generation might work only 15 hours a week. Whether shorter weeks become widespread is still an open question."]],
sp:["Keynes’s 15-hour week","In 1930, during the Great Depression, the British economist John Maynard Keynes wrote an essay imagining life a hundred years later. He predicted that technology and rising productivity would make people so much richer that by around 2030 they might need to work only about 15 hours a week, and that the main challenge would be how to use their free time well. Productivity did rise enormously, but working hours fell much less than he expected. Economists still debate why, pointing to rising consumption, inequality and changing kinds of work. The four-day week movement often cites Keynes’s prediction as an inspiration."],
g:[["Four-day week","A schedule of four working days a week"],["Compressed workweek","The usual hours worked in fewer, longer days"],["Productivity","The amount of work produced in a given time"],["Burnout","Exhaustion caused by long-term work stress"]],
f:["Henry Ford adopted a five-day week in 1926.","About 60 UK companies tested a four-day week in 2022.","Keynes predicted a 15-hour week."],
fun:"In 1930 economist John Maynard Keynes predicted that people would work only about 15 hours a week by 2030."});

ADDART({id:"ubi",t:"Universal Basic Income",c:"Economics and Society",i:"💵",w:"Universal basic income",r:["inequality","automation","costliving","money","sdgs"],
p:[["Money for everyone","Universal basic income is a regular cash payment given to every person, with no conditions."],["Experiments worldwide","Pilot programs have been tested in Finland, Kenya, the United States and elsewhere."],["A lively debate","Supporters see it as a safety net; critics worry about cost and work incentives."]],
l:"Universal basic income, or UBI, is the idea that a government should give every adult, or every person, a regular cash payment that is enough to cover basic needs, without conditions such as working or proving need. Supporters argue that UBI would reduce poverty, give people security as jobs change because of automation, simplify welfare systems and respect individual freedom. Critics argue that it would be extremely expensive, might discourage work, could raise taxes or prices and may be less effective than targeted help for the poorest. No country has introduced a full national UBI, but pilot programs and related cash transfer schemes, including conditional cash transfers such as the Philippines’ Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program, have provided evidence for the debate.",
s:[["Key features","A true universal basic income has several features: it is paid to individuals rather than households, in cash, regularly, to everyone regardless of income, and without requirements to work or look for work. Related ideas include guaranteed income for people below a certain level and negative income taxes."],["History of the idea","Thinkers have proposed versions of basic income for centuries. In 1797 the writer Thomas Paine suggested giving every citizen a payment funded by land taxes. In the 20th century, economists from different political traditions, including Milton Friedman, supported versions such as a negative income tax."],["Why supporters back it","Supporters say a basic income would give everyone a floor below which they cannot fall, reduce poverty and stress, allow people to study, care for family, start businesses or find better jobs, and prepare societies for automation and artificial intelligence. Some argue it would reduce the cost and stigma of complicated welfare programs."],["Why critics oppose it","Critics say giving money to everyone, including the rich, is costly and inefficient, and that money would be better targeted at those in need. They worry it could reduce incentives to work, require high taxes or cuts to other services, and cause inflation. Some argue that work gives people dignity and purpose beyond income."]],
k:[["Short name","UBI"],["Main features","Universal, unconditional, regular cash"],["Early proposal","Thomas Paine, 1797"],["Long-running dividend","Alaska Permanent Fund, since 1982"],["Philippine cash transfer program","Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program (4Ps)"]],
d:[["1797","Thomas Paine proposes payments funded by land taxes"],["1982","Alaska begins paying an annual dividend to residents from oil revenues"],["2008","The Philippines launches the Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program"],["2016","Kenya-based long-term basic income experiments are announced"],["2017","Finland begins a two-year basic income experiment"],["2019","The 4Ps is made permanent by Philippine law"]],
s2:[["Finland’s experiment","From 2017 to 2018, Finland gave 2,000 unemployed people a monthly payment of 560 euros with no conditions, even if they found work. The results showed that recipients reported better wellbeing and less stress, while employment rose slightly compared with others. Researchers debated how much the findings would apply to a full national program."],["Kenya’s long-term study","The charity GiveDirectly began a large, long-term study in rural Kenya in which thousands of people receive regular cash payments, some for twelve years. Early results suggested people invested in businesses, food and homes rather than wasting the money, and that cash helped communities cope with shocks such as the COVID-19 pandemic."],["Alaska’s dividend","Since 1982, every resident of Alaska, including children, has received a yearly payment from a fund built from the state’s oil revenues. The amount varies each year, usually from several hundred to over a thousand US dollars. It is not enough to live on but is often cited as a real-world example of a universal payment."],["The 4Ps and conditional cash","The Philippines’ Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program, launched in 2008 and made permanent by law in 2019, gives cash grants to poor families on condition that children attend school and receive health checkups. It is not universal or unconditional, but studies have found it improved school attendance and health care use. Debates continue over targeting, conditions and how families leave the program."],["Pandemic payments","During the COVID-19 pandemic, many governments, including the Philippines through its Social Amelioration Program, sent emergency cash to millions of households. These payments renewed interest in basic income and showed both the value and the challenges of delivering cash quickly to large numbers of people."],["Automation and the future","Some technology leaders and economists argue that artificial intelligence and robots may replace many jobs, making a basic income necessary. Others believe new kinds of jobs will appear, as in past technological revolutions, and that education and training are better responses. The debate is likely to continue as technology changes work."]],
sp:["Paine’s idea of a fair share","In 1797, the political writer Thomas Paine, famous for supporting the American and French revolutions, published a pamphlet called Agrarian Justice. He argued that the Earth was originally the common property of all people, and that landowners owed society a payment for the land they held. With this money, he proposed, every person reaching the age of 21 should receive a lump sum to help them start adult life, and every person over 50 should receive a yearly pension. More than two centuries later, supporters of universal basic income often point to Paine as one of the first to suggest that everyone should share in society’s wealth."],
g:[["Universal basic income","A regular cash payment to everyone with no conditions"],["Conditional cash transfer","Cash given to families who meet certain conditions"],["Pilot program","A small trial to test an idea"],["Safety net","Programs that protect people from falling into poverty"]],
f:["No country has a full national UBI.","Finland tested basic income in 2017 and 2018.","The Philippines’ 4Ps is a conditional cash transfer."],
fun:"Every resident of Alaska, including newborn babies, receives a yearly payment from the state’s oil fund."});

ADDART({id:"digitalid",t:"Digital and National IDs",c:"Technology and Society",i:"🪪",w:"Philippine Identification System",r:["dataprivacy","digitalpay","cybersecurity","onlinescams","digitaldivide"],
p:[["Proving who you are","National ID systems give people an official way to prove their identity."],["The Philippine system","The Philippine Identification System began registering Filipinos in 2018."],["Benefits and risks","Digital IDs make services easier but raise privacy and security concerns."]],
l:"A national identity system gives residents of a country an official way to prove who they are, often with an ID card and, increasingly, a digital identity that can be checked online. Having proof of identity helps people open bank accounts, receive government aid, register for school, vote, travel and use health services. Around the world, hundreds of millions of people still lack official identification. Countries such as India, Estonia and Singapore have built large digital ID systems. In the Philippines, the Philippine Identification System Act of 2018 created PhilSys, which has registered tens of millions of Filipinos and issues the PhilID card and a digital version. Digital IDs bring convenience and inclusion but also raise concerns about privacy, data security and exclusion.",
s:[["Why identity matters","Without proof of identity, people may be unable to open a bank account, get a SIM card, receive social benefits, enroll children in school or claim property. The World Bank has estimated that hundreds of millions of people worldwide lack official ID, most of them in poorer countries. The Sustainable Development Goals include providing legal identity for all, including birth registration."],["How digital IDs work","A digital ID system usually stores basic information, such as name, birth date and address, along with biometrics such as fingerprints, iris scans or a photograph. Each person receives a unique number. Services can verify identity by checking the number and biometrics, or by scanning a QR code on an ID card or phone."],["PhilSys","The Philippine Identification System Act, signed in 2018, established a single national ID for all citizens and resident foreigners, managed by the Philippine Statistics Authority. Registration began in 2018 and expanded nationwide from 2020. People receive a PhilSys number and a PhilID card, and a printable or mobile digital version, the ePhilID and later the digital National ID, was introduced."],["Using the national ID","The PhilID is meant to be accepted as valid proof of identity for government and private transactions, such as opening bank accounts, applying for benefits and registering SIM cards. It aims to replace the need for multiple IDs and help people without other documents, especially the poor, access services."]],
k:[["Philippine law","Philippine Identification System Act, 2018"],["Managing agency","Philippine Statistics Authority"],["Card","PhilID"],["Registered","More than 80 million Filipinos"],["Large digital ID example","India’s Aadhaar"]],
d:[["2002","Estonia introduces a national ID card with digital signatures"],["2009","India launches Aadhaar, which becomes the world’s largest biometric ID system"],["2018","The Philippine Identification System Act is signed"],["2020","PhilSys registration expands nationwide"],["2022","The printable ePhilID is introduced"],["2025","More than 80 million Filipinos are confirmed as registered after duplicate checks"]],
s2:[["India’s Aadhaar","India’s Aadhaar system, launched in 2009, has given unique identity numbers based on fingerprints and iris scans to well over a billion people. It is used for welfare payments, bank accounts and phone connections. Supporters say it reduced fraud and helped millions access services; critics raised concerns about privacy, data leaks and people excluded when authentication failed. India’s Supreme Court upheld the system in 2018 but limited some of its uses."],["Estonia’s e-government","Estonia, a small country in northern Europe, issues digital ID cards that let citizens sign documents, vote online, file taxes and access health records. Almost all government services can be used online. Estonia’s system is often cited as a model, though it also faced a security flaw in its ID cards in 2017 that required millions of cards to be updated."],["Privacy and security","Because national ID systems hold sensitive personal and biometric data for millions of people, they are attractive targets for hackers and could be misused. Privacy advocates call for strong legal protections, limits on who can access data and independent oversight. The Philippines’ Data Privacy Act of 2012 and the PhilSys law include rules on data protection and penalties for misuse."],["Challenges in rollout","PhilSys faced delays in printing and delivering physical cards, and some people had problems with registration or with banks and offices that did not yet accept the ID. The digital version was introduced partly to address delays. People in remote areas, older people and those without phones or internet may face difficulties using digital IDs."],["Inclusion","Supporters say national IDs especially help poor and marginalized people, including those without birth certificates, to prove their identity and receive aid, open bank accounts and access digital payments. Critics warn that if services require digital ID, people who cannot register or authenticate may be left out."],["Digital wallets for IDs","Governments are developing ways to store IDs in mobile phone apps, along with driver’s licenses, health records and certificates. These can be convenient but depend on secure phones and reliable internet. Experts recommend keeping alternative options so that no one is excluded."]],
sp:["An ID for the unbanked","Before PhilSys, many Filipinos, especially in poor and remote communities, struggled to open bank accounts because they lacked the two valid IDs banks often required. Some had no birth certificate or had never held a government ID. During PhilSys registration drives, the Philippine Statistics Authority partnered with a government bank so that registrants could open a basic bank account on the spot. Millions of accounts were opened this way, helping people receive government aid, save money and use digital payments. Supporters saw this as one of the system’s clearest benefits for financial inclusion."],
g:[["National ID","An official identity document issued by a government"],["Biometrics","Physical traits such as fingerprints used to identify people"],["PhilSys","The Philippine Identification System"],["Authentication","Checking that a person is who they claim to be"]],
f:["PhilSys was created by law in 2018.","More than 80 million Filipinos have registered.","India’s Aadhaar is the largest biometric ID system."],
fun:"In Estonia, citizens have been able to vote online using their digital ID cards since 2005."});

