// Space, more articles: binary stars, radio astronomy, star clusters, Sputnik and the Rosetta comet mission.
ADDART({id:"binarystars",t:"Binary Stars",c:"Astronomy",i:"✨",w:"Binary star",r:["stars","exoplanet","pulsar","supernova","gravwaves","stellarbh","novae"],
p:[["Stars in pairs","A binary star is a pair of stars that orbit each other."],["Very common","Many stars, perhaps around half of Sun-like stars, have at least one companion."],["Measuring stars","Binary stars let astronomers measure the masses of stars."]],
l:"A binary star system consists of two stars bound together by gravity, orbiting a shared center of mass. Systems with three or more stars are called multiple stars. Binary and multiple stars are very common: many stars in our galaxy, including a large share of stars similar to the Sun, have at least one companion. Some binaries are far apart and take thousands of years to complete an orbit, while others are so close that they share gas or even touch. Binary stars are very important to astronomers, because watching how the two stars move lets scientists calculate their masses. Close binaries can produce dramatic events such as novae, some supernovae and the gravitational waves made when two dead stars merge.",
s:[["How binaries are found","Some binaries can be seen as two separate stars through a telescope; these are visual binaries. Others are too close to separate, but their light shows periodic shifts caused by their motion, revealing spectroscopic binaries. In eclipsing binaries, one star passes in front of the other as seen from Earth, causing regular dips in brightness."],["Measuring the masses of stars","Using the laws of gravity worked out by Johannes Kepler and Isaac Newton, astronomers can calculate the masses of the two stars from the size and period of their orbit. Binary stars provided the first reliable measurements of stellar masses, which are essential for understanding how stars live and die."],["Nearby examples","The nearest star system to the Sun, Alpha Centauri, is a triple system: two Sun-like stars, Alpha Centauri A and B, orbit each other, and a small red dwarf, Proxima Centauri, orbits farther away. Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky, has a faint white dwarf companion called Sirius B."],["Planets with two suns","Planets can orbit binary stars. In 2011 NASA’s Kepler telescope found Kepler-16b, a planet orbiting two stars, which reminded many people of the fictional desert world with two suns in the Star Wars films. Astronomers have since found other such circumbinary planets."]],
k:[["Definition","Two stars orbiting a common center"],["Nearest multiple system","Alpha Centauri (three stars)"],["Brightest star’s companion","Sirius B"],["Famous eclipsing binary","Algol"],["First planet found orbiting two stars","Kepler-16b, 2011"]],
d:[["1650","Giovanni Riccioli reports that the star Mizar is double"],["1782","John Goodricke explains Algol’s dimming as an eclipsing binary"],["1803","William Herschel shows that some double stars orbit each other"],["1862","Alvan Graham Clark sees Sirius B, the companion of Sirius"],["2011","Kepler-16b is found orbiting two stars"],["2015","Gravitational waves from two merging black holes are detected"]],
s2:[["The demon star","Algol, in the constellation Perseus, dims noticeably every few days. Its name comes from Arabic words meaning the demon’s head. In 1782 a young deaf English astronomer, John Goodricke, suggested that Algol dims because a darker companion star regularly passes in front of it. He was right; Algol was one of the first eclipsing binaries understood."],["Herschel’s discovery","In the late 1700s the astronomer William Herschel carefully measured the positions of many double stars, hoping to use them to measure distances. Instead, by 1803 he found that some pairs had changed their positions because they were orbiting each other, showing that Newton’s law of gravity applied beyond the solar system."],["Stars that share gas","In some close binaries, one star swells up as it ages and spills gas onto its companion. If the companion is a white dwarf, the gas can build up and ignite in a nova, a sudden brightening. If too much gas piles on, the white dwarf can explode completely as a type of supernova, which astronomers use to measure distances across the universe."],["Colliding dead stars","When both stars in a binary end their lives as neutron stars or black holes, they can slowly spiral together and merge, sending out ripples in space-time called gravitational waves. In 2017 detectors recorded two neutron stars colliding, and telescopes saw the explosion, which produced heavy elements such as gold."],["Albireo","Albireo, in the constellation Cygnus, is one of the most beautiful double stars in a small telescope: a golden-yellow star beside a blue companion. Amateur astronomers enjoy observing such colorful pairs, though recent measurements suggest Albireo’s two stars may not be orbiting each other but merely lined up by chance."],["Why binaries matter","Because so many stars are in binaries, understanding them is essential for understanding how stars form and evolve. Many stars are born in pairs or groups from the same collapsing cloud of gas, and interactions between companions can change how long they live and how they die."]],
sp:["Two sunsets","Imagine standing on a planet orbiting two stars. Each evening, two suns would set, one after the other, casting double shadows that change shape as the stars move. Such a scene appears in the Star Wars films on the planet Tatooine, and for years it was only science fiction. Then in 2011 astronomers studying data from NASA’s Kepler space telescope announced Kepler-16b, a cold, Saturn-sized planet orbiting a pair of stars about 245 light-years away. Since then, more planets with two suns have been discovered, showing that such worlds are real, though none yet known is like Earth."],
g:[["Binary star","Two stars orbiting each other"],["Eclipsing binary","A binary in which one star passes in front of the other"],["Center of mass","The balance point around which two objects orbit"],["Nova","A sudden brightening caused by gas igniting on a white dwarf"]],
f:["Alpha Centauri is a triple star system.","Sirius has a white dwarf companion.","Kepler-16b orbits two stars."],
fun:"Algol’s name comes from Arabic for the demon’s head, perhaps because ancient observers noticed it dimming like a winking eye."});

ADDART({id:"radioastronomy",t:"Radio Astronomy",c:"Astronomy",i:"📡",w:"Radio astronomy",r:["scope","pulsar","cmb","bh","radio","m87bh","centaurusa"],
p:[["Listening to space","Radio astronomy studies the universe by detecting radio waves from space."],["Giant dishes","Radio telescopes use huge dishes or arrays of antennas."],["Big discoveries","Radio astronomers discovered pulsars and the afterglow of the Big Bang."]],
l:"Radio astronomy is the study of objects in space through the radio waves they give off. Radio waves are a form of light with much longer wavelengths than visible light, and they can pass through dust and clouds that block ordinary telescopes. Radio astronomy began in the 1930s when Karl Jansky detected radio noise coming from the center of the Milky Way. Since then, radio telescopes, from single giant dishes to networks of antennas spread across continents, have revealed pulsars, quasars, the cosmic microwave background left over from the Big Bang, cold gas between stars and the first images of black holes. Major projects today include China’s FAST telescope and the Square Kilometre Array.",
s:[["Radio waves from space","Many objects in space give off radio waves, including the Sun, Jupiter, clouds of gas, exploding stars, pulsars and the regions around black holes. Radio waves can pass through dust that hides the center of our galaxy, so radio telescopes can see places that optical telescopes cannot."],["How radio telescopes work","A radio telescope usually has a large curved dish that collects faint radio waves and focuses them onto a receiver, like a giant satellite dish. The signals are amplified and recorded by computers. Because radio waves are so long, very large dishes are needed to see fine detail."],["Telescopes working together","Astronomers combine signals from many antennas spread over large distances, a technique called interferometry. The farther apart the antennas, the sharper the images. Arrays such as the Very Large Array in the United States and ALMA in Chile’s Atacama Desert use dozens of dishes working together."],["Imaging black holes","The Event Horizon Telescope links radio observatories around the world to act like a telescope the size of Earth. In 2019 it released the first image of a black hole, at the center of the galaxy M87, showing a bright ring around a dark shadow. In 2022 it revealed the black hole at the center of the Milky Way."]],
k:[["Founded by","Karl Jansky, 1930s"],["Largest single dish","FAST, China, 500 meters"],["First black hole image","Event Horizon Telescope, 2019"],["Pulsars discovered","1967"],["Future giant","Square Kilometre Array"]],
d:[["1932","Karl Jansky detects radio waves from the Milky Way"],["1937","Grote Reber builds the first dish-shaped radio telescope in his backyard"],["1965","Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson report the cosmic microwave background"],["1967","Jocelyn Bell Burnell discovers the first pulsar"],["2016","China completes the 500-meter FAST telescope"],["2019","The Event Horizon Telescope releases the first image of a black hole"]],
s2:[["Jansky’s static","In the early 1930s Karl Jansky, an engineer at Bell Telephone Laboratories, built a rotating antenna to track down sources of radio static that disturbed transatlantic telephone calls. He found a faint hiss that rose and fell once a day, and realized it came from the direction of the center of the Milky Way. It was the first detection of radio waves from beyond the solar system."],["The afterglow of the Big Bang","In 1964 and 1965 Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson, testing a radio antenna in New Jersey, found a faint background noise coming from every direction. After ruling out other causes, even cleaning pigeon droppings from the antenna, they learned it was the cosmic microwave background, leftover radiation from the early universe. They received the Nobel Prize in 1978."],["Pulsars","In 1967 Jocelyn Bell Burnell, a graduate student in Cambridge, noticed a strange, regular pulse in her radio telescope data, repeating every 1.3 seconds. The source was a pulsar, a rapidly spinning neutron star sweeping a beam of radio waves across space like a lighthouse. Her supervisor shared the 1974 Nobel Prize, an omission many have since criticized."],["Arecibo and FAST","For decades the largest single-dish radio telescope was the 305-meter Arecibo telescope in Puerto Rico, used to study pulsars, planets and asteroids and to send a famous radio message into space in 1974. It collapsed in 2020 after cable failures. China’s FAST telescope, 500 meters across, built in a natural limestone basin in Guizhou, is now the largest."],["Searching for signals","Radio telescopes are also used to search for signals from possible intelligent life elsewhere, an effort known as SETI. No confirmed signal has been found. In 1977 a telescope in Ohio recorded a strong, unexplained signal, nicknamed the Wow! signal after an astronomer wrote Wow! beside it on a printout; it was never detected again."],["Quiet places","Radio telescopes are so sensitive that phones, microwave ovens and other electronics can interfere with them. Many are built in remote areas, such as deserts and valleys, with radio quiet zones where certain devices are restricted. The Square Kilometre Array, under construction in South Africa and Australia, uses remote sites for this reason."]],
sp:["The pulse that seemed artificial","When Jocelyn Bell Burnell first noticed a regular pulse in her data in 1967, she and her supervisor, Antony Hewish, were puzzled. The signal ticked like a clock, every 1.33 seconds, more regularly than anything known in nature. Half-jokingly, they labeled it LGM-1, for little green men, wondering whether it could be a signal from aliens. When Bell Burnell found a second pulsing source elsewhere in the sky, it became clear that a natural explanation was more likely. The sources turned out to be pulsars, spinning neutron stars, one of the most important astronomical discoveries of the 20th century."],
g:[["Radio telescope","A telescope that detects radio waves from space"],["Interferometry","Combining signals from several telescopes to make sharper images"],["Pulsar","A spinning neutron star that sends out beams of radio waves"],["Cosmic microwave background","Faint radiation left over from the early universe"]],
f:["Radio astronomy began in the 1930s.","FAST in China is 500 meters across.","The first black hole image was made with radio telescopes."],
fun:"The first pulsar signal was jokingly nicknamed LGM-1, for little green men, because it ticked so regularly."});

ADDART({id:"starclusters",t:"Star Clusters",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌟",w:"Star cluster",r:["stars","milky","constellations","galaxies","nebula","imbh","pleiades"],
p:[["Families of stars","A star cluster is a group of stars born together and held by gravity."],["Open and globular","Clusters come in two main types: loose open clusters and dense globular clusters."],["The Pleiades","The Pleiades, or Seven Sisters, is a famous open cluster visible without a telescope."]],
l:"A star cluster is a group of stars that formed together from the same cloud of gas and dust and are held together, at least for a time, by their mutual gravity. There are two main kinds. Open clusters, found in the disk of the Milky Way, contain dozens to a few thousand young stars loosely grouped together, such as the Pleiades. Globular clusters are dense, ball-shaped swarms of hundreds of thousands to millions of very old stars that orbit around the center of the galaxy. Because the stars in a cluster are about the same age and distance, clusters are natural laboratories that help astronomers understand how stars are born, age and die, and how old the universe is.",
s:[["Open clusters","Open clusters are loose groups of young stars, often only tens or hundreds of millions of years old. They lie in the spiral arms of the Milky Way, where new stars are forming. Over time, the gravity of passing clouds and stars pulls them apart, so their stars drift away to join the general population of the galaxy, as the Sun probably did long ago."],["Globular clusters","Globular clusters are tightly packed spheres of stars, some containing millions of members within a region about a hundred light-years across. Their stars are among the oldest in the galaxy, often more than ten billion years old. The Milky Way has about 150 known globular clusters, found mostly in a halo surrounding the galaxy."],["Clusters you can see","Several clusters are visible to the naked eye. The Pleiades, in Taurus, looks like a tiny dipper of stars. The Hyades form the V-shaped face of Taurus. Omega Centauri, the largest globular cluster of the Milky Way, appears as a fuzzy star in southern skies and can be seen from the Philippines."],["Clues to stellar life","Because cluster stars are born at the same time but have different masses, they show how stars of different sizes age. Massive stars burn out first, while smaller ones live longer. By seeing which stars have aged, astronomers can estimate a cluster’s age, which helped set limits on the age of the universe."]],
k:[["Main types","Open and globular"],["Famous open cluster","Pleiades (Seven Sisters)"],["Largest Milky Way globular cluster","Omega Centauri"],["Globular clusters in the Milky Way","About 150"],["Typical globular cluster age","Over 10 billion years"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BC","The Pleiades are mentioned in ancient texts and myths"],["1610","Galileo sees many more stars in the Pleiades through his telescope"],["1665","The globular cluster M22 is discovered"],["1764","Charles Messier begins cataloging clusters and nebulae"],["1918","Harlow Shapley uses globular clusters to locate the center of the Milky Way"],["2010s","The Gaia spacecraft maps cluster stars in unprecedented detail"]],
s2:[["The Seven Sisters","The Pleiades have been known since ancient times and appear in the myths of many cultures. In Greek mythology they were seven sisters, daughters of the titan Atlas. In Japan they are called Subaru, the name of a car company whose logo shows the cluster. Many Pacific and Southeast Asian peoples used the rising of the Pleiades to mark seasons for planting and fishing."],["Finding the galaxy’s center","In 1918 the American astronomer Harlow Shapley studied the distances of globular clusters and found that they formed a sphere centered on a point far from the Sun, in the direction of Sagittarius. He concluded that the Sun is not at the center of the Milky Way but far out in its disk, a major change in our view of our place in the galaxy."],["Messier’s catalog","In the 1700s the French astronomer Charles Messier, a comet hunter, made a list of fuzzy objects that could be confused with comets. His catalog of about 110 objects includes many star clusters, such as M13 in Hercules and M45, the Pleiades, as well as nebulae and galaxies. Amateur astronomers still use Messier numbers today."],["Omega Centauri","Omega Centauri contains millions of stars and is the largest and brightest globular cluster of the Milky Way. Some astronomers think it may be the core of a small galaxy that was swallowed by the Milky Way long ago. In 2024 researchers reported evidence of a medium-sized black hole at its center."],["Clusters and star birth","Most stars, including the Sun, are thought to be born in clusters within giant clouds of gas and dust. Young clusters, such as those in the Orion Nebula, can be seen still surrounded by the glowing gas from which they formed. Studying them helps scientists understand how planetary systems like ours begin."],["Sending messages to a cluster","In 1974 the Arecibo radio telescope beamed a message toward the globular cluster M13, about 25,000 light-years away, describing human beings, DNA and our solar system in a simple picture code. It was mainly a demonstration; the message will take about 25,000 years to arrive, and by then the cluster will have moved."]],
sp:["Stars that mark the seasons","For many peoples around the world, the appearance of the Pleiades in the sky was a calendar. In parts of the Philippines, farmers traditionally watched for the cluster before dawn to know when to clear fields and plant rice. Some Filipino peoples have names for it in their own languages, and similar traditions exist in Indonesia, Malaysia and the Pacific islands, where navigators and fishers watched its rising and setting. In New Zealand, the Māori celebrate Matariki, the rising of the Pleiades in midwinter, as their new year, now a national public holiday."],
g:[["Star cluster","A group of stars born together and bound by gravity"],["Open cluster","A loose group of young stars"],["Globular cluster","A dense ball of very old stars"],["Messier catalog","A list of about 110 clusters, nebulae and galaxies"]],
f:["The Pleiades are called the Seven Sisters.","Omega Centauri contains millions of stars.","Globular clusters are among the oldest things in the galaxy."],
fun:"The logo of the Subaru car company shows the stars of the Pleiades, which are called Subaru in Japanese."});

ADDART({id:"sputnik",t:"Sputnik",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Sputnik 1",r:["space","sat","rocket","coldwar","apollo"],
p:[["The first satellite","Sputnik 1, launched by the Soviet Union in 1957, was the first artificial satellite."],["A beeping ball","It was a small metal sphere that sent out radio beeps heard around the world."],["The space race begins","Its launch shocked the United States and started the space race."]],
l:"Sputnik 1 was the first artificial satellite to orbit Earth. The Soviet Union launched it on 4 October 1957 from what is now the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan. Sputnik, meaning fellow traveler, was a polished metal sphere about 58 centimeters across, weighing about 84 kilograms, with four long antennas. It circled Earth every 96 minutes, broadcasting simple radio beeps that could be picked up by amateur radio operators around the world. Its launch, during the Cold War, shocked the United States and triggered the space race, leading to the creation of NASA, new investment in science education and, eventually, the Moon landings. Sputnik burned up in the atmosphere in January 1958.",
s:[["The launch","Sputnik was launched by an R-7 rocket, originally designed as an intercontinental missile. The project was led by the Soviet chief designer Sergei Korolev, whose identity was kept secret. The satellite was simple, but getting it into orbit required a rocket powerful enough to reach about 28,000 kilometers per hour."],["Beep, beep","Sputnik’s two radio transmitters sent out beeps on frequencies that ordinary radio receivers could pick up. People around the world listened to the signal, and newspapers printed the times it would pass overhead. Its batteries lasted about three weeks before the beeping stopped."],["Shock in America","The launch alarmed Americans, who feared that if the Soviet Union could put a satellite in orbit, it could also launch nuclear missiles across the world. Newspapers spoke of a Sputnik crisis, and politicians demanded action. The first American attempt, Vanguard, exploded on the launch pad in December 1957."],["Sputnik 2 and Laika","Just a month later, on 3 November 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 2, carrying a dog named Laika, the first animal to orbit Earth. Laika did not survive the flight, which was planned without a way to return. Her mission showed that a living creature could survive launch and weightlessness, paving the way for human spaceflight."]],
k:[["Launched","4 October 1957"],["Country","Soviet Union"],["Diameter","About 58 cm"],["Mass","About 84 kg"],["Orbit time","About 96 minutes"]],
d:[["1955","The United States and the Soviet Union announce plans to launch satellites"],["1957","Sputnik 1 is launched on 4 October"],["1957","Sputnik 2 carries the dog Laika into orbit"],["1958","Explorer 1 becomes the first American satellite"],["1958","Sputnik 1 burns up in the atmosphere, and NASA is founded"],["1961","Yuri Gagarin becomes the first human in space"]],
s2:[["The International Geophysical Year","Sputnik was launched during the International Geophysical Year, an 18-month period of worldwide scientific cooperation from 1957 to 1958. Both the United States and the Soviet Union had announced they would launch satellites to study Earth. The Soviet success came first, turning a scientific project into a symbol of Cold War rivalry."],["America responds","The United States launched its first satellite, Explorer 1, on 31 January 1958. It carried an instrument that discovered belts of radiation around Earth, now called the Van Allen belts. In July 1958 Congress created NASA to lead civilian space exploration, and the military began new rocket programs."],["Sputnik and schools","The Sputnik shock led the United States to invest heavily in science, mathematics and engineering education. The National Defense Education Act of 1958 funded schools, universities and student loans. Many scientists and engineers of the following decades were inspired by the space race."],["Watching from the Philippines","Sputnik and its rocket stage could be seen crossing the night sky from many parts of the world, including Asia. Newspapers in the Philippines and elsewhere reported on the satellite, and amateur radio operators tuned in to its beeps. The space race became a major news story of the late 1950s."],["From Sputnik to today","Since Sputnik, people have launched thousands of satellites for communication, weather forecasting, navigation, Earth observation and science. Today there are many thousands of active satellites in orbit, and the growing number raises concerns about crowding and space debris."],["A lasting symbol","The word sputnik entered languages around the world, and the phrase Sputnik moment is still used to describe a sudden realization that a rival has taken the lead, prompting a nation to respond. Replicas of Sputnik are displayed in museums in Moscow, Washington and elsewhere."]],
sp:["The sound heard around the world","On the night of 4 October 1957, radio operators began picking up a strange, repeating sound: beep, beep, beep. It came from a small shiny ball racing around Earth more than 200 kilometers up. Radio stations broadcast the beeps to millions of listeners, and people stepped outside to look for the tiny moving light, though what most saw was actually the much larger rocket stage that followed it. For many, it was thrilling and frightening at once: humanity had reached space, but the achievement belonged to one side of the Cold War. The beeps marked the beginning of the space age."],
g:[["Artificial satellite","A human-made object that orbits Earth or another body"],["Space race","The Cold War competition in space between the US and the Soviet Union"],["Orbit","The curved path of an object around another"],["Cold War","The rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union, 1947–1991"]],
f:["Sputnik 1 was launched on 4 October 1957.","It orbited Earth every 96 minutes.","Sputnik 2 carried the dog Laika."],
fun:"The word sputnik means fellow traveler or companion in Russian."});

ADDART({id:"rosetta",t:"The Rosetta Mission",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☄️",w:"Rosetta (spacecraft)",r:["comet","spaceagencies","solarform","astrobio","rocket"],
p:[["Chasing a comet","Rosetta was a European spacecraft that orbited a comet for two years."],["A landing on a comet","Its lander, Philae, made the first landing on a comet in 2014."],["Clues to our origins","Rosetta helped scientists learn about the early solar system."]],
l:"Rosetta was a space probe built by the European Space Agency that became the first spacecraft to orbit a comet and to send a lander to its surface. Launched in 2004, it spent ten years traveling through the solar system, using flybys of Earth and Mars to gain speed, before reaching comet 67P/Churyumov–Gerasimenko in August 2014. In November 2014 its small lander, Philae, touched down on the comet, the first landing ever on a comet’s nucleus, though it bounced and came to rest in a shadowed spot. Rosetta studied the comet for more than two years as it swung past the Sun, discovering organic molecules, unusual water and a surprisingly rugged, duck-shaped nucleus. The mission ended in 2016 with a controlled landing on the comet.",
s:[["The long journey","Rosetta was launched by an Ariane 5 rocket on 2 March 2004. To reach its fast-moving target, it flew past Earth three times and Mars once, using their gravity to change speed and direction. It also passed two asteroids, Steins and Lutetia. For about 31 months it was put into hibernation to save power before waking up in January 2014."],["A duck-shaped comet","When Rosetta arrived in August 2014, it found that comet 67P has two lobes joined by a narrow neck, resembling a rubber duck about four kilometers long. Its surface has cliffs, pits, boulders and smooth plains of dust. As the comet approached the Sun, jets of gas and dust burst from its surface."],["The Philae landing","On 12 November 2014 Rosetta released Philae, a lander about the size of a washing machine. Its harpoons failed to fire, and it bounced twice before settling in a shaded spot where its solar panels received little light. Philae sent back data for about 60 hours before its batteries ran down. Rosetta photographed its final resting place in 2016."],["Discoveries","Rosetta found that the comet contains organic molecules, including the amino acid glycine, a building block of proteins, and phosphorus, important for life. It also found that the comet’s water has a different chemical signature from Earth’s oceans, suggesting that comets like 67P were not the main source of Earth’s water."]],
k:[["Agency","European Space Agency"],["Launched","2 March 2004"],["Target","Comet 67P/Churyumov–Gerasimenko"],["Philae landing","12 November 2014"],["Mission ended","30 September 2016"]],
d:[["2004","Rosetta is launched"],["2007","Rosetta flies past Mars"],["2011","Rosetta enters hibernation"],["2014","Rosetta reaches comet 67P and Philae lands"],["2015","The comet makes its closest approach to the Sun"],["2016","Rosetta ends its mission with a controlled descent onto the comet"]],
s2:[["Names from Egypt","Rosetta was named after the Rosetta Stone, the ancient Egyptian slab whose inscriptions helped scholars decipher hieroglyphs. Scientists hoped the mission would similarly unlock the secrets of comets and the early solar system. The lander Philae was named after an island in the Nile where an obelisk was found that also helped with decipherment."],["Why study comets","Comets are icy bodies left over from the formation of the solar system about 4.6 billion years ago. They have changed little since then, so studying them is like opening a time capsule. Scientists wonder whether comets delivered water and the ingredients for life to the early Earth."],["A dramatic landing","The Philae landing captured worldwide attention. Mission controllers in Darmstadt, Germany, cheered when the first signals arrived, then learned that Philae had bounced more than a kilometer back into space before settling. Despite the difficult landing, it completed most of its planned experiments."],["Watching a comet wake up","Unlike earlier missions that flew quickly past comets, Rosetta stayed with 67P as it moved closer to the Sun and heated up. It watched the comet’s activity grow, with jets of gas and dust, outbursts and changes in its surface, and then die down as it moved away."],["The final descent","As the comet moved away from the Sun and power for Rosetta’s solar panels declined, the team decided to end the mission by slowly crashing Rosetta onto the comet on 30 September 2016. During the descent, it took close-up photographs and measurements until contact was lost on impact."],["Public engagement","Rosetta was followed closely by the public through social media, where the spacecraft and lander had cartoon versions that posted updates. A short science fiction film called Ambition was made to promote the mission. The excitement helped many people learn about comets and space science."]],
sp:["Ten years to catch a comet","Catching a comet is like trying to catch a speeding bullet with another bullet. Comet 67P travels around the Sun on a long, elliptical orbit, moving tens of thousands of kilometers per hour. To match its speed and path, Rosetta took a winding ten-year route, swinging past Earth and Mars to steal a little of their orbital energy each time. Engineers then put it to sleep for more than two and a half years to save power while it traveled far from the Sun. In January 2014 the world waited anxiously as Rosetta’s alarm clock went off. Hours later, its signal reached Earth: it had woken up, ready for its rendezvous."],
g:[["Comet","An icy body that releases gas and dust near the Sun"],["Nucleus","The solid core of a comet"],["Lander","A spacecraft that touches down on a surface"],["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change a spacecraft’s speed"]],
f:["Rosetta was the first spacecraft to orbit a comet.","Philae made the first landing on a comet.","Comet 67P looks like a rubber duck."],
fun:"Rosetta’s journey to its comet took ten years and included three flybys of Earth and one of Mars."});

;
