// The Human Body, more articles: high blood pressure, myopia, leptospirosis, anemia and dyslexia.
ADDART({id:"hypertension",t:"High Blood Pressure",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🩺",w:"Hypertension",r:["heart","stroke","heartdisease","kidney","exercise"],
p:[["The silent killer","High blood pressure, or hypertension, often has no symptoms but damages the body over time."],["Very common","More than a billion adults worldwide have high blood pressure."],["Can be controlled","Lifestyle changes and medicines keep blood pressure in a healthy range."]],
l:"Blood pressure is the force of blood pushing against the walls of the arteries as the heart pumps. High blood pressure, or hypertension, occurs when this force stays too high over time. It usually causes no symptoms, which is why it is called the silent killer, but it strains the heart and blood vessels and is a leading cause of heart attacks, strokes, kidney disease and early death. The World Health Organization estimates that about 1.3 billion adults aged 30 to 79 have hypertension, and many do not know it. In the Philippines it is one of the most common health problems among adults. Regular checks, a healthy lifestyle and, when needed, medicines can control it and prevent serious complications.",
s:[["Reading blood pressure","Blood pressure is written as two numbers, such as 120 over 80, measured in millimeters of mercury. The top number, systolic pressure, is the pressure when the heart beats; the bottom number, diastolic pressure, is the pressure between beats. A reading below about 120 over 80 is considered normal, and readings repeatedly at or above 140 over 90 are generally considered hypertension."],["Why it is dangerous","High pressure makes the heart work harder and damages the inner walls of arteries, making them stiff and narrow. Over years this raises the risk of heart attacks, heart failure, strokes, kidney damage, vision loss and dementia. Because it usually causes no warning signs, many people learn they have it only after a serious event."],["Causes and risk factors","Risk rises with age and family history. Other factors include eating too much salt, being overweight, lack of physical activity, smoking, drinking too much alcohol, stress and conditions such as diabetes and kidney disease. In many cases there is no single cause."],["Checking regularly","The only way to know whether you have high blood pressure is to have it measured, at a clinic, health center, pharmacy or with a home monitor. Adults are advised to have their blood pressure checked regularly. In the Philippines, barangay health stations often offer free checks."]],
k:[["Also called","Hypertension"],["Normal reading","Below about 120/80 mmHg"],["Hypertension","Often defined as 140/90 mmHg or higher"],["Adults affected worldwide","About 1.3 billion (ages 30–79)"],["World Hypertension Day","17 May"]],
d:[["1733","Stephen Hales first measures blood pressure, in a horse"],["1896","Scipione Riva-Rocci invents the inflatable cuff for measuring blood pressure"],["1905","Nikolai Korotkov describes the sounds used to measure blood pressure"],["1950s","The first effective blood pressure medicines become available"],["2005","The first World Hypertension Day is held"],["2023","The WHO publishes its first global report on hypertension"]],
s2:[["Measuring pressure","In 1733 the English clergyman and scientist Stephen Hales inserted a tube into a horse’s artery and watched the blood rise high up the tube, the first measurement of blood pressure. In 1896 the Italian doctor Scipione Riva-Rocci invented the inflatable arm cuff, and in 1905 the Russian surgeon Nikolai Korotkov discovered the tapping sounds heard through a stethoscope that are still used to take readings."],["Salt and Filipino food","Many people eat far more salt than recommended. Common sources in Filipino diets include patis, toyo, bagoong, salted dried fish, instant noodles, canned goods, processed meats and salty snacks. Health experts suggest reducing added salt, choosing fresh foods, reading labels and using herbs, garlic, calamansi and spices for flavor."],["Treatment","Lifestyle changes, such as eating less salt, eating more vegetables and fruit, staying active, keeping a healthy weight, limiting alcohol and not smoking, can lower blood pressure. Many people also need medicines, which must usually be taken every day, even when they feel well. Stopping medicines without advice can be dangerous."],["Hypertension in the Philippines","Surveys have found that a large share of Filipino adults have high blood pressure, and many are unaware or not receiving treatment. Government programs provide some maintenance medicines for free at health centers, and campaigns encourage regular checks, especially during May, Hypertension Awareness Month."],["High blood pressure in pregnancy","Some women develop high blood pressure during pregnancy. A serious form called pre-eclampsia can harm both mother and baby, so blood pressure is checked at prenatal visits. With proper care, most women with high blood pressure in pregnancy have healthy babies."],["Low blood pressure","Blood pressure can also be too low, causing dizziness or fainting, especially when standing up quickly. Dehydration, heat, some medicines and certain illnesses can lower it. Mild low blood pressure without symptoms is usually not a concern."]],
sp:["The horse and the glass tube","In 1733 Stephen Hales, an English clergyman with a passion for experiments, wanted to know how strongly the heart pushes blood. He tied down a horse, inserted a brass pipe into an artery in its neck and connected it to a glass tube nearly three meters tall. When he released the blood, it shot up the tube to a height of about 2.5 meters and rose and fell slightly with each heartbeat. It was a crude and, by modern standards, cruel experiment, but it was the first measurement of blood pressure. Today the same measurement takes a minute with a soft cuff around the arm."],
g:[["Blood pressure","The force of blood against the walls of the arteries"],["Hypertension","Blood pressure that stays too high"],["Systolic pressure","The pressure when the heart beats"],["Diastolic pressure","The pressure between heartbeats"]],
f:["High blood pressure often has no symptoms.","Salt can raise blood pressure.","The blood pressure cuff was invented in 1896."],
fun:"The first blood pressure measurement, in 1733, used a horse and a glass tube nearly three meters tall."});

ADDART({id:"myopia",t:"Nearsightedness",c:"Health",i:"👓",w:"Near-sightedness",r:["eye","screentime","light","onlinelearning","exercise"],
p:[["Blurry far away","Nearsightedness, or myopia, makes distant objects look blurry."],["Rising worldwide","It is becoming much more common, especially among children in cities."],["Time outdoors helps","Spending time outdoors may help protect children’s eyesight."]],
l:"Nearsightedness, known medically as myopia, is a common eye condition in which nearby objects can be seen clearly but distant objects appear blurry. It happens when the eyeball is slightly too long, or the cornea too curved, so that light focuses in front of the retina instead of on it. Myopia usually begins in childhood and can worsen during the school years. It is easily corrected with eyeglasses, contact lenses or surgery, but severe myopia raises the risk of serious eye diseases later in life. Myopia has become much more common in recent decades, particularly in East and Southeast Asian cities, and researchers estimate that about half the world’s population could be nearsighted by 2050. Spending more time outdoors appears to help prevent it in children.",
s:[["How myopia works","For clear vision, the cornea and lens must focus light exactly on the retina at the back of the eye. In a nearsighted eye, which is usually a little too long, light from distant objects comes to a focus in front of the retina, so the image is blurred. Light from nearby objects focuses correctly, so reading and close work remain clear."],["Signs of myopia","Children with myopia may squint, sit close to the television or board, hold books very close, rub their eyes or complain of headaches. Many do not realize their vision is blurry because they have never seen clearly. Regular eye checks help catch it early."],["Correcting myopia","Glasses and contact lenses with concave lenses spread light out so that it focuses on the retina. Laser eye surgery can reshape the cornea in adults. Special lenses, eye drops and contact lenses worn overnight can slow the worsening of myopia in some children."],["A growing problem","Studies have found that in some East Asian cities, the large majority of young adults are nearsighted. A widely cited study estimated that about a third of the world’s population had myopia in 2020 and that about half may have it by 2050. Scientists think changes in how children spend their time are a major reason."]],
k:[["Medical name","Myopia"],["Cause","Eyeball too long or cornea too curved"],["Correction","Glasses, contact lenses or surgery"],["Protective habit","Time outdoors"],["Projected by 2050","About half of people worldwide"]],
d:[["c. 350 BC","Aristotle describes people who see better up close"],["c. 1286","Eyeglasses are invented in Italy"],["1450s","Concave lenses for nearsightedness come into use"],["1880s","Contact lenses are first made"],["1980s","Laser eye surgery for myopia is developed"],["2016","A study projects that half the world may be nearsighted by 2050"]],
s2:[["Why is myopia increasing?","Genes play a part, but they cannot explain such a rapid rise. Researchers point to children spending more time indoors, doing close work such as reading, studying and using screens, and less time outdoors. Bright outdoor light appears to help regulate eye growth in children."],["The power of sunlight","Studies in Asia, Australia and elsewhere have found that children who spend more time outdoors are less likely to become nearsighted. Some schools, such as in Taiwan, encouraged children to spend more time outside during breaks, and rates of new myopia among children fell. Experts often suggest at least two hours a day outdoors."],["Screens and close work","Long periods of close work, including reading and using phones and tablets, may contribute to myopia, though the evidence is still being studied. Eye doctors often recommend the 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look at something about 20 feet, or 6 meters, away for 20 seconds."],["Myopia and the pandemic","During the COVID-19 pandemic, children in many countries, including the Philippines, spent long periods at home learning online. Several studies reported an increase in new cases of myopia among children during this time, adding to concerns about screen time and time indoors."],["Eye care in the Philippines","Many Filipino children with vision problems do not receive eye checks or glasses, which can affect their learning. School vision screening programs, community eye missions and government health insurance help, and groups provide free or low-cost glasses to students."],["High myopia","Very strong nearsightedness, called high myopia, increases the risk of serious conditions later in life, such as retinal detachment, glaucoma and cataracts. People with high myopia are advised to have regular eye examinations and to seek urgent care if they notice sudden flashes of light, floaters or a shadow in their vision."]],
sp:["Glasses through the ages","Eyeglasses were invented in northern Italy around 1286, possibly in Pisa or Venice. The first glasses were convex lenses that helped older people read. Lenses for nearsightedness, which are concave, came later, in the 1400s. Early glasses were balanced on the nose or held by hand, and arms that hook over the ears became common only in the 1700s. For centuries glasses were expensive, but today they are among the most effective and affordable health tools in the world, allowing millions of children to see the board at school and millions of adults to keep working."],
g:[["Myopia","Nearsightedness, difficulty seeing distant objects clearly"],["Retina","The light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye"],["Concave lens","A lens thinner in the middle that spreads light out"],["Optometrist","A health professional who checks eyes and prescribes glasses"]],
f:["Nearsighted people see near objects clearly.","Time outdoors may protect children’s eyes.","Eyeglasses were invented around 1286."],
fun:"The 20-20-20 rule suggests looking at something 20 feet away for 20 seconds every 20 minutes of close work."});

ADDART({id:"leptospirosis",t:"Leptospirosis",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🐀",w:"Leptospirosis",r:["phtyph","immune","antibiotics","sanitation","dengue"],
p:[["A flood-borne disease","Leptospirosis is a bacterial infection often caught by wading in floodwater."],["Spread by rats","The bacteria come from the urine of infected animals, especially rats."],["Treatable","Early treatment with antibiotics can save lives."]],
l:"Leptospirosis is an infection caused by bacteria called Leptospira, which are carried in the urine of infected animals, especially rats, but also dogs, pigs, cattle and other mammals. People can be infected when water or soil contaminated with this urine comes into contact with cuts or scratches on the skin or with the eyes, nose or mouth. In tropical countries such as the Philippines, outbreaks often follow typhoons and floods, when people wade through floodwater. Symptoms range from fever, headache and muscle pain to severe illness affecting the kidneys, liver and lungs. Leptospirosis can be treated with antibiotics, and avoiding floodwater, wearing boots and getting early care help prevent deaths.",
s:[["How it spreads","Rats and other animals can carry Leptospira bacteria in their kidneys and pass them out in their urine. The bacteria survive in water and moist soil for weeks. During floods, contaminated water spreads widely through streets and homes. The bacteria enter the body through cuts, scratches or soft skin softened by long exposure to water, or through the eyes, nose and mouth."],["Symptoms","Symptoms usually begin about 2 to 14 days after exposure and include high fever, headache, muscle pain especially in the calves, red eyes, chills and vomiting. Many cases are mild, but some people develop a severe form, sometimes called Weil’s disease, with yellowing of the skin, kidney failure, bleeding and lung problems."],["Treatment","Leptospirosis is treated with antibiotics such as doxycycline or penicillin. Early treatment works best, so anyone who develops fever after wading in floodwater should see a doctor and mention the exposure. Severe cases need hospital care, sometimes including dialysis for kidney failure."],["Prevention","The best protection is to avoid wading or swimming in floodwater. People who must enter it should wear rubber boots and gloves, cover wounds and wash with soap and clean water afterward. Controlling rats, keeping garbage covered and improving drainage also reduce risk. Doctors may give preventive antibiotics to people with high exposure."]],
k:[["Cause","Leptospira bacteria"],["Main carriers","Rats and other animals"],["Common source","Floodwater"],["Severe form","Weil’s disease"],["Treatment","Antibiotics"]],
d:[["1886","Adolf Weil describes a severe disease with jaundice and kidney failure"],["1907","Leptospira bacteria are first seen in a patient’s kidney tissue"],["1915","Scientists in Japan identify the bacteria as the cause"],["2009","Leptospirosis cases surge in Metro Manila after Typhoon Ondoy’s floods"],["2012","Monsoon floods in Luzon are followed by more leptospirosis cases"],["2020s","Health authorities expand flood-season warnings and preventive treatment"]],
s2:[["Floods and outbreaks","In the Philippines, leptospirosis cases usually rise during the rainy season, especially one to two weeks after major floods. After Typhoon Ondoy flooded much of Metro Manila in 2009, hospitals treated large numbers of patients, and many people died. Similar surges have followed other typhoons and monsoon floods."],["Who is at risk","People who wade through floodwater, farmers working in wet rice fields, sewer workers, animal handlers and people living in crowded areas with poor drainage and many rats face the highest risk. Children playing in floodwater are also at risk."],["Hospitals under strain","During major outbreaks, hospitals can be overwhelmed by patients with leptospirosis, many needing dialysis for kidney failure. Health authorities may open special wards and urge people to seek early treatment rather than wait until symptoms become severe."],["Rats in cities","Large rat populations in cities increase the risk of leptospirosis. Improper garbage disposal, clogged drains and crowded housing provide food and shelter for rats. Better waste collection, sanitation and drainage benefit public health in many ways."],["A global disease","Leptospirosis occurs worldwide but is most common in tropical and subtropical regions, including Southeast Asia, Latin America and the Pacific islands. Scientists estimate there are about a million severe cases and tens of thousands of deaths each year. It is considered a neglected disease because it receives relatively little attention."],["Animals and vaccines","Vaccines are used to protect dogs, pigs and cattle from leptospirosis, which can also make animals sick. Human vaccines exist in a few countries but are not widely used. Keeping pets vaccinated and away from rats helps protect families."]],
sp:["After the flood","When floodwater finally recedes after a typhoon, many families return to clean their homes, scrubbing mud from floors and throwing out ruined furniture. Health workers warn that this is also a dangerous time. The water that filled the streets may have carried rat urine, and small cuts on hands and feet can let Leptospira bacteria enter the body. A week or two later, some people develop fever and aching calves and think it is only the flu. Doctors urge anyone who has waded through floodwater and then falls ill to seek medical care quickly and to tell the doctor about the flood exposure, because early antibiotics can prevent severe illness."],
g:[["Leptospirosis","A bacterial disease spread by the urine of infected animals"],["Leptospira","The spiral-shaped bacteria that cause leptospirosis"],["Weil’s disease","A severe form of leptospirosis"],["Dialysis","A treatment that filters blood when the kidneys fail"]],
f:["Leptospirosis is often caught from floodwater.","Rats are the main carriers.","Antibiotics can treat it."],
fun:"Leptospira bacteria are shaped like tiny spirals or question marks and can wriggle through water."});

ADDART({id:"anemia",t:"Anemia",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🩸",w:"Anemia",r:["blood","nutri","pregnancy","heart","hygiene"],
p:[["Too few red blood cells","Anemia is a condition in which the blood cannot carry enough oxygen."],["Tired and pale","It can cause tiredness, weakness, pale skin and shortness of breath."],["Often preventable","Eating iron-rich foods and treating its causes can prevent and cure many cases."]],
l:"Anemia is a condition in which a person has too few healthy red blood cells or too little hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. As a result, the body’s tissues do not receive enough oxygen, causing tiredness, weakness, pale skin, dizziness and shortness of breath. The most common cause worldwide is iron deficiency, often due to a diet low in iron, blood loss or the extra needs of pregnancy and growth. Other causes include lack of vitamin B12 or folate, infections such as malaria and intestinal worms, and inherited conditions such as thalassemia and sickle cell disease. Anemia affects a large share of young children and women worldwide, including in the Philippines. Many cases can be prevented with a good diet, fortified foods, supplements and treatment of infections.",
s:[["What anemia does","Red blood cells carry oxygen from the lungs to every part of the body using hemoglobin, which contains iron. When there is not enough hemoglobin, the heart and lungs must work harder. People may feel tired, weak and short of breath, have trouble concentrating, look pale or feel cold."],["Iron deficiency","Iron deficiency anemia is the most common type. It can result from not eating enough iron-rich foods, poor absorption of iron, heavy menstrual bleeding, blood loss from intestinal worms or ulcers, and the extra iron needs of babies, growing children and pregnant women."],["Other causes","Anemia can also be caused by a lack of vitamin B12 or folate, long-term diseases such as kidney disease, infections like malaria, and inherited blood disorders. In thalassemia, the body makes abnormal hemoglobin; in sickle cell disease, red blood cells become stiff and curved."],["Who is most at risk","Young children, adolescent girls, women of reproductive age and pregnant women are at greatest risk. Anemia in pregnancy can lead to premature birth and low birth weight, while in children it can affect growth, learning and energy for play and school."]],
k:[["Main problem","Too little hemoglobin"],["Most common cause","Iron deficiency"],["Common symptoms","Tiredness, weakness, pale skin"],["Groups most affected","Young children and women"],["Philippine fortification law","Food Fortification Law of 2000"]],
d:[["1660s","Doctors use iron to treat a condition called chlorosis, a form of anemia"],["1849","Thomas Addison describes a form of anemia later called pernicious anemia"],["1926","George Minot and William Murphy show that eating liver treats pernicious anemia"],["1934","Minot, Murphy and George Whipple receive the Nobel Prize for their work on anemia"],["1948","Vitamin B12 is isolated"],["2000","The Philippines passes a law requiring fortification of rice, flour and other foods"]],
s2:[["Iron-rich foods","Good sources of iron include red meat, liver, fish, chicken, eggs, beans, lentils and dark green leafy vegetables such as malunggay, kangkong and alugbati. Iron from meat and fish is absorbed more easily than iron from plants. Eating foods rich in vitamin C, such as calamansi, guava and tomatoes, with plant foods helps the body absorb iron."],["Fortified foods","Many countries add iron and other nutrients to staple foods to prevent deficiencies. The Philippine Food Fortification Law of 2000 requires the addition of iron to rice and wheat flour, vitamin A to cooking oil and sugar, and other nutrients. Iron-fortified rice has been distributed in some government programs and schools."],["Supplements","Health workers give iron and folic acid supplements to pregnant women and iron supplements or micronutrient powders to young children in many countries. Deworming programs for schoolchildren also reduce anemia caused by intestinal worms, which feed on blood."],["Liver for anemia","In the 1920s, American doctors George Minot and William Murphy found that patients with pernicious anemia, then usually fatal, recovered when they ate large amounts of raw or lightly cooked liver. The key ingredient was later identified as vitamin B12. Minot, Murphy and George Whipple received the Nobel Prize in 1934."],["Thalassemia in the Philippines","Thalassemia is an inherited blood disorder that is relatively common in Southeast Asia, including the Philippines. People with severe forms need regular blood transfusions. Genetic counseling and screening help families understand their risk, and awareness campaigns promote early diagnosis."],["Diagnosis","Anemia is diagnosed with a simple blood test that measures hemoglobin. Because tiredness has many causes, doctors look for the reason behind anemia before treating it. Treating the underlying cause, such as worms, heavy bleeding or poor diet, is important for lasting recovery."]],
sp:["Sinigang with malunggay","Many Filipino family recipes naturally help fight anemia. A bowl of tinola with chicken and malunggay leaves, monggo stew cooked with dried fish and leafy greens, or adobong atay, liver cooked in vinegar and soy sauce, are all good sources of iron. Squeezing calamansi over dishes or eating a guava afterward adds vitamin C, helping the body absorb iron from plant foods. Nutritionists in health centers and schools often teach parents about these everyday foods, showing that a healthy diet need not be expensive."],
g:[["Anemia","A lack of healthy red blood cells or hemoglobin"],["Hemoglobin","The iron-containing protein that carries oxygen in blood"],["Iron deficiency","Not having enough iron in the body"],["Food fortification","Adding vitamins and minerals to common foods"]],
f:["Iron deficiency is the most common cause of anemia.","Vitamin C helps the body absorb iron.","The Philippines requires fortified rice and flour."],
fun:"In the 1920s, patients with a deadly form of anemia were cured by eating large amounts of liver."});

ADDART({id:"dyslexia",t:"Dyslexia",c:"Brain and Learning",i:"📖",w:"Dyslexia",r:["brain","memory","psychology","alphabet","onlinelearning"],
p:[["A learning difference","Dyslexia is a learning difference that makes reading and spelling difficult."],["Not about intelligence","People with dyslexia are just as intelligent as others."],["Help works","Early support and special teaching methods help people with dyslexia succeed."]],
l:"Dyslexia is a common learning difference that mainly affects reading, spelling and writing. People with dyslexia often find it hard to connect letters with the sounds they represent, to decode words and to read fluently, even though they have normal intelligence, vision and opportunities to learn. It is thought to affect a significant share of people, often estimated at 5 to 10 percent, and it tends to run in families. Dyslexia is not caused by laziness or poor teaching, and it is not a sign of low intelligence. With early identification and structured, multisensory teaching, people with dyslexia can learn to read well, and many become successful in every field. Many also have strengths in areas such as creative thinking, problem solving and visual reasoning.",
s:[["What dyslexia is","Reading requires the brain to link written symbols to the sounds of spoken language. In dyslexia, this process works differently, particularly the ability to break words into individual sounds, called phonological awareness. As a result, decoding new words, spelling and reading quickly can be difficult."],["Signs of dyslexia","Signs can include difficulty learning letter sounds, reading slowly or inaccurately, guessing words, spelling the same word in different ways, trouble remembering sequences such as the days of the week, and avoiding reading aloud. Signs differ from person to person, and dyslexia can range from mild to severe."],["Myths about dyslexia","A common myth is that people with dyslexia see letters backwards. While young children often reverse letters such as b and d, this is normal and not the main feature of dyslexia. Another myth is that dyslexia can be outgrown; it is lifelong, but people can learn effective strategies."],["Diagnosis","Dyslexia is usually identified through assessments by psychologists, specialist teachers or other professionals who test reading, spelling, sound skills and memory. Early identification, ideally in the first years of school, allows support to begin before children fall far behind or lose confidence."]],
k:[["Main area affected","Reading and spelling"],["Estimated share of people","Often 5 to 10 percent"],["Not related to","Intelligence"],["Effective teaching","Structured, multisensory phonics"],["First described","1880s and 1890s"]],
d:[["1877","Adolph Kussmaul describes word blindness"],["1887","Rudolf Berlin coins the term dyslexia"],["1896","W. Pringle Morgan describes a bright boy who cannot learn to read"],["1930s","Samuel Orton and Anna Gillingham develop teaching methods for dyslexia"],["1949","The International Dyslexia Association’s forerunner is founded"],["2022","The Philippines passes a law on inclusive education for learners with disabilities"]],
s2:[["Discovering dyslexia","In 1896 the British doctor W. Pringle Morgan described a 14-year-old boy, Percy, who was bright and quick in many subjects but could not learn to read despite years of teaching. Morgan called the condition congenital word blindness. The term dyslexia, from Greek words meaning difficulty with words, had been coined in 1887 by the German eye doctor Rudolf Berlin."],["The brain and reading","Brain imaging studies show that people with dyslexia often use different brain areas when reading, particularly in regions on the left side of the brain involved in linking sounds and letters. With effective teaching, brain activity can change, showing that the brain can adapt."],["Teaching methods","Structured literacy approaches teach the sounds of language and how they connect to letters step by step, often using several senses at once, such as seeing, saying, hearing and tracing letters. The Orton-Gillingham approach, developed in the 1930s, inspired many programs used today."],["Tools that help","Audiobooks, text-to-speech software, speech-to-text tools, colored overlays for some readers, extra time on tests and permission to type instead of handwrite can help students with dyslexia show what they know. Fonts designed for readability and clear layouts can also help."],["Dyslexia in the Philippines","Awareness of dyslexia has grown in the Philippines, where parents’ groups and organizations provide information and support. Reading in more than one language, such as Filipino, English and a regional language, may present additional challenges. A 2022 law promotes inclusive education and support services for learners with disabilities, including learning disabilities."],["Strengths and success","Many people with dyslexia report strengths such as creativity, big-picture thinking, storytelling, spatial reasoning and problem solving. People with dyslexia work successfully as scientists, entrepreneurs, artists, engineers, athletes and leaders. Experts emphasize that recognizing strengths, along with support for reading, helps people thrive."]],
sp:["Percy’s story","In 1896 a British doctor named W. Pringle Morgan wrote about a puzzling patient, a 14-year-old boy called Percy. Percy was intelligent and good at arithmetic, and his teacher said he would be the smartest boy in the school if instruction were entirely oral. Yet after seven years of school, he could barely read. He wrote his own name as Precy and spelled simple words in strange ways. Morgan concluded that Percy had a specific difficulty with written words, unrelated to his intelligence. His short article in the British Medical Journal is often cited as one of the first descriptions of what we now call dyslexia."],
g:[["Dyslexia","A learning difference that mainly affects reading and spelling"],["Phonological awareness","The ability to hear and work with the sounds in words"],["Multisensory teaching","Teaching that uses sight, hearing, speech and touch together"],["Learning difference","A difference in how a person learns certain skills"]],
f:["Dyslexia is not related to intelligence.","It mainly affects reading and spelling.","The word dyslexia was coined in 1887."],
fun:"The word dyslexia comes from Greek words meaning difficulty with words."});

