ADDART({id:"oxygen",t:"Oxygen",c:"Chemistry",i:"🫧",w:"Oxygen",r:["periodictable","atmosphere","lungs","photo","reactions"],
p:[["The breath of life","Oxygen is the element that animals and people need to breathe."],["Everywhere","It makes up about a fifth of the air and is the most abundant element in Earth’s crust."],["Discovered in the 1770s","Scientists in Sweden, England and France discovered and named it."]],
l:"Oxygen is a chemical element with the symbol O and atomic number 8. At normal temperatures it is a colorless, odorless gas made of pairs of atoms, O2, and it makes up about 21 percent of Earth’s air. Oxygen is the most abundant element in Earth’s crust, found in rocks, sand and water, and by mass it is the most abundant element in the human body, mostly in the form of water. Nearly all living things depend on it: animals breathe it to release energy from food, and plants produce it through photosynthesis. Oxygen also allows fire to burn. It was discovered in the 1770s by Carl Wilhelm Scheele and Joseph Priestley, and named by Antoine Lavoisier, whose work on it helped found modern chemistry.",
s:[["Oxygen in the air","Air is about 78 percent nitrogen and 21 percent oxygen, with small amounts of argon, carbon dioxide and other gases. Each breath brings oxygen into the lungs, where it passes into the blood and is carried by red blood cells to every cell of the body."],["Oxygen and energy","Cells use oxygen to break down sugar and release energy in a process called cellular respiration, producing carbon dioxide and water as waste. This is why we breathe in oxygen and breathe out carbon dioxide. Without oxygen, brain cells begin to die within minutes."],["Where oxygen comes from","Most of the oxygen in the air was produced by photosynthesis, as plants, algae and certain bacteria use sunlight to turn carbon dioxide and water into sugar, releasing oxygen. Ocean plankton produce a large share of it. Early Earth had almost no free oxygen until photosynthetic microbes slowly filled the air over hundreds of millions of years."],["Oxygen and fire","Fire needs fuel, heat and oxygen. Burning is a fast reaction between a fuel and oxygen that releases heat and light. Rusting is a much slower reaction between iron and oxygen in the presence of water. Firefighters put out fires partly by cutting off their oxygen supply, for example with foam or blankets."]],
k:[["Symbol","O"],["Atomic number","8"],["Share of air","About 21 percent"],["Discovered","1770s, Scheele and Priestley"],["Named by","Antoine Lavoisier"]],
d:[["c. 2.4 billion years ago","Oxygen begins to build up in Earth’s atmosphere"],["1771","Carl Wilhelm Scheele produces oxygen in Sweden"],["1774","Joseph Priestley isolates oxygen in England"],["1777","Antoine Lavoisier names the element oxygen"],["1840","Christian Schönbein identifies ozone, a form of oxygen"],["1877","Oxygen is first liquefied"]],
s2:[["Who discovered oxygen?","The Swedish chemist Carl Wilhelm Scheele produced oxygen around 1771 by heating chemicals, calling it fire air, but his results were published only in 1777. The English minister and scientist Joseph Priestley independently made it in 1774 by heating mercury oxide and found that a candle burned brightly and a mouse lived longer in it. Both are usually credited with the discovery."],["Lavoisier’s revolution","The French chemist Antoine Lavoisier repeated these experiments and realized that burning and breathing involve combining with this gas, rather than releasing a mysterious substance called phlogiston, as many believed. He named it oxygen, from Greek words meaning acid-maker, because he wrongly thought all acids contained it. His work helped create modern chemistry."],["The Great Oxidation Event","For the first half of Earth’s history, the atmosphere had very little oxygen. About 2.4 billion years ago, oxygen produced by microbes called cyanobacteria began to accumulate in the air, an event known as the Great Oxidation Event. It transformed the planet’s chemistry and eventually made complex animal life possible."],["Ozone","Ozone is a form of oxygen with three atoms, O3. High in the atmosphere, the ozone layer absorbs most of the Sun’s harmful ultraviolet rays. Near the ground, however, ozone is a pollutant that forms from vehicle and factory emissions in sunlight and can harm the lungs."],["Medical oxygen","Hospitals give extra oxygen to patients who have trouble breathing because of conditions such as pneumonia, asthma attacks, heart failure or COVID-19. During the COVID-19 pandemic, shortages of medical oxygen in many countries, including parts of the Philippines, showed how vital it is."],["Oxygen in industry and space","Oxygen is used in steelmaking, welding and cutting metals, water treatment and chemical production. Liquid oxygen, which forms at about minus 183°C and is pale blue, is used with fuels to power rockets. Astronauts on the International Space Station get oxygen by splitting water with electricity."]],
sp:["The mouse in the jar","In 1774 Joseph Priestley focused sunlight through a large lens onto a red powder, mercury oxide, inside a sealed container. A gas was given off. When he placed a candle in it, the flame burned with remarkable brightness. He then put a mouse in a jar of the gas and found that it lived much longer than a mouse in the same amount of ordinary air. Curious, Priestley breathed the gas himself and wrote that his breath felt particularly light and easy, wondering whether this pure air might one day become a fashionable luxury. He had isolated oxygen."],
g:[["Element","A pure substance made of one kind of atom"],["Respiration","The process cells use to release energy from food using oxygen"],["Photosynthesis","The process by which plants make food and release oxygen"],["Ozone","A form of oxygen with three atoms"]],
f:["Oxygen makes up about 21 percent of air.","Oxygen is element number 8.","Plants release oxygen through photosynthesis."],
fun:"Liquid oxygen is pale blue and is attracted to magnets."});

ADDART({id:"negativenumbers",t:"Negative Numbers",c:"Mathematics",i:"➖",w:"Negative number",r:["zero","alg","infin","graphs","percent"],
p:[["Numbers below zero","Negative numbers are numbers less than zero, written with a minus sign."],["Everyday uses","They describe debts, temperatures below freezing and places below sea level."],["A slow acceptance","Mathematicians took centuries to fully accept them."]],
l:"Negative numbers are numbers less than zero, such as minus 1, minus 5 or minus 3.7. Together with zero and positive numbers, they form the integers and the wider set of real numbers. On a number line, negative numbers lie to the left of zero. They describe quantities that can go in opposite directions: money owed versus money owned, temperatures below freezing, floors below ground, heights below sea level and losses compared with gains. Negative numbers were used in ancient China and were explained with clear rules by Indian mathematicians such as Brahmagupta in the 600s. In Europe, many mathematicians regarded them with suspicion until the 1600s and later. Today they are essential in mathematics, science, finance and computing.",
s:[["The number line","A number line shows zero in the middle, positive numbers to the right and negative numbers to the left. The farther left, the smaller the number, so minus 10 is less than minus 2. The distance of a number from zero, ignoring its sign, is called its absolute value."],["Rules for calculating","Adding a negative number is like subtracting: 5 plus minus 3 equals 2. Subtracting a negative number is like adding: 5 minus minus 3 equals 8. Multiplying or dividing two negative numbers gives a positive result, while multiplying a positive and a negative number gives a negative result."],["Everyday negative numbers","Temperatures below zero degrees Celsius are negative. A bank account that is overdrawn shows a negative balance. Places below sea level, such as the shores of the Dead Sea at about minus 430 meters, have negative elevations. In sports, goal difference can be negative, and golf scores below par are written as negative numbers."],["Why negative times negative is positive","One way to understand it: losing a debt is like gaining money. If removing a debt of 10 pesos is written as minus times minus 10, the result is a gain of 10. Mathematicians also show that the rule is needed for arithmetic to stay consistent."]],
k:[["Definition","Numbers less than zero"],["Symbol","Minus sign (−)"],["Early use","Ancient China, red and black counting rods"],["Clear rules","Brahmagupta, 628"],["Negative times negative","Positive"]],
d:[["c. 200 BC–AD 200","The Chinese Nine Chapters on the Mathematical Art uses negative numbers"],["628","Brahmagupta sets out rules for negative numbers and zero"],["c. 1200","Fibonacci interprets negative results as debts"],["1545","Gerolamo Cardano works with negative solutions but calls them fictitious"],["1637","René Descartes calls negative roots false roots"],["1700s","Negative numbers become widely accepted in European mathematics"]],
s2:[["Red and black rods","In ancient China, people calculated with counting rods laid out on a board. The Nine Chapters on the Mathematical Art, compiled between about 200 BC and AD 200, used red rods for positive numbers and black rods for negative numbers, with rules for adding and subtracting them to solve problems such as balancing accounts. This is the reverse of modern accounting, where red often means loss."],["Brahmagupta’s rules","In 628 the Indian mathematician Brahmagupta wrote rules for working with positive numbers, which he called fortunes, negative numbers, called debts, and zero. He stated, for example, that a debt minus zero is a debt and that the product of two debts is a fortune. His work was one of the first clear treatments of negative numbers as numbers in their own right."],["Suspicion in Europe","Many European mathematicians long distrusted negative numbers, asking how a quantity could be less than nothing. Some called negative solutions absurd, false or fictitious and ignored them. Only gradually, through their use in algebra, accounting and coordinate geometry, did they become fully accepted."],["Coordinates","In the 1600s, René Descartes and others developed coordinate geometry, in which points are located by numbers on two perpendicular axes. Negative numbers are needed to describe points to the left of or below the origin. Graphs, maps and computer screens all rely on such coordinates."],["Negative numbers in science","Scientists use negative numbers for electric charges, such as the negative charge of an electron, for temperatures below zero, for directions of motion and for changes such as falling prices or populations. Absolute zero, the coldest possible temperature, is about minus 273.15 degrees Celsius."],["Negative numbers in computers","Computers store negative numbers using clever binary codes, the most common being a method called two’s complement, which lets the same circuits add and subtract both positive and negative numbers. Very occasionally, errors in handling negative numbers have caused famous software bugs in games and systems."]],
sp:["Fortunes and debts","When Brahmagupta wrote his great astronomy and mathematics book in 628, he needed words for numbers greater and less than zero. He chose dhana, meaning fortune or wealth, for positive numbers and rina, meaning debt, for negative numbers. With these, his rules read almost like advice about money: a fortune plus a fortune is a fortune; a debt plus a debt is a debt; and a debt subtracted from zero becomes a fortune. Thinking of negative numbers as debts helped people understand them, and teachers around the world still use the idea of money owed to explain negative numbers to students."],
g:[["Negative number","A number less than zero"],["Integer","A whole number, positive, negative or zero"],["Number line","A line showing numbers in order"],["Absolute value","The distance of a number from zero"]],
f:["Negative numbers are less than zero.","Ancient Chinese mathematicians used negative numbers.","Negative times negative is positive."],
fun:"Ancient Chinese mathematicians used black rods for negative numbers and red rods for positive ones, the opposite of modern accounting colors."});

ADDART({id:"nobel",t:"The Nobel Prize",c:"History of Science",i:"🏅",w:"Nobel Prize",r:["scimeth","curie","hrights","particles","novel","janeaddams","redcross"],
p:[["The world’s best-known prizes","The Nobel Prizes honor outstanding achievements in science, literature and peace."],["Alfred Nobel’s will","They were created by the will of Alfred Nobel, the inventor of dynamite."],["Awarded since 1901","The first prizes were awarded in 1901."]],
l:"The Nobel Prizes are international awards given each year for outstanding contributions in physics, chemistry, physiology or medicine, literature and peace, along with a related prize in economic sciences. They were established by the will of Alfred Nobel, a Swedish chemist, engineer and businessman who invented dynamite and left most of his fortune to fund prizes for those who have conferred the greatest benefit on humankind. The first prizes were awarded in 1901. The prizes are presented each December, in Stockholm, Sweden, except for the Peace Prize, which is awarded in Oslo, Norway. They are widely regarded as the most prestigious awards in their fields. In 2021 journalist Maria Ressa became the first Filipino Nobel laureate, sharing the Peace Prize.",
s:[["Alfred Nobel","Alfred Nobel was born in Stockholm in 1833. He invented dynamite in 1867, a safer way to handle the powerful explosive nitroglycerin, and built factories and companies in many countries, becoming very wealthy. He held more than 350 patents. He never married and, when he died in 1896, left most of his fortune to create the prizes."],["The prize categories","Nobel’s will named five prizes: physics, chemistry, physiology or medicine, literature and peace. In 1968 Sweden’s central bank established a prize in economic sciences in Nobel’s memory, first awarded in 1969. Each prize can be shared by up to three people, and the Peace Prize can also go to organizations."],["How winners are chosen","Different institutions choose the winners. The Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences selects physics, chemistry and economics; the Karolinska Institute selects physiology or medicine; the Swedish Academy selects literature; and a committee appointed by the Norwegian parliament selects peace. Nominations come from invited experts, and records are kept secret for 50 years."],["The ceremonies","The prizes are presented on 10 December, the anniversary of Nobel’s death. Laureates receive a gold medal, a diploma and a cash award, and give lectures about their work. The ceremony in Stockholm is followed by a grand banquet, while the Peace Prize ceremony takes place in Oslo City Hall."]],
k:[["Founder","Alfred Nobel"],["First awarded","1901"],["Ceremony date","10 December"],["Cities","Stockholm and Oslo"],["First Filipino laureate","Maria Ressa, Peace, 2021"]],
d:[["1867","Alfred Nobel patents dynamite"],["1895","Nobel signs his will establishing the prizes"],["1901","The first Nobel Prizes are awarded"],["1903","Marie Curie becomes the first woman to win a Nobel Prize"],["1969","The first prize in economic sciences is awarded"],["2021","Maria Ressa becomes the first Filipino Nobel laureate"]],
s2:[["The merchant of death","According to a well-known story, in 1888 a French newspaper mistakenly published Alfred Nobel’s obituary when it was actually his brother Ludvig who had died. The article reportedly called him the merchant of death because his inventions were used in war. Historians debate how much the story influenced him, but it is often said to have encouraged him to leave a more positive legacy."],["Remarkable laureates","Marie Curie is the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different sciences, physics in 1903 and chemistry in 1911. Linus Pauling won two unshared prizes, chemistry and peace. Malala Yousafzai became the youngest laureate at 17 when she shared the Peace Prize in 2014. Some organizations, such as the International Committee of the Red Cross, have won the Peace Prize several times."],["Maria Ressa","In 2021 the Filipino journalist Maria Ressa, co-founder of the news website Rappler, shared the Nobel Peace Prize with Russian journalist Dmitry Muratov for their efforts to safeguard freedom of expression. She became the first Filipino to receive a Nobel Prize. The award was praised by press freedom groups, while her reporting and legal cases have been the subject of debate in the Philippines."],["Asian laureates","The first Asian laureate was the Indian poet Rabindranath Tagore, who won the literature prize in 1913. Since then, scientists, writers and peacemakers from Japan, China, India, South Korea, Vietnam, Myanmar, East Timor, Bangladesh and other Asian countries have received prizes."],["Criticism and controversy","The Nobel Prizes have been criticized for choosing mostly men and people from Europe and North America, for overlooking important figures, and for limiting science prizes to three people when modern research involves large teams. Some Peace Prize choices have been controversial. The Nobel institutions say they are working to recognize a wider range of achievements."],["The Ig Nobel Prizes","Since 1991, a humorous award called the Ig Nobel Prize has honored real research that first makes people laugh and then makes them think, such as studies of why woodpeckers do not get headaches. Real Nobel laureates often help present the awards, celebrating the curiosity behind science."]],
sp:["The will that surprised everyone","When Alfred Nobel died in Italy in December 1896, his family expected to inherit his large fortune. Instead, his will, signed in Paris in November 1895, left most of his wealth to fund annual prizes for physics, chemistry, physiology or medicine, literature and peace, to be given to those who had conferred the greatest benefit on humankind regardless of nationality. Relatives challenged the will, and the institutions he named were unsure at first how to carry out his wishes. It took several years of legal work by his assistant Ragnar Sohlman before the Nobel Foundation was established and the first prizes were awarded in 1901."],
g:[["Laureate","A person who has won a major prize such as the Nobel"],["Nobel Foundation","The organization that manages Nobel’s fortune and the prizes"],["Dynamite","An explosive invented by Alfred Nobel"],["Nomination","A formal suggestion of a candidate for a prize"]],
f:["The first Nobel Prizes were awarded in 1901.","Alfred Nobel invented dynamite.","Maria Ressa was the first Filipino Nobel laureate."],
fun:"Marie Curie is the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different sciences."});

