// Technology, more articles: ocean energy, biofuels, barcodes and QR codes, and shipping containers.
ADDART({id:"oceanenergy",t:"Ocean Energy",c:"Energy",i:"🌊",w:"Marine energy",r:["renew","tides","hydropower","energytransition","pacific"],
p:[["Power from the sea","Ocean energy is electricity made from tides, waves, currents and the heat of seawater."],["Tidal power","Tidal power plants have run in France since 1966 and in South Korea since 2011."],["Promise for islands","Island nations such as the Philippines have strong tidal currents that could be harnessed."]],
l:"Ocean energy, also called marine energy, is renewable energy captured from the movement and temperature of the sea. Its main forms are tidal energy, from the rise and fall of tides and the strong currents they create; wave energy, from the up-and-down motion of waves; ocean current energy; and ocean thermal energy conversion, which uses the temperature difference between warm surface water and cold deep water. Tides are highly predictable, which is an advantage over wind and solar power. Ocean energy still produces only a tiny share of the world’s electricity, because the technology is expensive and must survive harsh sea conditions, but researchers and companies are developing new devices. Archipelagos such as the Philippines, with many narrow straits and strong currents, are considered promising locations.",
s:[["Tidal barrages","A tidal barrage is a dam built across an estuary or bay. As the tide rises and falls, water flows through turbines in the dam, generating electricity. The La Rance tidal power station in France, opened in 1966, was the first large one, and the Sihwa Lake station in South Korea, opened in 2011, is now the largest."],["Tidal stream turbines","Tidal stream turbines work like underwater wind turbines, placed in places where tidal currents flow fast, such as narrow channels between islands. Because water is much denser than air, slower currents can still turn turbines powerfully. Projects in Scotland have operated arrays of such turbines."],["Wave energy","Wave energy devices capture the motion of waves using floating buoys, hinged rafts, oscillating water columns or underwater pads. Waves carry enormous energy, especially along coasts facing large oceans, but devices must survive storms and saltwater corrosion, and few have operated commercially for long."],["Ocean thermal energy","In tropical seas, surface water can be over 20 degrees Celsius warmer than water a kilometer deep. Ocean thermal energy conversion uses this difference to run a heat engine that produces electricity. Small pilot plants have operated in Hawaii and Japan, and tropical islands are seen as possible sites."]],
k:[["Main forms","Tidal, wave, current, thermal"],["First large tidal plant","La Rance, France, 1966"],["Largest tidal plant","Sihwa Lake, South Korea"],["Main advantage","Predictable tides"],["Main challenges","Cost and harsh sea conditions"]],
d:[["c. 1100s","Tide mills grind grain along European coasts"],["1966","La Rance tidal power station opens in France"],["1984","A tidal plant opens at Annapolis Royal in Canada"],["2011","South Korea’s Sihwa Lake tidal station begins operating"],["2016","Tidal stream turbines begin supplying power in Scotland"],["2020s","Several countries test new wave and tidal devices"]],
s2:[["Tide mills","People have used the power of tides for centuries. Medieval tide mills in Europe trapped water in a pond at high tide and released it through a waterwheel as the tide fell, grinding grain into flour. Some old tide mills survive as museums."],["Ocean energy in the Philippines","Studies have identified many straits in the Philippines with strong tidal currents, such as the San Bernardino Strait between Luzon and Samar and channels in the Visayas, as potential sites for tidal stream energy. The country’s renewable energy law includes ocean energy, and developers have proposed pilot projects. Supporters see it as a way to power islands that now rely on expensive diesel."],["Environmental questions","Tidal barrages can change water levels, sediment and salinity in estuaries, affecting fish, birds and wetlands. Tidal stream turbines and wave devices have smaller footprints but may affect marine animals, noise and fishing. Scientists study their effects to design projects that protect ecosystems."],["Why so little ocean energy?","Despite its huge potential, ocean energy remains small because devices are costly to build, install and maintain in rough seas, saltwater corrodes equipment, and connecting offshore devices to power grids is expensive. Wind and solar costs have fallen much faster. Governments and researchers hope costs will drop as technology matures."],["Predictable power","Unlike wind and sunlight, tides follow the Moon’s orbit and can be predicted years in advance. This makes tidal power valuable for grids that need reliable supplies, as it can complement solar and wind. However, tidal power peaks and dips during each tidal cycle, so it still requires storage or other sources."],["Other ocean ideas","Researchers are also exploring salinity gradient power, which captures energy released when fresh river water mixes with salty seawater, and floating solar farms on the sea. Offshore wind turbines, while not strictly ocean energy, are the most successful way of generating electricity at sea so far."]],
sp:["The dam that runs on the Moon","Across the mouth of the Rance River in Brittany, France, stretches a 750-meter dam that also serves as a road. Inside it, 24 turbines turn as the tides rush in and out of the estuary, where the difference between high and low tide can reach 13 meters, among the largest in the world. Since 1966 the La Rance power station has generated electricity powered, ultimately, by the gravitational pull of the Moon and Sun on Earth’s oceans. For decades it was the largest tidal power plant in the world, and it remains a popular attraction, showing how the rhythm of the tides can be turned into electric power."],
g:[["Ocean energy","Renewable energy captured from the sea"],["Tidal barrage","A dam that generates electricity from tides"],["Tidal stream","A fast current created by the tides"],["Ocean thermal energy conversion","Making electricity from temperature differences in seawater"]],
f:["La Rance in France opened in 1966.","Tides can be predicted years ahead.","Water is about 800 times denser than air."],
fun:"Because seawater is about 800 times denser than air, a slow tidal current can push a turbine as strongly as a very strong wind."});

ADDART({id:"biofuels",t:"Biofuels",c:"Energy",i:"🌽",w:"Biofuel",r:["energytransition","cars","foodsecurity","climchange","fossilfuels"],
p:[["Fuel from plants","Biofuels are fuels made from plants, algae or organic waste."],["Ethanol and biodiesel","The most common biofuels are ethanol, made from sugar or starch crops, and biodiesel, made from oils and fats."],["A debated solution","Biofuels can cut fossil fuel use, but they raise questions about food prices and land use."]],
l:"Biofuels are fuels produced from living things, such as crops, trees, algae and organic wastes, rather than from fossil fuels like oil and coal. The two most widely used are ethanol, an alcohol made by fermenting sugar from crops such as sugarcane and corn, and biodiesel, made from vegetable oils such as coconut, palm and soybean oil, or from used cooking oil and animal fats. Biofuels are usually blended with gasoline and diesel. Countries promote them to reduce oil imports, support farmers and cut greenhouse gas emissions. Brazil and the United States are the largest producers. The Philippines requires gasoline to contain ethanol and diesel to contain coconut-based biodiesel. Critics warn that growing fuel crops can compete with food, raise prices and drive deforestation.",
s:[["Ethanol","Ethanol is made by fermenting sugars with yeast, the same process used to make alcoholic drinks, and then distilling the alcohol. In Brazil it comes mainly from sugarcane, and in the United States mainly from corn. Most cars can run on gasoline with about 10 percent ethanol, called E10, and flex-fuel cars can use much higher blends."],["Biodiesel","Biodiesel is made by chemically treating vegetable oils or animal fats so they can be used in diesel engines. Rudolf Diesel himself demonstrated an engine running on peanut oil in 1900. In the Philippines, biodiesel is made from coconut oil and is known as coco-biodiesel or coconut methyl ester."],["Advanced biofuels","Newer biofuels are made from non-food materials, such as crop leftovers, grasses, wood chips, algae and used cooking oil. Sustainable aviation fuels made from such sources are being developed to reduce emissions from air travel, which is hard to power with batteries."],["How green are biofuels?","Plants absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, so burning fuels made from them can release less net carbon than fossil fuels. However, growing, harvesting, fertilizing and processing crops uses energy, and clearing forests or grasslands to plant fuel crops can release large amounts of carbon. Studies reach different conclusions depending on crops and methods."]],
k:[["Main types","Ethanol and biodiesel"],["Largest producers","United States and Brazil"],["Philippine law","Biofuels Act of 2006"],["Philippine biodiesel source","Coconut oil"],["Common blend","E10 (10 percent ethanol)"]],
d:[["1900","Rudolf Diesel shows an engine running on peanut oil"],["1908","Henry Ford’s Model T is designed to run on ethanol as well as gasoline"],["1975","Brazil launches its national ethanol program"],["2006","The Philippines passes the Biofuels Act"],["2007","Rising food prices spark a food versus fuel debate"],["2024","The Philippines raises the coconut biodiesel blend in diesel to 3 percent"]],
s2:[["Brazil’s ethanol","After the oil crisis of the 1970s, Brazil launched a national program in 1975 to produce ethanol from sugarcane. Today most new cars in Brazil are flex-fuel vehicles that can run on any mix of gasoline and ethanol, and all gasoline sold contains a large share of ethanol. Brazil’s sugarcane ethanol is considered one of the more efficient biofuels."],["Biofuels in the Philippines","The Biofuels Act of 2006 requires blending ethanol into gasoline and coconut biodiesel into diesel, aiming to reduce oil imports, cut emissions and support farmers. Gasoline contains about 10 percent ethanol, from sugarcane and imports. The biodiesel blend was raised to 3 percent in 2024, but a planned increase to 4 percent was suspended in 2025 because of high coconut oil prices and supply concerns, a decision debated by coconut farmers and the fuel industry."],["Food versus fuel","When food prices rose sharply in 2007 and 2008, some experts blamed the growing use of corn, sugar and vegetable oils for fuel. Others argued that oil prices, weather and other factors were more important. The debate continues, and many countries have limited the share of food crops used for biofuels."],["Palm oil and forests","Palm oil is used to make biodiesel in Indonesia, Malaysia and elsewhere. The expansion of oil palm plantations has been linked to the clearing of tropical rainforests and peatlands, threatening orangutans and other wildlife and releasing carbon. Certification schemes and rules aim to ensure sustainable production, though critics say they are not always effective."],["Used cooking oil","Used cooking oil from restaurants and food factories can be collected and turned into biodiesel or aviation fuel, reducing waste and avoiding the need for new crops. Demand for it has grown so much that authorities watch for fraud, such as fresh oil being sold as used."],["Biofuels and the future","Electric vehicles may reduce the need for biofuels in cars, but biofuels may remain important for ships, aircraft and heavy machinery. Researchers continue to explore algae and other sources that could produce fuel without competing for farmland."]],
sp:["The car that ran on alcohol","When Henry Ford designed the Model T, introduced in 1908, he made it able to run on gasoline, kerosene or ethanol. Ford was enthusiastic about fuel made from plants, imagining farmers growing their own fuel and saying that it could be made from anything that could be fermented, from fruit to weeds. Cheap gasoline soon won out, and ethanol was largely forgotten until the oil shocks of the 1970s. Today, more than a century later, the idea of running cars on fuel grown in fields is again part of the debate over how to reduce dependence on oil."],
g:[["Biofuel","A fuel made from plants, algae or organic waste"],["Ethanol","An alcohol fuel made by fermenting sugar"],["Biodiesel","A diesel fuel made from vegetable oils or fats"],["Flex-fuel vehicle","A car that can run on gasoline, ethanol or any mix"]],
f:["Philippine diesel contains coconut biodiesel.","Brazil makes ethanol from sugarcane.","Rudolf Diesel ran an engine on peanut oil."],
fun:"Rudolf Diesel demonstrated one of his engines running on peanut oil at the 1900 World’s Fair in Paris."});

ADDART({id:"qrcodes",t:"Barcodes and QR Codes",c:"Technology",i:"🔳",w:"QR code",r:["ecommerce","digitalpay","smartphone","binary","onlinescams","barcodes"],
p:[["Codes machines can read","Barcodes and QR codes store information in patterns that machines can scan."],["From groceries to payments","They are used to price groceries, track parcels, pay bills and open websites."],["Invented in 1994","The QR code was invented in Japan to track car parts."]],
l:"Barcodes and QR codes are patterns that store information in a form that machines can read quickly. A traditional barcode is a row of black and white lines of different widths that represents numbers, scanned by a laser or camera. The first product sold using a barcode scan was a pack of chewing gum in the United States in 1974. A QR code, short for quick response code, is a square grid of black and white modules that can store much more information, such as web addresses and payment details. It was invented in 1994 by the Japanese company Denso Wave to track car parts. With the spread of smartphone cameras, QR codes became part of daily life, used for payments, menus, tickets and contact tracing during the COVID-19 pandemic.",
s:[["How barcodes work","A barcode represents numbers using the widths of bars and spaces. A scanner shines light across the code and measures the reflected pattern, turning it into digits that a computer looks up in a database to find the product, its price and stock levels. The most common retail barcodes are the UPC in North America and EAN codes used in many other countries."],["How QR codes work","A QR code stores data in two directions, horizontally and vertically, so it can hold far more information than a barcode. Three large squares in the corners help scanners find and align the code from any angle. Error correction allows a QR code to be read even if part of it is damaged or covered."],["Everyday uses","Barcodes help shops speed up checkout and track inventory, airlines handle luggage, hospitals identify patients and medicines, and libraries manage books. QR codes link to websites, store event tickets and boarding passes, share contact details, connect to Wi-Fi and allow quick payments from phones."],["QR payments","In many Asian countries, including the Philippines, China and India, paying by scanning QR codes with mobile wallets has become very common, even at small stores and market stalls. The Philippines introduced a national QR code standard, called QR Ph, in 2019 so that different banks and e-wallets can accept the same codes."]],
k:[["First barcode scan at checkout","1974"],["QR code invented","1994"],["Inventor of QR code","Masahiro Hara, Denso Wave"],["QR stands for","Quick response"],["Philippine QR standard","QR Ph, 2019"]],
d:[["1952","Norman Woodland and Bernard Silver patent a barcode design"],["1974","A pack of chewing gum becomes the first product sold by scanning a UPC barcode"],["1994","Denso Wave invents the QR code"],["2000","The QR code becomes an international standard"],["2019","The Philippines launches the QR Ph standard for payments"],["2020","QR codes are widely used for contact tracing and menus during the COVID-19 pandemic"]],
s2:[["A barcode drawn in sand","According to a famous story, the barcode was inspired on a beach in Florida in 1948, when the engineer Norman Joseph Woodland drew lines in the sand with his fingers while thinking about Morse code, stretching dots and dashes into thin and thick lines. He and Bernard Silver patented a barcode design in 1952, though it took two decades and the invention of lasers and cheap computers before barcodes became practical."],["The first scan","At 8:01 in the morning on 26 June 1974, at a supermarket in Troy, Ohio, a cashier scanned a 10-pack of Wrigley’s chewing gum, the first retail product sold using a Universal Product Code. The pack is now kept in a museum in Washington, DC. Barcodes soon spread worldwide, changing how shops operate."],["Inventing the QR code","In the early 1990s, workers in Japanese car factories had to scan many barcodes to track parts. Masahiro Hara, an engineer at Denso Wave, a company related to Toyota, set out to design a code that could hold more information and be read faster. Inspired partly by the black and white stones of the game Go, he created the QR code in 1994. Denso Wave chose not to enforce its patent, allowing anyone to use it freely, which helped it spread."],["The pandemic boost","During the COVID-19 pandemic, QR codes became common for contactless menus, payments and health check-ins. In the Philippines, many local governments used QR-based contact tracing systems for entering stores and offices. The pandemic made millions of people comfortable scanning codes with their phones."],["QR code scams","Criminals sometimes place fake QR codes over real ones on parking meters, posters or payment stands, directing people to fraudulent websites or payment accounts, a scam sometimes called quishing. Experts advise checking the website address after scanning, avoiding codes that look tampered with and being cautious with codes received in unexpected messages."],["Beyond codes","Newer technologies such as RFID tags, which use radio waves, and near-field communication, used in contactless cards and phones, can identify items and allow payments without a visible pattern. Even so, barcodes and QR codes remain popular because they are cheap, easy to print and readable by almost any camera."]],
sp:["Inspired by a board game","Masahiro Hara liked to play Go, an ancient board game in which players place black and white stones on a grid. One day, while thinking about how to make a better code for tracking car parts, he imagined information arranged like stones on a Go board, in a pattern that could be read in two directions. His team experimented with designs and added three distinctive squares in the corners, carefully chosen so that their pattern would rarely appear in ordinary printed material, allowing scanners to find the code instantly. The result was the QR code, which can be read in a fraction of a second, even upside down."],
g:[["Barcode","A pattern of lines representing numbers for machines to read"],["QR code","A square, two-dimensional code that stores information"],["Scanner","A device that reads codes using light"],["Error correction","A method that lets damaged codes still be read"]],
f:["The first barcode scan was a pack of chewing gum in 1974.","The QR code was invented in Japan in 1994.","QR Ph is the Philippine payment QR standard."],
fun:"The inventor of the QR code was partly inspired by the black and white stones of the board game Go."});

ADDART({id:"containers",t:"Shipping Containers",c:"Transport",i:"🚢",w:"Containerization",r:["ships","ecommerce","canals","panamacountry","rail"],
p:[["Steel boxes","Shipping containers are standard steel boxes used to move goods around the world."],["A trade revolution","Containers made shipping faster and cheaper, transforming global trade."],["Giant ships","The largest container ships carry more than 20,000 containers."]],
l:"A shipping container is a large, standard-sized steel box used to transport goods by ship, train and truck without unloading the contents in between. Before containers, cargo was loaded piece by piece by teams of dockworkers, which was slow, costly and prone to theft and damage. In 1956 the American trucking businessman Malcom McLean sent the first commercially successful container ship from New Jersey to Texas. Containers were standardized internationally in the late 1960s, and container shipping grew rapidly, sharply reducing the cost of moving goods. Today millions of containers travel the world’s seas, and most manufactured goods, from clothes and electronics to food, spend part of their journey in one. Containerization is considered one of the most important forces behind globalization.",
s:[["Standard sizes","Most containers are 20 or 40 feet long, about 6 or 12 meters, and about 2.4 meters wide. Their size is measured in twenty-foot equivalent units, or TEU; a 40-foot container counts as two TEU. Standard corner fittings allow cranes, ships, trains and trucks around the world to lift and lock them in place."],["How containers changed shipping","Before containers, loading a ship could take days or weeks. With containers, giant cranes can load or unload a container in a couple of minutes, and ships spend far less time in port. Goods are sealed from factory to destination, reducing theft and damage. The cost of shipping fell dramatically."],["Container ships","Container ships have grown enormously, from a few hundred containers in the 1950s to more than 20,000 TEU today. The largest are about 400 meters long, longer than the Eiffel Tower is tall. They sail between major ports in Asia, Europe and the Americas along busy routes, many passing through the Suez and Panama canals."],["Ports","Container ports have huge cranes, stacking yards and connections to railways and highways. The busiest are in Asia, especially China, along with Singapore. In the Philippines, the Manila International Container Terminal is the largest, and other ports such as Batangas, Subic, Cebu and Davao handle container traffic."]],
k:[["First container ship voyage","Ideal X, 1956"],["Pioneer","Malcom McLean"],["Standard unit","TEU (twenty-foot equivalent unit)"],["Largest ships","More than 20,000 TEU"],["Largest Philippine container port","Manila International Container Terminal"]],
d:[["1956","Malcom McLean’s Ideal X carries containers from Newark to Houston"],["1966","The first transatlantic container service begins"],["1968","International standards for container sizes are agreed"],["1988","The Manila International Container Terminal begins private operation"],["2021","The container ship Ever Given blocks the Suez Canal for six days"],["2023","The largest container ships exceed 24,000 TEU"]],
s2:[["Malcom McLean’s idea","Malcom McLean, a trucking company owner in North Carolina, grew frustrated waiting at ports while his trucks’ cargo was unloaded by hand. He decided to load entire truck trailers, and then just their boxes, onto ships. On 26 April 1956 his converted oil tanker, the Ideal X, sailed from Newark, New Jersey, to Houston, Texas, carrying 58 containers. Loading costs fell dramatically compared with traditional methods."],["Globalization","By making shipping cheap and reliable, containers allowed companies to manufacture goods wherever costs were lowest and ship them around the world. Many economists consider containerization as important to globalization as trade agreements. It helped Asian economies, including China and Southeast Asian countries, become major exporters."],["Dockworkers and ports","Containers sharply reduced the number of dockworkers needed, causing job losses and strikes in many port cities. Old ports in city centers declined, while new container terminals were built on open land with deep water. Some former docklands, such as in London, were redeveloped as offices and homes."],["The Ever Given","In March 2021 the Ever Given, one of the world’s largest container ships, ran aground in the Suez Canal during a sandstorm, blocking it for six days. Hundreds of ships were stuck waiting, delaying global trade worth billions of dollars. The incident showed how much the world economy depends on container shipping routes."],["Supply chain shocks","During the COVID-19 pandemic, shortages of containers, port congestion and high demand caused shipping costs to soar and delayed deliveries worldwide. The Philippines and other import-dependent countries saw higher prices for goods. Businesses began rethinking long supply chains."],["Containers as buildings","Old shipping containers are often reused as homes, shops, offices, cafes, classrooms and emergency shelters. Container architecture can be quick and relatively cheap, though containers need insulation and ventilation, especially in hot climates. Container parks with food stalls have appeared in Philippine cities."]],
sp:["The box that changed the world","On 26 April 1956, a crowd gathered at a port in Newark, New Jersey, to watch an unusual sight. A crane lifted 58 aluminum truck bodies onto the deck of an old tanker named the Ideal X. Five days later, in Houston, trucks were waiting to collect the boxes and drive them straight to their destinations. Malcom McLean calculated that loading cost had fallen from several dollars per metric ton to just a few cents. Dockworkers and shipping companies were skeptical at first, but within a few decades almost all general cargo traveled in containers, and the humble steel box had reshaped the world economy."],
g:[["Shipping container","A standard steel box for moving goods"],["TEU","Twenty-foot equivalent unit, a measure of container capacity"],["Containerization","The use of standard containers to move goods"],["Supply chain","The steps that bring a product from maker to buyer"]],
f:["The first container ship voyage was in 1956.","A 40-foot container equals two TEU.","The Ever Given blocked the Suez Canal in 2021."],
fun:"The largest container ships can carry more than 24,000 twenty-foot containers, enough to hold hundreds of millions of bananas."});

