// Arts and Culture, more articles: stage magic, film festivals, kulintang and tattooing.
ADDART({id:"stagemagic",t:"Stage Magic",c:"Performing Arts",i:"🎩",w:"Magic (illusion)",r:["theatre","circus","illusions","psychology","tv"],
p:[["The art of illusion","Stage magic is the performing art of creating seemingly impossible effects."],["Tricks and secrets","Magicians use skill, misdirection and clever devices, not real magic."],["Famous magicians","Performers such as Robert-Houdin and Harry Houdini made magic world-famous."]],
l:"Stage magic is a performing art in which entertainers, called magicians or illusionists, create effects that seem impossible, such as making objects appear and vanish, reading minds, escaping from chains or sawing a person in half. Magicians do not use supernatural powers; they rely on sleight of hand, misdirection, psychology, hidden mechanisms and careful rehearsal. Magic has been performed for thousands of years, from street performers in ancient times to grand theater shows, television specials and close-up tricks at parties. The French magician Jean-Eugène Robert-Houdin is often called the father of modern magic, and Harry Houdini became famous worldwide for his daring escapes. Magicians traditionally keep their methods secret.",
s:[["Kinds of magic","Close-up magic is performed right in front of a small audience, often with cards, coins or everyday objects. Stage illusions use large props and assistants on a theater stage. Mentalism creates the impression of mind reading or prediction. Escape artistry involves breaking free from locks, ropes, handcuffs or tanks of water."],["Misdirection","A key skill in magic is misdirection: guiding the audience’s attention away from the secret move. A magician may look at one hand while the other does the work, tell a joke at the crucial moment or use a big gesture to draw eyes elsewhere. Scientists studying attention have learned from magicians."],["Sleight of hand","Sleight of hand, or legerdemain, is the skillful use of the hands to secretly move, hide or switch objects. Card and coin magicians practice moves for many hours to make them look natural and invisible. Some techniques have been passed down for centuries."],["The magician’s code","Magicians generally follow an informal code: never reveal the secret of a trick to non-magicians, never perform the same trick twice for the same audience and always practice. Magic societies such as The Magic Circle in London, founded in 1905, promote the art and share knowledge among members."]],
k:[["Father of modern magic","Jean-Eugène Robert-Houdin"],["Famous escape artist","Harry Houdini"],["Key techniques","Misdirection, sleight of hand"],["The Magic Circle founded","1905, London"],["Famous illusion","Sawing a person in half"]],
d:[["c. 2500 BC","Egyptian stories tell of magicians performing wonders"],["1584","Reginald Scot’s book explains how conjuring tricks are done"],["1845","Robert-Houdin opens his theater of magic in Paris"],["1905","The Magic Circle is founded in London"],["1921","The illusion of sawing a woman in half is first performed"],["1983","David Copperfield makes the Statue of Liberty appear to vanish on television"]],
s2:[["Robert-Houdin","Jean-Eugène Robert-Houdin, a French clockmaker who became a magician, opened a small theater in Paris in 1845. Instead of the traditional wizard’s robes, he performed in elegant evening clothes, presenting magic as refined entertainment. He used his mechanical skills to build ingenious automata and illusions. Later magicians honored him as the father of modern magic."],["Harry Houdini","Harry Houdini, born Erik Weisz in Hungary in 1874 and raised in the United States, became the most famous magician in history through his escapes from handcuffs, straitjackets, locked trunks and water tanks. He also spent his later years exposing fake mediums who claimed to contact the dead. He died in 1926."],["Magic and science","Magicians and scientists have long been connected. Some magicians, like Robert-Houdin, were skilled engineers. Others have helped scientists test people claiming psychic powers. Today, neuroscientists study magic tricks to understand attention, perception and memory, because magic reveals how easily the brain can be fooled."],["Exposing fake powers","Some people have claimed real supernatural powers, such as communicating with spirits or bending spoons with their minds. Magicians such as Houdini and later James Randi argued that such feats could be done with ordinary tricks and challenged performers to prove their powers under controlled conditions."],["Magic on screen","Television brought magic to huge audiences. Large illusions, such as making landmarks vanish or walking through walls, were staged as television specials, while street magic shows filmed reactions of passersby. Online videos have made magic tricks, and sometimes their secrets, widely accessible."],["Learning magic","Many magicians begin as children with simple tricks from magic sets and books. Learning magic builds hand skills, confidence and public speaking. Magic clubs, conventions and competitions bring together amateurs and professionals, including in the Philippines, where magicians perform at parties, events and on television."]],
sp:["Sawing a woman in half","In 1921 the British magician P. T. Selbit presented a shocking new illusion in London: a woman lay inside a wooden box, and he appeared to saw straight through it, and her. The trick caused a sensation, and theater managers reportedly placed nurses and ambulances outside to add to the drama. Soon many magicians were performing their own versions, and arguments broke out over who had invented it. A century later, the illusion remains one of the most famous in magic, and its secret, though widely guessed, still delights audiences when performed well."],
g:[["Magician","A performer who creates illusions"],["Misdirection","Guiding attention away from a secret move"],["Sleight of hand","Skillful secret movement of the hands"],["Mentalism","Magic that seems to show mind reading"]],
f:["Robert-Houdin is called the father of modern magic.","Houdini was famous for escapes.","The Magic Circle was founded in 1905."],
fun:"Harry Houdini took his stage name in honor of the French magician Jean-Eugène Robert-Houdin."});

ADDART({id:"filmfestivals",t:"Film Festivals",c:"Film",i:"🎞️",w:"Film festival",r:["cinema","phcinema","documentary","anim","festivals","hollywood"],
p:[["Celebrating films","Film festivals are events where new films are shown, judged and celebrated."],["Famous festivals","Venice, Cannes and Berlin are among the oldest and most prestigious."],["Filipino films abroad","Filipino filmmakers and actors have won top awards at international festivals."]],
l:"A film festival is an organized event, usually held in one city over several days, where films are screened for audiences, critics and filmmakers, and often compete for awards. Festivals introduce new films, launch careers, help independent and foreign films find audiences and distributors, and celebrate cinema as an art. The oldest major festival, the Venice Film Festival, began in 1932, followed by Cannes in France and Berlin in Germany, which are often considered the three most prestigious. Thousands of festivals now take place worldwide, from huge events to small local ones. In the Philippines, the Metro Manila Film Festival, held every Christmas season since 1975, and independent festivals such as Cinemalaya are major events, and Filipino filmmakers have won top prizes abroad.",
s:[["What happens at a festival","Festivals screen new films, often for the first time, called premieres. Juries of filmmakers, actors and critics award prizes. Filmmakers attend to present their work, answer audience questions and meet distributors who buy rights to show films in cinemas, on television or on streaming services. Many festivals also hold film markets and workshops."],["The big three","The Venice Film Festival, founded in 1932, awards the Golden Lion. The Cannes Film Festival, first held in 1946, awards the Palme d’Or and is famous for its red carpet on the French Riviera. The Berlin International Film Festival, begun in 1951, awards the Golden Bear. Together they are often called the big three."],["Other major festivals","The Toronto International Film Festival in Canada is a major showcase and is often seen as an early indicator for awards season. The Sundance Film Festival in the United States champions independent films. The Busan International Film Festival in South Korea, founded in 1996, is one of Asia’s leading festivals."],["Independent cinema","Festivals are especially important for independent filmmakers who work outside big studios with small budgets. A festival award or strong reviews can help a small film find a distributor and an audience. Many celebrated directors first gained attention at festivals."]],
k:[["Oldest major festival","Venice, 1932"],["Cannes top prize","Palme d’Or"],["Berlin top prize","Golden Bear"],["Metro Manila Film Festival","Since 1975"],["Philippine independent festival","Cinemalaya, since 2005"]],
d:[["1932","The first Venice Film Festival is held"],["1946","The first Cannes Film Festival takes place"],["1951","The Berlin International Film Festival begins"],["1975","The first Metro Manila Film Festival is held"],["2005","The Cinemalaya Philippine Independent Film Festival is launched"],["2016","Lav Diaz wins the Golden Lion and Jaclyn Jose wins best actress at Cannes"]],
s2:[["Why Cannes began","The Cannes Film Festival was planned for 1939 partly in response to the Venice festival, which had come under the influence of Italy’s fascist government. The first edition was canceled when the Second World War began, and the festival finally opened in 1946. It has since become a glamorous event attended by film stars from around the world."],["Filipino triumphs abroad","Filipino filmmakers have won major international awards. Brillante Mendoza won best director at Cannes in 2009 for Kinatay. In 2016 Jaclyn Jose became the first Southeast Asian actor to win best actress at Cannes, for Ma’ Rosa, and Lav Diaz won the Golden Lion at Venice for The Woman Who Left, after winning a Silver Bear in Berlin earlier that year. Earlier, directors such as Lino Brocka brought Filipino films to Cannes in the 1970s and 1980s."],["The Metro Manila Film Festival","Every year from Christmas Day into early January, cinemas across Metro Manila, and later the whole country, show only Filipino films entered in the Metro Manila Film Festival. Families often watch films together during the holidays. The festival features a parade of floats and an awards night. Debates often arise over whether entries should favor commercial blockbusters or artistic films."],["Cinemalaya and independent film","Cinemalaya, launched in 2005 at the Cultural Center of the Philippines, gives grants to independent filmmakers and screens their works, helping launch many new directors and actors. Other festivals, such as QCinema in Quezon City and regional film festivals, have expanded opportunities for Filipino independent cinema."],["Festivals and streaming","Streaming services now buy many festival films, and some festivals debate whether films made mainly for streaming should compete alongside films made for cinemas. The COVID-19 pandemic led festivals to hold online editions, making films accessible to wider audiences."],["Small and local festivals","Beyond the famous events, thousands of festivals focus on particular kinds of films, such as short films, documentaries, animation, children’s films, environmental films or films from certain regions. Student film festivals in schools and universities help young filmmakers share their work."]],
sp:["A Filipino on the Croisette","In May 2016, at the Cannes Film Festival, the jury announced the best actress award, and the name called was Jaclyn Jose, for her role in Brillante Mendoza’s Ma’ Rosa, about a mother who runs a small store in a poor Manila neighborhood and is arrested in a drug raid. She became the first Filipino and first Southeast Asian actor to win the prize. Back home, the news was celebrated in newspapers and on social media, and the award brought international attention to Filipino cinema. Jose, a veteran of many films and television shows, died in 2024, remembered as one of the country’s finest actors."],
g:[["Film festival","An event where films are screened and often judged"],["Premiere","The first public showing of a film"],["Palme d’Or","The top prize at the Cannes Film Festival"],["Distributor","A company that brings films to cinemas or streaming services"]],
f:["Venice is the oldest major film festival.","The Metro Manila Film Festival began in 1975.","Jaclyn Jose won best actress at Cannes in 2016."],
fun:"The first Cannes Film Festival was planned for September 1939 but was canceled when the Second World War broke out."});

ADDART({id:"kulintang",t:"Kulintang",c:"Music",i:"🔔",w:"Kulintang",r:["phdance","indianmusic","drums","orchestra","phfolk"],
p:[["A row of gongs","The kulintang is a row of small, bossed gongs played with sticks."],["Music of Mindanao","It is the heart of gong ensembles of the Maguindanao, Maranao and other peoples."],["Shared across islands","Related gong traditions are found in Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei."]],
l:"The kulintang is a musical instrument made of a row of small, knobbed gongs laid horizontally on a wooden frame and played with two wooden beaters. It is also the name of the ensemble it leads, which includes larger hanging gongs and a drum. Kulintang music is a living tradition of peoples in the southern Philippines, especially the Maguindanao, Maranao and Tausug of Mindanao and the Sulu Archipelago, and related gong-chime traditions exist in Indonesia, Malaysia, Brunei and East Timor. Played at weddings, festivals, celebrations and gatherings, kulintang music is mostly learned by ear and features lively rhythms and improvised melodies. It is a proud symbol of the cultural heritage of the Bangsamoro and other Mindanao peoples.",
s:[["The instrument","A kulintang set usually has eight gongs of different sizes and pitches, arranged from lowest to highest in a row on a wooden frame called an antangan. Each gong has a raised knob, or boss, in the center, which the player strikes with soft wooden beaters to produce clear, ringing tones."],["The ensemble","The kulintang leads an ensemble that, among the Maguindanao, may include the agung, a pair of large hanging gongs with deep tones; the gandingan, four large hanging gongs; the babendil, a small gong played on its rim for rhythm; and the dabakan, a goblet-shaped drum. Each instrument has its own role in creating a rich, layered sound."],["How it is played","Kulintang music is based on rhythmic modes rather than fixed written compositions. Musicians learn by listening and imitating, and the kulintang player improvises melodies within the patterns of a chosen mode. Performances can be energetic and virtuosic, and good players are admired for their creativity."],["Where it is heard","Kulintang music is played at weddings, religious and community celebrations, welcoming guests and festivals. It once also played a role in courtship, as young people performed for one another. Today it is also performed in schools, cultural shows and concerts in the Philippines and abroad."]],
k:[["Instrument","Row of small knobbed gongs"],["Typical number of gongs","Eight"],["Main peoples","Maguindanao, Maranao, Tausug"],["Ensemble instruments","Agung, gandingan, babendil, dabakan"],["Region","Mindanao, Sulu and neighboring countries"]],
d:[["c. 1000s","Gong-chime instruments spread through maritime Southeast Asia"],["1500s","Gongs are traded across the Sulu and Celebes seas"],["1950s","Researchers begin recording kulintang music in Mindanao"],["1960s","Folk dance companies bring kulintang music to world stages"],["1993","Kutyapi master Samaon Sulaiman receives the National Living Treasures Award"],["2000s","University programs in the Philippines and abroad teach kulintang"]],
s2:[["Origins","Gongs came to Southeast Asia through trade, possibly from mainland Asia, many centuries ago. Local peoples developed their own instruments and music, including gong-chime instruments such as the kulintang in the southern Philippines, the bonang of Java and the kulintangan of Borneo. Bronze gongs were also valued as heirlooms, dowry gifts and symbols of wealth."],["Maguindanao and Maranao styles","Different peoples have distinct kulintang styles. Maguindanao music is often described as lively and rhythmically complex, while Maranao music is known for its flowing melodies. Players from each group recognize the modes and pieces of their own tradition."],["Women and kulintang","Traditionally, the kulintang has often been played by women, while men frequently play the agung and other instruments, though both may play all instruments. Skilled kulintang players are highly respected in their communities."],["Kulintang and dance","Kulintang music accompanies traditional dances of Mindanao, such as the singkil, a Maranao dance in which a princess steps gracefully between clapping bamboo poles. Philippine folk dance companies, such as the Bayanihan, have performed these dances with kulintang music around the world."],["Masters of the tradition","Master musicians have helped preserve kulintang music. Some, such as the Maguindanao musician Danongan Kalanduyan, taught in the United States, where kulintang ensembles formed in universities and Filipino American communities. The Philippines’ National Living Treasures Award has honored masters of Mindanao music, such as Samaon Sulaiman, a master of the kutyapi lute."],["Keeping the music alive","Changing lifestyles, conflict and the popularity of modern music have challenged traditional music in parts of Mindanao. Schools of living traditions, cultural centers, universities and community groups teach young people to play the kulintang and other instruments, helping keep the tradition alive."]],
sp:["The singkil and the gongs","In the Maranao dance called singkil, a princess wearing a tall headdress and holding fans steps calmly in and out of pairs of bamboo poles that are clapped together in faster and faster rhythms, while an attendant shades her with an umbrella. The dance is said to retell an episode from the Darangen, the great epic of the Maranao people. Behind the dancers, musicians play the kulintang, gongs and drum, their ringing patterns guiding the dancers’ steps. When performed by folk dance companies on stages around the world, the singkil and its gong music have become one of the best-known images of Philippine culture."],
g:[["Kulintang","A row of small, knobbed gongs, and the ensemble it leads"],["Boss","The raised knob in the center of a gong"],["Agung","A pair of large hanging gongs"],["Dabakan","A goblet-shaped drum used in kulintang ensembles"]],
f:["A kulintang usually has eight gongs.","It is played by peoples of Mindanao and Sulu.","Kulintang music is learned by ear."],
fun:"In some Mindanao communities, sets of bronze gongs were valued heirlooms and could be given as part of a marriage dowry."});

ADDART({id:"tattoo",t:"Tattooing",c:"Art",i:"🖋️",w:"Tattoo",r:["skin","phfolk","modernart","streetart","caveart"],
p:[["Art on skin","Tattooing is the art of putting permanent designs into the skin with ink."],["Ancient roots","People have been tattooed for more than 5,000 years."],["Philippine traditions","Peoples such as the Kalinga and Visayans have long tattoo traditions."]],
l:"A tattoo is a permanent design made by inserting ink or pigment into the dermis, the layer of skin beneath the surface. Tattooing is one of the oldest art forms: Ötzi the Iceman, who lived about 5,300 years ago, had dozens of tattoos. Across the world, tattoos have marked identity, status, bravery, religious devotion, healing, love and memory. In the Philippines, many peoples had rich tattoo traditions; Spanish chroniclers called Visayans pintados, the painted ones, and the Kalinga of the Cordillera still practice traditional hand-tapped tattooing, made famous by the master tattooist Apo Whang-od. Tattoos have had different meanings in different times and places, being honored in some cultures and stigmatized in others. Today they are widely popular as personal art.",
s:[["How tattoos are made","Traditional methods include tapping ink into the skin with a sharpened stick, thorn or comb, or pricking it with needles. Modern tattoo machines, invented in 1891, use electrically powered needles that move up and down rapidly. The ink settles in the dermis, where it stays for life, although it may fade over time."],["Meanings of tattoos","In many traditional cultures, tattoos showed a person’s family, community, achievements or passage into adulthood. Warriors might earn tattoos for bravery, and women might receive them at marriage or for beauty. Some tattoos were believed to protect or heal. Today people get tattoos for many personal reasons, such as remembering loved ones or expressing their identity."],["Tattoos around the world","Polynesian peoples of the Pacific developed elaborate tattoo traditions; the English word tattoo comes from the Samoan or Tahitian word tatau, brought to Europe after Captain James Cook’s voyages in the 1760s and 1770s. Japan has a long tradition of full-body tattoos called irezumi. The Māori of New Zealand practice tā moko, including facial designs."],["Health and safety","Tattooing breaks the skin, so it must be done with sterile, single-use needles, clean equipment and safe inks to prevent infections such as hepatitis. Licensed studios follow hygiene rules. Laws in many places, including the Philippines, set age limits for getting tattoos. Tattoos can be removed with lasers, but removal is costly and may not be complete."]],
k:[["Oldest known tattooed person","Ötzi the Iceman, about 5,300 years ago"],["Word origin","Polynesian tatau"],["Modern tattoo machine","1891"],["Spanish name for tattooed Visayans","Pintados"],["Famous Kalinga tattoo artist","Apo Whang-od"]],
d:[["c. 3300 BC","Ötzi the Iceman bears about 61 tattoos"],["c. 1590","The Boxer Codex illustrates tattooed Visayans"],["1769","Captain James Cook’s crew records the Tahitian word tatau"],["1891","Samuel O’Reilly patents an electric tattoo machine"],["1990s","Traditional tattooing begins a revival among Filipinos and Pacific peoples"],["2023","Apo Whang-od appears on the cover of Vogue Philippines at age 106"]],
s2:[["The pintados","When Spanish explorers arrived in the Visayas in the 1500s, they were struck by the extensive tattoos of many men and women, covering arms, chests, backs and faces. They called the islands the Islas de los Pintados, the islands of the painted ones. Tattoos showed bravery in battle and were considered marks of beauty. The Boxer Codex, an illustrated manuscript from around 1590, shows tattooed Visayans."],["Kalinga batok","Among the Kalinga of the Cordillera in northern Luzon, traditional tattooing, called batok or batek, is done by tapping a thorn attached to a stick with another stick, using ink made of soot and water. In the past, men earned tattoos as warriors, while women were tattooed for beauty and identity. The designs represent things such as centipedes, snakes, ferns, rice terraces and eagles."],["Apo Whang-od","Whang-od Oggay of Buscalan, Kalinga, born around 1917, became known as the last traditional mambabatok of her generation. Thousands of visitors have traveled to her mountain village to receive her tattoos, and she has trained younger relatives to carry on the tradition. In 2023 she appeared on the cover of Vogue Philippines, becoming its oldest cover model. Her fame has brought tourism and attention, along with debates about protecting the tradition’s cultural meaning."],["Tattoos and stigma","In many places, tattoos have at times been associated with sailors, soldiers, prisoners or gangs, and people with tattoos have faced discrimination in jobs or public places. In Japan, some baths and gyms still restrict people with visible tattoos. In the Philippines, attitudes have changed, though some employers and institutions have rules about visible tattoos."],["Revival of traditional tattoos","In recent decades, many Indigenous peoples, including Filipinos at home and abroad, Polynesians and the Māori, have revived traditional tattooing as a way to reconnect with ancestors and identity. Filipino tattoo artists and researchers have studied old records and elders’ memories to bring back traditional designs, with attention to their meanings."],["Tattoos today","Tattoos are now popular across many countries and age groups, with styles ranging from fine-line and watercolor designs to realistic portraits and traditional patterns. Tattoo conventions attract artists and fans, and tattoo artists are increasingly recognized as skilled artists."]],
sp:["The village in the clouds","Reaching Buscalan, a village high in the mountains of Kalinga, once meant hours of travel by road followed by a steep hike up rice terrace paths. Yet thousands of visitors have made the journey, hoping to receive a tattoo from Apo Whang-od, the legendary tattoo artist who learned her craft as a teenager. Using a thorn from a citrus tree fixed to a bamboo stick, she taps soot-based ink into the skin with a steady rhythm, creating designs passed down for generations. In her later years, her signature was often three simple dots. Her grandnieces and other young women now continue the tradition, ensuring that Kalinga tattooing lives on."],
g:[["Tattoo","A permanent design made by putting ink into the skin"],["Dermis","The layer of skin beneath the surface where tattoo ink stays"],["Batok","Traditional Kalinga hand-tapped tattooing"],["Pintados","The painted ones, a Spanish name for tattooed Visayans"]],
f:["Ötzi the Iceman had about 61 tattoos.","The word tattoo comes from Polynesia.","Apo Whang-od is a famous Kalinga tattoo artist."],
fun:"Spanish explorers named the Visayan islands the islands of the painted ones because so many people there were tattooed."});

;
/* data/earth7.js */
// Planet Earth, more articles: how mountains form, coasts and river deltas.
ADDART({id:"mountainform",t:"How Mountains Form",c:"Earth Science",i:"🏔️",w:"Mountain formation",r:["tect","himal","volc","everest","apo"],
p:[["Built by moving plates","Most mountains form where Earth’s plates collide, pull apart or push up magma."],["Folds, faults and volcanoes","Mountains can be folded, raised along faults or built by volcanic eruptions."],["Worn down by erosion","Wind, water and ice slowly wear mountains away."]],
l:"Mountains are large landforms that rise high above the surrounding land. Most are created by the slow movement of the tectonic plates that make up Earth’s outer shell. When plates collide, rock layers can be crumpled and pushed upward into fold mountains, as in the Himalayas, which formed when India crashed into Asia. When the crust is stretched or squeezed, blocks of rock can be lifted along faults. Where molten rock reaches the surface, volcanoes build mountains from layers of lava and ash, like Mayon and Mount Fuji. Mountain building takes millions of years, and at the same time weathering and erosion by rain, rivers, ice and wind slowly wear mountains down. The Philippines’ mountains were built mainly by volcanic activity and plate collisions along its active margins.",
s:[["Fold mountains","When two plates push against each other, layers of rock buckle and fold like a rug pushed against a wall. Over millions of years, these folds rise into long mountain ranges. The Himalayas, Alps, Andes and Rockies all include folded rock layers. Fossils of sea creatures are found high in the Himalayas, showing that the rocks once lay beneath an ocean."],["Fault-block mountains","Where Earth’s crust is stretched or compressed, it can crack along faults. Huge blocks of rock may be pushed up on one side and drop down on the other, forming steep mountain fronts. The Sierra Nevada in the United States and parts of the Great Rift Valley in Africa have mountains formed this way."],["Volcanic mountains","Volcanoes form where molten rock, or magma, rises to the surface, often along the edges of plates. Repeated eruptions pile up lava and ash into cone-shaped mountains. Many of the Philippines’ highest and most famous peaks, such as Mount Apo, Mayon Volcano and Mount Kanlaon, are volcanoes."],["Dome mountains","Sometimes magma pushes up beneath the surface without erupting, bending the overlying rock into a dome. When erosion later removes the softer outer layers, a rounded mountain of hard rock may be left standing."]],
k:[["Main cause","Movement of tectonic plates"],["Types","Fold, fault-block, volcanic, dome"],["Highest mountains","Himalayas, formed by India colliding with Asia"],["Everest’s rise","A few millimeters a year"],["Philippine example","Mount Apo, a volcano"]],
d:[["c. 480 million years ago","Mountain building begins to form the Appalachians"],["c. 50 million years ago","India begins colliding with Asia, raising the Himalayas"],["c. 35 million years ago","The Alps begin rising as Africa pushes against Europe"],["1912","Alfred Wegener proposes continental drift"],["1960s","Plate tectonics explains how mountains form"],["2020","China and Nepal announce a new height for Everest of 8,848.86 meters"]],
s2:[["Plate tectonics","Until the 20th century, scientists struggled to explain why mountains exist. In 1912 Alfred Wegener proposed that continents drift slowly across Earth’s surface, but his idea was widely rejected. In the 1960s, discoveries about the seafloor led to the theory of plate tectonics, which showed that colliding and separating plates build mountains, cause earthquakes and form volcanoes."],["The Himalayas","About 50 million years ago, the Indian plate, moving north, collided with the Eurasian plate. Because both were continental crust, neither sank easily, so the rock crumpled upward to form the Himalayas and the Tibetan Plateau. The collision continues, and the Himalayas are still rising by a few millimeters each year, though erosion and earthquakes also change their height."],["Mountains of the Philippines","The Philippines lies where several plates meet, including the Philippine Sea Plate and the Eurasian Plate. Subduction, where one plate slides beneath another, melts rock and feeds volcanoes, while collisions and faults have raised mountain ranges such as the Cordillera Central of Luzon and the Sierra Madre, the country’s longest mountain range. Mount Apo in Mindanao, the country’s highest peak, is a dormant volcano."],["Erosion and the life of mountains","As soon as mountains rise, rain, rivers, glaciers, frost and wind begin to wear them down. Young mountains, such as the Himalayas, are high and jagged, while old mountains, such as the Appalachians in North America, have been worn into lower, rounded hills over hundreds of millions of years."],["Mountains under the sea","The longest mountain range on Earth is mostly underwater: the mid-ocean ridge system winds around the globe for more than 60,000 kilometers. It forms where plates pull apart and new crust is created by rising magma. Some volcanic mountains rise from the seafloor to form islands, such as Hawaii, whose Mauna Kea is taller than Everest when measured from its base on the ocean floor."],["Why mountains matter","Mountains shape weather, causing rain on one side and dry areas on the other. They store water in snow and glaciers, are home to unique plants and animals, and support millions of people. Many mountains are sacred in different cultures, and they attract climbers, hikers and tourists."]],
sp:["Seashells on the roof of the world","Near the summit of Mount Everest, nearly nine kilometers above sea level, the rock contains fossils of tiny sea creatures, such as trilobites and crinoids, that lived on the floor of an ancient ocean hundreds of millions of years ago. Climbers have found these fossils in a band of limestone called the Yellow Band and the summit rocks. How did seafloor end up at the top of the world? The answer is plate tectonics: as India pushed into Asia, the seabed between them was crumpled and lifted higher and higher, eventually becoming the tallest mountains on Earth."],
g:[["Tectonic plate","A large moving piece of Earth’s outer shell"],["Fold mountain","A mountain formed by rock layers bending under pressure"],["Fault","A crack in Earth’s crust along which rocks move"],["Subduction","When one plate slides beneath another"]],
f:["The Himalayas formed when India collided with Asia.","Everest’s summit contains marine fossils.","Mount Apo is a dormant volcano."],
fun:"Mauna Kea in Hawaii is taller than Mount Everest when measured from its base on the ocean floor."});

ADDART({id:"coasts",t:"Coasts",c:"Geography",i:"🏖️",w:"Coast",r:["sea","tides","reef","sealevel","tsunami"],
p:[["Where land meets sea","A coast is the strip of land along the edge of the sea."],["Always changing","Waves, tides and currents constantly shape coasts by erosion and deposition."],["A long Philippine coastline","The Philippines has one of the longest coastlines in the world."]],
l:"A coast, or coastline, is the area where land meets the sea or ocean. Coasts include beaches, cliffs, bays, headlands, estuaries, mangroves, dunes and coral shores. They are among the most dynamic places on Earth, constantly reshaped by waves, tides, currents, wind, storms and changes in sea level. Waves erode some coasts, carving cliffs, caves and arches, while others grow as sand and sediment are deposited, forming beaches, spits and deltas. Coasts are home to a large share of the world’s people and cities, support fisheries, ports and tourism, and provide habitats for many species. As an archipelago of thousands of islands, the Philippines has one of the longest coastlines in the world, making coastal management especially important.",
s:[["Erosion","Waves carry enormous energy. They pound against rocks, hurl stones and sand, force air into cracks and slowly dissolve some rocks. Over time, they carve cliffs, wave-cut platforms, sea caves, arches and stacks, isolated pillars of rock standing offshore. Soft rocks erode faster than hard rocks, creating bays and headlands."],["Deposition","Where waves are gentler, they drop sand, pebbles and shells, building beaches. Currents moving along the shore, called longshore drift, carry sand along the coast and can build spits, long ridges of sand extending into the sea, and tombolos, which connect islands to the mainland."],["Living coasts","Mangrove forests, salt marshes, seagrass beds and coral reefs form living coastlines. They protect shores from waves and storms, trap sediment, and provide nurseries for fish and homes for birds and other wildlife. The Philippines has extensive mangroves and coral reefs, though many have been damaged."],["Coasts and people","Many of the world’s largest cities, including Manila, Tokyo, Shanghai, Mumbai and New York, lie on coasts. People depend on coasts for fishing, trade, tourism and recreation. But building too close to the shore, removing sand, cutting mangroves and polluting the sea can damage coasts and increase risks from storms and erosion."]],
k:[["Shaped by","Waves, tides, currents, wind"],["Erosion landforms","Cliffs, caves, arches, stacks"],["Deposition landforms","Beaches, spits, tombolos"],["Philippine coastline","About 36,000 km"],["Natural defenses","Mangroves, reefs, dunes"]],
d:[["c. 20,000 years ago","Sea levels are about 120 meters lower than today during the last ice age"],["c. 7,000 years ago","Sea levels approach their present heights, shaping modern coasts"],["1953","A North Sea storm surge floods coasts in the Netherlands and Britain"],["2004","The Indian Ocean tsunami devastates coasts across Asia"],["2005","One of the Twelve Apostles sea stacks in Australia collapses into the sea"],["2013","Typhoon Haiyan’s storm surge strikes the coasts of Leyte and Samar"]],
s2:[["The Philippine coastline","With more than 7,000 islands, the Philippines has a coastline of roughly 36,000 kilometers, among the longest in the world. Many Filipinos live in coastal towns and depend on the sea for food and work. Its coasts range from white sand beaches and limestone cliffs, as in Palawan, to mangrove-lined bays and busy port cities."],["Coastal hazards","Coasts face hazards such as storm surges, tsunamis, erosion, flooding and sea level rise. In the Philippines, typhoons bring powerful winds and surges, and many coastal communities have been hit hard. Early warnings, evacuation plans, protective mangroves and careful land use help reduce risks."],["Protecting coasts","Coastal defenses include hard structures such as sea walls, breakwaters and groynes, and soft approaches such as replenishing beaches with sand and restoring mangroves, dunes and reefs. Hard defenses can protect one area but may increase erosion nearby, so engineers increasingly combine methods and work with nature."],["Reclamation","Land reclamation creates new land by filling in shallow seas. Cities such as Singapore, Hong Kong and parts of Manila Bay have expanded this way. Supporters see reclamation as a way to create space for development, while critics warn of effects on fisheries, flooding, ecosystems and coastal communities."],["Famous coastal landforms","Well-known coastal features include the White Cliffs of Dover in England, the sea stacks of the Twelve Apostles in Australia, the fjords of Norway and the limestone islands and lagoons of El Nido and Coron in Palawan. Many are protected and attract visitors from around the world."],["Coastlines and measurement","Measuring the length of a coastline is surprisingly tricky, because the result depends on how closely one measures every bay and inlet. A coast measured with a shorter ruler appears longer. This puzzle, known as the coastline paradox, helped inspire the mathematical idea of fractals."]],
sp:["The coastline paradox","How long is the coast of the Philippines? The answer depends on how you measure it. Using a ruler 100 kilometers long, you would skip over many bays and coves and get a shorter total. Using a ruler 1 kilometer long, you would follow more twists and turns, and the length would grow. With a ruler one meter long, tracing around every rock, it would be longer still. In the 1960s the mathematician Benoit Mandelbrot wrote about this puzzle, called the coastline paradox, and it helped inspire his ideas about fractals, shapes that show similar detail at every scale."],
g:[["Coast","The land along the edge of the sea"],["Erosion","The wearing away of land by water, wind or ice"],["Longshore drift","The movement of sand along a coast by waves"],["Sea stack","A pillar of rock left standing in the sea by erosion"]],
f:["The Philippines has one of the world’s longest coastlines.","Waves carve cliffs, caves and arches.","Mangroves protect coasts from storms."],
fun:"The measured length of a coastline gets longer the more closely you measure it, a puzzle called the coastline paradox."});

ADDART({id:"deltas",t:"River Deltas",c:"Geography",i:"🔺",w:"River delta",r:["nile","mekong","ganges","mississippi","sealevel"],
p:[["Where rivers meet the sea","A delta is land built from sediment where a river flows into a sea or lake."],["Fertile and crowded","Deltas are rich farmland and home to hundreds of millions of people."],["Sinking and threatened","Many deltas are sinking and threatened by rising seas."]],
l:"A river delta is a landform created where a river flows into a sea, ocean or lake and slows down, dropping the sand, silt and clay it carries. Over thousands of years, this sediment builds up into low, flat land, often split by many channels called distributaries. The name comes from the Greek letter delta, shaped like a triangle, which the historian Herodotus used to describe the Nile’s delta. Deltas include some of the most fertile and densely populated places on Earth, such as the Nile, Ganges–Brahmaputra, Mekong and Mississippi deltas, and support farming, fishing and major cities. However, many deltas are sinking, losing sediment because of dams and threatened by rising seas, storms and pollution.",
s:[["How deltas form","Rivers carry sediment eroded from mountains and land. When a river reaches still water, it slows and can no longer carry its load, so the sediment settles. Layers build up, and the river splits into branches that spread sediment across a wide area, gradually extending the land outward."],["Shapes of deltas","Deltas take different shapes depending on the balance between the river, waves and tides. The Nile delta is a classic triangle, or fan. The Mississippi delta has long fingers, called a bird’s-foot shape. In places with strong tides, such as the Ganges–Brahmaputra delta, the land is cut by many tidal channels."],["Rich land","Delta soils, renewed by floods that spread fresh silt, are very fertile. For thousands of years, deltas have supported intensive farming, especially rice. The Mekong delta in Vietnam is one of the world’s great rice-growing regions, and the Nile delta was the breadbasket of ancient Egypt."],["Deltas and people","Hundreds of millions of people live on deltas, which also host large cities such as Cairo and Alexandria, Dhaka, Kolkata, Ho Chi Minh City, Bangkok, Shanghai and New Orleans. Their flat land and waterways make transport and trade easy."]],
k:[["Named after","The Greek letter delta (Δ)"],["Named by","Herodotus, about 450 BC"],["Largest delta","Ganges–Brahmaputra"],["Bird’s-foot delta","Mississippi"],["Main threats","Sinking land, dams, rising seas"]],
d:[["c. 5,000 years ago","Many of today’s great deltas begin forming as sea levels stabilize"],["c. 450 BC","Herodotus describes the delta of the Nile"],["1970","The Aswan High Dam is completed, cutting sediment to the Nile delta"],["1970","The Bhola cyclone devastates the Ganges delta"],["2005","Hurricane Katrina floods New Orleans on the Mississippi delta"],["2008","Cyclone Nargis strikes Myanmar’s Irrawaddy delta"]],
s2:[["The Sundarbans","The Ganges–Brahmaputra delta, shared by Bangladesh and India, is the largest delta in the world. Its seaward edge holds the Sundarbans, the world’s largest mangrove forest and a UNESCO World Heritage Site, home to Bengal tigers, crocodiles and river dolphins. The delta is also extremely densely populated."],["Sinking deltas","Many deltas are sinking because sediment naturally compacts, groundwater and gas are pumped out, and dams upstream trap sediment that would otherwise rebuild the land. Combined with rising sea levels, this means some deltas are losing land. Parts of the Mississippi delta lose wetlands at a rapid rate."],["Storms and floods","Low-lying deltas are very vulnerable to cyclones and storm surges. The 1970 Bhola cyclone in what is now Bangladesh and Cyclone Nargis in Myanmar in 2008 caused enormous loss of life. Improved warnings, cyclone shelters and evacuation plans have greatly reduced deaths in Bangladesh since then."],["Deltas in the Philippines","The Philippines has many river deltas, including those of the Pampanga River flowing into Manila Bay, the Cagayan River in northern Luzon and the Rio Grande de Mindanao near Cotabato City. The Pampanga delta region, with its fishponds and farms, faces flooding made worse by land subsidence, a concern for nearby towns."],["Dams and sediment","Dams provide electricity, water and flood control, but they trap sediment upstream. The Aswan High Dam in Egypt, completed in 1970, greatly reduced the sediment reaching the Nile delta, which began to erode along its coast. Planners now look for ways to let more sediment pass through dams."],["Saving deltas","Approaches to protecting deltas include restoring wetlands and mangroves, allowing controlled floods to spread sediment, limiting groundwater pumping, building flood defenses and planning where people live. The Netherlands, built largely on the delta of the Rhine, Meuse and Scheldt rivers, has long experience managing delta land."]],
sp:["A triangle named by a historian","Around 450 BC, the Greek historian Herodotus traveled to Egypt and marveled at the land at the mouth of the Nile, where the river split into branches and spread across a broad, triangular plain before reaching the Mediterranean Sea. Its shape reminded Greek writers of the letter delta, Δ, and the name stuck. Herodotus also observed that the land seemed to be built from mud carried by the river, and called Egypt the gift of the Nile. Today the word delta is used for river mouths all over the world, whatever their shape."],
g:[["Delta","Land built from sediment at the mouth of a river"],["Sediment","Sand, silt and clay carried by water"],["Distributary","A branch of a river that flows away from the main channel"],["Subsidence","The gradual sinking of land"]],
f:["Deltas are named after the Greek letter delta.","The Ganges–Brahmaputra delta is the largest.","Many deltas are sinking."],
fun:"The historian Herodotus called Egypt the gift of the Nile because its fertile land was built by the river’s mud."});

