ADDART({id:"pangolins",t:"Pangolins",c:"Mammals",i:"🦔",w:"Pangolin",r:["ants","termite","biodivloss","conservation","palawan","anteaters"],
p:[["Scaly mammals","Pangolins are the only mammals covered in hard scales."],["Ant eaters","They eat ants and termites with long, sticky tongues."],["Most trafficked","They are among the most illegally traded mammals in the world."]],
l:"Pangolins are shy, nocturnal mammals covered in overlapping scales made of keratin, the same material as human fingernails. There are eight species, four in Asia and four in Africa, including the Palawan pangolin, found only in the Philippines. Pangolins have no teeth; they use strong claws to break open ant and termite nests and catch insects with a sticky tongue that can be longer than their body. When threatened, they roll into a tight, armored ball. Pangolins are thought to be among the most trafficked mammals in the world, hunted for their meat and for their scales, which are used in some traditional medicines despite having no proven healing properties. All species are threatened, and international trade in them is banned.",
s:[["Armor of scales","A pangolin’s scales cover most of its body and make up a large share of its weight. When danger comes, it curls into a ball, protecting its soft belly. Its tough scales can defend it even from lions, which may roll it around but cannot bite through."],["Tongue and claws","Pangolins have no teeth. Their tongues can stretch up to about 40 centimeters, longer than their bodies in some species, and are anchored near the pelvis. They swallow small stones that help grind food in their stomachs. A pangolin may eat tens of millions of insects a year."],["Eight species","Asia has the Chinese, Sunda, Indian and Philippine, or Palawan, pangolins; Africa has the giant, ground, white-bellied and black-bellied pangolins. Some live mainly on the ground, digging burrows, while others climb trees using their prehensile tails."],["Life of a pangolin","Pangolins are solitary and mostly active at night. A mother usually has a single baby, which rides on the base of her tail and curls up with her when she rolls into a ball. Pangolins are hard to keep in captivity because of their specialized diet."]],
k:[["Species","Eight"],["Scales made of","Keratin"],["Diet","Ants and termites"],["Philippine species","Palawan pangolin"],["International trade","Banned since 2017"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the pangolin genus"],["1998","The Palawan pangolin is described as a separate species"],["2014","All pangolin species are listed as threatened"],["2016","Countries agree to ban international trade in pangolins"],["2019","The Palawan pangolin is listed as critically endangered"],["2020","China removes pangolin scales from its official list of traditional medicines"]],
s2:[["The Palawan pangolin","The Palawan pangolin, called balintong locally, lives only in Palawan and nearby islands. It is critically endangered because of poaching and habitat loss. Philippine authorities have seized large shipments of pangolins and scales, and conservation groups work with communities to protect the animals."],["Illegal trade","Pangolins are hunted for meat considered a delicacy in some places and for scales used in traditional remedies. Scientists say the scales have no medical benefit. Huge seizures, sometimes of several metric tons of scales, have been made in Asia and Africa."],["Protecting pangolins","In 2016 countries agreed under the CITES treaty to ban all commercial international trade in pangolins, which took effect in 2017. Conservation efforts include stronger law enforcement, campaigns to reduce demand, rescue and rehabilitation centers and community patrols."],["Pangolins and ecosystems","By eating huge numbers of ants and termites, pangolins help control insect populations, and their digging aerates soil. Their burrows can also provide shelter for other animals."],["Pangolins and disease","During the COVID-19 pandemic, scientists investigated whether pangolins might have played a role in spreading the virus, because related coronaviruses were found in smuggled pangolins. The evidence did not show that pangolins were the source, but the research highlighted risks from wildlife trade."],["World Pangolin Day","World Pangolin Day, held on the third Saturday of February, raises awareness about these little-known animals. Many people first learn on that day that pangolins exist and that they are in danger."]],
sp:["The armored ball","When a pangolin senses danger, it does something remarkable: it tucks its head into its belly, wraps its scaly tail around its body and becomes a tight, armored ball. Its scales have sharp edges, and the muscles holding it closed are so strong that a person can hardly pry it open. This defense works well against lions and leopards. Sadly, it is useless against human poachers, who can simply pick the curled-up animal off the ground. This is one reason pangolins are so vulnerable to illegal hunting."],
g:[["Pangolin","A scaly mammal that eats ants and termites"],["Keratin","A tough protein that forms scales, hair and nails"],["Nocturnal","Active at night"],["Trafficking","Illegal trade, for example in wildlife"]],
f:["Pangolins are the only scaly mammals.","They have no teeth.","The Palawan pangolin lives only in the Philippines."],
fun:"A pangolin’s tongue is attached near its hip bones and can be longer than its body."});

ADDART({id:"dugongs",t:"Dugongs and Manatees",c:"Sea Life",i:"🐋",w:"Dugong",r:["coral","whale","seaturtle","conservation","palawan"],
p:[["Gentle sea cows","Dugongs and manatees are large, slow marine mammals that graze on seagrass and water plants."],["Related to elephants","Their closest living relatives on land are elephants."],["Threatened","Habitat loss, fishing nets and boat strikes threaten them."]],
l:"Dugongs and manatees are large, plant-eating marine mammals known together as sirenians or sea cows. The dugong lives in warm coastal waters of the Indian and western Pacific oceans, including the Philippines, and feeds almost entirely on seagrass. Three species of manatees live in the Atlantic region, in coastal waters and rivers of the Americas and West Africa. Sirenians are slow, gentle and long-lived, and their closest relatives on land are elephants. They are thought to have inspired some old sailors’ tales of mermaids. Today all sirenians are vulnerable, threatened by the loss of seagrass beds, accidental capture in fishing nets, boat strikes and hunting. In the Philippines, dugongs are protected by law.",
s:[["Dugongs","Dugongs can grow about three meters long and weigh around 400 kilograms. They have a fluked tail like a whale and a downturned snout suited to grazing on the seafloor. They spend most of their time eating seagrass, pulling up whole plants and leaving feeding trails across seagrass meadows."],["Manatees","Manatees have rounded, paddle-shaped tails and live in the Caribbean, Florida, the Amazon River and West Africa. Some move between salt water and fresh water. They are sometimes seen in warm springs and near power plants in winter, where water stays warm."],["Life of a sea cow","Sirenians breathe air and surface every few minutes. They live long lives, sometimes 70 years for dugongs, but reproduce slowly, with females giving birth to one calf every few years. Calves stay with their mothers for a year or more."],["Seagrass gardeners","By grazing, dugongs help keep seagrass meadows healthy and spread their seeds. Seagrass beds are nurseries for fish, store carbon and protect coasts. Losing seagrass to pollution, dredging and coastal development is a major threat to dugongs."]],
k:[["Group name","Sirenians or sea cows"],["Diet","Seagrass and water plants"],["Closest land relatives","Elephants"],["Dugong length","About 3 meters"],["Philippine name","Dugong or baboy-dagat"]],
d:[["1741","Steller’s sea cow is discovered in the Bering Sea"],["1768","Steller’s sea cow is hunted to extinction"],["1973","Manatees are protected under the US Endangered Species Act"],["1982","Dugongs are listed as vulnerable"],["2001","The Philippines passes the Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act"],["2020s","Communities in Palawan monitor dugongs and protect seagrass beds"]],
s2:[["Dugongs in the Philippines","Dugongs live in scattered populations in the Philippines, with important areas around northern Palawan, including Busuanga and Coron, and parts of Mindanao. Local fishers and conservation groups monitor them and protect seagrass, and some communities have become dugong guardians. Dugong watching, done responsibly, supports local tourism."],["Mermaid legends","Sailors long ago may have mistaken dugongs or manatees, glimpsed at a distance holding calves to their chests, for mermaids. The scientific name of the group, Sirenia, comes from the sirens of Greek mythology. In 1493 Christopher Columbus wrote that he had seen mermaids that were not as beautiful as he had imagined, probably manatees."],["Steller’s sea cow","A giant relative, Steller’s sea cow, lived in the cold Bering Sea and could grow about eight meters long. It was discovered by European explorers in 1741 and hunted to extinction for its meat within 27 years, one of the fastest extinctions caused by humans."],["Threats","Dugongs and manatees drown when caught in fishing nets, are injured by boat propellers and lose food when seagrass dies. In some places they are still hunted. Pollution and algae blooms have caused large die-offs of manatees in Florida in recent years."],["Protection","Dugongs are protected under Philippine law, and killing or trading them is illegal. Protected areas, rules on fishing nets, boat speed limits and seagrass restoration help sirenians. International agreements also promote their conservation across their range."],["Elephants of the sea","Fossils show that sirenians evolved from four-legged land mammals about 50 million years ago, sharing ancestors with elephants and hyraxes. Like elephants, they have teeth that are replaced from the back of the jaw, and manatees have nails on their flippers resembling elephant toenails."]],
sp:["The dugong guardians of Busuanga","In the waters of northern Palawan, fishers who once saw dugongs only as accidental catches have become their protectors. Trained by conservation groups and local governments, some volunteer as wardens, monitoring dugongs and reporting threats. Visitors who join guided tours may glimpse a dugong surfacing for air before gliding back down to graze in the seagrass. Strict rules keep boats at a distance and forbid touching or chasing the animals. The program shows how protecting wildlife can also bring income to coastal communities."],
g:[["Sirenian","A member of the group that includes dugongs and manatees"],["Seagrass","A flowering plant that grows underwater in shallow seas"],["Fluke","The flat, horizontal tail of a dugong or whale"],["Vulnerable","At high risk of becoming endangered"]],
f:["Dugongs eat seagrass.","Their closest land relatives are elephants.","Steller’s sea cow went extinct in 1768."],
fun:"Christopher Columbus reported seeing mermaids in 1493 that were not as beautiful as described; they were probably manatees."});

ADDART({id:"sloths",t:"Sloths",c:"Mammals",i:"🦥",w:"Sloth",r:["amazon","amazonriver","monkeys","animalbehavior","extinction","anteaters"],
p:[["Slow movers","Sloths are tree-dwelling mammals famous for moving very slowly."],["Upside-down life","They spend most of their lives hanging upside down in rainforest trees."],["Giant ancestors","Extinct ground sloths were as big as elephants."]],
l:"Sloths are slow-moving mammals that live in the tropical rainforests of Central and South America. There are six living species, divided into two-toed and three-toed sloths, named after the number of claws on their front limbs. Sloths spend almost their entire lives hanging upside down from branches, where they eat, sleep, mate and even give birth. They move slowly because their diet of leaves provides little energy and takes a long time to digest. Algae grow in their fur, giving them a greenish tint that helps camouflage them. Their slow, quiet lifestyle makes them hard for predators to notice. Thousands of years ago, giant ground sloths as large as elephants roamed the Americas.",
s:[["Two-toed and three-toed","Both groups actually have three toes on their back feet; the names refer to the claws on their front limbs. Two-toed sloths are somewhat larger and eat leaves, fruit and sometimes insects, while three-toed sloths eat mostly leaves and have a face that looks like it is smiling."],["Why so slow","Sloths eat leaves, which are low in energy and hard to digest. Their many-chambered stomachs may take days or even weeks to digest a meal. To save energy, sloths have a slow metabolism, little muscle and a low body temperature, and they move only a few meters a minute at most."],["A hanging life","Sloths’ long, curved claws hook over branches, and special tendons let them hang without effort, even while asleep. Some of their organs are attached to their ribs so that they do not press on their lungs while upside down. They can turn their heads almost all the way around."],["A walking ecosystem","Sloth fur grows in the opposite direction to that of most mammals, so rain runs off when they hang upside down. Grooves in their hairs host algae, as well as moths, beetles and fungi. The algae give camouflage and may provide some nutrients."]],
k:[["Living species","Six"],["Home","Central and South American rainforests"],["Diet","Mostly leaves"],["Famous trait","Very slow movement"],["Extinct relatives","Giant ground sloths"]],
d:[["c. 35 million years ago","Early sloths appear in South America"],["c. 10,000 years ago","Giant ground sloths die out in the Americas"],["1796","Georges Cuvier describes the giant ground sloth Megatherium"],["1990s","Sloth sanctuaries begin rescuing injured animals in Costa Rica"],["2014","A study explains how sloths’ fur moths and algae help each other"],["2010s","Sloths become popular symbols of a relaxed lifestyle online"]],
s2:[["Giant ground sloths","Extinct relatives of sloths, such as Megatherium, could weigh several metric tons and reach about six meters when standing on their hind legs. They lived on the ground and browsed on trees. They disappeared about 10,000 years ago, around the time humans spread through the Americas and the climate changed."],["The weekly trip down","Sloths climb down from their trees only about once a week, to relieve themselves at the base of the tree. This is risky, because they are vulnerable on the ground. Scientists have suggested reasons, such as fertilizing their tree or delivering the moths that live in their fur to lay eggs in the dung."],["Surprisingly good swimmers","Although slow on land, sloths are good swimmers. They use their long arms to paddle and can slow their heart rate to hold their breath for a long time underwater. They sometimes swim across rivers to reach new trees."],["Threats","Sloths are threatened by deforestation, power lines, road traffic, dogs and the illegal pet trade. One species, the pygmy three-toed sloth, lives only on a tiny island off Panama and is critically endangered. Rescue centers care for injured and orphaned sloths."],["Sloths and people","Sloths have become popular on social media and in films as symbols of relaxation. Conservationists warn that taking selfies with captive sloths in tourist attractions can stress the animals and encourage their capture from the wild."],["Name and meaning","The word sloth also means laziness, one of the seven deadly sins in Christian tradition. Scientists point out that sloths are not lazy but well adapted to an energy-saving lifestyle that has helped them survive for millions of years."]],
sp:["The slowest trip to the bathroom","For a sloth, the weekly trip to the forest floor is a major event. Slowly, it climbs down its tree, digs a small hole with its tail, relieves itself and covers it up, then climbs back up. A sloth can lose a large share of its body weight in one go. This journey takes precious energy and exposes it to predators such as jaguars. Scientists are still not completely sure why sloths take this risk rather than simply letting their droppings fall from the branches, and the question has inspired several research studies."],
g:[["Sloth","A slow-moving tree mammal of the Americas"],["Metabolism","The chemical processes that use energy in the body"],["Camouflage","Coloring that helps an animal blend into its surroundings"],["Megatherium","An extinct giant ground sloth"]],
f:["There are six living species of sloth.","Algae grow in sloth fur.","Giant ground sloths were as big as elephants."],
fun:"Sloths can hold their breath underwater for a long time by slowing their heart rate."});

ADDART({id:"otters",t:"Otters",c:"Mammals",i:"🦦",w:"Otter",r:["sea","rodents","wetlands","palawan","conservation"],
p:[["Playful swimmers","Otters are playful, fish-eating mammals that live in rivers, lakes and seas."],["Sea otters","Sea otters use stones as tools and hold hands while sleeping."],["In the Philippines","The Asian small-clawed otter lives in Palawan."]],
l:"Otters are carnivorous mammals in the weasel family, adapted for life in and around water. There are 13 species, living in rivers, lakes, wetlands and coastal seas on every continent except Australia and Antarctica. Otters have long, streamlined bodies, webbed feet and thick, waterproof fur, which keeps them warm. They feed mainly on fish, crabs, shellfish and other water animals. Otters are known for their playful behavior, such as sliding down muddy banks. Sea otters, which live in the North Pacific, use stones to crack open shellfish and are a key species that keeps kelp forests healthy. In the Philippines, the Asian small-clawed otter, the smallest otter species, lives in Palawan.",
s:[["Built for water","Otters have streamlined bodies, strong tails for swimming and webbed feet. They can close their ears and nostrils underwater. Their whiskers detect movement in murky water, helping them find prey."],["The thickest fur","Sea otters have the densest fur of any animal, with up to around a million hairs per square centimeter in places. Unlike whales and seals, they have no thick layer of blubber, so they rely on air trapped in their fur to stay warm and spend much time grooming."],["Tool users","Sea otters float on their backs and use stones to crack open clams, sea urchins and mussels on their bellies. They often keep a favorite stone tucked in a pouch of loose skin under their arm. They are among the few mammals besides primates known to use tools."],["Social life","Some otters live alone, while others, such as giant otters and small-clawed otters, live in family groups. Otters communicate with whistles, chirps and squeaks. Sea otters sometimes hold paws or wrap themselves in kelp while sleeping so they do not drift apart."]],
k:[["Species","13"],["Family","Weasel family (mustelids)"],["Densest fur","Sea otter"],["Smallest species","Asian small-clawed otter"],["Largest species","Giant otter of South America"]],
d:[["1741","European explorers encounter sea otters in the North Pacific"],["1911","An international treaty protects sea otters from fur hunting"],["1938","A small group of sea otters is rediscovered off California"],["1970s","Studies show sea otters protect kelp forests by eating sea urchins"],["2010s","Smooth-coated otters thrive in the city waterways of Singapore"],["2015","The Asian small-clawed otter is listed as vulnerable"]],
s2:[["Otters in the Philippines","The Asian small-clawed otter, known locally as dungon, is the only otter found in the Philippines and lives in Palawan’s rivers, mangroves and wetlands. Weighing only a few kilograms, it uses its nimble, almost clawless fingers to feel for crabs and shellfish. It is threatened by habitat loss, pollution and the illegal pet trade."],["Sea otters and kelp forests","Sea otters eat sea urchins, which graze on kelp. Where otters were hunted out, urchin numbers exploded and destroyed kelp forests. Where otters returned, kelp recovered, along with the many fish and animals that live in it. Ecologists call sea otters a keystone species."],["Saved from the fur trade","Sea otters were hunted almost to extinction for their luxurious fur in the 1700s and 1800s. An international treaty in 1911 ended commercial hunting. Small surviving groups slowly recovered, though populations in some areas remain threatened by oil spills and disease."],["Giant otters","Giant otters of the Amazon can grow nearly two meters long, including the tail. They live in noisy family groups, hunt fish together and defend their territories from other groups and even caimans."],["City otters","In Singapore, families of smooth-coated otters have made the city’s rivers and reservoirs their home, becoming local celebrities with their own fan groups. Their return is seen as a sign of cleaner waterways, though residents sometimes complain when otters raid fish ponds."],["Otters and pets","Videos of pet otters have become popular online, but conservationists warn that the demand fuels illegal capture from the wild, especially of small-clawed otters in Asia. Otters need water, space and the company of other otters, and do not make good pets."]],
sp:["Holding hands at sea","Sea otters spend much of their lives floating on their backs in the cold waters of the North Pacific. When they rest, groups called rafts gather together, and some otters hold paws or wrap strands of kelp around their bodies like anchors so they do not drift apart while sleeping. Mothers carry their fluffy pups on their bellies, grooming them and keeping them out of the cold water. Photos of hand-holding sea otters have charmed people around the world and helped draw attention to the need to protect their ocean home."],
g:[["Otter","A water-loving mammal in the weasel family"],["Keystone species","A species that has a large effect on its ecosystem"],["Kelp","Large brown seaweed that forms underwater forests"],["Raft","A group of resting sea otters"]],
f:["There are 13 otter species.","Sea otters use stones as tools.","Palawan is home to the Asian small-clawed otter."],
fun:"Sea otters often keep a favorite stone tucked in a skin pouch under their arm to use for cracking shellfish."});

ADDART({id:"hyenas",t:"Hyenas",c:"Mammals",i:"🐾",w:"Hyena",r:["lion","grasslands","animalbehavior","cheetah","wolf"],
p:[["Skilled hunters","Hyenas are powerful carnivores of Africa and parts of Asia."],["Not just scavengers","Spotted hyenas hunt most of their own food."],["Female leaders","In spotted hyena clans, females are in charge."]],
l:"Hyenas are carnivorous mammals found across Africa and parts of the Middle East and South Asia. There are four species: the spotted hyena, the striped hyena, the brown hyena and the aardwolf, which eats mainly termites. Although they look somewhat like dogs, hyenas are more closely related to cats and mongooses. Hyenas are often portrayed as cowardly scavengers, but spotted hyenas are skilled hunters that catch most of their own food, often working in groups to bring down large animals such as wildebeest and zebras. Their powerful jaws can crush bones, which they digest completely. Spotted hyenas live in large clans led by females and communicate with a range of calls, including the famous laugh.",
s:[["Four species","The spotted hyena is the largest and most social. Striped hyenas live in northern and eastern Africa, the Middle East and India and are more solitary. Brown hyenas live in dry southern Africa. The small aardwolf eats termites, licking up hundreds of thousands in a night."],["Bone-crushing jaws","Hyenas have some of the strongest bites of any mammal, able to crack open large bones to reach the marrow. Their digestive systems can process bones, hooves and even teeth, leaving droppings that turn white from the calcium."],["Hunters and scavengers","Spotted hyenas hunt animals ranging from gazelles to wildebeest and zebras, chasing them over long distances. They also scavenge, and both hyenas and lions steal food from each other. Their reputation as mere scavengers is a myth."],["Clan life","Spotted hyenas live in clans that can number dozens of members, with a strict ranking. Females are larger and dominant over males, and cubs inherit their mother’s rank. Clan members greet each other with special rituals and cooperate to defend territory."]],
k:[["Species","Four"],["Largest species","Spotted hyena"],["Closest relatives","Mongooses and cats"],["Termite-eating hyena","Aardwolf"],["Clan leaders","Females"]],
d:[["c. 25 million years ago","Early hyena ancestors appear"],["c. 10,000 years ago","Cave hyenas die out in Europe and Asia"],["1777","The spotted hyena is described scientifically"],["1960s","Field studies reveal spotted hyenas as skilled hunters"],["1994","The Lion King portrays hyenas as villains"],["2010s","Long-term studies in Kenya reveal the complexity of hyena societies"]],
s2:[["The hyena’s laugh","Spotted hyenas make a range of sounds, including whoops that carry for kilometers and a high-pitched giggle often called laughing. The giggle usually happens when hyenas are excited or frustrated, for example when competing for food. Scientists have found that the calls carry information about age and status."],["Rivalry with lions","Lions and spotted hyenas compete for the same prey and frequently steal kills from each other. Large groups of hyenas can drive lions away, while male lions often kill hyenas. This rivalry is one of the most dramatic relationships on the African savanna."],["Cave hyenas","During the last ice age, cave hyenas, larger relatives of the spotted hyena, lived across Europe and Asia. They left behind bones in caves, along with the remains of animals they ate. Ancient people painted them on cave walls."],["Myths and reputation","Hyenas have often been feared or disliked. In some African folklore they are linked with witchcraft, and in films they are cast as villains. Researchers argue that hyenas are intelligent, social animals that play an important role in cleaning up carcasses and keeping ecosystems healthy."],["Intelligence","Spotted hyenas have shown impressive problem-solving and social intelligence, cooperating to solve puzzles and recognizing relationships among clan members. Some scientists compare their social skills to those of primates."],["Threats","Hyenas are killed by people who see them as threats to livestock, and their habitats are shrinking. Striped and brown hyenas are classed as near threatened. Spotted hyenas remain common in protected areas such as the Serengeti but have declined elsewhere."]],
sp:["Queens of the clan","In a spotted hyena clan, even the lowest-ranking adult female outranks the highest-ranking male. Females are larger and more aggressive, and young hyenas take on their mother’s place in the hierarchy, supported by her allies. When a new male joins a clan from outside, he starts at the bottom and must win acceptance. Scientists studying hyenas in Kenya for decades have found that a cub’s social connections, inherited from its mother, strongly influence its chances of survival and success."],
g:[["Hyena","A carnivorous mammal of Africa and Asia"],["Clan","A large group of spotted hyenas"],["Scavenger","An animal that eats dead animals"],["Aardwolf","A small, termite-eating member of the hyena family"]],
f:["There are four hyena species.","Spotted hyenas hunt most of their food.","Female spotted hyenas lead their clans."],
fun:"Hyena droppings can turn white because their bodies digest the calcium from the bones they eat."});

ADDART({id:"leopards",t:"Leopards",c:"Mammals",i:"🐆",w:"Leopard",r:["tiger","lion","cheetah","cats","amazon"],
p:[["Spotted big cats","Leopards are powerful big cats with spotted coats called rosettes."],["Masters of adaptation","They live in more kinds of places than any other big cat."],["Tree climbers","Leopards often drag their prey up into trees."]],
l:"The leopard is a large wild cat found across much of Africa and parts of Asia, from deserts and grasslands to rainforests and mountains. It has a golden coat covered with dark spots grouped into rosettes, which provide camouflage in dappled light. Leopards are strong, solitary and secretive hunters that stalk prey at night, catching animals from insects to antelopes. They are excellent climbers and often haul their kills into trees, out of reach of lions and hyenas. Leopards are the most widespread big cat, but they have disappeared from much of their former range because of habitat loss and hunting, and some subspecies, such as the Amur leopard, are critically endangered. Black leopards, often called black panthers, have dark coats due to a genetic variation.",
s:[["Spots and rosettes","A leopard’s spots are arranged in rose-shaped clusters on its body, while its head and legs have solid spots. Each leopard’s pattern is unique. The pattern helps it blend into grass, shadows and leafy branches."],["Hunting","Leopards stalk silently and pounce from close range, killing with a bite to the neck or throat. They eat a wide variety of animals, including antelopes, deer, monkeys, birds, rodents and fish. This flexible diet helps them survive in many habitats."],["Tree larders","Leopards are extremely strong for their size and can carry a kill heavier than themselves up a tree. Storing food in branches keeps it away from lions, hyenas and other competitors. They also rest in trees during the day."],["Black panthers","Some leopards have a genetic variation called melanism that makes their coats very dark, although their rosettes can still be seen in certain light. These black leopards are more common in dense, shady forests, such as in Southeast Asia."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Panthera pardus"],["Range","Africa and Asia"],["Coat pattern","Rosettes"],["Dark form","Black panther"],["Critically endangered subspecies","Amur leopard"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the leopard"],["1990s","Fewer than about 50 Amur leopards are thought to survive"],["2000s","Camera traps begin monitoring Amur leopards in Russia"],["2012","Russia creates Land of the Leopard National Park"],["2016","The leopard is reclassified as vulnerable"],["2019","A rare black leopard is photographed in Kenya"]],
s2:[["The most widespread big cat","Leopards live across sub-Saharan Africa and in scattered populations in North Africa, the Middle East, India, Sri Lanka, China, Southeast Asia and the Russian Far East. They can survive near villages and even on the edges of cities, such as Mumbai in India, where they live in a national park surrounded by the city."],["The Amur leopard","The Amur leopard lives in the cold forests of the Russian Far East and northeastern China. By the 1990s fewer than about 50 remained. Protection, including the creation of Land of the Leopard National Park, has helped numbers rise to around a hundred or more, though it remains critically endangered."],["Leopards and people","Leopards sometimes kill livestock or, rarely, attack people, especially where their habitat has been destroyed. Conflict leads to leopards being killed. Programs that protect livestock, compensate farmers and educate communities help reduce conflict."],["Leopard relatives","The snow leopard of Central Asia’s mountains and the clouded leopard of Southeast Asia’s forests share the name but are different species. The clouded leopard has the longest canine teeth relative to its size of any cat. Neither lives in the Philippines today."],["Leopards and cheetahs","Leopards and cheetahs are often confused. Cheetahs are slimmer, built for speed, with solid round spots and black tear lines on their faces, and hunt by day. Leopards are stockier, with rosettes, and are stealthy night hunters and climbers."],["Symbol and fashion","Leopards appear in heraldry, mythology and art, symbolizing strength and stealth. Leopard skins were once highly prized, and leopard print remains popular in fashion, now usually on synthetic fabrics. International trade in leopard skins is restricted."]],
sp:["A leopard in the city","In Mumbai, one of the world’s largest cities, leopards live in Sanjay Gandhi National Park, a forest surrounded by apartment blocks and roads. At night, leopards sometimes wander into neighborhoods, hunting stray dogs and occasionally appearing on security cameras. Researchers have worked with residents to reduce conflicts, teaching people to keep areas clean, light pathways and supervise children at night. The story shows both the adaptability of leopards and the challenges of sharing space with large predators as cities grow."],
g:[["Leopard","A large spotted wild cat of Africa and Asia"],["Rosette","A rose-shaped cluster of spots"],["Melanism","A genetic variation that causes dark coloring"],["Camera trap","A camera that photographs animals automatically"]],
f:["Leopards are the most widespread big cat.","Black panthers are often leopards with dark coats.","Leopards drag prey up trees."],
fun:"A leopard can carry a carcass heavier than itself several meters up a tree."});

