// Animal Kingdom, more articles: zebras, the carabao, the tamaraw, vultures, pigeons and doves, and rays.
ADDART({id:"zebras",t:"Zebras",c:"Mammals",i:"🦓",w:"Zebra",r:["horses","grasslands","lion","giraffe","palawan","antelopes"],
p:[["Striped horses","Zebras are African members of the horse family with bold black-and-white stripes."],["Every pattern unique","No two zebras have exactly the same stripes."],["Fly protection","Scientists think the stripes help keep biting flies away."]],
l:"Zebras are wild members of the horse family that live in the grasslands, savannas and mountains of eastern and southern Africa. There are three living species: the plains zebra, the most common; Grévy’s zebra, the largest; and the mountain zebra. Zebras are famous for their black-and-white stripes, which are unique to each animal, like fingerprints. They live in herds and graze mainly on grasses. Plains zebras take part in the great migration across the Serengeti and Masai Mara alongside millions of wildebeest. Scientists have long debated why zebras have stripes; current evidence suggests the stripes help deter biting flies. One type of zebra, the quagga, was hunted to extinction in the 1800s.",
s:[["Three species","Plains zebras have broad stripes and live in large numbers across eastern and southern Africa. Grévy’s zebras, found in Kenya and Ethiopia, have narrow stripes and large rounded ears and are endangered. Mountain zebras live in rugged areas of South Africa, Namibia and Angola."],["Why stripes?","Ideas have included camouflage, confusing predators, keeping cool and helping zebras recognize each other. Experiments in which horses wore striped coats found that biting flies had trouble landing on stripes, and many scientists now think fly protection is the main reason."],["Herd life","Plains zebras live in family groups of a stallion with several mares and their young, which join together into large herds. When a predator attacks, zebras flee together, and their swirling stripes may make it hard to pick out one animal. Zebras also kick and bite fiercely to defend themselves."],["The great migration","Every year, hundreds of thousands of zebras move with wildebeest and gazelles across the Serengeti in Tanzania and the Masai Mara in Kenya, following rains and fresh grass. Zebras often lead, eating tougher grass tops and leaving shorter grass for wildebeest."]],
k:[["Species","Three"],["Family","Horse family"],["Largest species","Grévy’s zebra"],["Extinct form","Quagga"],["Stripes","Unique to each zebra"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus describes the zebra"],["1883","The last known quagga dies in an Amsterdam zoo"],["1977","Zebras and giraffes are brought from Kenya to Calauit Island in Palawan"],["1986","Grévy’s zebra is listed as endangered"],["1987","The Quagga Project begins breeding zebras to resemble the quagga"],["2019","A study with striped horse coats supports the fly-protection theory"]],
s2:[["Black with white stripes?","Scientists studying zebra embryos and skin have found that zebras have dark skin and that their fur color develops from pigment cells; many describe them as dark animals with white stripes. Either way, the pattern is unique to each individual."],["The quagga","The quagga was a zebra of South Africa with stripes only on the front of its body and a brown rear. It was hunted to extinction, and the last known animal died in an Amsterdam zoo in 1883. DNA studies showed it was a type of plains zebra, and a project in South Africa has bred plains zebras to resemble it."],["Zebras in the Philippines","In 1977 zebras, giraffes and other African animals were brought from Kenya to Calauit Island in Palawan, which became the Calauit Safari Park. Their descendants still live there alongside endemic Philippine animals such as the Calamian deer. The park has been both a tourist attraction and the subject of debate over displacement of local residents when it was created."],["Zebras and horses","Zebras have never been widely domesticated, unlike horses and donkeys. They are more nervous, aggressive and unpredictable. A few people in history trained zebras to pull carriages, but it never became common."],["Zebra crossings","The black-and-white striped pedestrian crossings found on roads in many countries, including the Philippines, are called zebra crossings because they resemble the animal’s stripes. They were introduced in Britain in the late 1940s and early 1950s."],["Threats","Plains zebras remain common in protected areas, but their numbers have declined in some places. Grévy’s zebras number only a few thousand, threatened by competition with livestock for water and grass, hunting and drought. Mountain zebras have recovered from very low numbers thanks to protection."]],
sp:["African animals on a Philippine island","On Calauit Island in northern Palawan, visitors are sometimes surprised to see zebras grazing and giraffes browsing among Philippine trees. In 1977 a shipment of African animals arrived from Kenya, and the island was turned into a game reserve. Over the decades, the zebras and giraffes adapted and multiplied, while rangers also cared for endangered native species such as the Calamian deer and the Philippine mouse-deer. Today the park is an unusual blend of African savanna and Philippine island, though its history, including the relocation of residents, remains debated."],
g:[["Zebra","A striped wild member of the horse family"],["Herd","A group of grazing animals"],["Migration","A seasonal journey of animals"],["Quagga","An extinct type of zebra"]],
f:["There are three zebra species.","Each zebra has unique stripes.","Zebras live on Calauit Island in Palawan."],
fun:"Zebra crossings on roads are named after the animal’s black-and-white stripes."});

ADDART({id:"carabao",t:"The Carabao",c:"Mammals",i:"🐃",w:"Carabao",r:["rice","domestication","horses","tamaraw","phfood"],
p:[["The farmer’s friend","The carabao is a domestic water buffalo that has long worked Philippine rice fields."],["Strong and patient","It pulls plows and carts and loves wallowing in mud."],["More than a work animal","Today it is also raised for milk, cheese and meat."]],
l:"The carabao is a type of domestic swamp buffalo found in the Philippines and other parts of Southeast Asia. For centuries it has been the farmer’s most important helper, pulling plows through flooded rice paddies, hauling carts and carrying loads. Strong, patient and hard-working, the carabao has become a cherished symbol of the Filipino farmer and is widely regarded as the national animal of the Philippines, though this has not been set in law. Carabaos keep cool by wallowing in mud and water, since they have few sweat glands. With the spread of farm machinery, fewer carabaos work the fields, but government programs now breed them for milk, used to make kesong puti and pastillas, as well as for meat.",
s:[["A swamp buffalo","Carabaos belong to the swamp type of water buffalo, adapted to wet, muddy farmland. They have large, backward-curving horns, gray to black skin and sparse hair. Adult carabaos can weigh several hundred kilograms and work for many years."],["Working the fields","For generations, Filipino farmers relied on carabaos to plow and harrow rice fields, pull sledges and carts, and carry harvests. A carabao and farmer working together in a muddy paddy is one of the most familiar images of rural Philippine life."],["Keeping cool","Carabaos have relatively few sweat glands and dark skin that absorbs heat, so they cool off by wallowing in mud and water, sometimes for hours. The mud also protects their skin from insects and the sun."],["Milk and meat","Carabao milk is rich and creamy, with more fat than cow’s milk. It is used to make kesong puti, a soft white cheese, pastillas de leche and other treats. The Philippine Carabao Center breeds carabaos with dairy buffalo breeds to increase milk production and support farmers’ incomes."]],
k:[["Type","Domestic swamp buffalo"],["Main traditional work","Plowing rice fields"],["Products","Milk, cheese, meat, leather"],["Government agency","Philippine Carabao Center (since 1992)"],["Famous festival","Carabao Festival of Pulilan, Bulacan"]],
d:[["c. 5,000 years ago","Water buffalo are domesticated in Asia"],["c. 1,000s","Water buffalo are used in rice farming in the Philippine islands"],["1992","The Philippine Carabao Act creates the Philippine Carabao Center"],["2000s","Crossbreeding programs increase dairy carabao production"],["2010s","Carabao milk products become popular across the Philippines"],["2020s","Government programs promote carabao-based enterprises for farmers"]],
s2:[["Symbol of hard work","The carabao is often used as a symbol of the patient, hard-working Filipino. It appears on old coins, in paintings, in folk songs and in children’s stories. The Philippine national hero José Rizal and many artists depicted rural scenes with carabaos."],["Carabao festivals","In Pulilan, Bulacan, farmers celebrate the feast of San Isidro Labrador, patron of farmers, each May with a carabao festival in which decorated carabaos parade and kneel before the church. In Angono and other towns, similar festivities honor farmers and their animals."],["Machines and change","Since the late 20th century, hand tractors and other machines have replaced many carabaos in the fields, especially in lowland farms. Carabaos remain important in mountainous and remote areas where machines cannot easily go, and for small farmers who cannot afford tractors."],["The Philippine Carabao Center","Created by law in 1992, the Philippine Carabao Center conserves and improves carabaos, runs breeding programs using artificial insemination, and helps farmers start dairy enterprises. It has research centers across the country, including its national headquarters in the Science City of Muñoz, Nueva Ecija."],["The carabao and the tamaraw","The carabao is often confused with the tamaraw, a small wild buffalo found only on Mindoro. The tamaraw is a separate, critically endangered species and has never been domesticated, while carabaos are domestic animals found throughout the country."],["Protecting carabaos","Philippine laws have long restricted the slaughter of working carabaos to protect farmers’ livelihoods. Today, carabaos are also valued as part of the country’s agricultural heritage, and programs aim to keep healthy, productive herds."]],
sp:["Kesong puti from the farm","Early in the morning in towns of Laguna, Bulacan and Cebu, vendors sell small blocks of soft, white cheese wrapped in banana leaves. This is kesong puti, made from fresh carabao milk curdled with vinegar or rennet and salted lightly. It is often eaten for breakfast with warm pandesal. For many dairy farmers, selling milk and cheese has become an important source of income, giving the carabao a new role beyond the rice field. Programs by the Philippine Carabao Center have helped farming cooperatives produce and sell carabao milk products across the country."],
g:[["Carabao","A domestic swamp buffalo of the Philippines"],["Wallowing","Rolling or lying in mud or water to cool off"],["Kesong puti","A soft white cheese made from carabao milk"],["Artificial insemination","A breeding method used to improve livestock"]],
f:["The carabao is widely regarded as the national animal.","Carabaos cool off in mud.","Carabao milk is used to make kesong puti."],
fun:"Carabao milk has more fat than cow’s milk, making it ideal for rich cheeses and sweets like pastillas."});

ADDART({id:"tamaraw",t:"The Tamaraw",c:"Mammals",i:"🐂",w:"Tamaraw",r:["carabao","phbio","conservation","extinction","eagle"],
p:[["Mindoro’s wild buffalo","The tamaraw is a small wild buffalo found only on the island of Mindoro."],["Critically endangered","Only a few hundred remain in the wild."],["A national treasure","It is protected by law and is a symbol of Philippine wildlife."]],
l:"The tamaraw is a small, stocky wild buffalo found only on the island of Mindoro in the Philippines. It is one of the country’s most famous endemic animals and is critically endangered. Smaller than the domestic carabao, the tamaraw has dark brown to grayish-black hair and short, sturdy horns that grow in a V shape. It once roamed much of Mindoro, but hunting, disease brought by cattle and the loss of its grassland and forest habitat reduced it to a few small areas. Most surviving tamaraws live in Mounts Iglit–Baco Natural Park, where rangers conduct an annual count. In 2026 the count there recorded 425 animals. Tamaraws are protected by law, and October is celebrated as Tamaraw Month.",
s:[["Appearance","The tamaraw stands about one meter tall at the shoulder and weighs around 200 to 300 kilograms, much smaller than a carabao. Its horns are short, thick and point upward in a V shape, unlike the carabao’s wide, curving horns. It often has light markings on its legs and face."],["Life in the wild","Tamaraws live in grasslands and forests in Mindoro’s mountains. They are mainly solitary, though females stay with their calves for several years. Once active by day, they became more active at night to avoid people. They graze on grasses and young bamboo shoots."],["Decline","In the early 1900s there may have been around 10,000 tamaraws on Mindoro. A disease outbreak among cattle in the 1930s spread to tamaraws, and hunting, logging, farming and settlement destroyed much of their habitat. By the 1960s and 1970s, their numbers had fallen dramatically."],["Counting tamaraws","Every year, rangers, volunteers and conservation workers carry out a simultaneous count in Mounts Iglit–Baco Natural Park, watching from vantage points across the grasslands. The count in 2026 recorded 425 tamaraws, an increase of about 12 percent from the previous year, a hopeful sign for the species."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Bubalus mindorensis"],["Found only on","Mindoro"],["Status","Critically endangered"],["2026 count in Mts. Iglit–Baco","425"],["Tamaraw Month","October"]],
d:[["1888","The tamaraw is described scientifically"],["1930s","A cattle disease devastates tamaraw populations"],["1954","A law protects the tamaraw from hunting"],["1970","Mounts Iglit–Baco is declared a national park"],["2002","October is declared Tamaraw Month"],["2026","The annual count records 425 tamaraws in Mounts Iglit–Baco"]],
s2:[["Protecting the tamaraw","The tamaraw has been protected by law since the 1950s, and killing one is a serious crime. The Tamaraw Conservation Program of the Department of Environment and Natural Resources works with local communities, Indigenous peoples and partners to patrol habitats, reduce poaching and manage grasslands with controlled burning."],["The Mangyan and the tamaraw","The Indigenous Mangyan peoples of Mindoro live in and around tamaraw habitat. Conservation programs increasingly involve Mangyan communities, recognizing their ancestral domains and traditional knowledge. Balancing conservation with the rights and livelihoods of Indigenous people is an important part of protecting the species."],["Captive breeding","In the 1980s a captive breeding program tried to increase tamaraw numbers, but only one calf, named Kalibasib, was born and survived. The program showed how difficult breeding tamaraws in captivity is, so conservation now focuses on protecting wild populations."],["A national symbol","The tamaraw appeared on the Philippine one-peso coin and has been used as the name and mascot of sports teams and vehicles, such as the Toyota Tamaraw. Its image represents strength, resilience and the uniqueness of Philippine wildlife."],["Other populations","Small numbers of tamaraws also live in other parts of Mindoro, such as Mount Aruyan and Mount Calavite, where conservation groups have been surveying and protecting them. Finding and protecting these scattered groups is important for the species’ genetic health."],["Hope for the future","Thanks to protection, the tamaraw population in Mounts Iglit–Baco has generally increased since the early 2000s, when only a few hundred were counted. Conservationists say continued protection, community support and habitat management are needed for the species to recover."]],
sp:["The count on the mountain","Every April or May, teams of rangers, scientists, students and volunteers hike into the grasslands of Mounts Iglit–Baco Natural Park in Mindoro. They spread out to dozens of vantage points on ridges and hilltops and, at the same time over several days, scan the slopes with binoculars, recording each tamaraw they see, its sex, age and markings, so the same animal is not counted twice. It is hot, tiring work, often in rugged terrain. When the results are announced, conservationists across the country watch for good news. In 2026 the count reached 425, a record that encouraged everyone working to save Mindoro’s wild buffalo."],
g:[["Tamaraw","A small wild buffalo found only on Mindoro"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"],["Critically endangered","Facing an extremely high risk of extinction"],["Natural park","A protected area for wildlife and nature"]],
f:["The tamaraw lives only on Mindoro.","It is critically endangered.","The 2026 count in Mts. Iglit–Baco found 425."],
fun:"The tamaraw’s horns grow upward in a V shape, unlike the wide, curving horns of the carabao."});

ADDART({id:"vultures",t:"Vultures",c:"Birds",i:"🦅",w:"Vulture",r:["eagle","birds","grasslands","extinction","conservation","falcons"],
p:[["Nature’s cleaners","Vultures are large birds that feed mainly on the bodies of dead animals."],["Soaring giants","They soar for hours on warm air currents searching for food."],["In danger","Many vulture species have declined sharply because of poisoning."]],
l:"Vultures are large birds of prey that feed mostly on carrion, the bodies of dead animals. By quickly cleaning up carcasses, they help prevent the spread of diseases and play a vital role in ecosystems. There are two groups that evolved separately: Old World vultures of Africa, Asia and Europe, related to eagles and hawks, and New World vultures of the Americas, including condors. Vultures have broad wings for soaring on rising warm air for hours, keen eyesight and, in some species, an excellent sense of smell. Their heads are often bare, which helps keep them clean while feeding. Many vulture species are now endangered, especially in Asia and Africa, where poisoning has caused dramatic declines.",
s:[["Two groups","Old World vultures, such as the griffon and the lappet-faced vulture, belong to the same family as eagles and hawks. New World vultures, such as the turkey vulture, black vulture and Andean condor, form a separate family. Their similar appearance is an example of convergent evolution."],["Built for scavenging","Many vultures have bare heads and necks, which stay cleaner when feeding inside carcasses and help regulate body temperature. Their stomachs are extremely acidic, killing dangerous bacteria, such as those causing anthrax and botulism, that would sicken other animals."],["Finding food","Vultures soar high on thermals, columns of rising warm air, scanning the ground. Old World vultures find food by sight and by watching other vultures descend. Turkey vultures can smell the gases from decaying flesh, even beneath forest canopies."],["The bone eater","The bearded vulture, or lammergeier, of mountains in Europe, Africa and Asia, eats mainly bones. It drops large bones from great heights onto rocks to break them, then swallows the pieces, digesting them with powerful stomach acid."]],
k:[["Diet","Mostly carrion"],["Groups","Old World and New World vultures"],["Largest flying bird by wingspan among land birds","Andean condor"],["Bone eater","Bearded vulture"],["Main threat in Asia","Poisoning by the drug diclofenac"]],
d:[["1980s","California condors are taken into captivity to save the species"],["1990s","Vulture numbers in South Asia collapse"],["2004","Scientists identify the drug diclofenac as the cause"],["2006","India bans diclofenac for use in livestock"],["2009","International Vulture Awareness Day is first held"],["2010s","Poisoning causes large vulture deaths in Africa"]],
s2:[["The Asian vulture crisis","In the 1990s, the populations of several vulture species in India, Pakistan and Nepal fell by more than 95 percent. Scientists found that vultures were dying from kidney failure after eating carcasses of cattle treated with diclofenac, a painkiller. India, Pakistan and Nepal banned veterinary use of the drug, and breeding programs aim to restore vultures."],["Consequences of losing vultures","Without vultures, carcasses rot for longer, and feral dogs and rats increase, spreading diseases such as rabies. Researchers have estimated that the loss of vultures in India led to increases in human disease and deaths. The crisis showed how important scavengers are to human health."],["The California condor","By 1987 only 27 California condors were left, and all were captured for a breeding program. Through captive breeding and release, the population has grown to several hundred birds, many living in the wild. Lead poisoning from bullets in carcasses remains a major threat."],["Vultures in Africa","Many African vultures are endangered. Some die after eating carcasses deliberately poisoned by poachers, who kill vultures because circling birds can alert rangers to illegal kills. Others are poisoned by farmers targeting predators or are killed for use in traditional medicine."],["Vultures and the Philippines","The Philippines has no native vultures; the large birds of prey seen there are eagles, hawks and kites, such as the Philippine eagle and the brahminy kite. Vultures are found in nearby parts of mainland Asia."],["Vultures in culture","In ancient Egypt the vulture symbolized protection and motherhood and was linked with the goddess Nekhbet. In Tibet, sky burials traditionally leave the bodies of the dead for vultures. In some cultures, vultures are seen as symbols of death, but scientists emphasize their role as essential cleaners."]],
sp:["The bone breaker of the mountains","High in the mountains of the Himalayas, the Alps and East Africa lives a remarkable bird: the bearded vulture. With feathers often stained a rusty orange from bathing in iron-rich mud, it looks fierce, yet it lives mainly on bones. When a bone is too big to swallow, the bird carries it high into the air and drops it onto rocks below, breaking it into pieces. Its stomach acid is strong enough to dissolve bone within a day. In the Alps, the species was wiped out in the early 1900s, but a reintroduction program begun in the 1980s has brought bearded vultures back to the skies."],
g:[["Vulture","A large bird that feeds mainly on dead animals"],["Carrion","The flesh of dead animals"],["Thermal","A column of rising warm air"],["Convergent evolution","When unrelated animals evolve similar features"]],
f:["Vultures eat mostly dead animals.","Old and New World vultures evolved separately.","The Philippines has no native vultures."],
fun:"Vultures’ stomach acid is so strong it can kill the bacteria that cause anthrax."});

ADDART({id:"pigeons",t:"Pigeons and Doves",c:"Birds",i:"🕊️",w:"Columbidae",r:["birds","birdmigr","domestication","extinction","phbio"],
p:[["Familiar birds","Pigeons and doves are a large family of plump birds found worldwide."],["Amazing navigators","Homing pigeons can find their way home from hundreds of kilometers away."],["Philippine species","The Philippines has many beautiful native doves, including the Luzon bleeding-heart."]],
l:"Pigeons and doves make up the bird family Columbidae, with more than 300 species found on every continent except Antarctica. The names are often used interchangeably, though smaller species tend to be called doves. They have plump bodies, small heads and short legs, and feed mainly on seeds and fruit. The rock dove, the wild ancestor of city pigeons, was domesticated thousands of years ago for food, messages and racing. Homing pigeons can find their way home over long distances, and they carried messages in wars and before modern communication. The family also includes colorful forest species, such as the Philippines’ Luzon bleeding-heart, and extinct birds such as the passenger pigeon and the dodo.",
s:[["A worldwide family","Pigeons and doves range from tiny ground doves to the large, crowned pigeons of New Guinea, which are about the size of a chicken. Many tropical fruit doves are brightly colored in green, orange, purple and yellow. They are important seed dispersers in forests."],["Pigeon milk","Unlike most birds, pigeons and doves feed their chicks with a nutritious substance called crop milk, produced in the throat by both parents. This allows them to raise young even when seeds are scarce."],["Homing pigeons","Homing pigeons can return to their home lofts from hundreds of kilometers away. Scientists think they use the Sun’s position, Earth’s magnetic field, smells and landmarks. Pigeon racing remains a popular hobby in many countries, including the Philippines."],["City pigeons","Feral pigeons, descended from domestic birds, live in cities worldwide, nesting on buildings that resemble the cliffs of their wild ancestors. Some people enjoy feeding them, while others see them as pests because of their droppings and the damage they cause."]],
k:[["Family","Columbidae"],["Species","More than 300"],["Wild ancestor of city pigeons","Rock dove"],["Chicks fed","Crop milk"],["Philippine endemic","Luzon bleeding-heart"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Pigeons are kept by people in Mesopotamia and Egypt"],["1681","The dodo, a giant flightless pigeon, is last reliably seen"],["1870–1871","Pigeons carry messages during the Siege of Paris"],["1914","Martha, the last passenger pigeon, dies"],["1918","The pigeon Cher Ami helps save surrounded soldiers in the First World War"],["2010s","Studies reveal how pigeons use magnetic fields to navigate"]],
s2:[["The passenger pigeon","In the 1800s, passenger pigeons may have been the most numerous birds in North America, with flocks of billions darkening the sky for hours. Massive hunting and forest clearing caused their collapse, and the last one, named Martha, died in the Cincinnati Zoo in 1914. Their extinction became a warning about how even abundant species can disappear."],["The dodo","The dodo, a large, flightless bird of the island of Mauritius, was a relative of pigeons. Discovered by Europeans in the late 1500s, it was extinct within about a century, because of hunting and animals such as rats and pigs brought by sailors. The phrase dead as a dodo comes from its fate."],["Pigeons in war","Pigeons carried messages in many wars. During the Siege of Paris in 1870 and 1871, they carried tiny messages on microfilm. In the First World War, a pigeon named Cher Ami delivered a message that helped rescue surrounded American soldiers, despite being wounded."],["Philippine doves","The Philippines has many native pigeons and doves, including the Luzon bleeding-heart, whose white breast has a red patch that looks like a wound, and the Nicobar pigeon, with shimmering green feathers. Several Philippine species, such as the Negros bleeding-heart and the Mindoro bleeding-heart, are critically endangered."],["Symbols of peace","The white dove carrying an olive branch is a symbol of peace in many cultures, linked to the story of Noah’s Ark in the Bible. In 1949 the artist Pablo Picasso’s drawing of a dove was used as the emblem of a peace congress, making it a famous modern symbol."],["Clever birds","Pigeons are more intelligent than they are often given credit for. Experiments have shown that they can recognize faces, learn to tell apart paintings by different artists, count small numbers and even help spot signs of cancer in medical images after training."]],
sp:["Cher Ami’s flight","In October 1918, during the First World War, a group of American soldiers known as the Lost Battalion was trapped behind enemy lines in the Argonne Forest in France. To make matters worse, their own artillery was mistakenly shelling them. Their commander released their last carrier pigeon, Cher Ami, with a note giving their location and pleading for the shelling to stop. Despite being shot, losing an eye and nearly losing a leg, Cher Ami flew about 40 kilometers back to headquarters in less than half an hour. The shelling stopped, and nearly 200 men were rescued. The brave pigeon was awarded a French medal and is preserved in a museum in Washington, DC."],
g:[["Columbidae","The bird family of pigeons and doves"],["Homing pigeon","A pigeon trained to return home from far away"],["Crop milk","A food made in a pigeon’s throat to feed its chicks"],["Feral","Living wild after being domesticated"]],
f:["There are more than 300 species of pigeons and doves.","The dodo was a relative of pigeons.","Martha, the last passenger pigeon, died in 1914."],
fun:"Pigeons have been trained to tell the difference between paintings by Monet and Picasso."});

ADDART({id:"rays",t:"Rays and Mantas",c:"Sea Life",i:"🐟",w:"Batoidea",r:["shark","fishes","wshark","coral","sea"],
p:[["Flat fish","Rays are flat-bodied fish related to sharks."],["Gentle giants","Manta rays are among the largest fish, gliding with wing-like fins."],["Spiny tails","Stingrays have a sharp spine on their tail for defense."]],
l:"Rays are a large group of fish with flattened bodies and wide, wing-like fins joined to their heads. Like sharks, their skeletons are made of cartilage rather than bone, and they are close relatives of sharks. There are hundreds of species, including stingrays, electric rays, skates, guitarfish, sawfish and the giant manta rays. Most rays live on the seafloor, where they hunt crabs, clams and worms, but mantas swim in open water, filtering tiny plankton from the sea. Stingrays have a venomous spine on their tail for self-defense. Rays live in oceans worldwide and in some rivers. Many species are threatened by overfishing, and the Philippines has important manta and mobula ray sites.",
s:[["Built flat","A ray’s mouth and gill slits are on its underside, suited to feeding on the seabed. Many rays breathe through openings called spiracles on top of their heads, so they can rest on the sand without clogging their gills. They swim by flapping or rippling their large pectoral fins."],["Manta rays","Manta rays can have wingspans of up to about seven meters. They feed by swimming with their mouths open, filtering plankton, and use two fleshy flaps on their heads to funnel food in. Mantas have the largest brains for their size of any fish and are known to visit cleaning stations on reefs."],["Stingrays","Stingrays have one or more sharp, barbed spines on their tails, which can deliver painful venom. They use them only in self-defense, usually when stepped on. Beachgoers in shallow water are advised to shuffle their feet so stingrays swim away."],["Electric rays and sawfish","Electric rays can produce electric shocks to stun prey and defend themselves. Sawfish have a long, toothed snout used to slash at fish and sense prey. Sawfish are among the most endangered marine fish."]],
k:[["Skeleton","Cartilage"],["Closest relatives","Sharks"],["Largest ray","Giant oceanic manta"],["Manta wingspan","Up to about 7 meters"],["Defense of stingrays","Venomous tail spine"]],
d:[["c. 150 million years ago","Early rays appear in the fossil record"],["2006","Wildlife presenter Steve Irwin dies after a stingray injury"],["2009","Scientists recognize two species of manta ray"],["2013","Manta rays receive international trade protections"],["2017","Mobula rays are added to international trade protections"],["2020s","Philippine sites protect manta and mobula ray populations"]],
s2:[["Rays in the Philippines","Philippine waters are home to manta rays, mobula rays, stingrays and other species. Sites such as the Ticao Pass between Masbate and Sorsogon, known for manta and mobula rays, attract divers. Mobula rays were once hunted in parts of Bohol for their meat and gill plates, and protections have been introduced."],["The gill plate trade","Manta and mobula rays have been hunted for their gill plates, which are sold in some markets for use in remedies claimed to have health benefits. Because mantas reproduce very slowly, often giving birth to a single pup every few years, this hunting has caused steep declines."],["Manta tourism","Diving and snorkeling with mantas has become a major tourism activity in places such as Indonesia, the Maldives and Hawaii. Studies suggest a living manta can be worth far more through tourism over its lifetime than as fishing catch, encouraging protection."],["Skates","Skates are relatives of rays that lay eggs in tough cases often called mermaid’s purses, which sometimes wash up on beaches. Most rays, by contrast, give birth to live young."],["Mantas and intelligence","Studies of manta rays have suggested that they may recognize themselves in mirrors, a sign of self-awareness seen in few animals. They also show curiosity toward divers. Scientists continue to study their behavior and social lives."],["Protecting rays","Many rays are caught accidentally in fishing nets, and some are targeted. International agreements regulate trade in manta and mobula rays and sawfish, and some countries have banned catching mantas. Marine protected areas help protect their feeding and breeding grounds."]],
sp:["Flying underwater","Divers who see a manta ray for the first time often describe the experience as magical. The huge, dark shape appears out of the blue, glides overhead with slow beats of its wide fins, and turns gracefully to look at the swimmers before disappearing. Sometimes mantas leap entirely out of the water, crashing back with a loud splash, behavior scientists are still trying to explain. Each manta has a unique pattern of spots on its belly, which researchers use to identify individuals in photographs and track their movements over many years."],
g:[["Ray","A flat-bodied fish related to sharks"],["Cartilage","Flexible tissue that forms the skeleton of sharks and rays"],["Spiracle","An opening behind the eyes used for breathing"],["Plankton","Tiny organisms that drift in water"]],
f:["Rays are related to sharks.","Manta rays can be about 7 meters wide.","Stingrays use their spines only for defense."],
fun:"Each manta ray has a unique pattern of spots on its belly, like a fingerprint."});

