ADDART({id:"echinoderms",t:"Starfish and Sea Urchins",c:"Sea Life",i:"⭐",w:"Echinoderm",r:["coral","sea","octopus","mollusc","tubbataha","sponges"],
p:[["Spiny-skinned animals","Starfish, sea urchins and sea cucumbers belong to a group called echinoderms."],["Five-part bodies","Most echinoderms have bodies arranged in five parts around a center."],["Regrowing parts","Many starfish can regrow lost arms."]],
l:"Echinoderms are a group of marine animals that includes starfish, also called sea stars, as well as sea urchins, sand dollars, sea cucumbers, brittle stars and feather stars. Their name means spiny skin. There are about 7,000 living species, found only in the sea, from rocky shores and coral reefs to the deepest ocean trenches. Most adult echinoderms have bodies arranged in five parts around a central point, a pattern called five-fold symmetry. They move and feed using hundreds of tiny tube feet powered by a unique water vascular system. Many can regenerate lost body parts. Echinoderms play important roles in ocean ecosystems, and some, such as sea urchins and sea cucumbers, are harvested for food, including in the Philippines.",
s:[["Starfish","Starfish usually have five arms, though some species have many more. They crawl slowly using tube feet on their undersides. Many eat clams and mussels by prying the shells open slightly and pushing their stomachs out of their bodies into the shell to digest the animal inside."],["Sea urchins","Sea urchins are round and covered in movable spines, which protect them from predators. They graze on algae with a five-toothed mouth called Aristotle’s lantern. Some urchin spines are venomous, so swimmers should avoid stepping on them."],["Sea cucumbers","Sea cucumbers have soft, sausage-shaped bodies and live on the seafloor, eating sand and mud and digesting the organic material in it, which helps recycle nutrients and clean sediment. When threatened, some can push out sticky threads or even parts of their insides, which they later regrow."],["Water power","Echinoderms have a water vascular system, a network of canals filled with seawater that operates their tube feet by changing water pressure. This allows them to move, cling to rocks, breathe and catch food."]],
k:[["Group name","Echinoderms"],["Name meaning","Spiny skin"],["Living species","About 7,000"],["Body plan","Five-fold symmetry"],["Movement","Tube feet"]],
d:[["c. 500 million years ago","Early echinoderms appear in the oceans"],["1960s","Crown-of-thorns starfish outbreaks damage reefs in the Pacific"],["1983","Disease kills most long-spined sea urchins in the Caribbean"],["2013","Sea star wasting disease strikes starfish along the Pacific coast of North America"],["2020s","Scientists study how starfish regrow their arms"],["2020s","Sea cucumber farming expands in the Philippines and Asia"]],
s2:[["Crown-of-thorns starfish","The crown-of-thorns starfish is covered in venomous spines and eats coral. When its numbers explode in outbreaks, it can destroy large areas of coral reefs, including in the Philippines and Australia’s Great Barrier Reef. Divers and conservation groups sometimes remove or inject them to protect reefs."],["Echinoderms as food","Sea urchin roe, called uni in Japanese, is a delicacy, and in the Philippines sea urchins called salawaki or swaki are eaten fresh in coastal areas. Dried sea cucumbers, known as balatan or trepang, are highly valued in Chinese cuisine. Overharvesting has reduced their numbers in many places, leading to regulations and farming."],["Regeneration","Many starfish can regrow lost arms, and some species can grow a whole new body from a single arm if part of the central disc remains. Scientists study echinoderm regeneration to understand how tissues regrow, which could have medical uses."],["Sea star wasting disease","Beginning in 2013, a disease caused starfish along the Pacific coast of North America to waste away, killing millions. The loss of starfish such as the sunflower sea star allowed sea urchins to multiply and destroy kelp forests, showing how one species can affect an entire ecosystem."],["Related to us?","Surprisingly, echinoderms are more closely related to animals with backbones, including humans, than to most other invertebrates such as insects or snails. Their larvae are two-sided, like ours, and only develop five-fold symmetry as they grow."],["Not really fish","Starfish are not fish at all; they have no backbone, gills or fins. Many scientists prefer the name sea star. Similarly, sea cucumbers are animals, not plants, and sand dollars are flattened relatives of sea urchins."]],
sp:["The stomach that leaves the body","A starfish attacking a mussel is a slow but remarkable sight. It wraps its arms around the shell and grips with hundreds of tube feet, pulling steadily for hours. Eventually the mussel tires and its shell opens by just a fraction of a millimeter. That is enough: the starfish pushes its stomach out through its mouth and into the gap, releasing digestive juices that turn the mussel’s soft body into soup. When the meal is finished, it pulls its stomach back inside. This method allows starfish to eat prey much larger than their mouths."],
g:[["Echinoderm","A spiny-skinned sea animal such as a starfish or sea urchin"],["Tube feet","Small, water-powered feet used for moving and feeding"],["Five-fold symmetry","A body arranged in five parts around a center"],["Regeneration","Regrowing lost body parts"]],
f:["Starfish are not fish.","Most echinoderms have five-part bodies.","Some starfish can regrow lost arms."],
fun:"Starfish have no brain and no blood; seawater pumped through their bodies does much of the work."});

ADDART({id:"eels",t:"Eels",c:"Sea Life",i:"🐍",w:"Eel",r:["fishes","sea","coral","birdmigr","phfood"],
p:[["Snake-like fish","Eels are long, slender fish that look like snakes."],["Mysterious journeys","Freshwater eels travel thousands of kilometers to breed in the open ocean."],["Reef hiders","Moray eels hide in reef crevices with a second set of jaws in their throats."]],
l:"Eels are long, snake-shaped fish belonging to the order Anguilliformes, with more than 800 species. They include moray eels of coral reefs, conger eels, garden eels that live in burrows on the seafloor, and freshwater eels of the genus Anguilla, which spend most of their lives in rivers and lakes but migrate to the open ocean to breed. Most eels have smooth, slimy skin and long fins running along their backs and bellies. The life cycle of freshwater eels was one of biology’s great mysteries for centuries. Eels are an important food in Japan, Europe and parts of Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, but some freshwater eel species are now endangered. The so-called electric eel is not a true eel but a type of knifefish.",
s:[["Freshwater eels","Freshwater eels hatch in the ocean, drift as transparent larvae, and enter rivers as young glass eels. They grow for years in fresh water before returning to the sea to spawn and die. European and American eels breed in the Sargasso Sea in the Atlantic, while Japanese eels breed near the Mariana Islands in the Pacific."],["Moray eels","Moray eels live in holes and crevices on coral and rocky reefs, often with only their heads poking out. They open and close their mouths constantly to breathe, which can look threatening. They have a second set of jaws in their throats that shoots forward to pull prey down, a feature unique among fish."],["Garden eels","Garden eels live in colonies in sandy seafloors, each in its own burrow. They poke their upper bodies out to catch plankton drifting by, swaying like plants in a garden, and quickly retreat into the sand when danger approaches."],["Electric eels","Electric eels of South American rivers can produce shocks of several hundred volts to stun prey and defend themselves. Despite their name, they are more closely related to carp and catfish than to true eels. In 2019 scientists described a species that can produce up to about 860 volts."]],
k:[["Order","Anguilliformes"],["Species","More than 800"],["Atlantic eel breeding area","Sargasso Sea"],["Unique feature of morays","Second set of throat jaws"],["Not a true eel","Electric eel"]],
d:[["c. 350 BC","Aristotle suggests eels arise from mud because no eggs are found"],["1904","Johannes Schmidt begins searching for the eel’s breeding grounds"],["1922","Schmidt identifies the Sargasso Sea as the breeding area of European eels"],["2008","European eels are listed as critically endangered"],["2009","Japanese eel eggs are collected in the wild for the first time"],["2019","A powerful new species of electric eel is described"]],
s2:[["The mystery of the eel","For over 2,000 years, people wondered where eels came from, because no one had ever found eggs or baby eels in rivers. Aristotle suggested they came from mud. In the 1870s a young Sigmund Freud, later famous in psychology, dissected hundreds of eels searching for their reproductive organs without success. In the early 1900s the Danish scientist Johannes Schmidt traced tiny eel larvae across the Atlantic to the Sargasso Sea."],["Eels in the Philippines","In the Philippines, eels called igat or kasili live in rivers and lakes, and are cooked in dishes such as adobo and ginataan. Glass eels caught in Philippine rivers have been exported to East Asian eel farms, which has raised concern about overfishing, and the government has at times restricted this trade."],["Eel farming","Eels cannot yet be bred in large numbers in captivity, so eel farms raise young glass eels caught from the wild. High demand, especially for Japanese grilled eel, called unagi, has led to overfishing and illegal trade in glass eels, worth large sums of money."],["Endangered eels","European, Japanese and American eel populations have declined sharply because of overfishing, dams that block migration, pollution, parasites and changes in ocean currents. The European eel is critically endangered, and trade in it is restricted."],["Eels in culture","Eels have long been eaten in many cultures, from jellied eels in London to smoked eel in the Netherlands and kabayaki in Japan. They also appear in myths and folklore, sometimes as mysterious creatures linked with water spirits."],["Slippery skin","Most eels produce a thick layer of slime that protects their skin from infection and helps them slip through mud and crevices. Some freshwater eels can even wriggle short distances over damp land between ponds."]],
sp:["The long journey to the Sargasso Sea","When a European eel is about 10 or 20 years old, something changes. Its eyes grow larger, its belly turns silver and it stops eating. On dark, rainy autumn nights it slips downstream from rivers across Europe, and some even cross wet fields, heading for the sea. Then it swims thousands of kilometers across the Atlantic to the Sargasso Sea, a calm region near Bermuda, where it is thought to spawn and die. The eggs hatch into leaf-shaped larvae that drift with the Gulf Stream back toward Europe. Even today, no one has ever directly observed eels spawning in the wild."],
g:[["Eel","A long, snake-shaped fish"],["Glass eel","A young, transparent eel entering rivers"],["Sargasso Sea","A region of the Atlantic where European and American eels breed"],["Moray","A reef-dwelling eel with throat jaws"]],
f:["Electric eels are not true eels.","European eels breed in the Sargasso Sea.","Moray eels have a second set of jaws."],
fun:"No one has ever directly observed freshwater eels spawning in the wild ocean."});

ADDART({id:"geckos",t:"Geckos",c:"Reptiles",i:"🦎",w:"Gecko",r:["lizard","chameleon","insect","phbio","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Sticky-footed lizards","Geckos are small lizards famous for climbing walls and ceilings."],["Noisy lizards","Unlike most lizards, many geckos make calls."],["A famous call","The tokay gecko, or tuko, is known in the Philippines for its loud call."]],
l:"Geckos are small to medium-sized lizards found in warm regions around the world, with more than 1,500 species. Many geckos can climb smooth walls, glass and even ceilings thanks to millions of tiny hairs on their toe pads that cling to surfaces using weak molecular forces. Most geckos are active at night and eat insects, which makes them welcome guests in many homes. Unlike most lizards, many geckos communicate with chirps, clicks and barks. Most species cannot blink; they lick their eyes clean instead. Many can drop their tails to escape predators and grow new ones. In the Philippines, house geckos called butiki are common in homes, and the larger tokay gecko, or tuko, is known for its loud to-kay call.",
s:[["Sticky feet without glue","Gecko toe pads are covered with ridges carrying millions of tiny hairs called setae, each branching into hundreds of even smaller tips. These get so close to a surface that weak attractive forces between molecules, called van der Waals forces, hold the gecko in place. It can peel its toes off easily by changing the angle."],["Night hunters","Most geckos hunt at night, catching insects, spiders and other small animals. House geckos gather around lights where insects swarm. Many geckos have large eyes with excellent night vision, even able to see colors in dim light."],["Voices","The word gecko comes from a Malay word imitating the call of the tokay gecko. Many geckos chirp, click or squeak to defend territories or attract mates. In the Philippines, the house gecko’s chirping is familiar in homes at night."],["Dropping tails","When grabbed by a predator, many geckos can shed their tails, which keep wriggling to distract the attacker while the gecko escapes. A new tail grows back, though it often looks different from the original."]],
k:[["Species","More than 1,500"],["Climbing secret","Tiny hairs called setae"],["Philippine house gecko","Butiki"],["Philippine large gecko","Tokay gecko or tuko"],["Name origin","Malay imitation of the tokay’s call"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus describes the tokay gecko"],["2000","Scientists explain how gecko feet stick using van der Waals forces"],["2008","The world’s smallest known gecko-sized reptiles are described in the Caribbean"],["2010s","Engineers develop gecko-inspired adhesives and climbing robots"],["2011","Rumors drive a surge in illegal trade of tokay geckos in the Philippines"],["2019","Tokay geckos are added to international trade regulations"]],
s2:[["The tuko","The tokay gecko is one of the largest geckos, growing up to about 30 centimeters, with blue-gray skin covered in orange or red spots. Its loud call, to-kay or tu-ko, can be heard at night in many Philippine homes and forests. Some Filipinos believe counting its calls predicts luck. Tokays have a strong bite and can be aggressive when handled."],["The tuko trade frenzy","Around 2011, false rumors spread that tokay geckos could be used to cure diseases such as AIDS, cancer and asthma, and that dealers would pay huge sums for large ones. Many people in the Philippines captured tuko to sell, and authorities warned that the trade was illegal and the claims untrue. The episode showed how rumors can threaten wildlife."],["Inspiring inventions","Scientists and engineers have studied gecko feet to create dry adhesives that stick and unstick easily without leaving residue. Gecko-inspired grippers have been tested on robots that climb walls and on devices for handling objects in space."],["Geckos as pets","Some geckos, such as the leopard gecko and crested gecko, are popular pets because they are small, calm and easy to care for. Experts advise buying captive-bred animals rather than wild-caught ones, and learning about their care before adopting."],["Tiny and giant geckos","Geckos range from tiny species in the Caribbean, which are among the smallest reptiles in the world, to the New Caledonian giant gecko, which can grow longer than 35 centimeters. A giant gecko from New Zealand, known only from a single museum specimen, was even larger."],["House guests","In tropical countries, house geckos are often welcome because they eat mosquitoes, flies and cockroaches. They are harmless to people, though their droppings can be a nuisance."]],
sp:["Counting the tuko’s calls","On a quiet night in a Philippine province, a deep, croaking call rings out: tu-ko, tu-ko, tu-ko. Families may pause and count. In some traditions, an odd or even number of calls is said to bring good or bad luck, or to answer a question. Scientists explain that the tokay gecko is simply calling to defend its territory or attract a mate. Whether or not people believe the old sayings, the tuko’s call is one of the most familiar night sounds in many Filipino homes, and the gecko itself helps control insects around the house."],
g:[["Gecko","A small lizard, often able to climb walls"],["Setae","Tiny hairs on gecko toes that help them stick"],["Van der Waals forces","Weak attractions between molecules"],["Butiki","The Filipino name for small house geckos"]],
f:["Geckos can climb glass and ceilings.","Many geckos lick their eyes clean.","The tuko is named after its call."],
fun:"A gecko can hang from a ceiling by a single toe."});

ADDART({id:"mantises",t:"Praying Mantises",c:"Insects",i:"🦗",w:"Mantis",r:["insect","beetle","animalbehavior","flower","butterfly"],
p:[["Praying posture","Mantises hold their front legs folded as if praying."],["Ambush hunters","They wait motionless and strike prey with lightning-fast front legs."],["Masters of disguise","Many mantises look like leaves, sticks or flowers."]],
l:"Praying mantises are predatory insects named for the way they hold their spiny front legs folded together, as if in prayer. There are about 2,400 species, mostly in warm regions, including many in the Philippines and Southeast Asia. Mantises are ambush hunters: they wait motionless, often camouflaged among leaves or flowers, then strike with their front legs in a fraction of a second, grabbing insects and sometimes small animals. They are the only insects that can turn their heads to look over their shoulders, and they have excellent vision with depth perception. Female mantises sometimes eat males during or after mating. Gardeners value mantises because they eat many pest insects.",
s:[["Deadly front legs","A mantis’s front legs are lined with sharp spines that trap prey. Its strike can take less than a tenth of a second, too fast for many insects to escape. Mantises eat flies, crickets, moths and other insects, and large species occasionally catch lizards, frogs or even small birds."],["Seeing in 3D","Mantises have large compound eyes set wide apart on a triangular head that can swivel about 180 degrees. In 2016 scientists fitted mantises with tiny 3D glasses and showed that they use stereo vision to judge distance, the first insects shown to do so."],["Camouflage","Many mantises are green or brown and blend in with leaves and bark. The orchid mantis of Southeast Asia looks like a pink and white flower, attracting insects that come close looking for nectar. Others resemble dead leaves, twigs or even ants when young."],["Life cycle","Female mantises lay eggs in a frothy mass that hardens into a protective case called an ootheca, attached to twigs or walls. Dozens or hundreds of tiny nymphs hatch, resembling small adults. They grow by shedding their skin several times before becoming adults with wings."]],
k:[["Species","About 2,400"],["Hunting style","Ambush"],["Head rotation","About 180 degrees"],["Egg case","Ootheca"],["Famous flower mimic","Orchid mantis"]],
d:[["c. 100 million years ago","Early mantises are preserved in amber"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the European mantis"],["1890s","European mantises are introduced to North America"],["2016","Scientists show that mantises use 3D vision"],["2017","Researchers report mantises catching birds around the world"],["2020s","New mantis species continue to be discovered in Southeast Asian forests"]],
s2:[["Mantises in the Philippines","The Philippines has many mantis species, including leaf-like and flower-like mantises in its forests. Filipinos often call them sasamba or similar names, and children sometimes keep them briefly as pets. Some species are found only in the Philippines, and scientists continue to describe new ones."],["The orchid mantis","For a long time people thought the orchid mantis hid among orchids, but research showed that it usually sits alone on leaves, where its flower-like appearance itself attracts pollinating insects. It is one of the few known animals that mimics a flower to lure prey."],["Cannibal mates","Female mantises sometimes eat males during or after mating, especially in captivity or when hungry. In the wild this happens less often than once thought. By eating the male, the female gains nutrients that may help her produce more eggs."],["Mantises and martial arts","The movements of mantises inspired a style of Chinese kung fu known as praying mantis style, which imitates their quick, grabbing strikes. The insect’s calm stance and sudden attacks have also been admired in art and poetry."],["Helpful in gardens","Gardeners sometimes buy mantis egg cases to release in gardens to control pests. However, mantises eat helpful insects such as bees and butterflies as well as pests, so they are not a perfect form of pest control."],["Close relatives","Mantises are closely related to cockroaches and termites, which may seem surprising. Together they form a group of insects with similar egg cases and wing structures."]],
sp:["Tiny glasses for a mantis","How do you test whether an insect sees in three dimensions? In 2016 scientists at Newcastle University in England glued tiny 3D glasses, with one blue lens and one green lens, onto praying mantises using beeswax. They then showed the mantises 3D films of buzzing prey on a screen, like those in a 3D cinema. When the images were made to appear within striking distance, the mantises lunged at them, showing that they combine views from both eyes to judge depth. It was the first proof of stereo vision in an insect, and the research may help design simple 3D vision systems for robots."],
g:[["Mantis","A predatory insect that holds its front legs as if praying"],["Ambush","Waiting hidden to attack by surprise"],["Ootheca","A hardened egg case laid by mantises"],["Nymph","A young insect that looks like a small adult"]],
f:["There are about 2,400 mantis species.","Mantises can turn their heads to look behind them.","Mantises see in 3D."],
fun:"Scientists once fitted praying mantises with tiny 3D glasses to prove they see in three dimensions."});

;
/* data/plants1.js */
// Plants: orchids, rafflesia, fruits, seaweeds, coconut and seeds.
ADDART({id:"orchids",t:"Orchids",c:"Plants",i:"🌸",w:"Orchid",r:["flower","fungi","bee","darwin","conservation","mycorrhiza"],
p:[["A huge family","Orchids are one of the largest families of flowering plants."],["Clever flowers","Many orchids trick insects into carrying their pollen."],["A Philippine queen","The Philippines is home to the famous waling-waling orchid."]],
l:"Orchids are flowering plants of the family Orchidaceae, one of the two largest plant families on Earth, with roughly 28,000 known species. They grow on every continent except Antarctica, from tropical rainforests to cold mountains, but are most varied in the tropics. Many tropical orchids are epiphytes, growing on tree branches rather than in soil. Orchid flowers are often elaborate and are shaped to attract particular pollinators, sometimes by deception. Their seeds are as fine as dust and need fungi to sprout. Vanilla comes from an orchid. The Philippines has a rich orchid flora, with many species found nowhere else, including the waling-waling of Mindanao, often called the queen of Philippine orchids.",
s:[["Flower design","Orchid flowers usually have three sepals and three petals, one of which forms a special lip called the labellum that serves as a landing platform for insects. Their pollen is packed into sticky bundles called pollinia, which attach to a visiting insect’s body and are carried to the next flower."],["Tricking pollinators","Many orchids offer no nectar at all. Some look and smell like female insects, so males try to mate with them and pick up pollen. Others imitate rotting meat to attract flies, or trap insects briefly so they must squeeze past the pollen to escape."],["Dust-like seeds","A single orchid seed pod can hold huge numbers of tiny seeds with almost no stored food. To grow, a seed must meet a suitable fungus that supplies it with nutrients, a partnership called mycorrhiza. This is one reason many orchids are hard to grow from seed."],["Living on trees","Epiphytic orchids cling to branches with thick roots covered by a spongy layer that soaks up rain and moisture from the air. They are not parasites; they simply use the tree for support and access to light."]],
k:[["Family","Orchidaceae"],["Known species","About 28,000"],["Seed type","Tiny, dust-like"],["Famous food orchid","Vanilla"],["Philippine star","Waling-waling"]],
d:[["c. 15–20 million years ago","A bee preserved in amber carries orchid pollen"],["1862","Charles Darwin publishes a book on orchid pollination"],["1882","The waling-waling is described for Western science"],["1903","The moth predicted by Darwin for a Madagascar orchid is formally named"],["1920s","Scientists learn to sprout orchid seeds in the laboratory"],["2010s","Growing orchids from tissue culture makes many species widely available"]],
s2:[["The waling-waling","The waling-waling, Vanda sanderiana, grows high on trees in the forests of Mindanao. Its large flowers are pink and white with reddish-brown patterns. It is admired by orchid growers worldwide and has been used to breed many hybrids. Some lawmakers have proposed making it a national flower alongside the sampaguita, while others prefer to keep the sampaguita alone; the debate has continued for years."],["Darwin’s prediction","In 1862 Charles Darwin examined a Madagascar orchid whose nectar lies at the bottom of a spur about 30 centimeters long. He predicted that a moth with an equally long tongue must exist to pollinate it. Many scientists doubted him, but a hawk moth with a very long tongue was later found and named in 1903."],["Orchid fever","In the 1800s, wealthy Europeans became obsessed with rare tropical orchids, a craze sometimes called orchidelirium. Collectors were sent into forests in Asia and South America, including the Philippines, and some stripped whole areas of orchids. This history still shapes laws protecting wild orchids today."],["Vanilla","Vanilla flavoring comes from the cured seed pods of vanilla orchids, native to Mexico and Central America. Outside their homeland, the flowers must usually be pollinated by hand, a technique developed in 1841 by Edmond Albius, a young enslaved worker on the island of Réunion. This labor is one reason real vanilla is expensive."],["Threats and protection","Many wild orchids are endangered by forest loss and illegal collection. International trade in wild orchids is regulated, and in the Philippines collecting rare species from the wild is restricted by law. Growing orchids from seed or tissue culture helps reduce pressure on wild plants."],["Orchids at home","Orchids are among the most popular houseplants in the world. Moth orchids are sold widely because they flower for months. In the Philippines, orchid shows and growers’ societies are popular, and many backyard gardens grow vandas, dendrobiums and other types."]],
sp:["A flower that pretends to be a bee","In parts of Europe and Australia, some orchids have flowers that look remarkably like female bees or wasps, complete with furry bodies and shiny wings. They even release scents that mimic the chemicals female insects use to attract mates. Male insects arrive and try to mate with the flower. In doing so, they pick up a bundle of pollen and carry it to the next deceptive flower. The orchid gets pollinated without offering any nectar at all. It is one of nature’s most extraordinary examples of mimicry, and Darwin himself was fascinated by such tricks."],
g:[["Orchid","A member of a large family of flowering plants"],["Epiphyte","A plant that grows on another plant for support"],["Pollinia","Bundles of orchid pollen"],["Labellum","The lip-shaped petal of an orchid"]],
f:["Vanilla comes from an orchid.","Orchid seeds are as fine as dust.","Some orchids imitate insects."],
fun:"Orchid seeds are so tiny and light that they can drift on the wind like dust."});

ADDART({id:"rafflesia",t:"Rafflesia",c:"Plants",i:"🌺",w:"Rafflesia",r:["flower","orchids","conservation","mindanao","deforestation"],
p:[["Giant flowers","Rafflesia produces some of the largest single flowers in the world."],["No leaves","It has no leaves, stems or roots of its own."],["Philippine species","Many rafflesia species grow only in the Philippines."]],
l:"Rafflesia is a genus of parasitic flowering plants found in the rainforests of Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand. Rafflesia arnoldii of Sumatra and Borneo produces the largest single flower known, which can measure about one meter across. Rafflesia has no leaves, stems or true roots; it lives entirely inside the stems and roots of tropical vines called Tetrastigma, drawing food and water from them. Only its flowers appear, bursting out of the vine as cabbage-like buds. The flowers smell like rotting meat, attracting flies that pollinate them, which is why rafflesia is sometimes called a corpse flower. The Philippines has many rafflesia species, several discovered in recent decades.",
s:[["A hidden parasite","For most of its life, rafflesia exists only as thread-like strands inside its host vine, almost like a fungus. It cannot make its own food through photosynthesis. Scientists found that it has lost many of the genes most plants use for photosynthesis, an unusual case in the plant world."],["The bloom","A rafflesia bud may take many months to grow, swelling like a dark cabbage on the vine. When it opens, the flower has five thick, leathery, reddish lobes often covered with pale spots, surrounding a central bowl. Each flower lasts only about a week before turning black and rotting."],["Smell and pollination","Rafflesia flowers give off a smell like decaying flesh and are warm to the touch, which attracts carrion flies looking for a place to lay eggs. The flies pick up pollen and may carry it to another flower. Because male and female flowers are separate and bloom rarely, pollination is difficult."],["Seeds","After pollination, female flowers produce fruits with many tiny seeds. Scientists still do not fully understand how these seeds reach a new host vine, though animals such as tree shrews or squirrels may help spread them."]],
k:[["Genus","Rafflesia"],["Largest species","Rafflesia arnoldii"],["Flower size","Up to about 1 meter"],["Host plant","Tetrastigma vines"],["Pollinators","Carrion flies"]],
d:[["1818","Europeans first record Rafflesia arnoldii in Sumatra"],["1821","Rafflesia is formally named after Stamford Raffles"],["1882","Rafflesia schadenbergiana is described from Mindanao"],["2014","A study finds that a Philippine rafflesia appears to have lost its chloroplast genome"],["2016","Rafflesia consueloae is discovered in Luzon"],["2023","Scientists warn that most rafflesia species are threatened"]],
s2:[["Rafflesia in the Philippines","The Philippines is a center of rafflesia diversity, with species found in Luzon, Mindoro, Panay, Samar, Leyte and Mindanao, many of them found nowhere else. Rafflesia schadenbergiana of Mindanao is the largest Philippine species, with flowers reaching about 80 centimeters. Rafflesia consueloae, discovered in Nueva Ecija in 2016, is one of the smallest."],["Named for Raffles","The genus is named after Sir Stamford Raffles, the British colonial official, and Joseph Arnold, a naturalist, who saw a giant flower in Sumatra in 1818. Local people had known the flower long before. Some modern scientists encourage giving greater recognition to the local guides and communities who know these plants best."],["Endangered flowers","Because rafflesia depends on its host vines and on undisturbed rainforest, it is very vulnerable to logging and land clearing. A 2023 study found that about two-thirds of rafflesia species are threatened. Some species are known from only a few sites."],["Tourism and protection","In some areas of the Philippines, Malaysia and Indonesia, local communities guide visitors to see rafflesia blooms and earn income that encourages protection of the forest. Conservationists warn that visitors must not trample host vines or buds."],["Growing rafflesia","Rafflesia has proven extremely difficult to grow in gardens, because the parasite must be introduced into a living host vine. Only a few botanical gardens, such as Bogor in Indonesia, have managed it. Scientists are experimenting with grafting infected vines to help save rare species."],["Not the titan arum","Rafflesia is often confused with the titan arum of Sumatra, another giant, smelly plant also called a corpse flower. The titan arum has the largest unbranched flowering structure in the world, made of many tiny flowers, while rafflesia has the largest single flower."]],
sp:["Searching for a flower in the forest","Finding a blooming rafflesia takes patience. Botanists and local guides trek through steep rainforest, looking for the twisting Tetrastigma vines that host the parasite. They search the ground and lower stems for small brown buds. A bud may take many months to mature, and then it opens for only a few days. When the team finally finds an open flower, the smell usually reaches them first. In 2016, researchers in Nueva Ecija, Luzon, found a previously unknown species this way and named it Rafflesia consueloae, showing that the Philippine forests still hold botanical surprises."],
g:[["Parasite","A living thing that feeds on another"],["Host","The plant a parasite lives on"],["Carrion","Dead, decaying flesh"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"]],
f:["Rafflesia has no leaves or roots.","Its flowers smell like rotting meat.","Many species are found only in the Philippines."],
fun:"A rafflesia flower can weigh several kilograms but has no leaves at all."});

ADDART({id:"fruits",t:"Fruits",c:"Plants",i:"🥭",w:"Fruit",r:["flower","seeds","agri","domestication","phfood","seeddispersal"],
p:[["Seeds inside","A fruit is the part of a flowering plant that carries seeds."],["Surprising fruits","Tomatoes, cucumbers and peppers are fruits to a botanist."],["Philippine fruits","Mangoes, bananas and durian are famous Philippine fruits."]],
l:"A fruit is the seed-bearing structure that develops from the flower of a flowering plant after pollination. Fruits protect seeds and help spread them, often by tempting animals to eat them and carry the seeds elsewhere. In everyday language, fruits are sweet foods such as mangoes, apples and bananas, but botanists also count tomatoes, eggplants, cucumbers, chili peppers, beans and nuts as fruits. Fruits are an important source of vitamins, fiber and energy in human diets. People domesticated fruit trees thousands of years ago, breeding them to be larger and sweeter. The Philippines grows many tropical fruits, including mangoes, bananas, pineapples, papayas, durian, lanzones, rambutan and calamansi, and is a major exporter of bananas and pineapples.",
s:[["How fruits form","After a flower is pollinated, its ovary swells and develops into a fruit while the ovules inside become seeds. Petals usually fall away. Some fruits, such as strawberries and apples, also include other parts of the flower."],["Types of fruit","Botanists divide fruits into types. Berries, such as grapes, tomatoes and bananas, have fleshy walls and many seeds. Drupes, such as mangoes and coconuts, have a single hard stone. Dry fruits include nuts, grains and pods. Aggregate fruits, such as raspberries, form from many parts of one flower."],["Spreading seeds","Fleshy fruits attract birds, bats and other animals, which eat them and drop the seeds far away. Other fruits are carried by wind, like dandelion parachutes, or by water, like coconuts. Some, such as burrs, hook onto fur."],["Ripening","Many fruits change color, soften and become sweeter as they ripen. A natural gas called ethylene triggers ripening in fruits such as bananas and mangoes. Placing an unripe fruit near a ripe one can speed ripening for this reason."]],
k:[["Develops from","A flower’s ovary"],["Purpose","Protect and spread seeds"],["Ripening gas","Ethylene"],["Botanical fruits","Tomatoes, peppers, cucumbers"],["Major Philippine exports","Bananas and pineapples"]],
d:[["c. 11,000 years ago","Figs are among the earliest cultivated fruit trees"],["c. 4,000 years ago","Mangoes are cultivated in South Asia"],["1500s","Pineapples, papayas and guavas reach Asia from the Americas"],["1893","The US Supreme Court rules tomatoes count as vegetables for tax purposes"],["1960s","Large-scale banana plantations expand in Mindanao"],["2020s","Philippine mangoes and bananas are exported to many countries"]],
s2:[["Philippine mangoes","The Carabao mango, also called the Manila mango, is known for its sweetness and smooth texture and is often called the national fruit of the Philippines. Guimaras and Zambales are famous for their mangoes. Mangoes are eaten ripe as desserts, green with bagoong, dried as snacks and blended into drinks."],["The king of fruits","The durian, grown widely in Davao, has a spiky husk and a strong smell that some people love and others find unbearable. It is banned on some public transport in Southeast Asia because of its odor. Supporters praise its creamy, sweet flesh."],["Is it a fruit or a vegetable?","Botanically, a tomato is a fruit because it contains seeds and develops from a flower. In cooking, however, it is treated as a vegetable because it is savory. In 1893 the United States Supreme Court ruled that tomatoes should be taxed as vegetables, following everyday usage rather than botany."],["Bananas","Banana plants are giant herbs, not trees, and their fruits are botanically berries. Most bananas sold worldwide are seedless Cavendish bananas, which are grown from cuttings. Because they are genetically very similar, diseases such as Panama disease threaten plantations, including in Mindanao."],["Breeding sweeter fruits","Over thousands of years, people selected the biggest, sweetest fruits to plant, gradually transforming wild plants. Wild bananas are full of hard seeds, and wild apples are small and sour. Modern breeding and grafting continue to create new fruit varieties."],["Fruits and health","Health organizations recommend eating fruits and vegetables every day because they provide vitamins, minerals and fiber. Calamansi and guava are rich in vitamin C. Nutritionists generally advise eating whole fruits rather than only drinking juices, which contain less fiber."]],
sp:["Why plants make fruit","To a plant, a juicy fruit is a deal with animals. The plant packs its seeds inside sweet flesh that animals want to eat. In return, the animal carries the seeds away and drops them elsewhere, often in a small pile of fertilizer. Bright red and orange colors signal ripeness to birds, which see color well, while bat-pollinated and bat-dispersed fruits are often dull but strong-smelling. Some seeds even sprout better after passing through an animal’s gut. Over millions of years, this partnership has shaped both fruits and the animals that eat them, including humans."],
g:[["Fruit","The seed-bearing part of a flowering plant"],["Ovary","The part of a flower that becomes the fruit"],["Drupe","A fruit with one hard stone"],["Ethylene","A gas that makes fruits ripen"]],
f:["Tomatoes are botanically fruits.","Bananas are berries.","Ethylene gas makes fruits ripen."],
fun:"Botanically, strawberries are not true berries, but bananas and eggplants are."});

ADDART({id:"seaweeds",t:"Seaweeds",c:"Plants",i:"🌿",w:"Seaweed",r:["sea","coral","oxygen","fishes","phfood"],
p:[["Ocean algae","Seaweeds are large algae that grow in the sea."],["Underwater forests","Giant kelp forms forests along cold coasts."],["Philippine farming","The Philippines is one of the world’s leading seaweed farmers."]],
l:"Seaweeds are large, multicellular algae that live in the sea, usually attached to rocks or the seafloor. Although they look like plants and make food through photosynthesis, most scientists do not classify them as true plants. Seaweeds are grouped by color into green, red and brown algae. They provide food and shelter for many sea creatures, and kelp forests are among the richest ocean habitats. Seaweeds are eaten in many cultures, especially in East and Southeast Asia, and are used to make thickeners such as agar and carrageenan found in ice cream, toothpaste and medicines. The Philippines is a major producer of farmed seaweed, much of it grown by coastal families in Mindanao and the Visayas.",
s:[["Three main groups","Green seaweeds include sea lettuce and the grape-like lato. Red seaweeds, the most varied group, include nori and the seaweeds used for carrageenan and agar. Brown seaweeds include kelps and sargassum, some of which grow very large."],["Holdfasts, not roots","Seaweeds attach to rocks with a structure called a holdfast, which anchors them but does not absorb nutrients like roots. They take in water and nutrients directly through their surfaces. Many have gas-filled bladders that keep them floating toward sunlight."],["Kelp forests","Giant kelp, a brown seaweed of cool coasts, can grow more than half a meter in a single day and reach over 40 meters long. Kelp forests shelter fish, sea otters, sea urchins and many other animals. They are threatened by warming seas and by overgrazing urchins."],["Making oxygen","Seaweeds and microscopic ocean algae carry out photosynthesis, releasing oxygen and absorbing carbon dioxide. Ocean algae as a whole produce a large share of the oxygen in Earth’s atmosphere."]],
k:[["Type of organism","Marine algae"],["Main groups","Green, red and brown"],["Anchor","Holdfast"],["Thickeners made","Agar, carrageenan, alginate"],["Major Philippine crop","Carrageenan seaweed"]],
d:[["c. 14,000 years ago","Early people in Chile use seaweeds for food and medicine"],["1600s","Agar is discovered in Japan"],["1949","Kathleen Drew-Baker uncovers the life cycle of nori"],["1970s","Commercial seaweed farming begins in the southern Philippines"],["2010s","Scientists test seaweed feed to cut methane from cattle"],["2020s","Seaweed farming is promoted to support coastal livelihoods and capture carbon"]],
s2:[["Seaweed farming in the Philippines","In the early 1970s, Philippine farmers working with scientists began farming red seaweeds such as Kappaphycus and Eucheuma in shallow waters of Mindanao and the Sulu Archipelago. Farmers tie seaweed cuttings to ropes or lines and harvest them after about six to eight weeks. The dried seaweed is processed into carrageenan. The Philippines was long the world’s top producer and remains a leading one, and seaweed supports many coastal families, though farmers face challenges from low prices, disease and typhoons."],["Seaweeds in Filipino food","Filipinos eat many seaweeds. Lato, or sea grapes, is served fresh with tomatoes and vinegar. Guso is used in salads, and gulaman, a jelly made from seaweed extracts, is a popular dessert ingredient. Seaweed is increasingly used in noodles, snacks and other products."],["The woman who saved nori","In Japan, nori farming once relied on luck because no one knew how its spores formed. In 1949 the British scientist Kathleen Drew-Baker discovered that nori spends part of its life growing inside seashells. Japanese farmers used her discovery to seed nori reliably, and a shrine in Japan honors her as the Mother of the Sea."],["Seaweed in everyday products","Carrageenan, agar and alginate extracted from seaweeds thicken and stabilize many foods, such as ice cream, chocolate milk and jelly, as well as toothpaste, cosmetics and medicines. Agar is also used in laboratories to grow bacteria. Some people raise questions about carrageenan’s effects on digestion, while food safety agencies have generally judged it safe at permitted levels."],["Seaweed and climate","Because seaweeds absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, some researchers hope that seaweed farming could help fight climate change. Others caution that much of the carbon may return to the air when seaweed rots or is eaten, so the benefits are still being studied. Seaweed farming also requires no fresh water or fertilizer."],["Sargassum blooms","Floating brown seaweed called sargassum forms huge mats in the Atlantic. In recent years, massive sargassum blooms have washed onto beaches in the Caribbean and West Africa, harming tourism and fisheries. Scientists are studying causes such as nutrient pollution and changes in ocean conditions."]],
sp:["A day on a seaweed farm","Before sunrise, a family in Tawi-Tawi paddles out to their seaweed farm, a grid of nylon lines strung between wooden stakes in clear, shallow water. They check each line, removing weeds and replacing broken cuttings. When the seaweed has grown thick, they pull it up into their boats and spread it out to dry on bamboo platforms in the sun. After a few days, the dried seaweed is packed into sacks and sold to traders, who send it to factories that extract carrageenan. Much of that carrageenan ends up in foods and products sold around the world."],
g:[["Algae","Simple organisms that make food from sunlight"],["Holdfast","The structure that anchors seaweed"],["Kelp","A large brown seaweed"],["Carrageenan","A thickener made from red seaweeds"]],
f:["Seaweeds are algae, not true plants.","Giant kelp grows very fast.","The Philippines is a major seaweed farmer."],
fun:"The gelatin-like agar in laboratory dishes comes from seaweed."});

ADDART({id:"coconut",t:"The Coconut Palm",c:"Plants",i:"🥥",w:"Coconut",r:["trees","phfood","agri","fruits","sea","seeddispersal"],
p:[["Tree of life","The coconut palm is called the tree of life because every part is useful."],["Floating seeds","Coconuts can float across oceans and sprout on distant beaches."],["Philippine crop","The Philippines is one of the world’s largest coconut producers."]],
l:"The coconut palm, Cocos nucifera, is a tall palm tree that grows along tropical coasts around the world. Its fruit, the coconut, is not a true nut but a drupe with a hard shell surrounded by a fibrous husk. Coconuts provide water, flesh, milk and oil, while the palm’s leaves, trunk and husks are used for roofing, building, rope, mats and fuel, which is why it is often called the tree of life. Coconuts can float and survive long journeys at sea, helping the palm spread across tropical islands. People in Southeast Asia and the Pacific have grown coconuts for thousands of years. The Philippines is one of the world’s biggest coconut producers, and coconut farming supports millions of rural Filipinos.",
s:[["Parts of a coconut","A coconut has a smooth outer skin, a thick fibrous husk, and a hard inner shell. Inside the shell are white flesh, called meat, and coconut water. The three eye-like pores on the shell include one through which the sprout emerges."],["Young and mature coconuts","Young coconuts, called buko in the Philippines, are full of sweet water and soft, jelly-like flesh. As the coconut matures, the water decreases and the flesh becomes thick and firm. Mature coconut flesh is grated and squeezed to make coconut milk, or dried to make copra, from which oil is pressed."],["Ocean voyagers","The fibrous husk traps air, allowing coconuts to float. Coconuts can drift on ocean currents and still sprout after long periods at sea. Humans also carried coconuts in canoes as they settled islands across the Pacific and Indian Oceans."],["Tall palms","Coconut palms can grow up to about 30 meters tall and live for many decades. They have no branches; a crown of long, feather-like leaves grows from the top of a single trunk. Dwarf varieties have been bred to make harvesting easier."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Cocos nucifera"],["Fruit type","Drupe"],["Dried flesh","Copra"],["Young coconut in Filipino","Buko"],["Major producers","Indonesia, the Philippines, India"]],
d:[["Ancient times","Coconuts are cultivated in Southeast Asia and the Pacific"],["1521","Antonio Pigafetta describes Filipinos’ many uses of the coconut"],["1949","Nata de coco is developed in the Philippines"],["1973","The Philippine Coconut Authority is established"],["2021","A law creates a trust fund from the coconut levy for farmers"],["2020s","The Philippines launches programs to replant aging coconut trees"]],
s2:[["Coconut in the Philippines","Coconut palms grow throughout the Philippines, especially in Quezon, the Bicol region, the Visayas and Mindanao. Coconut oil and copra have been major exports for over a century. Despite the industry’s size, many coconut farmers earn low incomes, and aging trees and typhoon damage have reduced harvests."],["Coconut in Filipino life","Coconut is central to Filipino cooking, from ginataan dishes cooked in coconut milk to desserts such as buko pie and bibingka. Lambanog is a strong drink distilled from coconut sap, and tuba is a fermented palm wine. Nata de coco, a chewy jelly made by fermenting coconut water, was developed in the Philippines and is now popular across Asia."],["The coconut levy","From 1971 to the early 1980s, the Philippine government collected a levy from coconut farmers, intended to develop the industry. How the funds were used became one of the country’s longest-running controversies, with farmers’ groups saying the money was misused and benefited a few people, while others defended some of the investments made. After decades of court cases, a 2021 law created a trust fund meant to return the benefits to coconut farmers."],["Coconut oil and health","Coconut oil is high in saturated fat. Some people promote it as a healthy oil, saying its fats are processed differently by the body. Many health organizations, however, advise limiting saturated fats, including coconut oil, because they can raise cholesterol. Researchers continue to study the question."],["Useful from top to bottom","Coconut leaves are woven into roofs, baskets and the small rice pouches called puso. The husk fiber, called coir, is made into ropes, mats and doormats. Shells become bowls, charcoal and crafts, and coconut lumber is used for building."],["Coconut crabs","The coconut crab is the largest land-dwelling arthropod, found on islands in the Indian and Pacific Oceans, including parts of the Philippines. It can climb palm trees and crack coconuts with its powerful claws. It is threatened by overharvesting in many areas."]],
sp:["Pigafetta and the tree of life","When Ferdinand Magellan’s expedition reached the Philippines in 1521, the chronicler Antonio Pigafetta was amazed by the coconut palm. He wrote that the people obtained bread, wine, oil and vinegar from it, and that a family could live from just a couple of trees. He described how the islanders tapped the palm for sweet sap and made oil and milk from the flesh. Five centuries later, coconut still plays a central role in Philippine life, in kitchens, farms, crafts and celebrations."],
g:[["Drupe","A fruit with a single hard stone"],["Copra","Dried coconut flesh"],["Coir","Fiber from coconut husks"],["Buko","A young coconut"]],
f:["A coconut is not a true nut.","Coconuts can float across oceans.","Nata de coco was developed in the Philippines."],
fun:"A coconut can survive months floating at sea and still sprout on a distant beach."});

ADDART({id:"seeds",t:"Seeds",c:"Plants",i:"🌱",w:"Seed",r:["fruits","flower","agri","rice","mendel","dandelions","seeddispersal"],
p:[["Baby plants","A seed contains a tiny plant and a supply of food."],["Waiting to grow","Some seeds can wait for years before sprouting."],["Seed vaults","People store seeds in vaults to protect crops for the future."]],
l:"A seed is a small, protected package that contains an embryonic plant and a supply of food, wrapped in a seed coat. Seeds allow plants to reproduce and to spread to new places, and they can survive cold, drought and long waits until conditions are right for growth. Flowering plants produce seeds enclosed in fruits, while conifers and other gymnosperms carry seeds exposed on cones. Seeds come in an enormous range of sizes, from dust-like orchid seeds to the giant seed of the coco de mer palm. Humans depend on seeds for much of their food, including rice, corn, wheat, beans and nuts. Seed banks around the world, including in the Philippines, store seeds to protect crop diversity.",
s:[["Inside a seed","A seed has three main parts. The embryo is the baby plant, with a tiny root and shoot. The stored food, in the endosperm or in seed leaves called cotyledons, feeds the embryo when it starts to grow. The seed coat protects it from damage and drying out."],["Germination","When a seed absorbs water and has enough warmth and oxygen, it begins to germinate. The seed coat splits, a root grows downward and a shoot grows upward toward the light. Once the shoot reaches the light and forms leaves, the seedling can make its own food."],["Dormancy","Many seeds remain dormant, or resting, until conditions are right. Some need cold winters, fire, smoke or passing through an animal’s gut before they will sprout. Dormancy prevents seeds from germinating at the wrong time, such as just before a drought."],["Getting around","Seeds travel in many ways. Some float on wind, like maple and dandelion seeds. Others are carried by water, animals or even explode out of their pods. Ants carry some seeds to their nests, helping plant them."]],
k:[["Main parts","Embryo, stored food, seed coat"],["Sprouting","Germination"],["Largest seed","Coco de mer"],["Oldest sprouted seed","About 2,000-year-old date palm"],["Global seed vault","Svalbard, Norway"]],
d:[["c. 365 million years ago","The first seed plants appear"],["c. 10,000 years ago","Farmers begin saving and planting seeds of crops"],["1866","Gregor Mendel publishes his pea seed experiments"],["1960s","The International Rice Research Institute begins collecting rice seeds in Los Baños"],["2005","A 2,000-year-old date palm seed is sprouted in Israel"],["2008","The Svalbard Global Seed Vault opens in Norway"]],
s2:[["Seeds and farming","Farming began when people learned to save seeds from the best plants and sow them. Over generations, farmers selected seeds that did not scatter, grew larger and tasted better, transforming wild grasses into crops such as rice and wheat. Many farmers in the Philippines and elsewhere still save seeds of traditional varieties."],["The rice gene bank","The International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños, Laguna, keeps the world’s largest collection of rice seeds, with well over 100,000 samples of rice varieties and wild relatives from around the world. Scientists and breeders use these seeds to develop rice that resists disease, floods and drought."],["The doomsday vault","The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, dug into a frozen mountain on a Norwegian island near the Arctic, opened in 2008. It stores backup copies of seeds from gene banks worldwide in case of disasters. In 2015 researchers made the first withdrawal, retrieving seeds that had been lost in Syria because of war."],["Ancient seeds","In 2005, scientists in Israel sprouted a date palm seed about 2,000 years old, found at the ancient fortress of Masada. The plant, nicknamed Methuselah, grew into a healthy tree. Sacred lotus seeds over 1,000 years old have also sprouted."],["Giants and specks","The coco de mer palm of the Seychelles produces the largest seed in the world, weighing up to about 25 kilograms. At the other extreme, orchid seeds are as fine as dust. Larger seeds carry more food for the young plant, while tiny seeds can be produced in huge numbers."],["Debates over seeds","Modern agriculture often uses hybrid or genetically modified seeds that farmers buy each season. Supporters say these seeds increase yields and resist pests. Critics worry that they make farmers dependent on seed companies and reduce the diversity of traditional varieties. Many countries, including the Philippines, have rules on seed saving and genetically modified crops."]],
sp:["The tree from Masada","In the 1960s, archaeologists digging at Masada, an ancient hilltop fortress in Israel, found a jar of date palm seeds about 2,000 years old. The seeds sat in a drawer for decades. In 2005, scientists soaked a few in water and fertilizer and planted them. To their surprise, one sprouted. The seedling, nicknamed Methuselah, grew into a tall date palm. Later, more ancient seeds were sprouted, including a female palm that was pollinated with Methuselah’s pollen and produced dates. The ancient Judean date, once famous in the region, is being brought back to life."],
g:[["Seed","A package containing a baby plant and its food"],["Embryo","The tiny plant inside a seed"],["Germination","When a seed begins to sprout"],["Dormancy","A resting state before sprouting"]],
f:["A 2,000-year-old seed has grown into a tree.","The coco de mer has the largest seed.","Seeds can stay dormant for years."],
fun:"The world’s largest seed, from the coco de mer palm, can weigh as much as a small child."});

