/* data/plants2.js */
// Plants: leaves, roots, medicinal plants, grasses, giant trees and water plants.
ADDART({id:"leaves",t:"Leaves",c:"Plants",i:"🍃",w:"Leaf",r:["photo","trees","oxygen","wcycle","flower","mimosa"],
p:[["Food factories","Leaves capture sunlight to make food for the plant."],["Tiny pores","Leaves breathe through tiny openings called stomata."],["Changing colors","In cold climates, many leaves turn red and gold before falling."]],
l:"Leaves are the main organs of photosynthesis in most plants. Using sunlight, water and carbon dioxide from the air, the green pigment chlorophyll in their cells makes sugars that feed the plant and releases oxygen. Leaves also release water vapor through tiny pores called stomata, a process called transpiration that pulls water up from the roots. Leaves come in an enormous variety of shapes and sizes, from tiny pine needles to raffia palm leaves longer than a bus, each adapted to its environment. Some leaves have become spines, tendrils or traps for insects. People use leaves for food, medicine, shelter and wrapping. In the Philippines, banana leaves, pandan and anahaw are part of everyday life and culture.",
s:[["Parts of a leaf","A typical leaf has a flat blade and a stalk called a petiole that joins it to the stem. Veins run through the blade, carrying water in and sugars out, and also give the leaf support. The outer surface is coated with a waxy layer that reduces water loss."],["Stomata","Stomata are tiny pores, usually on the underside of leaves, each surrounded by two guard cells that open and close it. When open, they let in carbon dioxide and release oxygen and water vapor. Plants close them in hot, dry weather to save water."],["Leaf shapes","Broad leaves capture lots of light in shady forests. Needle-like leaves of pines lose less water and shed snow. Thick, fleshy leaves of succulents store water in deserts. Floating leaves of water lilies have stomata on their upper surfaces."],["Autumn colors","In places with cold winters, many trees stop making chlorophyll as days shorten. As the green fades, yellow and orange pigments called carotenoids become visible, and some trees produce red pigments called anthocyanins. The trees then drop their leaves to save water and energy during winter."]],
k:[["Main job","Photosynthesis"],["Green pigment","Chlorophyll"],["Pores","Stomata"],["Water loss process","Transpiration"],["Largest leaves","Raffia palm"]],
d:[["c. 400 million years ago","Early plants begin to evolve true leaves"],["1771","Joseph Priestley finds that a mint plant restores air used up by a candle"],["1779","Jan Ingenhousz shows that plants need light to refresh the air"],["1817","Chlorophyll is named by French chemists"],["1950s","Melvin Calvin works out how leaves turn carbon dioxide into sugar"],["2010s","Scientists develop artificial leaves that use sunlight to split water"]],
s2:[["Leaves in Filipino life","Banana leaves are used to wrap and serve food, from suman and bibingka to the shared feasts known as boodle fights. Pandan leaves add fragrance to rice and desserts. Anahaw, a palm with large, fan-shaped leaves, is often regarded as a national leaf and is used for fans, roofing and decorations. Malunggay leaves are prized for their nutrition."],["Leaves that do other jobs","Some leaves have taken on new roles. Cactus spines are modified leaves that protect the plant and reduce water loss. Pea tendrils are leaves that coil around supports. Pitcher plants and Venus flytraps have leaves that trap insects. Onion bulbs are made of swollen leaves that store food."],["Giant leaves","The raffia palm of Africa has the longest leaves of any plant, sometimes over 20 meters. The giant Amazon water lily has round floating leaves up to about 3 meters across, strong enough to support a small child. Banana and taro plants also have very large leaves."],["How leaves cool the planet","Through transpiration, leaves release enormous amounts of water vapor into the air. This cools plants, much like sweating cools people, and adds moisture that forms clouds and rain. Large forests, such as the Amazon, help create their own rainfall in this way."],["Reading leaves","Scientists study fossil leaves to learn about past climates. Leaves with smooth edges are more common in warm climates, while toothed edges are more common in cool ones. The number of stomata on fossil leaves also reveals how much carbon dioxide was in the ancient air."],["Leaf eaters","Many animals eat leaves, including caterpillars, leaf-cutter ants, giraffes, koalas and sloths. Plants defend their leaves with tough textures, hairs, spines and bitter or poisonous chemicals. Some plants even release scents that attract predators of the insects eating them."]],
sp:["Priestley’s mint and the mouse","In 1771 the English scientist Joseph Priestley placed a burning candle in a sealed glass jar. The flame soon went out, and a mouse in such a jar could not survive. Then he put a sprig of mint in a jar where a candle had burned out. Days later, he found that a candle could burn there again. The mint had restored the air. A few years later, the Dutch scientist Jan Ingenhousz showed that plants refresh the air only in light. These experiments helped reveal that leaves produce oxygen, a gas later named and studied by Antoine Lavoisier."],
g:[["Chlorophyll","The green pigment that captures sunlight"],["Stomata","Tiny pores in leaves"],["Transpiration","The loss of water vapor from leaves"],["Petiole","The stalk joining a leaf to a stem"]],
f:["Leaves release oxygen.","Cactus spines are modified leaves.","Raffia palms have the longest leaves."],
fun:"A single large tree can release hundreds of liters of water through its leaves on a hot day."});

ADDART({id:"roots",t:"Roots",c:"Plants",i:"🥕",w:"Root",r:["soil","trees","fungi","leaves","wetlands"],
p:[["Hidden half","Roots anchor plants and soak up water and nutrients."],["Fungus partners","Most plant roots work together with fungi."],["Roots we eat","Carrots, cassava and sweet potatoes are roots."]],
l:"Roots are the parts of a plant that usually grow underground. They anchor the plant in the soil and absorb water and minerals, which they send up to the stems and leaves. Many roots also store food. Roots grow at their tips, pushing through soil guided by gravity, moisture and touch, and are covered with tiny root hairs that greatly increase their surface. Most plants form partnerships with fungi in their roots, and some, such as beans, host bacteria that capture nitrogen from the air. Roots help hold soil together and prevent erosion. Many important foods are roots, including carrots, cassava, sweet potatoes and the purple yam known in the Philippines as ube.",
s:[["Taproots and fibrous roots","Some plants, such as carrots and many trees, have a main taproot that grows straight down, with smaller roots branching off. Grasses, including rice and corn, have fibrous roots, a mass of thin roots spreading through the soil. Fibrous roots are especially good at holding soil in place."],["Root hairs","Just behind the growing tip, roots produce thousands of microscopic root hairs. These hair-like cells reach into tiny spaces between soil particles, absorbing water and dissolved minerals. They give roots a huge surface area for their size."],["Underground partners","Most land plants have mycorrhizae, partnerships in which fungi grow in or around their roots. The fungal threads reach far into the soil, bringing water and nutrients such as phosphorus to the plant, and the plant gives the fungi sugars in return. Bean and peanut roots form nodules filled with bacteria that turn nitrogen from the air into fertilizer."],["Growing the right way","Root tips sense gravity using tiny grains inside their cells, so they grow downward even if a seed lands upside down. Roots also grow toward water and away from obstacles, constantly probing the soil."]],
k:[["Main jobs","Anchor, absorb, store"],["Types","Taproot and fibrous"],["Tiny absorbing cells","Root hairs"],["Fungal partnership","Mycorrhiza"],["Philippine root crops","Kamote, cassava, ube, gabi"]],
d:[["c. 400 million years ago","The first true roots evolve"],["1880","Charles and Francis Darwin study how root tips sense their surroundings"],["1885","Albert Bernhard Frank names mycorrhizae"],["1888","Scientists show that bacteria in bean root nodules capture nitrogen"],["1937","A study measures the enormous total root length of a single rye plant"],["2010s","Scientists research how plants share resources through underground fungal networks"]],
s2:[["Roots on the table","Root crops are important foods in the Philippines, especially in upland and island areas. Kamote, or sweet potato, and cassava, also called kamoteng kahoy, are boiled, fried or made into desserts. Ube, the purple yam, is used in halaya, ice cream and pastries and has become famous worldwide. Gabi, or taro, is cooked in dishes such as laing, though it is technically a corm."],["Roots above ground","Some roots grow in the air. Mangroves have stilt roots that prop them up in mud and breathing roots that stick up out of the water to take in air. Orchids have aerial roots that absorb moisture. Strangler figs, called balete in the Philippines, send roots down from branches to the ground, eventually surrounding their host trees."],["A hidden giant","In 1937 the scientist Howard Dittmer carefully measured all the roots of a single rye plant grown in a box of soil for four months. He estimated that its roots alone stretched over 600 kilometers, and that its root hairs added thousands of kilometers more. His study showed how vast a plant’s hidden underground system can be."],["The deepest roots","Some plants send roots extremely deep to reach water. The roots of a wild fig tree in a South African cave have been traced to about 120 meters below the surface. Desert plants such as mesquite can also root very deeply."],["Holding the land together","Plant roots bind soil and help prevent erosion and landslides. When forests on steep slopes are cleared, the loss of roots can make landslides more likely during heavy rains. Planting vetiver grass and trees with strong roots is one way communities protect slopes and riverbanks."],["Do plants communicate underground?","Some scientists have suggested that trees share sugars and warning signals through fungal networks connecting their roots, sometimes called the wood wide web. Others argue that the evidence for widespread sharing between trees is weaker than popular accounts suggest. Research on how much plants interact underground is ongoing."]],
sp:["The root tip as a brain","In 1880 Charles Darwin and his son Francis published a book on how plants move. After many experiments, they noticed that the very tip of a root seemed to control its direction, sensing gravity, moisture and obstacles and steering the root around them. They wrote that the root tip acts somewhat like the brain of a lower animal. Most scientists today would not call it a brain, but research has confirmed that root tips process many signals at once. Darwin’s simple experiments with seedlings opened a field of research that continues today."],
g:[["Root hair","A tiny cell that absorbs water"],["Taproot","A main root that grows straight down"],["Mycorrhiza","A partnership between roots and fungi"],["Nodule","A swelling on roots that holds nitrogen-fixing bacteria"]],
f:["Most plant roots partner with fungi.","Ube and kamote are roots.","Roots grow downward by sensing gravity."],
fun:"A single rye plant was found to have over 600 kilometers of roots, not counting its root hairs."});

ADDART({id:"medicinalplants",t:"Medicinal Plants",c:"Plants",i:"🌿",w:"Medicinal plants",r:["malaria","antibiotics","flower","trees","conservation","ginkgo","willows"],
p:[["Medicine from nature","Many modern medicines were first found in plants."],["Traditional healing","People have used plants to treat illness for thousands of years."],["Philippine herbs","Lagundi and sambong are well-known Philippine herbal medicines."]],
l:"Medicinal plants are plants used to prevent or treat illness. People in every culture have used herbs, roots, leaves and bark as medicine for thousands of years, and traditional medicine systems in China, India, Africa, the Americas and the Philippines rely heavily on plants. Many modern drugs were first discovered in plants, including aspirin from willow bark, morphine from the opium poppy, quinine from cinchona bark and the malaria drug artemisinin from sweet wormwood. Scientists continue to search plants for new medicines. At the same time, not all herbal remedies are proven to work, and some can be harmful or interact with other medicines, so health experts encourage testing remedies scientifically and using them carefully.",
s:[["From plant to pill","Plants produce many chemicals to defend themselves against insects, germs and grazing animals. Some of these chemicals affect the human body. Scientists isolate the active substance, test it for safety and effectiveness, and then may produce it in pure form or make similar compounds in laboratories."],["Famous plant medicines","Aspirin is based on salicin, found in willow bark, which people chewed for pain for centuries. Morphine, a powerful painkiller, comes from the opium poppy. Digoxin, a heart medicine, comes from foxglove. Quinine, once the main malaria treatment, comes from the bark of the South American cinchona tree."],["Cancer drugs from plants","Vincristine and vinblastine, used to treat leukemia and other cancers, come from the Madagascar periwinkle. Paclitaxel, also called Taxol, was first found in the bark of the Pacific yew tree. These discoveries encouraged scientists to search rainforests for new medicines."],["Traditional medicine","Traditional Chinese medicine, Ayurveda in India and many indigenous healing traditions use complex mixtures of plants. Supporters value their long history and holistic approach, while critics point out that many traditional remedies have not been tested in rigorous studies. Some researchers study traditional remedies to find promising compounds."]],
k:[["Aspirin source","Willow bark"],["Quinine source","Cinchona tree"],["Artemisinin source","Sweet wormwood"],["Taxol source","Pacific yew"],["Philippine herbal law","Traditional and Alternative Medicine Act of 1997"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Medicinal plants are recorded on Sumerian clay tablets"],["1804","Morphine is isolated from the opium poppy"],["1820","Quinine is isolated from cinchona bark"],["1899","Aspirin is sold as a medicine"],["1972","Tu Youyou’s team isolates artemisinin"],["2015","Tu Youyou receives the Nobel Prize for discovering artemisinin"]],
s2:[["Herbal medicine in the Philippines","Filipinos have long used plants such as lagundi for coughs, sambong for kidney problems, ampalaya for diabetes and bayabas leaves for cleaning wounds. In the 1990s, the Department of Health promoted ten herbal plants it considered supported by research. The Traditional and Alternative Medicine Act of 1997 created an institute to study and regulate traditional remedies. Lagundi and sambong tablets developed by Philippine scientists are sold in pharmacies."],["The story of artemisinin","In the 1960s and 1970s, the Chinese scientist Tu Youyou searched ancient Chinese medical texts for malaria remedies. A 4th-century text mentioned sweet wormwood soaked in cold water. Her team found that extracting it at low temperature preserved the active substance, artemisinin. Artemisinin-based drugs have since saved millions of lives, and she shared the 2015 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."],["Natural does not mean safe","Some plants used as remedies can be poisonous in large amounts, and herbal products can interact with prescription medicines. Products sold as supplements may not be tested as strictly as medicines. Health authorities advise people to tell their doctors about any herbal remedies they take and not to replace proven treatments for serious diseases."],["Debates over evidence","Supporters of herbal medicine argue that traditional knowledge built over generations is valuable and that plant remedies can be cheaper and more accessible. Skeptics argue that only remedies proven in controlled trials should be promoted. Many scientists take a middle view, calling for traditional remedies to be studied carefully so that effective ones can be used safely."],["Protecting plants and knowledge","Many medicinal plants are harvested from the wild, and some are becoming rare. Conservationists work to protect them and encourage cultivation. There are also debates about biopiracy, when companies patent medicines based on indigenous knowledge without sharing benefits; international agreements now encourage fair sharing."],["Medicines still waiting","Scientists believe that many plant species have never been tested for medical uses. As forests disappear, potential medicines may be lost before they are discovered. This is one argument conservationists use for protecting biodiversity, including in the Philippine rainforests."]],
sp:["The willow and the aspirin","For thousands of years, people in ancient Egypt, Greece and elsewhere used willow bark and leaves to ease pain and fever. In the 1800s, chemists extracted salicin from willow and turned it into salicylic acid, which worked but irritated the stomach. In 1897 chemists at the German company Bayer produced a gentler form, acetylsalicylic acid. Sold from 1899 as aspirin, it became one of the most widely used medicines in history. Scientists later found that aspirin also helps prevent some heart attacks and strokes. A remedy from a riverside tree had become a modern drug."],
g:[["Medicinal plant","A plant used to treat illness"],["Active ingredient","The chemical in a remedy that has an effect"],["Herbal medicine","Treatment using plants"],["Clinical trial","A careful scientific test of a treatment"]],
f:["Aspirin was first made from a substance in willow bark.","Artemisinin treats malaria.","Lagundi is a Philippine herbal medicine for cough."],
fun:"The malaria drug artemisinin was rediscovered with help from a medical text written about 1,600 years ago."});

ADDART({id:"grasses",t:"Grasses",c:"Plants",i:"🌾",w:"Poaceae",r:["rice","wheat","maize","bamboo","grasslands"],
p:[["Feeding the world","Rice, wheat and corn are all grasses."],["Growing from the base","Grasses grow from near the ground, so they survive grazing and mowing."],["Covering the land","Grasslands cover a large part of Earth’s land."]],
l:"Grasses are flowering plants of the family Poaceae, with about 12,000 species. They are among the most important plants on Earth. The cereal grains that provide much of humanity’s food, including rice, wheat, corn, barley, oats and sorghum, are all grasses, as are sugarcane and bamboo. Grasslands such as savannas, prairies and steppes cover large areas of the planet and support grazing animals from zebras to cattle. Grasses grow from points near their base rather than from their tips, which allows them to regrow quickly after being grazed, mowed or burned. Their fibrous roots hold soil in place. In the Philippines, rice is the staple food, and cogon grass covers many hillsides.",
s:[["How grasses grow","Most plants grow from their tips, but grasses grow from regions near the base of their leaves and stems. When animals eat the tops or people mow lawns, the grass keeps growing from below. This has made grasses extremely successful in places with grazing animals and frequent fires."],["Grass flowers","Grasses have small, plain flowers without colorful petals, grouped in clusters called spikelets. They are pollinated by wind rather than insects, releasing large amounts of light pollen, which is a major cause of hay fever. Their fruits are grains."],["Hollow stems","Grass stems, called culms, are usually hollow between solid joints called nodes. This structure is light but strong. Bamboo, the giant of the grass family, uses the same design to grow as tall as trees."],["Saving water","Many tropical grasses, including corn, sugarcane and sorghum, use a special type of photosynthesis called C4, which works efficiently in heat and bright sunlight and uses less water. Scientists are researching whether rice could be engineered to use C4 photosynthesis to increase yields."]],
k:[["Family","Poaceae"],["Species","About 12,000"],["Major cereals","Rice, wheat, corn"],["Pollination","Wind"],["Philippine staple","Rice"]],
d:[["c. 66 million years ago","Grasses already exist, as shown by fossil dung of dinosaurs"],["c. 20 million years ago","Grasslands spread across many continents"],["c. 10,000 years ago","Wheat and barley are domesticated in the Middle East"],["c. 9,000 years ago","Corn is domesticated from teosinte in Mexico"],["1966","The International Rice Research Institute releases IR8 rice"],["2020s","Researchers work to develop rice with more efficient photosynthesis"]],
s2:[["Grains that built civilizations","The domestication of grasses changed human history. Wheat and barley supported the first farming villages and cities of the Middle East, rice fed civilizations in Asia, and corn fed the peoples of the Americas. Stored grain allowed populations to grow and supported trade, writing and the rise of states."],["Rice and the Philippines","Rice has been grown in the Philippines for thousands of years, and the Cordillera rice terraces are a UNESCO World Heritage Site. In 1966, the International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños released IR8, a high-yielding variety that helped spark the Green Revolution in Asia. Supporters credit such varieties with preventing famines, while critics point to greater dependence on fertilizers and the loss of traditional varieties."],["Cogon grass","Cogon grass, Imperata cylindrica, covers large areas of Philippine hillsides, often after forests are cleared and burned. It spreads quickly and is hard to remove, which makes it difficult for trees to regrow. Traditionally, cogon has been used for thatched roofs. It is considered an invasive weed in many countries."],["Sugarcane","Sugarcane is a tall grass that stores sugar in its stems. It was domesticated in New Guinea and spread across Asia and later to the Americas, where plantations relied on enslaved labor. In the Philippines, sugarcane is a major crop in Negros and Panay, where the sugar industry has shaped the region’s history and society."],["Lawns and sports","People plant grasses for lawns, parks, golf courses and sports fields. Lawns are popular for recreation, but some environmental groups note that they can use large amounts of water, fertilizer and pesticides. Some cities encourage native plants instead."],["Grasses and grazing animals","Grasses evolved alongside grazing animals. Grass leaves contain tiny particles of silica that wear down teeth, and grazing animals such as horses, cattle and zebras evolved tall, continuously growing or hard-wearing teeth to cope. Grasslands support some of the largest herds of animals on Earth."]],
sp:["The grass that became corn","About 9,000 years ago in southern Mexico, people began gathering a wild grass called teosinte. Its tiny ears held only a handful of hard kernels, each wrapped in a tough casing. Over thousands of years, farmers kept seeds from plants with bigger ears and softer kernels. Slowly, teosinte was transformed into maize, or corn, with large cobs packed with hundreds of kernels. Scientists were long puzzled about corn’s origins because it looks so different from its wild ancestor, but genetic studies have confirmed the link. Corn is now one of the most widely grown crops in the world."],
g:[["Grass","A plant of the family Poaceae"],["Cereal","A grass grown for its grain"],["Culm","The hollow stem of a grass"],["Teosinte","The wild grass ancestor of corn"]],
f:["Rice, wheat and corn are grasses.","Bamboo is a giant grass.","Grasses are pollinated by wind."],
fun:"Fossil dinosaur dung shows that grasses existed when dinosaurs were alive."});

ADDART({id:"giantrees",t:"Giant Trees",c:"Plants",i:"🌲",w:"List of superlative trees",r:["trees","deforestation","conservation","amazon","climate"],
p:[["The tallest","Coast redwoods are the tallest trees on Earth."],["The biggest","Giant sequoias are the largest trees by volume."],["The oldest","Some bristlecone pines are nearly 5,000 years old."]],
l:"Giant trees are among the largest and oldest living things on Earth. Coast redwoods in California grow taller than any other trees, with the tallest known, named Hyperion, measuring about 116 meters. Giant sequoias, which grow in California’s Sierra Nevada mountains, are the largest single trees by volume. Some bristlecone pines in the American West are nearly 5,000 years old, older than the Egyptian pyramids. Tropical forests also hold giants, such as the towering dipterocarp trees of Southeast Asia, including the Philippines. Giant trees store large amounts of carbon and provide homes for many species. Many have been lost to logging, and today they face threats from fire, drought and climate change.",
s:[["Coast redwoods","Coast redwoods grow along the foggy coast of northern California and southern Oregon. Fog provides moisture that helps them grow so tall. Their thick bark resists fire and insects. Hyperion, the tallest known tree, was discovered in 2006, and its exact location is kept secret to protect it."],["Giant sequoias","Giant sequoias do not grow as tall as redwoods, but their massive trunks make them the largest trees by volume. The General Sherman tree in Sequoia National Park is the largest known single tree. Sequoias need fire to open their cones and clear the ground for seedlings."],["Ancient pines","Great Basin bristlecone pines grow in harsh, dry mountains in the western United States. Their slow growth and dense wood help them survive for thousands of years. One tree, named Methuselah, has been dated by counting tree rings at more than 4,800 years old."],["Tropical giants","In the rainforests of Borneo, a yellow meranti tree named Menara was measured at about 100 meters in 2019, the tallest known tropical tree. Dipterocarps, the family it belongs to, dominate the lowland forests of Southeast Asia, including Philippine trees such as apitong, lauan and yakal."]],
k:[["Tallest species","Coast redwood"],["Tallest known tree","Hyperion, about 116 m"],["Largest by volume","General Sherman, giant sequoia"],["Oldest known individual","Methuselah, bristlecone pine"],["Tallest tropical tree","Menara, yellow meranti"]],
d:[["1852","Giant sequoias become widely known to Europeans and Americans"],["1890","Sequoia National Park is created in California"],["1957","Edmund Schulman dates the Methuselah bristlecone pine"],["2006","Hyperion is identified as the world’s tallest known tree"],["2019","Menara is measured as the tallest known tropical tree"],["2020","Wildfires kill many large giant sequoias"]],
s2:[["Giant trees of the Philippines","The Philippines once had vast forests of tall dipterocarp trees, which were heavily logged during the 20th century for timber, much of it exported as Philippine mahogany. Large trees still survive in protected areas. Famous old trees include ancient balete trees, such as the one in Lazi, Siquijor, and large narra and almaciga trees in the forests of Palawan and Mindanao."],["Climbing to measure","Measuring the world’s tallest trees is difficult. Scientists use lasers to estimate heights from the ground and then climb the trees with ropes, dropping a tape from the top to get exact measurements. Climbers also study the life in the canopy, where ferns, lichens, insects and even small animals live high above the ground."],["Fire and giant sequoias","Giant sequoias are adapted to frequent, low-intensity fires, which clear the forest floor and help seeds sprout. But after a century of fire suppression, forests became crowded with fuel. Severe wildfires in 2020 and 2021 killed a large share of the world’s big sequoias, alarming scientists. Forest managers now use controlled burns and thinning to reduce risks."],["Saving the redwoods","Logging removed most of the original coast redwood forests in the 1800s and 1900s. Conservation groups such as the Save the Redwoods League, founded in 1918, campaigned to protect the remaining old-growth forests. Today they are protected in national and state parks, which are a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["The oldest living things","Some trees grow as clones, sending up new trunks from the same roots. Pando, a grove of aspen trees in Utah, is a single organism connected underground and may be many thousands of years old. A Norway spruce in Sweden has roots estimated at nearly 10,000 years old, though its visible trunk is much younger."],["Why giant trees matter","Large old trees store huge amounts of carbon and produce many seeds. They provide nesting holes for birds and bats and shelter for many plants and insects. Scientists warn that big trees are declining worldwide because of logging, drought, heat and insect outbreaks."]],
sp:["The secret location of Hyperion","In 2006, two naturalists exploring a remote valley in Redwood National Park in California found a redwood that seemed taller than any known tree. A scientist climbed it and measured it at about 115.5 meters, making it the tallest known living tree. They named it Hyperion. Park officials kept its location secret to protect it. But after the location leaked online, many visitors hiked off-trail to find it, trampling the soil around its roots. In 2022, the park closed the area and warned of fines for anyone who visited, saying that people were damaging the very tree they came to admire."],
g:[["Redwood","A very tall conifer of California’s coast"],["Sequoia","A massive conifer of the Sierra Nevada"],["Dipterocarp","A family of tall tropical trees"],["Old-growth forest","A forest that has never been logged"]],
f:["The tallest known tree is about 116 meters tall.","Some bristlecone pines are nearly 5,000 years old.","Giant sequoias need fire to reproduce."],
fun:"The tallest known tree, Hyperion, is taller than a 30-story building."});

ADDART({id:"aquaticplants",t:"Water Plants",c:"Plants",i:"🪷",w:"Aquatic plant",r:["wetlands","lakes","flower","dugongs","seaweeds","lotus"],
p:[["Living in water","Aquatic plants grow in ponds, lakes, rivers and the sea."],["Floating leaves","Water lilies and lotuses float their leaves on the surface."],["A troublesome invader","Water hyacinth clogs many Philippine rivers and lakes."]],
l:"Aquatic plants are plants adapted to live in or on water. Some float freely on the surface, such as duckweed and water hyacinth; some are rooted in mud with floating leaves, such as water lilies and lotuses; others live fully underwater, such as pondweeds and seagrasses. Many have air-filled tissues that help them float and carry oxygen to their roots. Aquatic plants produce oxygen, provide food and shelter for fish, birds and insects, and filter pollutants from water. Some, such as rice, water spinach and lotus, are important foods. Others, especially water hyacinth, have become invasive pests that choke waterways. In the Philippines, kangkong is a favorite vegetable, while water hyacinth is a major problem in the Pasig River and Laguna de Bay.",
s:[["Floating plants","Free-floating plants drift on the water with their roots dangling below. Duckweeds are tiny, and one type, Wolffia, is the smallest flowering plant in the world. Water hyacinth and water lettuce have spongy leaves or stalks filled with air."],["Rooted with floating leaves","Water lilies and lotuses are rooted in the mud of ponds and lakes, with long stalks carrying leaves and flowers to the surface. Their leaves have stomata on top, unlike most plants. Lotus leaves are coated with tiny bumps and wax that make water roll off, carrying away dirt."],["Underwater plants","Submerged plants grow completely underwater. They have thin, flexible leaves that bend with currents and absorb nutrients and gases directly from the water. They provide hiding places for young fish and release oxygen into the water."],["Seagrasses","Seagrasses are flowering plants that live in shallow seawater, forming underwater meadows. They are the main food of dugongs and green sea turtles, and nurseries for many fish. Seagrass meadows store large amounts of carbon in their sediments. The Philippines has extensive seagrass meadows."]],
k:[["Main types","Floating, rooted-floating, submerged"],["Smallest flowering plant","Wolffia duckweed"],["Largest floating leaves","Giant water lily, Victoria"],["Philippine vegetable","Kangkong"],["Major invasive","Water hyacinth"]],
d:[["c. 700 AD","A sacred lotus seed that would sprout in modern times forms in China"],["1837","The giant Amazon water lily is named after Queen Victoria"],["1849","Joseph Paxton first makes the giant water lily bloom in England"],["c. 1912","Water hyacinth is introduced to the Philippines"],["1990s","Scientists study seagrass meadows as carbon stores"],["2022","A new species of giant water lily is identified at Kew Gardens"]],
s2:[["The lotus","The sacred lotus has been revered in Asian cultures for thousands of years, symbolizing purity because its beautiful flowers rise clean from muddy water. It is important in Hinduism and Buddhism and is the national flower of India. Its seeds, roots and leaves are eaten. Lotus seeds can remain alive for over a thousand years."],["Giant water lilies","Giant water lilies of South America have round leaves up to about 3 meters across, with upturned rims and spiny undersides. Ribs beneath the leaves give them great strength. In 1849 the gardener Joseph Paxton made one bloom in England, and the leaf’s structure is said to have inspired his design for the Crystal Palace. In 2022 botanists at Kew Gardens identified a third species, Victoria boliviana."],["The water hyacinth problem","Water hyacinth, native to South America, has beautiful purple flowers and was spread around the world as an ornamental plant. It grows extremely fast and forms thick mats that block boats, clog drains, reduce oxygen in water and worsen flooding. In the Philippines, it is a persistent problem in the Pasig River, Laguna de Bay and other waterways, and is regularly cleared by crews and machines."],["Turning weeds into products","Some Philippine communities harvest water hyacinth and weave its dried stems into baskets, bags, slippers and furniture, earning income while helping clear waterways. It has also been tested for making paper, compost and biogas."],["Food from water plants","Kangkong, or water spinach, grows quickly in wet places and is one of the most popular vegetables in the Philippines, cooked in sinigang, adobong kangkong and many other dishes. Lotus roots, watercress, water chestnuts and taro are eaten in many parts of Asia. Rice itself is grown in flooded fields."],["Cleaning water","Aquatic plants absorb nutrients and some pollutants from water. Engineers build artificial wetlands planted with reeds and other water plants to treat wastewater naturally. Too many nutrients from fertilizers and sewage, however, can cause overgrowth of algae and some water plants."]],
sp:["A leaf that inspired a palace","In 1849 Joseph Paxton, head gardener at Chatsworth House in England, built a special heated tank and became the first person to make the giant Amazon water lily bloom in Europe. To show how strong its leaves were, he placed his young daughter on one. Paxton studied the network of ribs beneath the leaf and said it gave him ideas for a building of iron and glass. Two years later, he designed the Crystal Palace, a huge glass hall for the Great Exhibition of 1851 in London. A water plant from the Amazon had helped inspire one of the most famous buildings of the age."],
g:[["Aquatic plant","A plant that lives in water"],["Seagrass","A flowering plant that grows in shallow seawater"],["Invasive species","A non-native living thing that spreads harmfully"],["Duckweed","Tiny floating plants"]],
f:["The smallest flowering plant is a duckweed.","Kangkong is a water plant.","Water hyacinth is invasive in the Philippines."],
fun:"A giant water lily leaf can support the weight of a small child."});

