/* data/science11.js */
// Science: water chemistry, static electricity, electric circuits, Kepler's laws and the discovery of the nucleus.
ADDART({id:"waterchem",t:"Water: The Remarkable Molecule",c:"Chemistry",i:"💧",w:"Properties of water",r:["molecules","states","wcycle","acid","mixtures"],
p:[["H₂O","A water molecule has two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom."],["Strange behavior","Ice floats, which is unusual for a solid."],["Universal solvent","Water dissolves more substances than almost any other liquid."]],
l:"Water is a simple molecule made of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom, written H₂O, yet it has unusual properties that make life on Earth possible. Water molecules are polar, with a slightly negative oxygen end and slightly positive hydrogen ends, so they attract one another through hydrogen bonds. This explains why water has a high boiling point for such a small molecule, why it can absorb lots of heat, why it has strong surface tension and why it dissolves so many substances. Unusually, solid water, or ice, is less dense than liquid water, so ice floats. Water covers about 71 percent of Earth’s surface, makes up most of the human body, and is the only common substance found naturally as a solid, liquid and gas on Earth.",
s:[["A bent molecule","The two hydrogen atoms in a water molecule are attached to the oxygen at an angle of about 104.5 degrees, giving the molecule a bent shape. Oxygen pulls electrons more strongly than hydrogen, so the molecule has a negative end and positive ends. This polarity is the key to many of water’s special properties."],["Hydrogen bonds","The positive hydrogen of one water molecule is attracted to the negative oxygen of another, forming a hydrogen bond. These bonds are weaker than the bonds inside a molecule but are constantly forming and breaking. Together, they hold water molecules close, giving water its high boiling point and surface tension."],["Why ice floats","When water freezes, hydrogen bonds lock the molecules into an open, six-sided crystal pattern with more space than in liquid water. This makes ice about 9 percent less dense than liquid water, so it floats. Water is densest at about 4 degrees Celsius. Floating ice insulates lakes and seas, allowing life to survive below in winter."],["A great dissolver","Because water molecules are polar, they surround and pull apart many substances, such as salt and sugar, dissolving them. This is why water is sometimes called the universal solvent. In living things, water carries nutrients, salts and wastes, and most chemical reactions in cells take place in water."]],
k:[["Formula","H₂O"],["Bond angle","About 104.5°"],["Boiling point at sea level","100 °C"],["Densest at","About 4 °C"],["Key attraction","Hydrogen bonding"]],
d:[["1781","Henry Cavendish makes water by burning hydrogen"],["1783","Antoine Lavoisier concludes that water is a compound of hydrogen and oxygen"],["1800","Water is split into hydrogen and oxygen using electricity"],["1805","Gay-Lussac and Humboldt show water contains two volumes of hydrogen to one of oxygen"],["1920s","Scientists describe hydrogen bonding"],["2020s","Scientists continue to study the unusual behavior of water under extreme conditions"]],
s2:[["Surface tension","Water molecules at the surface are pulled inward and sideways by their neighbors, creating a kind of skin. Surface tension lets water striders walk on ponds, makes water form round droplets and lets you float a needle on water if placed gently. Soap reduces surface tension, helping water wash away dirt."],["Holding heat","Water can absorb a lot of heat without its temperature rising much, a property called high specific heat. Oceans store enormous amounts of heat, moderating climates along coasts. This is why coastal places such as many Philippine islands have smaller differences between day and night temperatures than inland deserts."],["Climbing up","Water molecules stick to each other, called cohesion, and to other surfaces, called adhesion. Together these let water climb up narrow tubes, an effect called capillary action. It helps water rise through soil and through the thin tubes inside plant stems, and it is why a paper towel soaks up spills."],["Acids, bases and water","Pure water contains a tiny number of molecules that split into hydrogen ions and hydroxide ions, giving it a neutral pH of 7. Acids add hydrogen ions to water, and bases add hydroxide ions. Rainwater is slightly acidic because it dissolves carbon dioxide from the air."],["Heavy water","Some water molecules contain deuterium, a heavier form of hydrogen. This heavy water is slightly denser than ordinary water and is used in some nuclear reactors. During World War II, Allied commandos sabotaged a heavy water plant in occupied Norway to hinder German nuclear research."],["Water and life","Scientists searching for life beyond Earth often follow the water, because all known life needs liquid water. Its ability to dissolve substances, carry heat and remain liquid over a wide range of temperatures makes it an ideal medium for life’s chemistry. This is why discoveries of water on Mars and on moons such as Europa and Enceladus generate great excitement."]],
sp:["Why lakes freeze from the top","Imagine a lake in a cold country as winter arrives. As the surface water cools, it becomes denser and sinks, letting warmer water rise. But when the water cools below about 4 degrees Celsius, it starts to expand and become lighter, so the coldest water stays at the top and eventually freezes. The layer of ice then insulates the water below, which stays around 4 degrees. Fish, frogs and plants survive the winter in the liquid water beneath. If ice were denser than water, like most solids, lakes would freeze from the bottom up and many would become solid ice, making life in them nearly impossible."],
g:[["Polar molecule","A molecule with positive and negative ends"],["Hydrogen bond","An attraction between water molecules"],["Surface tension","The skin-like effect at a liquid’s surface"],["Solvent","A substance that dissolves others"]],
f:["Ice floats because it is less dense than water.","Water is densest at about 4 °C.","Water dissolves many substances."],
fun:"Hot water can sometimes freeze faster than cold water, a puzzling effect called the Mpemba effect, though scientists still debate when and why it happens."});

ADDART({id:"staticelec",t:"Static Electricity",c:"Physics",i:"⚡",w:"Static electricity",r:["elec","electron","atom","magnet","storms"],
p:[["Electric charges","Static electricity is a buildup of electric charge on a surface."],["Sparks and shocks","It causes small shocks when you touch a doorknob."],["Nature’s biggest spark","Lightning is a giant spark of static electricity."]],
l:"Static electricity is an imbalance of electric charge on the surface of an object. It happens when certain materials rub together and electrons, the tiny negatively charged particles in atoms, move from one material to another. The object that gains electrons becomes negatively charged, and the one that loses them becomes positively charged. Opposite charges attract and like charges repel, which is why a balloon rubbed on hair can stick to a wall and make hair stand on end. When enough charge builds up, it can jump through the air as a spark, giving a small shock. Lightning is a huge discharge of static electricity in storm clouds. Static electricity is used in photocopiers, air filters and paint sprayers, but can also damage electronics.",
s:[["Rubbing and charges","When two different materials touch and separate, electrons can transfer between them. Scientists rank materials in a list called the triboelectric series, showing which tend to gain or lose electrons. For example, rubbing a balloon on hair usually moves electrons to the balloon, giving it a negative charge."],["Attraction and repulsion","Charged objects push or pull on each other without touching. Like charges repel, while opposite charges attract. A charged object can even attract neutral objects, such as bits of paper, by pulling their charges slightly apart, an effect called induction."],["Sparks","When charge builds up enough, it can break down the air between two objects and jump across as a spark, producing light, heat and a crackling sound. Static shocks happen more often in dry weather, because moist air helps charges leak away gradually."],["Conductors and insulators","Metals are conductors that let charges move freely, so they do not usually hold static charge unless insulated. Materials such as rubber, plastic, glass and wool are insulators, which keep charges in place on their surfaces, making static electricity build up."]],
k:[["Cause","Transfer of electrons"],["Rule","Opposite charges attract"],["Unit of charge","Coulomb"],["Biggest natural example","Lightning"],["More common in","Dry weather"]],
d:[["c. 600 BC","Thales of Miletus is said to observe that rubbed amber attracts light objects"],["1600","William Gilbert coins a Latin word that leads to the term electricity"],["1663","Otto von Guericke builds a machine that produces static charge"],["1745","The Leyden jar is invented to store electric charge"],["1752","Benjamin Franklin’s kite experiment links lightning to electricity"],["1785","Charles-Augustin de Coulomb measures the force between charges"]],
s2:[["Where the word came from","The ancient Greeks noticed that amber, a fossilized tree resin, attracted feathers and straw when rubbed with fur. The Greek word for amber is elektron. In 1600 the English scientist William Gilbert used a related Latin word to describe this attraction, and from it came the words electric and electricity."],["Lightning","Inside thunderclouds, collisions between ice crystals and soft hail separate charges, with negative charge often building near the bottom of the cloud. When the difference becomes great enough, a giant spark leaps between the cloud and the ground or between clouds. Lightning heats the air to temperatures hotter than the surface of the Sun, causing the explosive sound of thunder. The Philippines, with its many thunderstorms, experiences frequent lightning, especially during the rainy season."],["Franklin and the kite","In 1752 Benjamin Franklin is said to have flown a kite in a thunderstorm with a metal key tied to the string, drawing sparks that showed lightning was electrical. The experiment was extremely dangerous, and others who tried similar experiments were killed. Franklin went on to invent the lightning rod, which protects buildings by safely guiding lightning into the ground."],["Useful static","Photocopiers and laser printers use static charges to attract toner powder onto paper in the shape of text and images. Electrostatic air filters and factory smokestack filters remove dust by charging particles and collecting them on plates. Car factories use static charges to make paint spray evenly onto metal."],["Dangers of static","Static sparks can damage delicate electronic parts, so technicians wear grounding straps and use antistatic bags. In places with flammable gases or dust, such as fuel stations, flour mills and mines, a static spark can cause fires or explosions. This is one reason gas stations advise people not to use phones or re-enter cars while refueling."],["Shocks in daily life","People often get static shocks after walking on carpets, sliding across car seats or taking off sweaters, especially in dry, air-conditioned rooms. Touching a metal object with a key or knuckle first can reduce the sting. Fabric softeners and humidifiers help reduce static buildup."]],
sp:["The Leyden jar shock","In 1745 and 1746, experimenters in Germany and the Netherlands discovered they could store static charge in a glass jar lined with metal foil, later called the Leyden jar. When the Dutch scientist Pieter van Musschenbroek touched one, the shock was so strong that he wrote he would not repeat it for the whole kingdom of France. Soon, showmen across Europe used Leyden jars to amaze audiences. In one famous demonstration in France, a long line of monks holding hands or wires jumped together as a shock passed through them all. These experiments led to deeper understanding of electricity and, eventually, to modern capacitors used in electronics."],
g:[["Static electricity","A buildup of electric charge on a surface"],["Electron","A tiny negatively charged particle"],["Conductor","A material that lets charge flow"],["Insulator","A material that blocks the flow of charge"]],
f:["Static shocks are more common in dry weather.","Lightning is a giant electric spark.","The word electricity comes from the Greek word for amber."],
fun:"A lightning bolt can heat the air to around five times hotter than the Sun’s surface."});

ADDART({id:"circuits",t:"Electric Circuits",c:"Physics",i:"🔌",w:"Electrical network",r:["elec","battery","lightbulb","chip","currents"],
p:[["A complete loop","Electricity flows only around a complete path called a circuit."],["Voltage, current, resistance","Three key ideas describe how circuits work."],["Everywhere","Circuits power everything from flashlights to computers."]],
l:"An electric circuit is a closed path through which electric current flows. A simple circuit has a source of energy, such as a battery, wires to carry the current, and a device, called a load, such as a bulb or motor, that uses the energy. When the circuit is complete, electrons flow through the wires and the device works; when a switch opens a gap in the circuit, the flow stops. Circuits are described using three main quantities: voltage, the push that drives the current; current, the rate of flow of charge; and resistance, which opposes the flow. Ohm’s law links them. Circuits can be connected in series or in parallel. Every electrical device, from a flashlight to a smartphone, depends on circuits.",
s:[["Voltage, current and resistance","Voltage, measured in volts, is like the pressure that pushes water through a pipe. Current, measured in amperes or amps, is how much charge flows per second. Resistance, measured in ohms, is how much a material opposes the flow, like a narrow pipe. Ohm’s law states that voltage equals current multiplied by resistance."],["Series circuits","In a series circuit, components are connected one after another in a single loop. The same current flows through each, and if one bulb breaks, the whole circuit stops. Old-style holiday lights were often wired in series, so a single dead bulb could make the whole string go dark."],["Parallel circuits","In a parallel circuit, components are connected on separate branches, each with its own path to the power source. If one branch breaks, the others keep working. Homes are wired in parallel so that each appliance can be switched on or off independently."],["Switches and safety","Switches open or close a circuit to control the flow of electricity. Fuses and circuit breakers protect circuits by cutting the current if it becomes too large, which could overheat wires and cause fires. Grounding provides a safe path for stray current."]],
k:[["Voltage unit","Volt"],["Current unit","Ampere"],["Resistance unit","Ohm"],["Key law","Ohm’s law, V = IR"],["Two basic types","Series and parallel"]],
d:[["1800","Alessandro Volta invents the battery, providing steady current"],["1827","Georg Ohm publishes the law linking voltage, current and resistance"],["1845","Gustav Kirchhoff states his laws for complex circuits"],["1879","Thomas Edison demonstrates a practical light bulb circuit"],["1947","The transistor is invented, transforming electronic circuits"],["1958","Jack Kilby builds the first integrated circuit"]],
s2:[["Direct and alternating current","Batteries provide direct current, which flows in one direction. Power plants supply homes with alternating current, which reverses direction many times a second. In the Philippines, household electricity is supplied at about 220 volts and 60 hertz. The rivalry between supporters of direct and alternating current in the late 1800s is known as the war of the currents."],["Circuit diagrams","Engineers draw circuits using standard symbols for batteries, wires, resistors, switches, bulbs and other components. Circuit diagrams make it easy to understand and build circuits, from simple school experiments to complex electronic devices."],["Electronic circuits","Electronic circuits use components such as resistors, capacitors, diodes and transistors to control electricity precisely. Transistors act as tiny switches and amplifiers. An integrated circuit, or microchip, packs billions of transistors onto a small piece of silicon, making computers and phones possible."],["Short circuits","A short circuit happens when current finds an unintended low-resistance path, such as when damaged wires touch. The current can become very large, heating wires and causing sparks or fires. Faulty wiring and overloaded outlets are a common cause of house fires, including in the Philippines, where fire officials urge people to avoid octopus connections that plug many devices into one outlet."],["Electricity and the body","The human body can conduct electricity, and currents passing through the heart can be deadly. Wet skin lowers the body’s resistance, which is why electrical appliances should never be used near water. Interestingly, the body itself uses tiny electrical signals in nerves and heart muscle."],["Superconducting circuits","Some materials lose all electrical resistance when cooled to very low temperatures, becoming superconductors. Superconducting circuits are used in MRI scanners, particle accelerators and some quantum computers. Scientists are searching for materials that superconduct at higher temperatures."]],
sp:["Ohm’s doubted discovery","In the 1820s, Georg Ohm, a German schoolteacher, carried out careful experiments with wires of different lengths and thicknesses. He found a simple relationship: the current through a wire is proportional to the voltage across it and inversely proportional to its resistance. When he published his work in 1827, many scientists in Germany dismissed it, and Ohm struggled to find a university post. Recognition came years later, when the Royal Society in London awarded him its Copley Medal in 1841. Today the unit of resistance, the ohm, is named after him, and Ohm’s law is taught to students around the world."],
g:[["Circuit","A closed path for electric current"],["Voltage","The push that drives current"],["Current","The flow of electric charge"],["Resistance","Opposition to the flow of current"]],
f:["A circuit must be complete for current to flow.","Homes are wired in parallel.","Ohm’s law links voltage, current and resistance."],
fun:"A modern microchip can hold billions of tiny circuit switches called transistors."});

ADDART({id:"keplerlaws",t:"Kepler’s Laws of Planetary Motion",c:"Astronomy",i:"🪐",w:"Kepler's laws of planetary motion",r:["copern","galileo","grav","solar","newton"],
p:[["Ellipses","Planets move in oval-shaped paths called ellipses."],["Faster when closer","Planets speed up when they are nearer the Sun."],["A mathematical pattern","Kepler found a rule linking a planet’s distance and its year."]],
l:"Kepler’s laws of planetary motion are three rules describing how planets move around the Sun, discovered by the German astronomer Johannes Kepler in the early 1600s. Using precise observations of Mars made by the Danish astronomer Tycho Brahe, Kepler found that planets travel in ellipses rather than perfect circles, that they move faster when closer to the Sun, and that there is a precise mathematical relationship between a planet’s distance from the Sun and the time it takes to complete an orbit. His laws supported the idea that the Sun, not Earth, is at the center of the solar system, and they later helped Isaac Newton develop his law of universal gravitation. They also apply to moons, comets, satellites and exoplanets.",
s:[["The first law","Each planet moves in an ellipse with the Sun at one of its two focus points. An ellipse is a stretched circle. Most planetary orbits are nearly circular, but Mars’s orbit is noticeably elongated, which is why Kepler’s study of Mars revealed the pattern."],["The second law","A line joining a planet to the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times. This means a planet moves faster when it is closer to the Sun and slower when it is farther away. Earth moves fastest in early January, when it is nearest the Sun."],["The third law","The square of a planet’s orbital period is proportional to the cube of its average distance from the Sun. Planets farther from the Sun take much longer to orbit. For example, Jupiter is about five times farther from the Sun than Earth and takes about 12 years to complete an orbit."],["Brahe’s data","Tycho Brahe made the most accurate naked-eye observations of the planets ever recorded, from his observatory in Denmark. After Brahe’s death in 1601, Kepler, his assistant, used these records. Fitting the data for Mars took him years of calculations."]],
k:[["Discoverer","Johannes Kepler"],["Data source","Tycho Brahe’s observations"],["First and second laws published","1609"],["Third law published","1619"],["Explained later by","Newton’s law of gravitation"]],
d:[["1543","Copernicus proposes a Sun-centered system"],["1576","Tycho Brahe begins observations at his island observatory"],["1600","Kepler joins Brahe in Prague"],["1609","Kepler publishes his first two laws in Astronomia Nova"],["1619","Kepler publishes his third law in Harmonices Mundi"],["1687","Newton explains Kepler’s laws using gravity in the Principia"]],
s2:[["Breaking with circles","For about 2,000 years, astronomers assumed that heavenly bodies moved in perfect circles, which seemed fitting for the heavens. Even Copernicus used circles. Kepler’s decision to abandon circles for ellipses, after his calculations for Mars disagreed with Brahe’s data by a small amount, was a major break with tradition and showed his commitment to evidence."],["Kepler’s life","Johannes Kepler was born in 1571 in Germany and suffered from poor eyesight after childhood smallpox. He was a devout Christian who believed that God had created the universe according to mathematical harmony. He faced religious persecution as a Lutheran, and his mother was put on trial for witchcraft; Kepler defended her, and she was eventually released."],["Newton’s explanation","Kepler described how planets move but could not fully explain why. In 1687 Isaac Newton showed that all three of Kepler’s laws follow mathematically from his laws of motion and his law of universal gravitation. This was a triumph that united the physics of Earth and the heavens."],["Using the laws today","Kepler’s laws are used to plan the paths of spacecraft and satellites and to predict the return of comets. Astronomers use his third law to calculate the masses of stars and planets from the orbits of objects around them, and to study exoplanets discovered around other stars."],["The Kepler telescope","NASA’s Kepler space telescope, launched in 2009 and named in his honor, watched a patch of sky for tiny dips in starlight caused by planets passing in front of their stars. It discovered thousands of exoplanets before retiring in 2018, showing that planets are common in our galaxy."],["Kepler and science fiction","Kepler also wrote Somnium, a story published after his death in 1634, about a journey to the Moon and what Earth would look like from there. Some consider it one of the earliest works of science fiction. It used real astronomy to imagine life on another world."]],
sp:["The war with Mars","When Kepler began studying the orbit of Mars, he expected to solve it in eight days. It took him about six years. He tried circle after circle, adjusting their positions, but his best model still disagreed with Brahe’s observations by about 8 minutes of arc, a tiny fraction of a degree. Kepler trusted Brahe’s data and refused to ignore the difference. He later wrote that those 8 minutes led to a reform of all astronomy. Finally, he tried an ellipse, and it fit perfectly. Kepler called his long struggle his war with Mars, and his victory changed how humanity understood the solar system."],
g:[["Ellipse","An oval-shaped curve"],["Orbit","The path of an object around another"],["Orbital period","The time to complete one orbit"],["Focus","One of two special points inside an ellipse"]],
f:["Planets move in ellipses.","Planets move faster when closer to the Sun.","Kepler used Tycho Brahe’s observations."],
fun:"Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, during the Northern Hemisphere’s winter."});

ADDART({id:"nucleusdisc",t:"Discovering the Atomic Nucleus",c:"History of Science",i:"⚛️",w:"Geiger–Marsden experiments",r:["atom","electron","radioact","quarks","curie"],
p:[["A tiny center","Almost all of an atom’s mass is packed into a tiny nucleus."],["The gold foil experiment","Rutherford’s team fired particles at thin gold foil."],["A big surprise","A few particles bounced back, revealing the nucleus."]],
l:"The atomic nucleus was discovered in 1911 by the New Zealand-born physicist Ernest Rutherford, based on experiments carried out by Hans Geiger and Ernest Marsden at the University of Manchester. They fired tiny, positively charged alpha particles at a very thin sheet of gold foil. Most passed straight through, but a few bounced off at large angles, and some came almost straight back. Rutherford concluded that an atom’s positive charge and nearly all its mass are concentrated in a tiny, dense center, the nucleus, with electrons moving around it through mostly empty space. This overturned the earlier plum pudding model and laid the foundation for nuclear physics. Later discoveries showed that the nucleus is made of protons and neutrons.",
s:[["The plum pudding model","In 1904 J. J. Thomson, who had discovered the electron, proposed that atoms were like a pudding of positive charge with negative electrons scattered through it like plums. In this model, the positive charge was spread out, so fast particles should pass through with only small deflections."],["The experiment","Between 1909 and 1913, Geiger and Marsden directed a beam of alpha particles from a radioactive source at gold foil only a few hundred atoms thick. In a darkened room, they counted tiny flashes of light on a screen, each marking where an alpha particle landed. Most went straight through, but about 1 in 8,000 bounced back at large angles."],["Rutherford’s explanation","Rutherford realized that large deflections could only happen if the alpha particles encountered a small, very concentrated positive charge. In 1911 he proposed the nuclear model: a tiny nucleus surrounded by electrons. If an atom were the size of a stadium, its nucleus would be about the size of a small marble at the center."],["Protons and neutrons","In 1917 Rutherford showed that the hydrogen nucleus, later called the proton, is found in other nuclei. In 1932 James Chadwick, his former student, discovered the neutron, a neutral particle in the nucleus. These discoveries explained the masses of atoms and the existence of isotopes."]],
k:[["Year proposed","1911"],["Scientist","Ernest Rutherford"],["Experimenters","Hans Geiger and Ernest Marsden"],["Target","Thin gold foil"],["Model replaced","Plum pudding model"]],
d:[["1897","J. J. Thomson discovers the electron"],["1904","Thomson proposes the plum pudding model"],["1909","Geiger and Marsden observe alpha particles bouncing back from gold foil"],["1911","Rutherford proposes the nuclear model of the atom"],["1913","Niels Bohr places electrons in fixed orbits around the nucleus"],["1932","James Chadwick discovers the neutron"]],
s2:[["Rutherford the experimenter","Ernest Rutherford, born on a farm in New Zealand in 1871, became one of the greatest experimental physicists. He had already won the 1908 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for his work on radioactivity, before discovering the nucleus. Later he led the Cavendish Laboratory in Cambridge, where his team split the atom. The element rutherfordium is named after him."],["Bohr’s improvement","Rutherford’s model had a problem: according to classical physics, orbiting electrons should lose energy and spiral into the nucleus. In 1913 the Danish physicist Niels Bohr, who had worked with Rutherford, suggested that electrons occupy fixed energy levels and jump between them by absorbing or releasing light. This step helped launch quantum physics."],["Mostly empty space","Rutherford’s discovery showed that atoms, and therefore everything around us, are mostly empty space. The nucleus is around 100,000 times smaller than the whole atom. Objects feel solid because the electrons of atoms repel those of other atoms."],["What holds the nucleus together","Protons in the nucleus all have positive charge and repel each other strongly. Scientists later discovered the strong nuclear force, which acts only at very short distances and binds protons and neutrons together. Releasing some of this binding energy powers nuclear reactors, atomic bombs and the Sun."],["Splitting the atom","In 1932, at Rutherford’s Cavendish Laboratory, John Cockcroft and Ernest Walton used a particle accelerator to split lithium nuclei, the first artificial splitting of an atom using accelerated particles. This work, and later discoveries of nuclear fission, led to both nuclear power and nuclear weapons."],["Inside protons","Scientists later found that protons and neutrons themselves are made of smaller particles called quarks, held together by gluons. Experiments similar in spirit to Rutherford’s, firing electrons at protons at the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center in the late 1960s, provided the first evidence for these particles."]],
sp:["Like a shell bouncing off tissue paper","Rutherford later described his amazement at the results of the gold foil experiment. He said it was almost as incredible as if you fired a large artillery shell at a piece of tissue paper and it came back and hit you. According to the plum pudding model, alpha particles should have passed through the foil with only slight nudges. Yet some were bounced right back. Rutherford spent months working out the mathematics before announcing in 1911 that the atom must have a tiny, massive nucleus. A careful look at a few unexpected flashes of light had revealed the heart of the atom."],
g:[["Nucleus","The tiny, dense center of an atom"],["Alpha particle","A positively charged particle given off by some radioactive materials"],["Plum pudding model","An early model of the atom"],["Neutron","A neutral particle in the nucleus"]],
f:["Most alpha particles passed straight through the gold foil.","The nucleus holds nearly all of an atom’s mass.","The neutron was discovered in 1932."],
fun:"If an atom were the size of a sports stadium, its nucleus would be about the size of a marble."});

