/* data/science12.js */
// Science: microscopes, fractions, topology, radiocarbon dating and the nitrogen cycle.
ADDART({id:"microscopes",t:"The Microscope",c:"History of Science",i:"🔬",w:"Microscope",r:["cell","microbe","pasteur","light","scope"],
p:[["Seeing the invisible","Microscopes reveal things too small for the naked eye."],["A hidden world","Early microscopes revealed cells and microbes."],["Down to atoms","Modern microscopes can show individual atoms."]],
l:"A microscope is an instrument that makes very small objects appear larger, revealing details invisible to the naked eye. Light microscopes use glass lenses to magnify objects up to about 1,000 or 2,000 times, enough to see cells and bacteria. Electron microscopes use beams of electrons instead of light and can magnify millions of times, showing viruses, molecules and even individual atoms. The invention of the microscope in the late 1500s and 1600s opened up a hidden world: Robert Hooke described cells in 1665, and Antonie van Leeuwenhoek was the first to observe bacteria and other microorganisms. Microscopes are essential tools in biology, medicine, materials science and many other fields, from diagnosing diseases to designing computer chips.",
s:[["How a light microscope works","A compound light microscope has two sets of lenses. The objective lens, close to the specimen, forms an enlarged image, which the eyepiece lens magnifies further. Light shines through a thin sample on a glass slide. Stains are often added to make cells and their parts easier to see."],["Limits of light","Light microscopes cannot clearly show objects smaller than about 200 nanometers, roughly half the wavelength of visible light, because light waves blur such tiny details. This limit, described by Ernst Abbe in 1873, means most viruses cannot be seen with ordinary light microscopes."],["Electron microscopes","Electron microscopes, first built in the 1930s, use electrons, which have much shorter wavelengths than light. Transmission electron microscopes send electrons through ultra-thin samples, while scanning electron microscopes bounce electrons off surfaces to create detailed three-dimensional-looking images. Samples usually must be placed in a vacuum."],["Beyond the limit","In recent decades, scientists developed super-resolution fluorescence microscopes that get around the light limit, using glowing molecules switched on and off. This won the 2014 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Cryo-electron microscopy, which freezes samples to study molecules in detail, won the 2017 prize."]],
k:[["Light microscope magnification","Up to about 2,000×"],["Electron microscope magnification","Millions of times"],["First described cells","Robert Hooke, 1665"],["First saw bacteria","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek"],["First electron microscope","1931"]],
d:[["c. 1590","Dutch spectacle makers build early compound microscopes"],["1665","Robert Hooke publishes Micrographia and names cells"],["1676","Van Leeuwenhoek reports tiny living creatures in water"],["1873","Ernst Abbe explains the resolution limit of light microscopes"],["1931","Ernst Ruska and Max Knoll build the first electron microscope"],["1981","The scanning tunneling microscope is invented, imaging individual atoms"]],
s2:[["Hooke’s Micrographia","In 1665 the English scientist Robert Hooke published Micrographia, a book of detailed drawings of things seen through his microscope, including a flea, a louse, the eye of a fly and thin slices of cork. The cork was made of tiny compartments that reminded him of monks’ cells, so he called them cells. The book became a bestseller and amazed readers."],["Van Leeuwenhoek’s animalcules","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, a Dutch cloth merchant with no university training, ground tiny, powerful lenses and built simple microscopes that magnified over 200 times. From the 1670s he sent letters to the Royal Society in London describing tiny moving creatures, which he called animalcules, in pond water, rainwater and scrapings from his teeth. These were bacteria and protists, seen for the first time."],["Microscopes and medicine","Microscopes allowed scientists such as Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch to identify the germs that cause diseases like tuberculosis, cholera and anthrax in the late 1800s. Today, doctors and laboratory technicians use microscopes to examine blood, tissue samples and bacteria, diagnosing illnesses such as malaria, tuberculosis and cancer."],["Seeing atoms","In 1981 Gerd Binnig and Heinrich Rohrer at IBM invented the scanning tunneling microscope, which uses a very sharp tip passing extremely close to a surface to map individual atoms. In 1989 IBM scientists used a similar instrument to arrange 35 xenon atoms to spell the company’s name, showing that atoms could be moved one by one."],["Foldscope","In 2014 researchers at Stanford University introduced the Foldscope, a paper microscope that can be folded from a flat sheet and costs about one US dollar. It was designed to bring microscopy to students and health workers in places with limited resources, and has been distributed in many countries."],["Microscopes in Philippine science","Philippine scientists use microscopes in research at institutions such as the University of the Philippines and the Research Institute for Tropical Medicine, studying tropical diseases, marine life and new materials. Electron microscopes are used to study the structure of materials and microorganisms in local laboratories."]],
sp:["Leeuwenhoek’s teeth","In 1683 Antonie van Leeuwenhoek scraped some white material from between his teeth, mixed it with clean rainwater and looked at it through one of his tiny handmade microscopes. He was astonished to see many very small living creatures moving about, some darting like fish, others spinning in circles. He wrote that there were more animals in his mouth than there were people in the whole Netherlands. He had observed bacteria, though it would take nearly two centuries before scientists understood their role in disease. Van Leeuwenhoek kept his lens-making methods secret, and for many years no one could match his microscopes."],
g:[["Microscope","An instrument that magnifies tiny objects"],["Lens","A curved piece of glass that bends light"],["Magnification","How much larger an object appears"],["Resolution","The ability to show fine detail"]],
f:["Robert Hooke named cells.","Van Leeuwenhoek first saw bacteria.","Electron microscopes can show atoms."],
fun:"A Foldscope paper microscope can be made from a flat sheet and costs about the price of a candy bar."});

ADDART({id:"fractions",t:"Fractions and Decimals",c:"Mathematics",i:"½",w:"Fraction",r:["percent","zero","alg","measure","negativenumbers"],
p:[["Parts of a whole","A fraction shows a part of a whole, like one half."],["Numerator and denominator","The top and bottom numbers have special names."],["Another way to write them","Decimals are another way of writing fractions."]],
l:"Fractions are numbers that represent parts of a whole or ratios between quantities. A fraction is written with a numerator on top, showing how many parts are taken, and a denominator below, showing how many equal parts the whole is divided into; for example, three quarters is written 3/4. Decimals express fractions using powers of ten, so one half equals 0.5 and three quarters equals 0.75. Fractions and decimals are used every day in cooking, shopping, measuring, money, sports statistics and science. Ancient Egyptians, Babylonians, Indians and Chinese all developed ways of writing fractions, and decimal notation spread widely in Europe after the 1500s. Percentages are fractions out of one hundred.",
s:[["Types of fractions","A proper fraction, such as 2/3, has a numerator smaller than its denominator and is less than one. An improper fraction, such as 7/4, is greater than one and can also be written as a mixed number, 1¾. Equivalent fractions, such as 1/2, 2/4 and 4/8, have the same value."],["Adding and subtracting","To add or subtract fractions, they must have the same denominator. For example, to add 1/2 and 1/3, both are rewritten in sixths, as 3/6 and 2/6, which add up to 5/6. Finding the least common denominator makes calculations simpler."],["Multiplying and dividing","To multiply fractions, multiply the numerators and the denominators: 2/3 times 3/4 equals 6/12, or 1/2. To divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal, the fraction turned upside down. So dividing by 1/2 is the same as multiplying by 2."],["Decimals","In a decimal number, digits to the right of the decimal point represent tenths, hundredths, thousandths and so on. Some fractions, such as 1/4, become decimals that end, 0.25. Others, such as 1/3, become repeating decimals, 0.333 and so on forever."]],
k:[["Top number","Numerator"],["Bottom number","Denominator"],["One half as decimal","0.5"],["Percent meaning","Out of one hundred"],["Decimal point in the Philippines","A period, as in 2.5"]],
d:[["c. 1800 BC","Babylonians write fractions using a base-60 system"],["c. 1650 BC","The Egyptian Rhind Papyrus shows calculations with unit fractions"],["c. 200 BC","Chinese mathematicians use decimal fractions in calculations"],["c. 1200","Fibonacci helps spread fractions written with a horizontal bar in Europe"],["1585","Simon Stevin publishes a booklet promoting decimal fractions"],["1795","France adopts the decimal metric system"]],
s2:[["Egyptian fractions","Ancient Egyptians wrote most fractions as sums of unit fractions, which have a numerator of one, such as 1/2, 1/4 and 1/7. For example, they would write 3/4 as 1/2 plus 1/4. The Rhind Mathematical Papyrus, copied about 1650 BC, contains tables for converting fractions in this way. Mathematicians still study puzzles about Egyptian fractions today."],["The decimal revolution","In 1585 the Flemish mathematician Simon Stevin published a short booklet called De Thiende, or The Tenth, arguing that decimal fractions would make calculations easier for merchants, surveyors and astronomers. He predicted that coins, weights and measures would one day be decimal. The metric system adopted in France in 1795 and later worldwide fulfilled his vision."],["Fractions in daily life","Recipes use fractions such as half a cup of sugar. Shops advertise discounts as percentages. Basketball statistics use decimals and percentages for shooting accuracy. Money in the Philippines is decimal: one peso equals 100 centavos, so 2.50 pesos means two pesos and 50 centavos."],["Ratios and rates","Fractions also describe ratios, such as 1 part concentrate to 4 parts water, and rates, such as kilometers per hour. Scale drawings and maps use ratios like 1:50,000. Probability is often expressed as a fraction, such as the 1/6 chance of rolling a particular number on a die."],["Rational and irrational numbers","Numbers that can be written as fractions of whole numbers are called rational numbers. Some numbers, such as the square root of 2 and pi, cannot be written as fractions and are called irrational numbers; their decimals go on forever without repeating. The discovery of irrational numbers by ancient Greek mathematicians is said to have shocked them."],["Learning fractions","Many students find fractions difficult, and research suggests that understanding fractions is an important predictor of later success in algebra. Teachers use pizzas, chocolate bars, number lines and measuring cups to help students picture fractions. Some educators debate how early and in what order fraction topics should be taught."]],
sp:["The third-pound burger","In the 1980s, the A&W restaurant chain in the United States introduced a third-pound burger to compete with McDonald’s quarter-pounder. It offered more meat for the same price and did well in taste tests. Yet it did not sell well. According to the company’s owner, research found that many customers thought a third of a pound was smaller than a quarter of a pound, because three is smaller than four. The story is often told to show why understanding fractions matters, since a larger denominator actually means smaller pieces."],
g:[["Fraction","A number showing part of a whole"],["Numerator","The top number of a fraction"],["Denominator","The bottom number of a fraction"],["Decimal","A number written using tenths, hundredths and so on"]],
f:["One half equals 0.5.","Percent means out of one hundred.","Egyptians wrote fractions as sums of unit fractions."],
fun:"The fraction 1/7 as a decimal repeats the digits 142857 forever."});

ADDART({id:"topology",t:"Topology",c:"Mathematics",i:"🍩",w:"Topology",r:["shapes","euclid","graphs","infin","maps"],
p:[["Rubber-sheet geometry","Topology studies shapes that can be stretched without tearing."],["Donut and mug","To a topologist, a donut and a coffee mug are the same shape."],["Bridges and knots","Topology began with a puzzle about seven bridges."]],
l:"Topology is a branch of mathematics that studies the properties of shapes that stay the same when they are stretched, twisted, bent or squeezed, but not torn or glued. It is sometimes called rubber-sheet geometry. In topology, distances and angles do not matter; what matters is how a shape is connected, such as how many holes it has. A doughnut and a coffee mug with one handle are considered the same shape because each has one hole and one could be smoothly deformed into the other. Topology grew from Leonhard Euler’s 1736 solution to the Seven Bridges of Königsberg puzzle. Today it is used in physics, biology, computer science, data analysis and the study of the shape of the universe.",
s:[["Holes matter","In topology, a sphere, a cube and a bowl are all equivalent because none has a hole through it. A doughnut, a ring and a mug have one hole. A pretzel shape may have two or three. Mathematicians call the number of holes in a surface its genus."],["The Möbius strip","A Möbius strip is made by giving a strip of paper a half twist and joining its ends. It has only one side and one edge: an ant walking along it could cover both faces without crossing an edge. If you cut it down the middle, it does not fall into two pieces but forms one longer loop."],["Knots","Knot theory, a part of topology, studies closed loops in three-dimensional space and when one knot can be untangled into another without cutting. It has applications in understanding how DNA strands tangle and untangle, and in physics and chemistry."],["Networks","Topology also studies how points are connected, regardless of distances. A subway map is a topological map: it shows the order of stations and connections correctly, even though distances and directions are distorted. Computer networks are also described by their topology."]],
k:[["Also called","Rubber-sheet geometry"],["Founding problem","Seven Bridges of Königsberg"],["Founder","Leonhard Euler, 1736"],["One-sided surface","Möbius strip"],["Famous solved problem","Poincaré conjecture"]],
d:[["1736","Leonhard Euler solves the Seven Bridges of Königsberg problem"],["1847","Johann Listing uses the word topology in print"],["1858","August Möbius and Johann Listing describe the one-sided strip"],["1904","Henri Poincaré poses his famous conjecture"],["2003","Grigori Perelman posts proofs of the Poincaré conjecture"],["2016","The Nobel Prize in Physics honors topological discoveries in materials"]],
s2:[["The seven bridges","The city of Königsberg, now Kaliningrad in Russia, had seven bridges connecting two islands and the riverbanks. People wondered whether one could walk through the city crossing each bridge exactly once. In 1736 Leonhard Euler proved it was impossible by representing land areas as points and bridges as lines, showing that the answer depends only on how they are connected. This founded both graph theory and topology."],["The Poincaré conjecture","In 1904 the French mathematician Henri Poincaré proposed a statement about the shapes of three-dimensional spaces, roughly that any such space without holes is equivalent to a sphere. It became one of the most famous unsolved problems in mathematics. In 2002 and 2003, the Russian mathematician Grigori Perelman posted proofs online. He declined both the Fields Medal in 2006 and a one-million-dollar prize offered in 2010."],["Topology in physics","In 2016 the Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded to David Thouless, Duncan Haldane and Michael Kosterlitz for using topology to explain unusual states of matter. Topological materials, whose electrical properties stay stable even when they are bent or imperfect, are being explored for future electronics and quantum computers."],["The shape of the universe","Topology helps cosmologists ask questions about the overall shape of space. Could the universe be finite but without edges, like the surface of a sphere or a doughnut extended to three dimensions? Observations of the cosmic microwave background have so far found no evidence of such a pattern, but the question remains open."],["Hairy ball theorem","A playful result in topology, the hairy ball theorem, states that you cannot comb all the hair on a hairy ball flat without creating at least one tuft or bald spot. One consequence is that at any moment, there must be at least one place on Earth where the horizontal wind speed is zero."],["Topology and data","Topological data analysis is a newer field that uses topology to find shapes and patterns in large, complex data sets, such as medical records or images. It looks for loops, clusters and holes in data that may reveal hidden structure."]],
sp:["Perelman’s refusals","For nearly a century, mathematicians tried and failed to prove the Poincaré conjecture. Then, in late 2002, a reclusive Russian mathematician named Grigori Perelman quietly posted a series of papers on the internet. Over the next few years, experts checked his work and confirmed that he had solved the problem. In 2006 he was awarded the Fields Medal, often called the Nobel Prize of mathematics, but he declined it. In 2010 he was offered a one-million-dollar Millennium Prize and declined that too, reportedly saying that he felt the contributions of another mathematician, Richard Hamilton, were equally important. Perelman then withdrew from mathematics and public life."],
g:[["Topology","The study of shapes under stretching and bending"],["Genus","The number of holes in a surface"],["Möbius strip","A surface with only one side"],["Conjecture","A mathematical statement not yet proven"]],
f:["To a topologist, a donut and a mug are the same.","A Möbius strip has only one side.","Euler founded topology with a bridge puzzle."],
fun:"Topology proves that somewhere on Earth, the horizontal wind is always perfectly still."});

ADDART({id:"carbondating",t:"Radiocarbon Dating",c:"Science",i:"🦴",w:"Radiocarbon dating",r:["isotopes","radioact","archaeology","fossils","carbonchem"],
p:[["A natural clock","Radiocarbon dating measures the age of once-living things."],["Carbon-14","It uses a radioactive form of carbon that decays at a known rate."],["Up to 50,000 years","It works for remains up to about 50,000 years old."]],
l:"Radiocarbon dating is a method of finding the age of materials that were once alive, such as wood, bone, charcoal, shells and cloth, by measuring how much carbon-14 they contain. Carbon-14 is a radioactive isotope of carbon formed in the atmosphere. Living things take in carbon-14 while they are alive, but after death the carbon-14 slowly decays, halving about every 5,730 years. By comparing how much carbon-14 remains with how much a living thing contains, scientists can calculate how long ago the organism died. Developed by the American chemist Willard Libby in the late 1940s, radiocarbon dating transformed archaeology, geology and climate science. It works for remains up to about 50,000 years old.",
s:[["How carbon-14 forms","Cosmic rays from space strike atoms in the upper atmosphere, producing neutrons that turn nitrogen atoms into carbon-14. This carbon-14 combines with oxygen to form carbon dioxide, which plants absorb through photosynthesis. Animals take it in by eating plants, so all living things contain a small, steady proportion of carbon-14."],["Half-life","After an organism dies, it stops taking in carbon, and its carbon-14 decays back into nitrogen. Half of it disappears in about 5,730 years, half of what remains in another 5,730 years, and so on. After about 50,000 years, too little remains to measure reliably."],["Measuring the carbon","Early methods counted the radiation given off by decaying carbon-14 atoms, requiring large samples. Since the late 1970s, accelerator mass spectrometry has allowed scientists to count carbon-14 atoms directly, using samples as small as a few milligrams, such as a single seed or a tiny piece of cloth."],["Calibration","The amount of carbon-14 in the atmosphere has varied over time, so raw radiocarbon ages must be adjusted, or calibrated. Scientists use tree rings, cave formations, corals and lake sediments with known ages to build calibration curves that convert radiocarbon years into calendar years."]],
k:[["Isotope used","Carbon-14"],["Half-life","About 5,730 years"],["Useful range","Up to about 50,000 years"],["Inventor","Willard Libby"],["Nobel Prize","Chemistry, 1960"]],
d:[["1940","Martin Kamen and Sam Ruben discover carbon-14"],["1949","Willard Libby’s team publishes the first radiocarbon dates"],["1960","Libby wins the Nobel Prize in Chemistry"],["1977","Accelerator mass spectrometry is first used for radiocarbon dating"],["1988","Radiocarbon tests date the Shroud of Turin to the Middle Ages"],["2020","A new international calibration curve is published"]],
s2:[["Testing the method","To prove that his method worked, Willard Libby tested samples of known age, such as wood from ancient Egyptian tombs dated by historical records. The radiocarbon dates matched closely. Archaeologists soon realized they could date sites anywhere in the world without written records."],["Radiocarbon in the Philippines","Radiocarbon dating has helped Philippine archaeologists date sites such as the Tabon Caves in Palawan, where human remains and artifacts tens of thousands of years old have been found, and the jar burials and shell middens of other regions. It has also been used to study the age of the Cordillera rice terraces. Some studies have suggested the terraces are younger than long believed, a question still debated among researchers."],["The bomb pulse","Nuclear weapons tests in the 1950s and early 1960s nearly doubled the amount of carbon-14 in the atmosphere. This bomb pulse has since been declining. Scientists use it to date things from recent decades, such as the age of human cells, ivory from poached elephants and fine wines, to detect fakes."],["The Shroud of Turin","In 1988, three laboratories radiocarbon-dated small samples of the Shroud of Turin, a cloth that some believe wrapped the body of Jesus. They dated the linen to between about 1260 and 1390. Many scientists accept this result, while some supporters of the shroud’s authenticity argue that the samples came from a medieval repair or were contaminated. The debate continues."],["Limits and errors","Radiocarbon dating can only be used on materials that contain carbon from living things, so it cannot date stone tools or rocks directly, though it can date charcoal or bones found with them. Contamination by younger or older carbon can produce misleading results, so samples must be carefully cleaned. Results are given with a margin of error."],["Other dating methods","For older materials, scientists use other radioactive clocks. Potassium-argon and uranium-lead dating can measure ages of millions or billions of years, and are used to date volcanic rocks and the age of Earth. Tree-ring counting, called dendrochronology, gives exact calendar years for wood."]],
sp:["Libby’s atomic clock","In the 1940s, Willard Libby, a chemist who had worked on the Manhattan Project, had an idea: if cosmic rays steadily produced carbon-14, then every living thing should contain the same proportion, and that amount should decline at a known rate after death. Many colleagues doubted that such tiny amounts could be measured. Libby and his students at the University of Chicago built sensitive detectors shielded from background radiation. In 1949 they published dates for samples of known age, and the results matched. Libby called it an atomic clock, and in 1960 he received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."],
g:[["Isotope","A form of an element with a different number of neutrons"],["Half-life","The time for half of a radioactive substance to decay"],["Calibration","Adjusting measurements to match known standards"],["Carbon-14","A radioactive form of carbon"]],
f:["Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years.","Radiocarbon dating works on once-living things.","Willard Libby invented the method."],
fun:"Nuclear bomb tests left a carbon-14 signal that scientists now use to date wines and catch ivory smugglers."});

ADDART({id:"nitrogencycle",t:"The Nitrogen Cycle",c:"Science",i:"🔄",w:"Nitrogen cycle",r:["soil","eco","microbe","agri","wcycle"],
p:[["Air full of nitrogen","Nitrogen makes up about 78 percent of the air."],["Needed by life","All living things need nitrogen to build proteins and DNA."],["Bacteria at work","Bacteria turn nitrogen gas into forms plants can use."]],
l:"The nitrogen cycle is the natural process by which nitrogen moves between the air, soil, water and living things. Nitrogen gas makes up about 78 percent of Earth’s atmosphere, but most organisms cannot use it directly because its two atoms are tightly bonded. Certain bacteria, some living in the roots of beans and other legumes, convert nitrogen gas into ammonia, a process called nitrogen fixation. Other bacteria turn ammonia into nitrates, which plants absorb to build proteins and DNA. Animals get nitrogen by eating plants. When living things die or produce waste, decomposers return nitrogen to the soil, and still other bacteria release it back into the air. Since the early 1900s, humans have greatly altered this cycle by making artificial fertilizers.",
s:[["Nitrogen fixation","Nitrogen-fixing bacteria break the strong bond in nitrogen gas and combine nitrogen with hydrogen to form ammonia. Some, such as Rhizobium, live in nodules on the roots of legumes like beans, peanuts and mung beans, exchanging nitrogen for sugars. Lightning also fixes small amounts of nitrogen in the air."],["Nitrification","Other soil bacteria convert ammonia first into nitrites and then into nitrates, a process called nitrification. Nitrates dissolve easily in water and are absorbed by plant roots. Plants use the nitrogen to make amino acids, proteins, chlorophyll and DNA."],["Decomposition","When plants and animals die, or when animals produce urine and dung, fungi and bacteria break down the nitrogen-containing compounds, releasing ammonia back into the soil. This step, called ammonification, recycles nitrogen so it can be used again."],["Denitrification","In waterlogged soils and other places with little oxygen, denitrifying bacteria convert nitrates back into nitrogen gas, which returns to the atmosphere. This completes the cycle. Flooded rice fields are important places for denitrification."]],
k:[["Nitrogen in air","About 78 percent"],["Key bacteria","Rhizobium and others"],["Plant-usable forms","Ammonium and nitrate"],["Industrial process","Haber–Bosch"],["Legume examples","Beans, peanuts, mung beans"]],
d:[["1772","Daniel Rutherford discovers nitrogen"],["1888","Hellriegel and Wilfarth show legumes fix nitrogen with bacteria"],["1909","Fritz Haber shows how to make ammonia from nitrogen and hydrogen"],["1913","The first industrial Haber–Bosch ammonia plant opens in Germany"],["1918","Haber receives the Nobel Prize in Chemistry"],["2009","Scientists identify nitrogen pollution as one of the planetary boundaries being exceeded"]],
s2:[["Feeding billions","In 1909 the German chemist Fritz Haber found a way to combine nitrogen from the air with hydrogen to make ammonia, and Carl Bosch scaled it up into an industrial process. The Haber–Bosch process produces the nitrogen fertilizers that grow much of the world’s food. Scientists estimate that roughly half of the nitrogen in people’s bodies today came originally from such factories."],["A complicated legacy","Fritz Haber’s discovery helped feed the world, but he also led Germany’s development of poison gas in World War I, and ammonia from his process was used to make explosives. This has made him one of the most debated figures in the history of science, admired for his contribution to food production and condemned for his role in chemical warfare."],["Too much nitrogen","Excess fertilizer and animal waste can wash into rivers, lakes and seas, causing algae to grow out of control. When the algae die and decay, they use up oxygen, creating dead zones where fish cannot survive. Nitrogen pollution also produces nitrous oxide, a powerful greenhouse gas, and contributes to air pollution."],["Nitrogen in Philippine farming","Filipino rice and corn farmers rely heavily on nitrogen fertilizers such as urea, and fertilizer prices strongly affect farm incomes and food prices. Researchers at the International Rice Research Institute and Philippine universities study ways to use fertilizer more efficiently, including applying it at the right time and amount. Some farmers grow mung beans or other legumes between rice crops to naturally add nitrogen to the soil."],["Organic and natural methods","Organic farming uses compost, manure, legumes and crop rotation instead of synthetic fertilizers to supply nitrogen. Supporters say this is better for soil health and the environment, while others argue that organic methods produce lower yields and could not feed the world’s population alone. Many farmers combine approaches."],["Nitrogen in the ocean","In the ocean, tiny blue-green algae called cyanobacteria fix nitrogen and help support marine food webs. Scientists continue to discover new nitrogen-fixing organisms in the sea. Changes in ocean temperature and oxygen may affect the ocean nitrogen cycle in the future."]],
sp:["Bread from air","At the start of the 20th century, scientists warned that the world was running out of natural fertilizers such as guano, bird droppings mined from islands off Peru, and nitrate deposits from the deserts of Chile. In 1898 the British scientist William Crookes predicted a coming wheat famine unless chemists found a way to capture nitrogen from the air. Within about a decade, Fritz Haber did just that, using high pressure, high temperature and an iron catalyst to make ammonia. The process was soon described as making bread from air. Today it uses about one to two percent of the world’s energy and supports the food supply of billions of people."],
g:[["Nitrogen fixation","Turning nitrogen gas into usable compounds"],["Legume","A plant such as a bean that hosts nitrogen-fixing bacteria"],["Nitrate","A form of nitrogen plants absorb"],["Fertilizer","A substance that adds nutrients to soil"]],
f:["Nitrogen makes up about 78 percent of the air.","Beans host bacteria that fix nitrogen.","The Haber–Bosch process makes ammonia fertilizer."],
fun:"About half the nitrogen atoms in your body may have passed through a fertilizer factory."});

