// Space: Io, Enceladus, Halley's Comet, Betelgeuse and the Magellanic Clouds.
ADDART({id:"io",t:"Io",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌋",w:"Io (moon)",r:["jupiter","europa","volc","moons","galileo"],
p:[["Volcano world","Io is the most volcanically active place in the solar system."],["Jupiter’s grip","Jupiter’s gravity squeezes Io, heating its interior."],["A colorful moon","Sulfur paints Io’s surface yellow, orange and red."]],
l:"Io is the innermost of Jupiter’s four large moons, discovered by Galileo Galilei in 1610. Slightly larger than Earth’s Moon, it is the most volcanically active body known in the solar system, with hundreds of active volcanoes, some shooting plumes of sulfur and gas hundreds of kilometers into space and others pouring out vast lava flows. Io’s heat comes not from radioactivity but from tidal heating: as it orbits Jupiter, gravitational tugs from Jupiter and from the neighboring moons Europa and Ganymede constantly flex its interior, generating heat by friction. Sulfur compounds color its surface in yellows, oranges, reds, whites and blacks, giving it a pizza-like appearance. Io was explored by the Voyager, Galileo and Juno spacecraft.",
s:[["Tidal heating","Io orbits Jupiter every 1.8 days in a slightly oval path, kept that way by regular tugs from Europa and Ganymede. As its distance from Jupiter changes, the giant planet’s gravity squeezes and stretches Io, raising tides in its solid surface of up to about 100 meters. This constant flexing heats Io’s interior, melting rock."],["Volcanoes and lava","Io’s volcanoes erupt lava hotter than most volcanoes on Earth today. Loki Patera is a huge lava lake about 200 kilometers across that brightens and dims as its crust overturns. Pele’s plume rises about 300 kilometers above the surface, raining sulfur in a giant red ring."],["A thin atmosphere","Io has a very thin atmosphere, made mostly of sulfur dioxide from volcanoes. It freezes onto the surface when Io passes into Jupiter’s shadow. Material escaping from Io forms a doughnut-shaped cloud of charged particles, called the Io plasma torus, around Jupiter."],["Electric connection","As Io moves through Jupiter’s powerful magnetic field, it generates an electric current of millions of amperes flowing between the moon and Jupiter. This current creates a glowing spot in Jupiter’s auroras, sometimes called Io’s footprint."]],
k:[["Planet","Jupiter"],["Discovered","1610, by Galileo"],["Diameter","About 3,640 km"],["Orbital period","About 1.8 days"],["Heat source","Tidal heating"]],
d:[["1610","Galileo discovers Io and three other moons of Jupiter"],["1979","Scientists predict Io’s volcanism just before Voyager 1 arrives"],["1979","Linda Morabito discovers a volcanic plume in Voyager 1 images"],["1995","The Galileo spacecraft begins orbiting Jupiter"],["2007","New Horizons photographs a huge plume on Io while passing Jupiter"],["2023","Juno makes close flybys of Io"]],
s2:[["A prediction just in time","In March 1979, three scientists, Stanton Peale, Patrick Cassen and Ray Reynolds, published a paper predicting that tidal heating would make Io volcanically active. Just days later, on March 9, Voyager 1 navigation engineer Linda Morabito noticed a strange crescent shape at the edge of Io in an image. It was a volcanic plume, the first active volcano seen beyond Earth."],["The Galilean moons","Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto were the first moons discovered around another planet, seen by Galileo in January 1610. Their orbits around Jupiter showed that not everything circled Earth, supporting the Copernican view. Io, Europa and Ganymede are linked in an orbital rhythm: for every orbit of Ganymede, Europa orbits twice and Io four times."],["Juno’s close look","NASA’s Juno spacecraft, orbiting Jupiter since 2016, made close flybys of Io in December 2023 and February 2024, passing about 1,500 kilometers above its surface. Its images revealed mountains, lava lakes and shifting volcanic features. Scientists also found evidence that Io does not have a global ocean of magma beneath its crust, as some had suspected."],["Mountains taller than Everest","Despite its volcanism, Io’s tallest mountains are not volcanoes. They are huge blocks of crust pushed up by stresses, some rising higher than Mount Everest. Boösaule Montes reaches about 17 kilometers high."],["Named for a myth","Io is named after a figure in Greek mythology, a priestess loved by Zeus, whom the Romans called Jupiter. Its volcanoes and features are named after fire, volcano, sun and thunder gods from many cultures, as well as people and places from the myth."],["A window on early Earth","Studying Io helps scientists understand how volcanism and tidal heating work on other worlds, including exoplanets that orbit close to their stars. Some scientists think Io’s eruptions resemble those that may have occurred on early Earth, when its interior was hotter."]],
sp:["The day Voyager found a volcano","On March 9, 1979, four days after Voyager 1 flew past Jupiter, Linda Morabito was studying an image of Io taken to help navigate the spacecraft. She deliberately brightened the image to see a faint star behind the moon. Instead, she saw a huge umbrella-shaped cloud rising from the edge of Io. At first she wondered if it was another moon peeking out from behind. But no moon belonged there. It was a volcanic plume, rising about 300 kilometers into space. For the first time, humans had seen an active volcano on another world, confirming a prediction published just days earlier."],
g:[["Tidal heating","Heat produced by a moon being squeezed by gravity"],["Plume","A column of gas and dust rising from a volcano"],["Plasma torus","A ring of charged particles around Jupiter"],["Galilean moons","Jupiter’s four largest moons"]],
f:["Io has hundreds of active volcanoes.","Io is slightly larger than Earth’s Moon.","Io was discovered by Galileo in 1610."],
fun:"Tides on Io raise its solid rock surface by up to about 100 meters."});

ADDART({id:"enceladus",t:"Enceladus",c:"Astronomy",i:"❄️",w:"Enceladus",r:["saturn","astrobio","europa","titan","cassini"],
p:[["Icy moon","Enceladus is a small, bright moon of Saturn covered in ice."],["Geysers","Jets of water spray from cracks near its south pole."],["A hidden ocean","Beneath its ice lies a global ocean of salty water."]],
l:"Enceladus is a small moon of Saturn, about 500 kilometers across, discovered by William Herschel in 1789. Covered in fresh ice, it is the most reflective body in the solar system. In 2005 NASA’s Cassini spacecraft discovered jets of water vapor and ice grains erupting from long cracks near its south pole, nicknamed tiger stripes. These plumes come from a global ocean of salty liquid water beneath the icy crust, kept warm by tidal heating from Saturn’s gravity. The plumes contain salts, organic molecules, hydrogen and phosphorus, ingredients that could support life. Because of this, many scientists consider Enceladus one of the most promising places to search for life beyond Earth. Its plumes also feed Saturn’s E ring.",
s:[["The tiger stripes","Near Enceladus’s south pole are four long, parallel cracks, each about 130 kilometers long, warmer than the surrounding ice. More than 100 jets of water vapor and ice grains spray from them, forming a huge plume. Scientists named the stripes after cities in a collection of Middle Eastern tales, such as Baghdad and Cairo."],["The ocean below","By measuring how Enceladus wobbles as it orbits Saturn, Cassini scientists showed in 2015 that its icy shell floats on a global ocean. The ocean is thought to lie beneath ice several kilometers thick near the south pole and thicker elsewhere, and it may be around 10 kilometers deep."],["Ingredients for life","Cassini flew through the plumes and sampled them. It detected salty water, simple and complex organic molecules, and molecular hydrogen, which suggests chemical reactions between water and rock on the seafloor, possibly at hydrothermal vents. In 2023 scientists reported phosphates in ice grains, completing the main chemical elements known to be needed by life."],["Feeding a ring","Ice grains from Enceladus’s plumes escape into orbit around Saturn, forming the wide, faint E ring. Some of the ice also falls back to the surface as snow, keeping Enceladus bright and fresh."]],
k:[["Planet","Saturn"],["Discovered","1789, by William Herschel"],["Diameter","About 500 km"],["Plumes discovered","2005, by Cassini"],["Global ocean confirmed","2015"]],
d:[["1789","William Herschel discovers Enceladus"],["1981","Voyager 2 shows that parts of Enceladus are smooth and young"],["2005","Cassini discovers plumes erupting from the south pole"],["2015","Scientists confirm a global ocean beneath the ice"],["2017","Hydrogen is detected in the plumes"],["2023","Phosphates are found in plume particles"]],
s2:[["Could life exist there?","On Earth, hydrothermal vents on the dark seafloor support communities of microbes that live on chemical energy rather than sunlight. If similar vents exist on Enceladus’s seafloor, they could provide energy for microbes. Scientists stress that no life has been detected, but Enceladus offers a rare chance to sample an alien ocean by flying through its plumes."],["Webb sees a giant plume","In 2023 the James Webb Space Telescope observed a plume of water vapor from Enceladus stretching more than 9,000 kilometers into space, many times wider than the moon itself. The observations showed how Enceladus supplies water to a doughnut-shaped cloud around Saturn."],["Why is it warm?","Enceladus is too small to have kept much heat from its formation. Instead, tidal heating keeps its ocean liquid. Its orbit around Saturn is slightly oval because of a rhythm with the larger moon Dione, so Saturn’s gravity flexes Enceladus as it moves, generating heat, especially at the south pole."],["Future missions","Scientists have proposed missions to orbit Enceladus or land near its tiger stripes to search for signs of life. The European Space Agency has considered an Enceladus mission as a future priority, and NASA scientists have proposed concepts such as an orbiter and lander. Such missions would take many years to plan and fly."],["Named after a giant","Enceladus is named after a giant in Greek mythology who was buried under Mount Etna by the goddess Athena. The name was suggested by John Herschel, William Herschel’s son. Its surface features are named after characters and places from The Thousand and One Nights."],["A moon of contrasts","Some regions of Enceladus are old and heavily cratered, while others are smooth, cracked and almost crater-free, showing they have been recently resurfaced. This variety on such a small body surprised scientists when Voyager 2 first photographed it in 1981."]],
sp:["Cassini’s surprise","When Cassini first flew past Enceladus in early 2005, its magnetometer detected something strange: Saturn’s magnetic field seemed to be bending around the moon, as if Enceladus had an atmosphere. That should have been impossible for such a small moon, whose weak gravity cannot hold onto gas. Mission planners decided to send Cassini much closer. In July 2005 the spacecraft passed just 175 kilometers above the surface and discovered the source: water vapor erupting from warm cracks at the south pole. Over the following years, Cassini flew through the plumes many times, even tasting them. A small, overlooked moon had become one of the most exciting places in the solar system."],
g:[["Plume","A spray of gas and particles"],["Tiger stripes","Warm cracks at Enceladus’s south pole"],["Hydrothermal vent","A hot spring on a seafloor"],["Tidal heating","Heat from a moon being flexed by gravity"]],
f:["Enceladus has a global ocean under its ice.","Its plumes feed Saturn’s E ring.","Enceladus is the most reflective body in the solar system."],
fun:"Enceladus reflects almost all the sunlight that hits it, making it brighter than fresh snow."});

ADDART({id:"halley",t:"Halley’s Comet",c:"Astronomy",i:"☄️",w:"Halley's Comet",r:["comet","kuiper","meteors","newton","rosetta"],
p:[["Once a lifetime","Halley’s Comet returns about every 76 years."],["A famous prediction","Edmond Halley predicted its return using Newton’s laws."],["Next visit","It will next pass near the Sun in 2061."]],
l:"Halley’s Comet is the most famous comet in history and the only short-period comet regularly visible to the naked eye from Earth. It returns to the inner solar system about every 76 years, so most people can see it at most once in their lifetime. Records of its appearances go back more than 2,000 years, including Chinese observations from 240 BC and its depiction in the Bayeux Tapestry in 1066. In 1705 the English astronomer Edmond Halley used Isaac Newton’s laws to show that comets seen in 1531, 1607 and 1682 were the same object and predicted its return in 1758. When it reappeared, it was named after him. Its last appearance was in 1986, when spacecraft flew close to its nucleus. It will return in 2061.",
s:[["A dirty snowball","Halley’s nucleus is a potato-shaped lump of ice, dust and rock about 15 kilometers long. It is one of the darkest objects in the solar system, blacker than coal, because of a crust of dark dust. When it nears the Sun, heat turns ice into gas, releasing dust and forming a glowing coma and long tails."],["An oval orbit","Halley’s Comet follows a long, stretched orbit that carries it from inside the orbit of Venus to beyond the orbit of Neptune. It travels around the Sun in the opposite direction to the planets. It reached the farthest point of its orbit in December 2023 and is now heading back toward the Sun."],["Meteor showers","As Halley’s Comet travels, it leaves a trail of dust along its orbit. When Earth passes through this trail twice a year, the dust burns up in the atmosphere, creating two meteor showers: the Eta Aquariids in May and the Orionids in October. These showers can be seen from the Philippines."],["Changing appearances","Some returns of Halley’s Comet have been spectacular, while others were faint, depending on where Earth was when the comet passed the Sun. In 837 it passed very close to Earth and was extremely bright. The 1986 appearance was one of the poorest for viewers on Earth."]],
k:[["Orbital period","About 76 years"],["Last near the Sun","1986"],["Next near the Sun","2061"],["Nucleus size","About 15 km long"],["Related meteor showers","Eta Aquariids and Orionids"]],
d:[["240 BC","Chinese astronomers record the comet"],["1066","The comet appears before the Norman Conquest of England"],["1705","Edmond Halley predicts the comet’s return"],["1758","The comet returns as predicted"],["1910","Earth passes through the comet’s tail"],["1986","The Giotto spacecraft photographs the comet’s nucleus"]],
s2:[["Halley’s prediction","Edmond Halley, a friend of Isaac Newton, studied records of comets and noticed that those of 1531, 1607 and 1682 had similar orbits. He proposed that they were one comet returning roughly every 76 years and predicted it would reappear in late 1758 or early 1759. Halley died in 1742 and did not live to see it, but on Christmas night 1758, a German farmer and amateur astronomer, Johann Georg Palitzsch, spotted it. The prediction was a triumph for Newton’s theory of gravity."],["An omen in history","For centuries, comets were seen as omens of disaster or change. Halley’s Comet appeared in 1066, shortly before William of Normandy conquered England, and it was embroidered into the Bayeux Tapestry with people pointing at it in wonder. Some historians believe the Italian painter Giotto depicted the comet as the Star of Bethlehem in a fresco painted in the early 1300s."],["Comet pills of 1910","In 1910 astronomers announced that Earth would pass through the comet’s tail, which contained a poisonous gas called cyanogen. Although scientists explained that the gas was far too thin to cause harm, some people panicked. Sellers made money from comet pills and gas masks. Nothing happened, and the comet put on a fine show."],["Spacecraft visit","When Halley returned in 1986, an international fleet of spacecraft went to meet it. The European Space Agency’s Giotto, named after the painter, flew within about 600 kilometers of the nucleus in March 1986 and took the first close-up images of a comet’s nucleus. Soviet Vega probes and Japanese probes also studied it."],["Mark Twain and the comet","The American writer Mark Twain was born in 1835, shortly after the comet appeared. He predicted he would die when it returned, saying he had come in with the comet and expected to go out with it. He died in April 1910, a day after the comet passed closest to the Sun."],["The 2061 return","Halley’s Comet will next pass closest to the Sun in July 2061. Astronomers expect it to be better placed for viewing from Earth than in 1986. Many people who are children today may get the chance to see it, perhaps with their own children or grandchildren."]],
sp:["The farmer who saw it first","As the year 1758 drew to a close, astronomers across Europe searched the skies for the comet Edmond Halley had predicted. Some doubted it would appear. Professional astronomers in France had calculated that the gravity of Jupiter and Saturn would delay its arrival, so they expected it in the spring of 1759. On Christmas night 1758, Johann Georg Palitzsch, a farmer near Dresden in Germany who loved astronomy, pointed his telescope at the sky and found a faint, fuzzy patch. It was the comet. It passed closest to the Sun in March 1759, within a month of the calculations. Halley had been dead for 16 years, but his prediction had come true."],
g:[["Comet","An icy object that forms a glowing tail near the Sun"],["Nucleus","The solid center of a comet"],["Coma","The cloud of gas and dust around a comet’s nucleus"],["Orbital period","The time to complete one orbit"]],
f:["Halley’s Comet returns about every 76 years.","It will return in 2061.","It causes two meteor showers each year."],
fun:"Halley’s nucleus is darker than coal, even though comets are often called dirty snowballs."});

ADDART({id:"betelgeuse",t:"Betelgeuse",c:"Astronomy",i:"🔴",w:"Betelgeuse",r:["stars","supernova","constellations","pulsar","bh","variablestars","hypergiants"],
p:[["A red giant","Betelgeuse is a huge red supergiant star in Orion."],["A sudden fade","In 2019 and 2020 it faded dramatically."],["Future supernova","One day it will explode as a supernova."]],
l:"Betelgeuse is a red supergiant star in the constellation Orion, marking the hunter’s shoulder. It is one of the brightest stars in the night sky and one of the largest known: if placed at the center of our solar system, it would extend beyond the orbit of Mars, and perhaps toward Jupiter. Betelgeuse is a few hundred light-years from Earth, with estimates ranging from about 500 to 700 light-years. It is a young but massive star near the end of its life, and it is expected to explode as a supernova sometime within the next 100,000 years or so. Its brightness varies, and in late 2019 and early 2020 it dimmed dramatically, which scientists later traced mainly to a cloud of dust it had ejected. In 2025 astronomers reported evidence of a small companion star.",
s:[["How big is it?","Betelgeuse’s radius is roughly 700 to 1,000 times that of the Sun, though it is hard to measure because the star has no sharp edge and its size changes. Its mass is about 15 to 20 times the Sun’s. It is so large and close that it was the first star after the Sun whose size was measured, in 1920."],["Red and cool","Betelgeuse glows reddish-orange because its surface is cooler than the Sun’s, at around 3,500 degrees Celsius. Although cooler, it is enormously bright, giving off tens of thousands of times as much energy as the Sun because of its huge surface."],["A restless star","Betelgeuse’s surface is covered with giant bubbling cells of hot gas, some larger than Earth’s orbit. The star pulsates, changing in size and brightness over months and years. It also loses large amounts of material into space, surrounding itself with clouds of gas and dust."],["The coming explosion","Betelgeuse is running out of fuel in its core. When fusion stops, its core will collapse and it will explode as a supernova, likely leaving behind a neutron star. Astronomers cannot predict exactly when; it could be tomorrow or tens of thousands of years from now."]],
k:[["Constellation","Orion"],["Type","Red supergiant"],["Distance","About 500–700 light-years"],["Size","Hundreds of times the Sun’s radius"],["Fate","Supernova"]],
d:[["1836","John Herschel notes that Betelgeuse varies in brightness"],["1920","Albert Michelson and Francis Pease measure its size"],["1995","Hubble takes the first direct image of the surface of a star other than the Sun"],["2019","Betelgeuse begins its Great Dimming"],["2021","Astronomers conclude a dust cloud caused most of the dimming"],["2025","Astronomers report evidence of a companion star"]],
s2:[["The Great Dimming","Beginning in October 2019, Betelgeuse faded until, by February 2020, it was less than half its usual brightness, noticeably changing the look of Orion. Some people wondered whether it was about to explode. Studies using the Very Large Telescope in Chile and the Hubble Space Telescope showed that the star had ejected a large bubble of gas, which cooled into dust and blocked part of its light, along with a cool patch on its surface. By mid-2020 it had brightened again."],["What would the supernova look like?","When Betelgeuse explodes, it will become far brighter than any star or planet in the night sky, possibly as bright as a half Moon, and visible during the day for weeks. It would then fade over months. Because it is several hundred light-years away, the explosion would not harm life on Earth."],["A companion star","Astronomers had long suspected that a small star orbits Betelgeuse, causing a slow change in its brightness every six years or so. In 2025 a team using the Gemini North telescope in Hawaii reported detecting a faint companion close to the giant. It is thought to be a young star that may eventually be swallowed by Betelgeuse."],["The name","The name Betelgeuse comes from Arabic, through a long chain of copying and mistranslation. The original Arabic name is usually interpreted as the hand of al-Jawzāʾ, a figure later associated with Orion. Over centuries, medieval European scholars misread the Arabic letters, producing the modern name."],["Orion from the Philippines","Orion is easy to see from the Philippines in the evening sky from about December to March. Betelgeuse is the bright reddish star at the upper left of the constellation as it rises in the east, opposite blue-white Rigel. The three stars of Orion’s Belt are known in Filipino Christian tradition as the Tatlong Maria or Tres Marias."],["Betelgeuse in culture","Betelgeuse has appeared in many stories and myths. Aboriginal Australian peoples, Polynesian navigators and other cultures had their own names and stories for the star. In modern popular culture, it gave its name to the comedy film Beetlejuice, whose title character’s name is pronounced like the star."]],
sp:["Watching a giant fade","In late 2019, amateur and professional astronomers noticed something unusual: Betelgeuse, one of the most familiar stars in the sky, was fading. By February 2020, it had dropped from among the ten brightest stars to about the twenty-fifth. Social media buzzed with speculation that the star might be about to explode as a supernova. Astronomers urgently pointed telescopes at it. Images from the Very Large Telescope revealed that the southern half of the star had become much darker. Later analysis showed that Betelgeuse had burped out a bubble of gas that cooled and formed dust, acting like a veil. The star had not exploded, but the event gave scientists a rare view of how dying giant stars lose material."],
g:[["Red supergiant","A huge, cool, bright star near the end of its life"],["Supernova","The explosion of a star"],["Light-year","The distance light travels in a year"],["Variable star","A star whose brightness changes"]],
f:["Betelgeuse is in Orion.","It dimmed dramatically in 2019 and 2020.","It will one day explode as a supernova."],
fun:"If Betelgeuse replaced the Sun, its surface would reach beyond the orbit of Mars."});

ADDART({id:"magellanic",t:"The Magellanic Clouds",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Magellanic Clouds",r:["galaxies","milky","magellan","supernova","andromeda","localgroup","dwarfgalaxies"],
p:[["Neighbor galaxies","The Magellanic Clouds are two small galaxies near the Milky Way."],["Southern skies","They are best seen from the Southern Hemisphere."],["A famous supernova","A supernova exploded in the Large Magellanic Cloud in 1987."]],
l:"The Large and Small Magellanic Clouds are two irregular dwarf galaxies close to the Milky Way, visible to the naked eye from the Southern Hemisphere as faint, glowing patches in the night sky. The Large Magellanic Cloud is about 160,000 light-years away and the Small Magellanic Cloud about 200,000 light-years away. They are among the nearest galaxies to our own. Named in Europe after Ferdinand Magellan, whose expedition described them in the 1500s, they had long been known to peoples of the Southern Hemisphere and to Arab and Persian astronomers. The clouds are rich in gas and young stars, and contain the Tarantula Nebula, one of the most active star-forming regions known. In 1987 a supernova exploded in the Large Magellanic Cloud.",
s:[["Two dwarf galaxies","The Large Magellanic Cloud has a mass of perhaps one-tenth or less that of the Milky Way, and the Small Magellanic Cloud is smaller still. Both have irregular shapes, partly because the Milky Way’s gravity and their interactions with each other have pulled and distorted them. The Large Magellanic Cloud shows signs of a central bar and a single spiral arm."],["The Tarantula Nebula","The Tarantula Nebula in the Large Magellanic Cloud is a huge cloud of glowing gas where new stars are forming. It contains some of the most massive stars known, including R136a1, one of the heaviest stars ever found. If the Tarantula were as close as the Orion Nebula, it would cast shadows on Earth."],["The Magellanic Stream","A long trail of gas called the Magellanic Stream stretches across much of the southern sky, pulled out of the clouds by gravity. A bridge of gas and stars also connects the two clouds, showing that they have interacted with each other."],["Visitors or companions?","Astronomers long thought the Magellanic Clouds were satellite galaxies orbiting the Milky Way. Precise measurements of their motions using the Hubble Space Telescope, published from 2006 onward, suggested they may be moving so fast that they are on their first pass near the Milky Way. Many astronomers expect the Large Magellanic Cloud eventually to collide and merge with the Milky Way, billions of years from now."]],
k:[["Type","Irregular dwarf galaxies"],["Distance of the Large Cloud","About 160,000 light-years"],["Distance of the Small Cloud","About 200,000 light-years"],["Famous nebula","Tarantula Nebula"],["Famous supernova","SN 1987A"]],
d:[["964","Persian astronomer al-Sufi describes the Large Magellanic Cloud"],["1519","Magellan’s expedition records the clouds during its voyage"],["1912","Henrietta Leavitt publishes her discovery of a rule for Cepheid stars in the Small Cloud"],["1987","Supernova 1987A explodes in the Large Magellanic Cloud"],["2006","Hubble measurements suggest the clouds may be on their first pass near the Milky Way"],["2022","The James Webb Space Telescope images the Tarantula Nebula in detail"]],
s2:[["Supernova 1987A","In February 1987, astronomers saw a star explode in the Large Magellanic Cloud. It was the closest supernova seen since 1604 and could be seen with the naked eye from the Southern Hemisphere. Detectors in Japan and the United States caught a burst of neutrinos hours before the light arrived, confirming theories of how massive stars collapse. Astronomers continue to study the expanding remains."],["Leavitt’s measuring stick","At Harvard College Observatory in the early 1900s, Henrietta Swan Leavitt studied photographs of the Small Magellanic Cloud. She found that brighter Cepheid variable stars pulsed more slowly. Because all the stars in the cloud were at roughly the same distance, this revealed a rule linking a Cepheid’s true brightness to its pulsing period. Her discovery gave astronomers a way to measure distances to other galaxies, which Edwin Hubble later used."],["Names and history","In Europe, the clouds were named after Ferdinand Magellan because Antonio Pigafetta, the chronicler of Magellan’s expedition, described them during the voyage of 1519 to 1522, which also reached the Philippines. Southern peoples such as Aboriginal Australians, Māori and Polynesian navigators, and Andean cultures had long had their own names and stories for them. Some astronomers have suggested renaming the clouds, citing the violence of Magellan’s expedition and earlier knowledge of the clouds, while others prefer to keep the long-established name; the question remains under discussion."],["Seeing them","The Magellanic Clouds are easy to see from Australia, New Zealand, southern Africa and South America on dark, moonless nights, far from city lights. From the Philippines they lie very low near the southern horizon, and can be glimpsed only from the southern islands under exceptionally clear, dark skies."],["Neighbors in the Local Group","The Magellanic Clouds are part of the Local Group, a family of galaxies that includes the Milky Way, the Andromeda Galaxy and dozens of small galaxies. The Large Magellanic Cloud is the fourth-largest galaxy in the group. It even has its own small satellite galaxies."],["A laboratory for astronomy","Because all the stars in each cloud are at nearly the same distance from Earth and are not hidden by much dust, the Magellanic Clouds are ideal for studying how stars form, evolve and die. Their gas contains fewer heavy elements than the Milky Way, resembling conditions in the early universe."]],
sp:["The night a star exploded","In the early hours of February 24, 1987, Ian Shelton, a Canadian astronomer working at Las Campanas Observatory in Chile, developed a photograph of the Large Magellanic Cloud. He noticed a bright star that had not been there the night before. He went outside, looked up, and could see it with his own eyes. Around the same time, other observers in Chile and New Zealand spotted it too. It was a supernova, the brightest seen since the time of Johannes Kepler in 1604. Meanwhile, neutrino detectors deep underground in Japan and the United States had already recorded a burst of particles from the dying star. For astronomers, it was the event of a lifetime."],
g:[["Dwarf galaxy","A small galaxy with relatively few stars"],["Cepheid variable","A pulsing star used to measure distances"],["Supernova","The explosion of a star"],["Local Group","The group of galaxies that includes the Milky Way"]],
f:["The Magellanic Clouds are galaxies.","Supernova 1987A exploded in the Large Magellanic Cloud.","They are best seen from the Southern Hemisphere."],
fun:"Light from the Large Magellanic Cloud left it about 160,000 years ago, when early modern humans lived in Africa."});

