/* data/space12.js */
// Space: white dwarfs, the Hubble Space Telescope, the Space Shuttle, New Horizons and Cassini.
ADDART({id:"whitedwarfs",t:"White Dwarfs",c:"Astronomy",i:"⚪",w:"White dwarf",r:["stars","sun","supernova","pulsar","nebula","hrdiagram","novae","planetarynebulae"],
p:[["Dead stars","A white dwarf is the hot, glowing core left after a star like the Sun dies."],["Incredibly dense","A teaspoon of white dwarf material would weigh metric tons."],["The Sun’s future","Our Sun will one day become a white dwarf."]],
l:"A white dwarf is the dense remnant of a star that has used up its nuclear fuel. Stars up to about eight times the mass of the Sun, including the Sun itself, end their lives this way. After swelling into a red giant and shedding its outer layers as a glowing cloud called a planetary nebula, the star leaves behind its hot core: a white dwarf about the size of Earth but with roughly the mass of the Sun. Its matter is so tightly packed that a teaspoonful would weigh several metric tons. White dwarfs no longer produce energy by fusion; they simply glow from stored heat and slowly cool over billions of years. Most stars in the Milky Way, including the Sun, will become white dwarfs.",
s:[["How they form","When a Sun-like star runs low on hydrogen in its core, it swells into a red giant, burning helium and other fuels. Eventually it puffs its outer layers into space, forming a planetary nebula. The exposed core, made mostly of carbon and oxygen, shrinks into a white dwarf, initially extremely hot."],["What holds them up","A white dwarf is not supported by heat and fusion like a normal star. Instead, its electrons are packed so tightly that a quantum effect called electron degeneracy pressure resists further squeezing. This pressure supports the star against its own gravity."],["The Chandrasekhar limit","In 1930 the young Indian physicist Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar calculated that a white dwarf cannot be more than about 1.4 times the mass of the Sun. Above this limit, electron pressure fails, and the star collapses. This idea helped explain neutron stars and black holes, and Chandrasekhar shared the 1983 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Cooling over time","A white dwarf starts out at temperatures of over 100,000 degrees Celsius and slowly cools and dims. As it cools, its interior gradually crystallizes into a solid. Over enormous spans of time, it would become a cold, dark black dwarf, but the universe is not yet old enough for any to exist."]],
k:[["Typical size","About the size of Earth"],["Typical mass","About 0.6 times the Sun"],["Maximum mass","About 1.4 times the Sun"],["Main makeup","Carbon and oxygen"],["Nearest famous example","Sirius B"]],
d:[["1844","Friedrich Bessel predicts an unseen companion of Sirius"],["1862","Alvan Graham Clark sees Sirius B through a telescope"],["1914","Astronomers find that a dim star, 40 Eridani B, is very hot"],["1922","Willem Luyten uses the term white dwarf"],["1930","Chandrasekhar calculates the maximum mass of a white dwarf"],["2019","Gaia data show white dwarfs crystallizing as they cool"]],
s2:[["Sirius B","Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky, has a faint companion called Sirius B. In 1844 the German astronomer Friedrich Bessel noticed Sirius wobbling and predicted that an unseen companion was tugging on it. In 1862 the American telescope maker Alvan Graham Clark spotted it. Astronomers were later amazed to find that it was hot but very small, meaning it must be extraordinarily dense."],["Exploding white dwarfs","If a white dwarf in a close binary system steals enough gas from a companion star, or merges with another white dwarf, it can approach the Chandrasekhar limit and explode in a type Ia supernova. These explosions have a fairly consistent brightness, so astronomers use them as standard candles to measure distances. Such measurements led to the discovery in 1998 that the expansion of the universe is speeding up."],["Novae","When a white dwarf pulls hydrogen from a companion, the gas can build up on its surface and ignite in a sudden burst of fusion, called a nova. The star brightens dramatically for days or weeks without being destroyed. Some systems produce repeated novae over decades."],["The Sun’s fate","In about five billion years, the Sun will swell into a red giant, possibly swallowing Mercury, Venus and perhaps Earth. It will then shed its outer layers and shrink into a white dwarf about the size of Earth. Its glowing remnant will fade over trillions of years."],["Planets around dead stars","Astronomers have found white dwarfs with rocky debris falling onto their surfaces, the remains of planets or asteroids torn apart by gravity. In 2020 astronomers discovered a giant planet orbiting close to a white dwarf, showing that some planets can survive their star’s death."],["Cosmic clocks","Because white dwarfs cool at predictable rates, astronomers can estimate the ages of star clusters and parts of the Milky Way by finding the coolest, faintest white dwarfs. This method suggests that the oldest parts of our galaxy are around 10 to 13 billion years old."]],
sp:["Chandrasekhar’s voyage","In 1930 Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, a 19-year-old graduate from Madras in India, sailed to England to study at Cambridge. During the long sea voyage, he worked on calculations about white dwarfs, combining Einstein’s relativity with quantum physics. He discovered that a white dwarf above about 1.4 solar masses could not support itself and would collapse. When he presented his findings a few years later, the famous astronomer Arthur Eddington publicly ridiculed the idea, and many scientists sided with Eddington. Chandrasekhar moved to the United States and continued his research. Decades later his conclusion was fully accepted, and in 1983 he received the Nobel Prize in Physics. NASA’s Chandra X-ray Observatory is named after him."],
g:[["White dwarf","The dense remnant of a Sun-like star"],["Planetary nebula","A glowing shell of gas thrown off by a dying star"],["Chandrasekhar limit","The maximum mass of a white dwarf"],["Black dwarf","A white dwarf that has cooled completely"]],
f:["The Sun will become a white dwarf.","White dwarfs are about the size of Earth.","Sirius has a white dwarf companion."],
fun:"A teaspoon of white dwarf material would weigh about as much as an elephant or more."});

ADDART({id:"hubblest",t:"The Hubble Space Telescope",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔭",w:"Hubble Space Telescope",r:["scope","jwst","hubble","galaxies","spaceshuttle","bulletcluster","whirlpool"],
p:[["Above the clouds","Hubble orbits above Earth’s atmosphere for clear views."],["A flawed start","Its mirror had a flaw that astronauts later fixed."],["Changing astronomy","Hubble has transformed our view of the universe."]],
l:"The Hubble Space Telescope is a large telescope orbiting Earth, launched by the Space Shuttle Discovery in April 1990. A joint project of NASA and the European Space Agency, it is named after the astronomer Edwin Hubble. Because it orbits about 540 kilometers above the surface, beyond the blurring effects of Earth’s atmosphere, Hubble can take extraordinarily sharp images in visible, ultraviolet and near-infrared light. Soon after launch, scientists found that its main mirror had been ground to the wrong shape, but astronauts installed corrective optics in 1993. Over five servicing missions, astronauts repaired and upgraded it. Hubble has helped measure the age of the universe, discover that its expansion is accelerating, and photograph galaxies billions of light-years away.",
s:[["How Hubble works","Hubble’s 2.4-meter main mirror collects light and reflects it to a smaller secondary mirror, which sends it to scientific instruments, including cameras and spectrographs. It orbits Earth about every 95 minutes. Gyroscopes and other systems keep it pointed precisely at targets for hours at a time."],["The flawed mirror","After launch, Hubble’s first images were disappointingly blurry. Investigators found that the main mirror was too flat at the edges by about 2 micrometers, a fraction of the width of a human hair, because of an error in a testing device. The mistake was embarrassing for NASA, and critics called the telescope a costly failure."],["The rescue","In December 1993, astronauts aboard the Space Shuttle Endeavour carried out a series of spacewalks to install corrective optics, like glasses for the telescope, and a new camera. The repair was a success, and Hubble’s images became sharp. Later missions replaced instruments, gyroscopes, batteries and solar panels."],["Servicing missions","Five shuttle missions serviced Hubble between 1993 and 2009. After the Columbia disaster in 2003, NASA at first canceled the final servicing mission because of safety concerns, but public and scientific support led it to be restored. The last mission, in 2009, extended Hubble’s life for many years."]],
k:[["Launched","April 24, 1990"],["Main mirror","2.4 meters"],["Orbit height","About 540 km"],["Partners","NASA and the European Space Agency"],["Named after","Edwin Hubble"]],
d:[["1946","Lyman Spitzer proposes a large telescope in space"],["1990","Hubble is launched aboard Space Shuttle Discovery"],["1993","Astronauts fix Hubble’s blurry vision"],["1995","Hubble takes the Hubble Deep Field and Pillars of Creation images"],["2009","The final servicing mission upgrades Hubble"],["2024","Hubble switches to operating with one gyroscope to extend its life"]],
s2:[["Deep fields","In 1995 astronomers pointed Hubble at a tiny, seemingly empty patch of sky for ten days. The resulting Hubble Deep Field revealed thousands of galaxies, some seen as they were billions of years ago. Later deep fields looked even farther, showing that the universe contains vastly more galaxies than once thought."],["Famous images","Hubble’s photograph of the Pillars of Creation, towering columns of gas and dust in the Eagle Nebula where stars are forming, became one of the most famous images in science. Its pictures of colorful nebulae, colliding galaxies and planets have appeared in textbooks, posters and stamps around the world, inspiring public interest in astronomy."],["Scientific discoveries","Hubble helped pin down the age of the universe at about 13.8 billion years and measure its expansion rate. Together with ground telescopes, it provided evidence that the expansion is accelerating, driven by dark energy. It showed that supermassive black holes lie at the centers of most large galaxies and observed comet Shoemaker-Levy 9 crashing into Jupiter in 1994."],["Studying other worlds","Hubble was used to make some of the first measurements of the atmospheres of planets orbiting other stars. It has watched seasons change on Mars, storms on Jupiter and Saturn, and plumes of water possibly erupting from Jupiter’s moon Europa. It also discovered several small moons of Pluto."],["Hubble and Webb","The James Webb Space Telescope, launched in 2021, observes mainly in infrared light and has a much larger mirror. Rather than replacing Hubble, it works alongside it: Hubble sees visible and ultraviolet light, while Webb sees farther and through dust. Astronomers often combine data from both."],["The end of Hubble","Without servicing, Hubble’s orbit is slowly decaying because of drag from the thin upper atmosphere. NASA expects the telescope to continue working into the 2030s. Options for its eventual fate include a controlled reentry into the ocean or a boost to a higher orbit, and some companies have proposed private missions to service it."]],
sp:["Ten days staring at nothing","In 1995 Robert Williams, director of the institute that runs Hubble, made a daring decision. He used his own discretionary observing time to point the telescope at a tiny patch of sky near the Big Dipper that seemed to contain nothing at all, about the size of a grain of sand held at arm’s length. Some colleagues worried it would be a waste of precious time. For ten days, Hubble collected faint light from that spot. When the images were combined, they revealed around 3,000 galaxies of all shapes, colors and ages, some among the most distant ever seen. The Hubble Deep Field showed that every small patch of sky is crowded with galaxies, and changed how people imagined the size of the universe."],
g:[["Space telescope","A telescope placed in orbit"],["Servicing mission","A mission to repair or upgrade a spacecraft"],["Deep field","A long exposure of a small area of sky"],["Spectrograph","An instrument that splits light into colors"]],
f:["Hubble was launched in 1990.","Astronauts fixed its flawed mirror in 1993.","Hubble orbits Earth about every 95 minutes."],
fun:"Hubble’s mirror flaw was smaller than a fiftieth of the width of a human hair."});

ADDART({id:"spaceshuttle",t:"The Space Shuttle",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"Space Shuttle",r:["rocket","iss","astronauts","space","hubblest"],
p:[["A reusable spaceship","The Space Shuttle was the first reusable crewed spacecraft."],["Thirty years of flights","It flew from 1981 to 2011."],["Two tragedies","Challenger and Columbia were lost with their crews."]],
l:"The Space Shuttle was a partly reusable spacecraft operated by NASA from 1981 to 2011. It launched like a rocket, orbited Earth like a spacecraft and landed like a glider on a runway. The system included a winged orbiter carrying astronauts and cargo, a large external fuel tank and two solid rocket boosters. Five orbiters flew in space: Columbia, Challenger, Discovery, Atlantis and Endeavour. Over 135 missions, the shuttles carried hundreds of astronauts from many countries, launched satellites and space probes, deployed and repaired the Hubble Space Telescope, and built much of the International Space Station. Two orbiters were destroyed in accidents, Challenger in 1986 and Columbia in 2003, killing 14 astronauts. The program ended in 2011.",
s:[["How it worked","At launch, the orbiter’s three main engines burned fuel from the external tank, while the two solid rocket boosters provided most of the thrust. The boosters separated about two minutes after launch and parachuted into the ocean to be recovered and reused. The external tank was released just before reaching orbit and burned up in the atmosphere."],["In orbit","The orbiter could carry up to seven astronauts and a large payload in its cargo bay, whose doors opened in space. A robotic arm, built by Canada, moved satellites and astronauts. Missions usually lasted one to two weeks."],["Coming home","To return, the orbiter fired its engines to slow down and reentered the atmosphere, protected by thousands of heat-resistant tiles and panels that withstood temperatures over 1,500 degrees Celsius. It then glided without engines to a landing on a long runway, usually in Florida or California."],["Promises and reality","The shuttle was planned to make spaceflight cheap and routine, with frequent flights. In practice, it required extensive refurbishment between missions, flew far less often than planned and was very expensive. Supporters point to its unique abilities to carry large cargo and crews and to repair satellites, while critics argue that it was too costly and risky."]],
k:[["Operator","NASA"],["Years flown","1981–2011"],["Missions","135"],["Orbiters flown in space","Columbia, Challenger, Discovery, Atlantis, Endeavour"],["Maximum crew","Usually up to 7"]],
d:[["1972","President Richard Nixon approves the Space Shuttle program"],["1981","Columbia makes the first shuttle flight"],["1986","Challenger breaks apart after launch"],["1990","Discovery launches the Hubble Space Telescope"],["2003","Columbia breaks apart during reentry"],["2011","Atlantis makes the final shuttle flight"]],
s2:[["The Challenger disaster","On January 28, 1986, the Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart 73 seconds after launch, killing all seven crew members, including Christa McAuliffe, a schoolteacher chosen to be the first teacher in space. An investigation found that a rubber seal, called an O-ring, in a booster had failed in cold weather. It also criticized NASA’s decision-making, as engineers had warned of the danger."],["The Columbia disaster","On February 1, 2003, Columbia broke apart while returning to Earth, killing its seven astronauts, including Ilan Ramon, Israel’s first astronaut, and Kalpana Chawla, the first woman of Indian origin in space. A piece of foam had broken off the external tank during launch and damaged the wing’s heat shield. The disaster led NASA to decide to retire the shuttle."],["Building the space station","Shuttles carried many of the large modules, trusses and solar panels that make up the International Space Station, and astronauts assembled them on spacewalks. They also carried crews and supplies to the station. After the shuttle’s retirement, the United States relied on Russian Soyuz spacecraft until private American spacecraft began carrying astronauts in 2020."],["Firsts and pioneers","Shuttle flights carried many pioneering astronauts. Sally Ride became the first American woman in space in 1983, and Guion Bluford the first African American in space the same year. Astronauts from Europe, Japan, Canada, Saudi Arabia, Mexico and other countries flew aboard the shuttle. Mae Jemison became the first African American woman in space in 1992."],["Where the shuttles are now","After retirement, the remaining orbiters were placed in museums. Discovery is displayed near Washington, D.C., Atlantis at the Kennedy Space Center in Florida, and Endeavour at the California Science Center in Los Angeles. The test orbiter Enterprise, which never flew in space, is in New York."],["The Soviet shuttle","The Soviet Union built its own shuttle, Buran, which looked similar to the American orbiter. In 1988 it made a single uncrewed flight, orbiting Earth twice and landing automatically. The program was canceled after the collapse of the Soviet Union, and Buran was destroyed in 2002 when a hangar roof collapsed."]],
sp:["Columbia’s first flight","On April 12, 1981, exactly 20 years after Yuri Gagarin became the first human in space, the Space Shuttle Columbia stood on its launch pad in Florida with two astronauts aboard, John Young and Robert Crippen. It was the first time NASA had launched a new spacecraft with a crew on its very first flight, without an uncrewed test. With a roar, the shuttle rose into the sky. After two days in orbit, Columbia reentered the atmosphere and glided to a smooth landing on a dry lakebed in California, watched by huge crowds. A new era of reusable spaceflight had begun, one that would last 30 years."],
g:[["Orbiter","The winged part of the shuttle that carried crew"],["Solid rocket booster","A rocket filled with solid fuel"],["Payload bay","The cargo area of the shuttle"],["Reentry","Returning into Earth’s atmosphere"]],
f:["The shuttle landed like a glider.","Shuttles flew from 1981 to 2011.","Shuttles helped build the International Space Station."],
fun:"The shuttle’s first launch was on the 20th anniversary of the first human spaceflight."});

ADDART({id:"newhorizons",t:"New Horizons",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"New Horizons",r:["pluto","kuiper","dwarfplanets","voyager","solar"],
p:[["To Pluto","New Horizons was the first spacecraft to visit Pluto."],["A heart on Pluto","It revealed a giant heart-shaped plain of ice."],["Farther still","It later flew past Arrokoth, the most distant object ever explored."]],
l:"New Horizons is a NASA space probe that made the first close-up exploration of Pluto and its moons in July 2015. Launched in January 2006, it was the fastest spacecraft ever launched from Earth, and it took nine and a half years to reach Pluto. Its images transformed Pluto from a blurry dot into a complex world with mountains of water ice, glaciers of frozen nitrogen, a hazy blue atmosphere and a vast heart-shaped plain. In January 2019 the spacecraft flew past Arrokoth, a small, snowman-shaped object in the Kuiper Belt, the most distant object ever explored up close. New Horizons continues to travel outward through the Kuiper Belt, studying the outer solar system.",
s:[["The journey","New Horizons launched on January 19, 2006, aboard an Atlas V rocket. In 2007 it flew past Jupiter, using the giant planet’s gravity to speed up and shorten its journey. It spent much of its trip in hibernation to save energy, waking periodically for checkups."],["Pluto revealed","On July 14, 2015, New Horizons passed about 12,500 kilometers above Pluto’s surface, taking detailed images and measurements. It found a young, active world with ice mountains several kilometers high, flowing nitrogen glaciers and layers of haze. Data from the flyby took more than a year to send back to Earth."],["The heart of Pluto","The most famous feature it revealed is a bright, heart-shaped region named Tombaugh Regio, after Pluto’s discoverer. Its left lobe, Sputnik Planitia, is a huge basin filled with nitrogen ice that slowly churns in large cells, renewing the surface. The heart made Pluto a favorite with the public."],["Pluto’s moons","New Horizons studied Pluto’s largest moon, Charon, which is half Pluto’s size and has a dark red polar region colored by material escaping from Pluto. It also photographed the small moons Nix and Hydra. Pluto and Charon are sometimes described as a double world."]],
k:[["Launched","January 19, 2006"],["Pluto flyby","July 14, 2015"],["Arrokoth flyby","January 1, 2019"],["Operator","NASA"],["Power source","A nuclear battery"]],
d:[["1930","Clyde Tombaugh discovers Pluto"],["2006","New Horizons is launched"],["2006","Pluto is reclassified as a dwarf planet"],["2007","New Horizons swings past Jupiter"],["2015","New Horizons flies past Pluto"],["2019","New Horizons flies past Arrokoth"]],
s2:[["A dwarf planet after launch","Just months after New Horizons launched, the International Astronomical Union reclassified Pluto as a dwarf planet in August 2006. Some scientists on the mission, including its leader Alan Stern, have argued strongly that Pluto should still be called a planet, while many other astronomers support the reclassification. The debate continues."],["Tombaugh’s ashes","New Horizons carries a small portion of the ashes of Clyde Tombaugh, the American astronomer who discovered Pluto in 1930 and died in 1997. It also carries US state quarter coins and a piece of SpaceShipOne, the first private crewed spacecraft. Tombaugh’s ashes will continue traveling out of the solar system."],["Arrokoth","On January 1, 2019, New Horizons flew past Arrokoth, a Kuiper Belt object about 36 kilometers long, made of two flattened lobes joined together. It is thought to be a pristine building block left over from the formation of the solar system. Its name comes from the Powhatan and Algonquian language and means sky, chosen with the consent of Powhatan elders."],["Nuclear power","Sunlight at Pluto is far too weak for solar panels, so New Horizons uses a radioisotope thermoelectric generator, which turns heat from decaying plutonium into electricity. Some groups protested the launch of nuclear material, while NASA argued the risks were very small. The same type of power has been used by the Voyager and Cassini probes."],["Slow messages","At Pluto’s distance, radio signals traveling at the speed of light took about four and a half hours to reach Earth. Because New Horizons’ transmitter was weak and the distance so great, data trickled back slowly, and the full set of Pluto flyby data took about 16 months to download."],["Still flying","New Horizons continues outward through the Kuiper Belt, measuring dust, charged particles and the faint glow of the universe. Scientists hope it might find another object to fly past. It is expected to eventually leave the solar system’s bubble of solar wind, following the path of the Voyager probes."]],
sp:["The phone-home moment","As New Horizons raced past Pluto on July 14, 2015, it was too busy collecting data to communicate with Earth. Mission controllers in Maryland had to wait about 13 hours, not knowing whether the spacecraft had survived passing through the Pluto system, where an unseen piece of debris could have destroyed it. That evening, a signal finally arrived, carrying the message that the spacecraft was healthy and had stored its data. The control room erupted in cheers. Over the following months, images revealing Pluto’s mountains, glaciers and heart streamed back, and a world that had been a mystery for 85 years came into view."],
g:[["Space probe","An uncrewed spacecraft sent to explore"],["Kuiper Belt","A region of icy objects beyond Neptune"],["Flyby","Passing close to an object without stopping"],["Dwarf planet","A small planet-like body"]],
f:["New Horizons flew past Pluto in 2015.","It found a heart-shaped region on Pluto.","It explored Arrokoth in 2019."],
fun:"New Horizons carries some of the ashes of the man who discovered Pluto."});

ADDART({id:"cassini",t:"The Cassini–Huygens Mission",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🪐",w:"Cassini–Huygens",r:["saturn","titan","enceladus","voyager","jupiter"],
p:[["Saturn explorer","Cassini orbited Saturn for 13 years."],["Landing on Titan","Its Huygens probe landed on Saturn’s moon Titan in 2005."],["A grand finale","Cassini ended its mission by diving into Saturn."]],
l:"The Cassini–Huygens mission was a joint project of NASA, the European Space Agency and the Italian Space Agency to study Saturn, its rings and its moons. Launched in October 1997, the spacecraft arrived at Saturn in 2004 and orbited the planet for 13 years. It carried the Huygens probe, built by the European Space Agency, which parachuted onto Saturn’s largest moon, Titan, in January 2005, the most distant landing ever made on another world. Cassini discovered water jets on the moon Enceladus, lakes of liquid methane on Titan, new moons and details of Saturn’s rings and storms. In 2017, running low on fuel, Cassini made a series of daring dives between Saturn and its rings before plunging into the planet’s atmosphere.",
s:[["A long journey","Cassini was one of the largest and most complex interplanetary spacecraft ever built, about the size of a small bus. It took nearly seven years to reach Saturn, swinging past Venus twice, Earth and Jupiter to gain speed. It arrived on July 1, 2004, firing its engine to slip into orbit through a gap in the rings."],["Huygens on Titan","On January 14, 2005, Huygens descended through Titan’s thick, orange haze for about two and a half hours, photographing river channels and hills, and landed on a damp plain scattered with rounded pebbles of ice. It sent data for over an hour from the surface. Its images were the first ever taken from the surface of a world in the outer solar system."],["Discoveries at Enceladus","In 2005 Cassini discovered plumes of water vapor and ice erupting from the south pole of the small moon Enceladus. Later flybys through the plumes revealed salts, organic molecules and evidence of a global ocean, making Enceladus a prime target in the search for life."],["Saturn’s secrets","Cassini studied a giant six-sided jet stream at Saturn’s north pole, called the hexagon, and watched a huge storm encircle the planet in 2010 and 2011. It revealed waves, clumps and small moonlets within the rings and helped measure the length of Saturn’s day."]],
k:[["Partners","NASA, ESA, Italian Space Agency"],["Launched","October 15, 1997"],["Arrived at Saturn","July 1, 2004"],["Huygens landing","January 14, 2005"],["Mission end","September 15, 2017"]],
d:[["1655","Christiaan Huygens discovers Titan"],["1675","Giovanni Cassini discovers a gap in Saturn’s rings"],["1997","Cassini–Huygens is launched"],["2004","Cassini arrives at Saturn"],["2005","Huygens lands on Titan"],["2017","Cassini ends its mission by plunging into Saturn"]],
s2:[["Named for astronomers","The orbiter is named after Giovanni Domenico Cassini, an Italian-French astronomer who discovered four of Saturn’s moons and the large gap in its rings now called the Cassini Division. The probe is named after the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens, who discovered Titan in 1655 and first explained that Saturn is surrounded by a ring."],["Lakes and seas on Titan","Cassini’s radar peered through Titan’s haze and found lakes and seas of liquid methane and ethane near its poles, some larger than the Great Lakes of North America. Titan is the only other world known to have stable liquids on its surface, with a cycle of methane rain, rivers and evaporation resembling Earth’s water cycle."],["The Grand Finale","Running low on fuel in 2017, Cassini began its Grand Finale: 22 dives through the narrow gap between Saturn and its innermost rings, a region never explored before. It measured the mass of the rings and sampled particles raining into Saturn. On September 15, 2017, it plunged into Saturn’s atmosphere, sending data until the end. This was done deliberately to avoid any chance of Cassini crashing into and contaminating Enceladus or Titan, which might harbor life."],["The day the Earth smiled","On July 19, 2013, Cassini turned its cameras toward the Sun while Saturn blocked its glare and photographed Earth as a tiny dot beneath Saturn’s rings, nearly 1.5 billion kilometers away. NASA invited people around the world to look up and wave at the same moment, an event called The Day the Earth Smiled."],["Launch controversy","Before its launch in 1997, some groups protested because Cassini carried plutonium for its nuclear power generators, fearing an accident could spread radioactive material. NASA and supporters said the risks were extremely low and that solar power was impractical so far from the Sun. The launch and an Earth flyby in 1999 went safely."],["Legacy","Cassini’s findings are still being analyzed and have reshaped scientists’ understanding of Saturn and its moons. They inspired NASA’s Dragonfly mission, a drone-like rotorcraft planned to explore Titan in the 2030s, and proposals for future missions to Enceladus."]],
sp:["Diving into Saturn","On the morning of September 15, 2017, scientists and engineers gathered at NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California for Cassini’s final moments. The spacecraft, after 20 years in space and 13 at Saturn, was falling toward the giant planet at more than 100,000 kilometers per hour. As it entered the atmosphere, it kept its antenna pointed at Earth, sending data about Saturn’s gases until its thrusters could no longer hold it steady. Because radio signals took about 83 minutes to reach Earth, by the time the signal disappeared, Cassini had already burned up. In the control room, people embraced, some in tears, as one of the most successful planetary missions in history came to a close."],
g:[["Orbiter","A spacecraft that circles a planet"],["Probe","A spacecraft sent to land or descend"],["Cassini Division","A large gap in Saturn’s rings"],["Grand Finale","Cassini’s last series of dives"]],
f:["Huygens landed on Titan in 2005.","Cassini orbited Saturn for 13 years.","Cassini ended its mission by diving into Saturn."],
fun:"Cassini photographed Earth from Saturn as a tiny pale dot beneath the rings."});

