// Animal Kingdom: seals, lemurs, hedgehogs, bison and the platypus.
ADDART({id:"seals",t:"Seals, Sea Lions and Walruses",c:"Mammals",i:"🦭",w:"Pinniped",r:["whale","otters","penguin","arctic","antarc"],
p:[["Flipper-footed","Seals, sea lions and walruses are called pinnipeds, meaning fin-footed."],["Deep divers","Elephant seals can dive more than 2,000 meters."],["Back from the brink","Some seal species recovered after near extinction."]],
l:"Pinnipeds are a group of marine mammals that includes true seals, eared seals such as sea lions and fur seals, and the walrus. Their name means fin-footed, because their legs have evolved into flippers. There are about 33 living species, found mostly in cold and temperate waters, from the Arctic and Antarctic to the coasts of California, South Africa and New Zealand. Pinnipeds spend most of their lives in the sea but come ashore or onto ice to rest, breed and give birth. They are excellent swimmers and divers, insulated by thick layers of blubber. Many species were hunted heavily for their fur, oil and ivory, and some were nearly wiped out, though protection has allowed several to recover.",
s:[["True seals","True seals, such as harbor seals and elephant seals, have no external ear flaps. On land they move clumsily on their bellies, but in water they swim powerfully by sweeping their hind flippers side to side. Most live in cold waters, though monk seals live in warm seas."],["Eared seals","Sea lions and fur seals have small external ears and can turn their hind flippers forward to walk on land. They swim mainly with their large front flippers. Sea lions are often trained in aquariums because they are intelligent and playful."],["The walrus","The walrus lives in the Arctic and is the only living species of its family. Both males and females have long tusks, which they use to haul themselves onto ice, defend themselves and display. Walruses feed mainly on clams, which they find by sensing the seafloor with their stiff whiskers and suck out of their shells."],["Diving champions","Pinnipeds slow their heart rates and send blood mainly to the brain and heart when they dive. Elephant seals can dive more than 2,000 meters deep and stay underwater for over an hour, sometimes nearly two. Weddell seals of Antarctica hunt under thick sea ice, keeping breathing holes open with their teeth."]],
k:[["Group","Pinnipeds"],["Living species","About 33"],["Main families","True seals, eared seals, walruses"],["Deepest diver","Elephant seals"],["Only freshwater seal","Baikal seal"]],
d:[["c. 23 million years ago","Early pinniped relatives live in the Arctic"],["1911","An international treaty limits the hunting of northern fur seals"],["1972","The United States passes the Marine Mammal Protection Act"],["1983","Europe bans imports of baby seal pelts"],["2008","The Caribbean monk seal is declared extinct"],["2009","The European Union bans most trade in seal products"]],
s2:[["Hunting and controversy","For centuries, seals were hunted for fur, meat, oil and skins. Commercial seal hunting, especially of young harp seals in Canada, has been one of the most heated debates in animal protection. Animal welfare groups argue that the hunt is cruel and unnecessary, while many Canadian Inuit and coastal communities defend it as a traditional, sustainable source of food and income. The European Union banned most seal products in 2009 but made exceptions for Inuit hunts."],["Elephant seals’ comeback","Northern elephant seals were hunted so intensively for their oil in the 1800s that by the 1890s only a tiny group survived on an island off Mexico. After protection by Mexico and the United States, their numbers grew to well over 100,000. Because they descend from so few survivors, they have very little genetic variety."],["Seals and climate change","Many Arctic seals depend on sea ice to rest, give birth and raise their pups. As warming shrinks sea ice, ringed seals, harp seals and walruses face greater challenges. Walruses have increasingly crowded onto beaches in huge groups when ice is unavailable, where stampedes can kill young animals."],["Monk seals","Monk seals live in warm waters. The Caribbean monk seal, last seen in the 1950s, was declared extinct in 2008, the first seal known to have been lost to human causes. The Hawaiian and Mediterranean monk seals are endangered and are protected through beach monitoring and fishing rules."],["Life on land and sea","Most pinnipeds return to the same beaches or ice floes each year to breed. Male elephant seals and sea lions fight for territories and groups of females. Pups are often fed rich, fatty milk and grow quickly; hooded seal pups are weaned in only about four days, one of the shortest nursing periods of any mammal."],["Seals near Asia","No seals live naturally in the Philippines, but several species live elsewhere in Asian waters, such as spotted seals in the Yellow Sea and the Sea of Japan, and the Baikal seal of Lake Baikal in Siberia, the only seal that lives only in fresh water."]],
sp:["The walrus that sank boats","In the summer of 2022, a young female walrus nicknamed Freya wandered far from the Arctic, appearing in the harbors of Norway, Denmark, the Netherlands and Britain. In Oslo, she climbed onto small boats to rest, sometimes sinking them under her weight of several hundred kilograms. Crowds gathered to see her, and some came dangerously close. Norwegian authorities warned people to keep their distance, but eventually decided she posed a risk to public safety and put her down. The decision caused outrage around the world and debate about how to handle wild animals that wander into cities."],
g:[["Pinniped","A seal, sea lion or walrus"],["Blubber","A thick layer of fat under the skin"],["Haul out","When a seal comes onto land or ice"],["Flipper","A flat limb used for swimming"]],
f:["Elephant seals can dive over 2,000 meters.","Walruses use their tusks to climb onto ice.","The Baikal seal lives in fresh water."],
fun:"Hooded seal pups are fed by their mothers for only about four days."});

ADDART({id:"lemurs",t:"Lemurs",c:"Mammals",i:"🐒",w:"Lemur",r:["madag","monkeys","tarsier","gorilla","conservation"],
p:[["Only in Madagascar","Lemurs live naturally only on the island of Madagascar."],["Female leaders","In many lemur groups, females are in charge."],["Highly threatened","Lemurs are among the most endangered mammals on Earth."]],
l:"Lemurs are primates found naturally only on Madagascar and a few nearby islands off the coast of Africa. They belong to the same order as monkeys, apes and humans, but to a separate branch that also includes lorises. There are around 100 species, ranging from mouse lemurs weighing about 30 grams, the smallest primates in the world, to the indri, which can weigh over 7 kilograms. Lemurs evolved in isolation for tens of millions of years after their ancestors reached Madagascar, producing a remarkable variety of forms and behaviors. Many lemurs live in groups led by females. Because of forest loss, hunting and the pet trade, the vast majority of lemur species are threatened with extinction, making them a top conservation priority.",
s:[["How they reached Madagascar","Scientists think the ancestors of lemurs reached Madagascar from mainland Africa perhaps 50 to 60 million years ago, possibly by drifting on rafts of floating vegetation. With few competitors, they spread into many habitats and evolved into dozens of different species."],["Ring-tailed lemurs","Ring-tailed lemurs, with their black-and-white striped tails, are the best-known lemurs. They live in groups and spend more time on the ground than most lemurs. They sunbathe in the morning, sitting upright with arms spread, and males hold stink fights, waving tails coated in scent at rivals."],["The aye-aye","The aye-aye is one of the strangest primates. It taps on tree bark with a long, thin middle finger, listening for hollow tunnels made by insect larvae, then gnaws a hole and fishes them out. Some people in Madagascar traditionally considered it a bad omen, and it was sometimes killed on sight."],["The indri","The indri is the largest living lemur and has only a stubby tail. Groups of indri sing loud, haunting songs that can be heard several kilometers through the forest, marking their territories. Indri do not survive in captivity, so their survival depends entirely on protecting their forests."]],
k:[["Found in","Madagascar"],["Species","About 100"],["Smallest","Madame Berthe’s mouse lemur"],["Largest living","Indri"],["Main threat","Forest loss"]],
d:[["c. 55 million years ago","Ancestors of lemurs may reach Madagascar"],["c. 2,000 years ago","Humans settle Madagascar"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the genus Lemur"],["1966","The Duke Lemur Center begins in the United States"],["2000","Madame Berthe’s mouse lemur is described"],["2020","Experts report that most lemur species are threatened"]],
s2:[["Giant lemurs of the past","Until about 1,000 to 2,000 years ago, Madagascar was home to giant lemurs, some as large as gorillas. Archaeoindris may have weighed around 160 kilograms. These giants disappeared after humans arrived, probably because of hunting and changes to the landscape, though climate change may also have played a role."],["Females in charge","In many lemur species, females are dominant over males. Females often get first choice of food and can chase males away. Scientists think this may help females meet the high energy demands of pregnancy and nursing in Madagascar’s unpredictable environment, where food can be scarce."],["Why they are endangered","More than 90 percent of Madagascar’s original forests have been lost or degraded, largely because of slash-and-burn farming, logging and charcoal production driven by poverty. Lemurs are also hunted for food and captured as pets. Conservationists stress that protecting lemurs must also improve the lives of local people."],["Conservation efforts","Protected areas such as Ranomafana and Andasibe-Mantadia national parks shelter many lemur species and attract ecotourists, providing jobs as guides and in lodges. Community forest management, tree planting and breeding programs in zoos also help. However, political instability and limited funding have made conservation difficult."],["Lemurs and tarsiers","Lemurs belong to the group of primates called strepsirrhines, along with lorises and galagos, which have moist noses like dogs. The Philippine tarsier, though small and big-eyed like many lemurs, belongs to the other group, haplorhines, which also includes monkeys, apes and humans."],["Name meaning","The name lemur comes from the Latin lemures, meaning ghosts or spirits of the dead. Carl Linnaeus chose it, possibly because of the animals’ nocturnal habits, glowing eyes and quiet movements in the night forest."]],
sp:["The singing forest","Before dawn in the rainforest of Andasibe in eastern Madagascar, a strange sound begins: a long, wailing cry that rises and falls like a siren or a whale song. It is a family of indri, the largest lemurs, singing together from the treetops. Other indri groups reply from across the valley. The songs help families stake out their territories and stay in contact. In Malagasy legend, the indri is related to humans, and in some communities it is taboo, or fady, to harm one. Visitors who come to hear the indri also bring income to local guides, giving people another reason to protect the forest."],
g:[["Lemur","A primate found only in Madagascar"],["Primate","The group of mammals that includes lemurs, monkeys, apes and humans"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"],["Fady","A Malagasy taboo"]],
f:["Lemurs live naturally only in Madagascar.","Mouse lemurs are the smallest primates.","Many lemur groups are led by females."],
fun:"The aye-aye finds food by tapping on wood and listening for hollow tunnels."});

ADDART({id:"hedgehogs",t:"Hedgehogs",c:"Mammals",i:"🦔",w:"Hedgehog",r:["rodents","insect","animalbehavior","phbio","mindanao"],
p:[["Spiny coats","Hedgehogs are covered in thousands of sharp spines."],["Rolling up","When threatened, they curl into a prickly ball."],["A Philippine relative","The Mindanao gymnure is a hedgehog relative."]],
l:"Hedgehogs are small, spiny mammals found in Europe, Asia and Africa, and introduced to New Zealand. There are about 17 species. Their backs are covered in thousands of stiff, hollow spines, which are modified hairs. When threatened, a hedgehog rolls into a tight ball, protecting its soft face and belly. Hedgehogs are mainly nocturnal and eat insects, worms, snails and other small animals, which makes them welcome in gardens. In cold climates, they hibernate through the winter. Despite their appearance, hedgehogs are not related to porcupines, which are rodents. Their closest relatives are the gymnures, or moonrats, of Southeast Asia, including the Mindanao gymnure found only in the Philippines. Some hedgehog species are kept as pets.",
s:[["Spines","An adult European hedgehog has around 5,000 to 7,000 spines. They are not barbed and do not detach easily, unlike porcupine quills. Each spine is hollow with internal ridges, making it light but strong. Hedgehogs shed and regrow spines gradually."],["Rolling into a ball","A strong band of muscle running along a hedgehog’s back lets it pull its spiny skin over its head, legs and tail like a drawstring bag. Few predators can get past this defense, though badgers, owls and foxes sometimes manage."],["Diet","Hedgehogs eat beetles, caterpillars, earthworms, slugs, snails and occasionally eggs, frogs and small snakes. They find food mainly by smell and hearing, snuffling and grunting as they forage, which is how they got their name: they root around like hogs in hedges."],["Hibernation","In cold places, hedgehogs build nests of leaves and hibernate from autumn to spring. Their body temperature drops and their heart slows dramatically, allowing them to survive months without eating on stored fat. Disturbing a hibernating hedgehog can be dangerous for it."]],
k:[["Species","About 17"],["Spines on adult","About 5,000–7,000"],["Activity","Mostly nocturnal"],["Closest relatives","Gymnures, or moonrats"],["Philippine relative","Mindanao gymnure"]],
d:[["c. 50 million years ago","Early hedgehog relatives live in North America"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the European hedgehog"],["1905","Beatrix Potter publishes The Tale of Mrs. Tiggy-Winkle"],["1990s","African pygmy hedgehogs become popular pets"],["2020","Hedgehogs are listed as vulnerable on Britain’s mammal red list"],["2024","The European hedgehog is listed as near threatened worldwide"]],
s2:[["The Mindanao gymnure","The Mindanao gymnure, Podogymnura truei, is a small, soft-furred relative of hedgehogs that lives only in the mountain forests of Mindanao, such as on Mount Apo and Mount Kitanglad. It has no spines and looks somewhat like a shrew. Very little is known about its life, and it depends on the survival of the island’s highland forests."],["Self-anointing","Hedgehogs sometimes chew strong-smelling substances and then lick frothy saliva onto their spines, a strange behavior called self-anointing. Scientists are not sure why. Theories include that it helps disguise their scent, adds a defensive coating or is simply a reaction to new smells."],["Decline in Europe","Hedgehog numbers in Britain and parts of Europe have fallen sharply in recent decades. Causes include loss of hedgerows and grassland, pesticides that reduce their insect food, roads and garden fences that block their movements. Many people now cut small holes in fences to create hedgehog highways between gardens."],["Hedgehogs as pets","The African pygmy hedgehog is a popular pet in many countries, including in the Philippines. Pet hedgehogs need warmth, a proper diet and space to exercise, and they can carry germs such as salmonella. Some places restrict keeping them. In the Philippines, importing and selling exotic animals requires permits from wildlife authorities."],["Venom resistance","European hedgehogs are partly resistant to the venom of adders, a European snake, and sometimes kill and eat them. Scientists have found that a protein in hedgehog blood helps neutralize some snake toxins, though a bite can still harm them."],["Hedgehogs in stories","Hedgehogs appear in many folk tales and children’s books, such as Beatrix Potter’s Mrs. Tiggy-Winkle and stories by the Brothers Grimm. In European folklore, people once falsely believed hedgehogs stole milk from cows and collected fruit on their spines."]],
sp:["Hedgehog highways","In Britain, where hedgehogs have declined dramatically, ordinary people have joined a nationwide effort to help them. Because hedgehogs travel up to two kilometers a night searching for food and mates, solid garden fences and walls can trap them in small areas. A campaign called Hedgehog Street encourages neighbors to cut small holes, about 13 centimeters wide, at the bottom of fences to link their gardens. Volunteers have registered many thousands of these hedgehog highways on an online map. Some new housing developments now include them by design. It is a simple example of how small actions by many people can help wildlife."],
g:[["Spine","A stiff, sharp modified hair"],["Hibernation","A deep sleep through winter"],["Nocturnal","Active at night"],["Gymnure","A soft-furred relative of hedgehogs"]],
f:["Hedgehogs are not related to porcupines.","They roll into a ball for protection.","The Mindanao gymnure is a hedgehog relative."],
fun:"Hedgehogs got their name because they snuffle and grunt like hogs while searching hedges for food."});

ADDART({id:"bison",t:"Bison",c:"Mammals",i:"🦬",w:"Bison",r:["carabao","grasslands","conservation","tamaraw","wolf"],
p:[["Giants of the plains","Bison are the largest land animals in North America and Europe."],["Nearly wiped out","Hunting reduced American bison from millions to about a thousand."],["Saved and returned","Conservation brought both American and European bison back."]],
l:"Bison are large, shaggy wild cattle with humped shoulders. There are two living species: the American bison of North America, often called buffalo, and the European bison, or wisent. They are the heaviest land animals in North America and Europe, with males weighing up to about 1,000 kilograms. Bison graze on grasses and once roamed in enormous herds across the Great Plains, shaping grassland ecosystems and the lives of many Native American peoples. In the 1800s, mass hunting reduced American bison from tens of millions to only about a thousand. European bison were wiped out in the wild by the 1920s. Conservation efforts saved both species, and bison have since been reintroduced to parks and reserves. They are relatives of cattle, carabao and the tamaraw.",
s:[["American bison","American bison have a massive head, a large shoulder hump of muscle and thick, woolly fur on the front of their bodies that protects them in harsh winters. They use their heads to sweep snow aside to reach grass. Despite their size, they can run at around 55 kilometers per hour and jump fences."],["European bison","The European bison is slightly taller and longer-legged than its American cousin and lives in forests as well as meadows. It is Europe’s heaviest land animal. After being wiped out in the wild, it was rebuilt from just a few animals kept in zoos and parks, and today free-roaming herds live in Poland, Belarus and other countries."],["Shaping the land","Grazing bison keep grasses short, while their wallowing in dust and mud creates depressions that hold water for plants and animals. Their dung fertilizes the soil, and the grasses they trample help seeds spread. Prairie dogs, birds and insects all benefit from bison herds."],["Herds and behavior","Bison live in herds of females and young, while adult males often travel alone or in small groups except during the breeding season. In summer, males fight by charging and butting heads to win mates. Bison can be dangerous, and visitors to parks such as Yellowstone are warned to stay well back."]],
k:[["Species","American bison and European bison"],["Weight of large males","Up to about 1,000 kg"],["Top speed","About 55 km/h"],["American bison low point","About 1,000 in 1889"],["Relatives","Cattle, carabao, tamaraw"]],
d:[["c. 10,000 years ago","Larger ancient bison species die out in North America"],["1870s","Mass commercial hunting of American bison peaks"],["1889","Only about 1,000 American bison are estimated to survive"],["1905","The American Bison Society is founded"],["1927","The last wild European bison is killed"],["1952","European bison are released into the wild in Poland"]],
s2:[["Bison and Native Americans","For many Plains Indian nations, such as the Lakota, Cheyenne and Comanche, bison provided food, clothing, shelter, tools and spiritual meaning. Hunters used nearly every part of the animal. The loss of the herds in the late 1800s devastated these peoples’ ways of life and made them more dependent on the United States government."],["The great slaughter","Between about 1870 and 1885, commercial hunters killed millions of bison for their hides, which were made into leather for industry, and many carcasses were left to rot. Railroads made shipping hides easier. Some US Army officers and politicians welcomed the destruction as a way to force Plains nations onto reservations. Historians debate how much this was official policy, but its effects on Native peoples are widely recognized."],["Saving the bison","A few ranchers, Native Americans, conservationists and zoos protected small herds. The American Bison Society, founded in 1905, worked to establish protected herds, and in 1907 the Bronx Zoo sent bison to a reserve in Oklahoma. Yellowstone National Park is the only place where bison have lived continuously since prehistoric times. In 2016 the bison was named the national mammal of the United States."],["Bison today","Today there are several hundred thousand American bison, though most are raised commercially on ranches for meat. Only a smaller number live in conservation herds that roam more freely. Many Native American tribes manage their own herds, restoring bison to their lands, through groups such as the InterTribal Buffalo Council."],["European bison’s rescue","The last wild European bison in Poland’s Białowieża Forest was killed in 1919 and the last in the Caucasus in 1927. Scientists and zoos traced the few remaining captive animals and began a careful breeding program. In 1952 bison were returned to Białowieża Forest. Their numbers have grown to several thousand, though all descend from about a dozen founders."],["Bison and the carabao","Bison belong to the same family as cattle, goats and antelopes, and to the same tribe as cattle, yaks and Asian water buffalo. The carabao of the Philippines and the tamaraw of Mindoro are their more distant relatives. Like bison, the tamaraw was once far more widespread before hunting and habitat loss reduced its numbers."]],
sp:["The bison of Yellowstone","By 1902, only about two dozen wild bison remained in Yellowstone National Park, hiding in remote valleys and threatened by poachers. Park managers brought in bison from private herds and protected the survivors, slowly rebuilding the population. Today Yellowstone’s bison, numbering several thousand, are among the few genetically pure wild bison left. They are also the subject of disputes. Some bison carry a disease called brucellosis, and ranchers in nearby Montana fear it could spread to cattle, so bison that leave the park have sometimes been captured or culled. Conservationists and tribes argue that bison should be allowed to roam more widely, and the debate continues."],
g:[["Bison","A large wild cattle with a shoulder hump"],["Wisent","The European bison"],["Herd","A group of grazing animals"],["Wallow","A shallow hole where animals roll in dust or mud"]],
f:["Bison are the largest land animals in North America.","European bison were saved from a few zoo animals.","The bison is the US national mammal."],
fun:"Despite weighing up to a metric ton, a bison can run about as fast as a horse over short distances."});

ADDART({id:"platypus",t:"The Platypus",c:"Mammals",i:"🦆",w:"Platypus",r:["kangaroo","evo","australiacountry","otters","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Egg-laying mammal","The platypus is a mammal that lays eggs."],["Duck-billed","It has a bill like a duck that senses electricity."],["Venomous","Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their back legs."]],
l:"The platypus is a semi-aquatic mammal found only in eastern Australia, including Tasmania. With a duck-like bill, a beaver-like tail, webbed feet and thick fur, it looks so unusual that when the first specimen reached Britain in 1798, some scientists thought it was a hoax. The platypus is one of the monotremes, the only mammals that lay eggs, along with the echidnas. Mothers feed their young milk that seeps through patches of skin, since they have no nipples. The platypus hunts underwater with its eyes, ears and nostrils closed, finding prey by sensing the tiny electric fields produced by their muscles. Males have venomous spurs on their hind legs. The platypus is protected in Australia but is threatened by drought, dams and habitat loss.",
s:[["Monotremes","Monotremes are an ancient branch of mammals that split from other mammals more than 160 million years ago. Only five species survive: the platypus and four species of echidnas, found in Australia and New Guinea. They lay leathery eggs but have fur and produce milk like other mammals."],["The electric bill","The platypus’s soft, rubbery bill is covered with tens of thousands of special receptors that detect electric fields and movements in water. As it swims, it sweeps its head from side to side, locating shrimp, insect larvae and worms hidden in mud, even with its eyes shut."],["Venomous spurs","Males have a sharp spur on each hind ankle connected to a venom gland. The venom is most active in the breeding season, suggesting males use it against rivals. It is not deadly to humans but causes intense, long-lasting pain that ordinary painkillers barely relieve."],["Burrows and eggs","Females dig long burrows in riverbanks, where they lay one to three small eggs and curl around them to keep them warm. The babies, called puggles by some, hatch after about ten days and lap milk from their mother’s skin. They leave the burrow after several months."]],
k:[["Found in","Eastern Australia"],["Group","Monotremes"],["Reproduction","Lays eggs"],["Special sense","Electroreception"],["Defense","Venomous spurs in males"]],
d:[["1798","A platypus specimen is sent to Britain"],["1799","George Shaw describes the platypus scientifically"],["1884","William Caldwell confirms that platypuses lay eggs"],["2008","The platypus genome is first published"],["2016","The platypus is listed as near threatened"],["2020","Scientists report that platypus fur glows under ultraviolet light"]],
s2:[["A suspected hoax","When the British zoologist George Shaw examined the first platypus skin in 1799, he wrote that it was impossible not to suspect some deception. At the time, taxidermists sometimes stitched together parts of different animals to sell as curiosities. Shaw searched for stitches around the bill but found none, and he concluded the animal was real."],["Proving the eggs","For decades, European scientists argued about whether platypuses laid eggs, as Aboriginal Australians reported. In 1884 the Scottish scientist William Caldwell, working in Queensland with Aboriginal guides, found a female with an egg. He sent a famous short telegram to a scientific meeting in Canada announcing that monotremes lay eggs."],["A mosaic of features","The platypus genome, published in 2008, showed a mix of genes found in mammals, birds and reptiles. For example, its venom genes resemble those of reptiles, though they evolved separately. Its unusual features help scientists understand how mammals evolved from ancient ancestors."],["No stomach","The platypus has no true stomach; its food passes from the esophagus straight to the intestine. It stores food in cheek pouches while hunting and grinds it with hard pads, since adults have no teeth. Platypuses eat a large share of their body weight every day."],["Threats","Platypuses need healthy rivers and creeks. Dams, drought, bushfires, land clearing and fishing nets and traps that drown them have caused declines. During the 2019 to 2020 bushfires and droughts, scientists warned that platypus populations were shrinking, and some have called for stronger protection."],["Aboriginal knowledge","Aboriginal Australians have known the platypus for thousands of years and tell stories about it. In one widely shared Dreamtime story, the platypus is the child of a duck and a water rat, explaining its mixture of features. Aboriginal knowledge helped European scientists learn about its behavior."]],
sp:["The telegram from Australia","In 1884 the young Scottish scientist William Caldwell traveled to Queensland determined to settle the question of how platypuses and echidnas reproduce. With the help of Aboriginal hunters, he examined many animals. Finally, he found a female platypus that had laid one egg and had another inside her body. Caldwell sent a brief telegram to the British Association for the Advancement of Science, meeting in Montreal, Canada. It read, in essence, that monotremes were egg-laying and that their eggs were like those of reptiles and birds. The message was read aloud to the meeting and caused a sensation, ending nearly a century of debate."],
g:[["Monotreme","A mammal that lays eggs"],["Electroreception","The ability to sense electric fields"],["Spur","A sharp spike on the leg"],["Burrow","A tunnel dug as a home"]],
f:["The platypus lays eggs.","Its bill senses electricity.","Male platypuses are venomous."],
fun:"Under ultraviolet light, platypus fur glows a blue-green color."});

;
