// Animal Kingdom: foxes, deer, the Philippine mouse-deer, cloud rats and the Visayan warty pig.
ADDART({id:"foxes",t:"Foxes",c:"Mammals",i:"🦊",w:"Fox",r:["wolf","dogs","arctic","animalbehavior","conservation"],
p:[["Clever hunters","Foxes are small members of the dog family."],["Found almost everywhere","The red fox lives on more continents than any other wild carnivore."],["From desert to Arctic","Fennec foxes live in deserts and Arctic foxes on the ice."]],
l:"Foxes are small to medium-sized members of the dog family, Canidae, with pointed ears, narrow snouts and bushy tails. The best-known are the true foxes of the genus Vulpes, which include the red fox, the Arctic fox and the tiny fennec fox of the Sahara. The red fox is the most widespread wild carnivore on Earth, living across North America, Europe, Asia and North Africa, and introduced to Australia. Foxes are mostly solitary hunters that eat rodents, rabbits, birds, insects, fruit and scraps, and many have adapted well to farmland and cities. They are known for their cunning in folklore around the world. Foxes are not native to the Philippines, but appear in many stories and fables familiar to Filipino readers.",
s:[["The red fox","Red foxes are adaptable animals with reddish-orange fur, white chests and black legs, though some are silver or dark. They hunt mice by listening carefully, then leaping high and pouncing down on prey hidden under grass or snow. Studies suggest they may use Earth’s magnetic field to aim these pounces."],["The Arctic fox","Arctic foxes survive temperatures far below freezing with thick fur, short ears and furry feet. Many turn white in winter and brown or gray in summer. They follow polar bears to eat leftovers and store food in summer for the long winter."],["The fennec fox","The fennec fox of the Sahara is the smallest fox, weighing around one kilogram, with enormous ears up to about 15 centimeters long. Its ears help it hear insects and small animals underground and release body heat in the desert. Its furry feet protect it from hot sand."],["Family life","Most foxes live alone or in pairs for much of the year but raise their young, called kits or cubs, together in dens. Both parents bring food to the young. Foxes communicate with barks, screams and scent marks, and their eerie nighttime screams are often mistaken for human cries."]],
k:[["Family","Canidae, the dog family"],["Most widespread species","Red fox"],["Smallest species","Fennec fox"],["Young","Kits or cubs"],["Native to the Philippines","No"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the red fox"],["1850s","Red foxes are introduced to Australia for hunting"],["1959","Dmitry Belyaev begins breeding tame foxes in the Soviet Union"],["2002","Hunting foxes with dogs is banned in Scotland"],["2004","Hunting with dogs is banned in England and Wales"],["2019","Scientists report an Arctic fox that walked about 3,500 km from Norway to Canada"]],
s2:[["Foxes in Australia","Red foxes were introduced to Australia in the 1800s so that settlers could hunt them for sport. They spread across much of the continent and, along with feral cats, have contributed to the extinction or decline of many small native mammals and birds. Australian authorities use baiting, fencing and other methods to control foxes and protect wildlife."],["The fox experiment","In 1959 the Soviet scientist Dmitry Belyaev began an experiment in Siberia, breeding silver foxes and selecting only the tamest each generation. After a few decades, many foxes wagged their tails, sought human attention and developed floppy ears and spotted coats. The experiment helped scientists understand how domestication may have changed wolves into dogs."],["Fox hunting debate","Hunting foxes with packs of hounds was a long tradition in Britain. Supporters argued that it controlled foxes that kill farm animals and preserved rural culture and jobs. Opponents called it cruel and unnecessary. After long debate, Scotland banned it in 2002 and England and Wales in 2004, though trail hunting, following a laid scent, continues and remains controversial."],["City foxes","Red foxes have moved into many cities, including London, Tokyo and Toronto, where they eat rubbish, pet food and rodents and raise cubs under sheds and in parks. Some people enjoy seeing them, while others consider them pests. City foxes tend to be bolder than their rural relatives."],["The clever fox in stories","Foxes appear as tricksters in folklore worldwide, from Aesop’s fable of the fox and the grapes to the medieval European tales of Reynard the Fox. In Japan and China, fox spirits called kitsune and huli jing can shape-shift into people. In Filipino schools, Aesop’s fox fables are commonly read, while Philippine folk tales give the trickster role to animals such as the monkey and the mouse-deer."],["The long-distance fox","In 2018, a young female Arctic fox fitted with a tracking collar left the Svalbard islands of Norway and walked across sea ice to Greenland and then to Canada, covering about 3,500 kilometers in 76 days. Scientists were astonished by the journey, which showed how sea ice connects Arctic animal populations and why its loss matters."]],
sp:["Foxes that wagged their tails","When Dmitry Belyaev started his experiment in 1959, his foxes came from fur farms and were fearful or aggressive toward people. Each year, his team tested the young foxes and allowed only the calmest, friendliest ones to breed. Within about ten generations, some foxes began wagging their tails and whimpering for attention like puppies. Over later generations, many developed curly tails, floppy ears and patches of white fur, features also seen in many domesticated animals. The experiment continued for decades after Belyaev’s death in 1985 and became one of the most famous studies of how domestication works."],
g:[["Canid","A member of the dog family"],["Kit","A young fox"],["Den","A shelter where foxes raise young"],["Domestication","Taming and breeding wild animals to live with people"]],
f:["The red fox is the most widespread wild carnivore.","The fennec fox is the smallest fox.","Foxes are not native to the Philippines."],
fun:"Red foxes may use Earth’s magnetic field to aim their pounces on hidden mice."});

ADDART({id:"deer",t:"Deer",c:"Mammals",i:"🦌",w:"Deer",r:["phbio","palawan","conservation","horses","tamaraw"],
p:[["Antlered animals","Most male deer grow antlers that they shed every year."],["Moose to pudú","Deer range from giant moose to tiny pudús."],["Philippine deer","The Philippines has three native deer species."]],
l:"Deer are hoofed mammals of the family Cervidae, found in forests, grasslands, mountains and tundra on every continent except Antarctica and Australia, where some have been introduced. There are about 50 species, from the moose, which can stand over 2 meters tall at the shoulder, to the pudú of South America, barely 40 centimeters tall. Most male deer grow bony antlers each year, shedding them after the breeding season. Deer are plant-eaters that browse on leaves, twigs and grasses. They have been hunted by people for food, hides and antlers since prehistoric times, and reindeer were domesticated. The Philippines has three native species: the Philippine brown deer, the endangered Visayan spotted deer and the Calamian deer of Palawan’s Calamian Islands.",
s:[["Antlers","Antlers are made of bone and grow from the skull each year, covered at first in a soft, furry skin called velvet that supplies blood. Antlers are among the fastest-growing tissues of any animal. When fully grown, the velvet dries and is rubbed off, and males use their antlers to fight rivals. They fall off after the breeding season and regrow the next year."],["Reindeer","Reindeer, called caribou in North America, live in the Arctic and are the only deer in which both males and females grow antlers. They migrate long distances in huge herds. Indigenous peoples such as the Sámi of northern Europe have herded reindeer for centuries, using them for meat, milk, hides and transport."],["Moose","The moose is the largest deer, with males weighing up to about 700 kilograms and carrying broad, flat antlers. Moose live in northern forests of North America, Europe and Asia, where they are called elk in Europe. They are good swimmers and feed on water plants as well as trees."],["Life as prey","Deer are prey for wolves, tigers, leopards, pythons and other predators. Their eyes on the sides of the head give a wide field of view, and their large ears catch faint sounds. Many species raise their white tails as an alarm signal when they flee."]],
k:[["Family","Cervidae"],["Species","About 50"],["Largest","Moose"],["Smallest","Pudú"],["Philippine species","Philippine brown deer, Visayan spotted deer, Calamian deer"]],
d:[["c. 15,000 years ago","Cave paintings in Europe depict deer"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the red deer"],["1967","Chronic wasting disease is first recognized in deer in Colorado"],["1976","The Calauit Island wildlife sanctuary is created in Palawan"],["1990s","A conservation breeding program for the Visayan spotted deer begins"],["2000s","Visayan spotted deer survive mainly on Negros and Panay"]],
s2:[["The Visayan spotted deer","The Visayan spotted deer, known locally as usa, is a small deer with dark brown fur covered in cream-colored spots. It once lived on several Visayan islands but now survives mainly in forests of Negros and Panay, and it is endangered because of hunting and forest loss. Conservation centers in Negros and Panay, working with local universities and foreign zoos, breed the deer and educate communities."],["The Philippine brown deer","The Philippine brown deer, also called the Philippine sambar, lives on Luzon, Mindanao, Mindoro, Samar, Leyte and other islands. It is the most widespread of the country’s deer but has declined because of hunting for meat, which is still sold illegally in some areas. It is listed as vulnerable."],["The Calamian deer","The Calamian deer is found only on a few islands of the Calamian group in northern Palawan, such as Busuanga, Culion and Calauit. It is endangered. Calauit Island, set aside as a wildlife sanctuary in 1976, also became famous for African giraffes and zebras brought there. The sanctuary’s creation required moving residents off the island, and some later returned and claimed rights to the land, making it a subject of long dispute."],["Deer in culture","Deer appear in art and stories worldwide, from prehistoric cave paintings to the story of Bambi. In Nara, Japan, hundreds of sika deer roam freely and bow to visitors who feed them special crackers. In Christianity, the deer is a symbol of the soul longing for God, and in Buddhism, the Buddha gave his first sermon in a deer park."],["Too many deer?","In parts of North America and Europe where wolves and other predators have disappeared, deer numbers have risen sharply. Large herds can eat young trees and plants faster than forests can regrow and cause many traffic accidents. Managers use regulated hunting, fencing and, in some places, the return of predators to control their numbers, though each approach has supporters and critics."],["Antlers and medicine","Deer antler velvet is used in traditional Chinese and Korean medicine, and is sold as a supplement elsewhere. Supporters claim it improves strength and health, but scientific evidence for most of these benefits is weak. Some deer farms in Asia and New Zealand raise deer for velvet."]],
sp:["The spotted deer of Negros","In the 1980s, biologists searching the forests of Negros found that the Visayan spotted deer had become very rare, hunted for meat and losing its habitat as forests were cleared for sugarcane and farms. In the 1990s, a conservation partnership involving the Philippine government, local groups, Silliman University in Dumaguete and zoos in Europe began breeding the deer in captivity. Some deer born in these programs have been sent to zoos abroad to form a backup population, while others are kept in breeding centers in Negros and Panay. Rangers and educators also work with upland communities, explaining that the usa is found nowhere else on Earth."],
g:[["Antler","A bony growth on a deer’s head, shed yearly"],["Velvet","Soft skin covering growing antlers"],["Browse","To feed on leaves and twigs"],["Usa","The Filipino word for deer"]],
f:["Most male deer shed their antlers every year.","Reindeer females also grow antlers.","The Visayan spotted deer is endangered."],
fun:"Deer antlers can grow over two centimeters in a single day."});

ADDART({id:"mousedeer",t:"The Philippine Mouse-Deer",c:"Mammals",i:"🦌",w:"Philippine mouse-deer",r:["deer","palawan","phbio","phfolk","conservation"],
p:[["Tiny hoofed animal","The pilandok is one of the smallest hoofed mammals."],["Not a true deer","Mouse-deer belong to an ancient family called chevrotains."],["Found only in the Philippines","It lives only on Balabac and nearby islands."]],
l:"The Philippine mouse-deer, known locally as pilandok, is a tiny hoofed mammal found only on Balabac and a few nearby islands off the southern tip of Palawan. Standing only about 40 centimeters tall, it is one of the smallest hoofed animals in the world. Despite its name, it is not a true deer or a mouse but a chevrotain, a member of an ancient family of hoofed mammals that has changed little over millions of years. Mouse-deer have no antlers; instead, males have small, tusk-like upper canine teeth. The pilandok is shy and active mostly at dawn, dusk and night. It is endangered because of hunting, the pet trade and loss of forest, and is protected under Philippine law.",
s:[["Chevrotains","Chevrotains are a family of small hoofed mammals found in the forests of Southeast Asia, India and Africa. They are among the most primitive living ruminants, the group of plant-eaters that chew the cud, which also includes deer, cattle and giraffes. Fossils show that chevrotain-like animals lived more than 30 million years ago."],["Appearance","The pilandok has a rounded body, very thin legs and small hooves. Its fur is dark brown with white stripes on its throat. It weighs only a few kilograms. Males have fang-like canine teeth, which they use in fights with rivals."],["Habitat and food","The Philippine mouse-deer lives in lowland forests and mangroves on Balabac, Bugsuk and Ramos islands. It eats fallen fruits, leaves, shoots and flowers on the forest floor, and is a shy animal that hides in dense undergrowth."],["Threats","Hunting for meat, capture for the pet trade, and clearing of forests for farms and development have reduced its numbers. Its very small range makes it especially vulnerable. It is listed as endangered, and capturing, selling or keeping one without a permit is illegal under the Philippine Wildlife Act."]],
k:[["Local name","Pilandok"],["Scientific name","Tragulus nigricans"],["Height","About 40 cm"],["Range","Balabac and nearby islands, Palawan"],["Status","Endangered"]],
d:[["c. 30 million years ago","Chevrotain-like animals live in Asia and Europe"],["1892","The Philippine mouse-deer is described for science"],["2001","The Philippine Wildlife Act protects threatened species"],["2008","The Philippine mouse-deer is listed as endangered"],["2010s","Rescue centers in Palawan care for confiscated mouse-deer"],["2019","A related mouse-deer species is rediscovered in Vietnam"]],
s2:[["Pilandok the trickster","In Philippine folklore, especially among the Maranao and other peoples of Mindanao and Palawan, Pilandok is a clever trickster who outwits kings, crocodiles and powerful enemies with quick thinking. In some stories Pilandok is a mouse-deer, and in others a clever man. Similar tales of the cunning mouse-deer, called Sang Kancil, are told in Malaysia and Indonesia."],["The crocodile bridge","In a famous Southeast Asian tale, the mouse-deer wants to cross a river full of crocodiles. He tells them the king has ordered him to count them all, so the crocodiles line up across the river. The mouse-deer hops across their backs, counting, and leaps safely onto the far bank, laughing. Versions of this story are told in the Philippines, Malaysia and Indonesia."],["A rediscovered relative","In 2019 scientists announced that camera traps in Vietnam had photographed the silver-backed chevrotain, a mouse-deer species that had not been recorded by scientists for nearly 30 years. The rediscovery gave hope for other little-known chevrotains, including the Philippine mouse-deer."],["Protecting the pilandok","Conservation groups, Palawan authorities and local communities work to stop illegal hunting and pet trade and to protect remaining forests on Balabac. Some confiscated mouse-deer are cared for at rescue centers in Puerto Princesa. Expanding protected areas and developing alternative livelihoods for local people are seen as key to its survival."],["Development pressures","Parts of Balabac and the surrounding islands have been proposed for tourism resorts, plantations and other development. Supporters argue these could bring jobs and income to one of Palawan’s poorer areas, while conservationists warn that clearing forests and mangroves could push the pilandok and other endemic species closer to extinction."],["Tiny hooves, big leaps","Although small and delicate-looking, mouse-deer are quick and agile, darting through undergrowth and leaping away from danger. When alarmed, some chevrotains drum their hooves rapidly on the ground. Mouse-deer can also swim and sometimes escape into water."]],
sp:["Counting the crocodiles","Long ago, the story goes, Pilandok the mouse-deer wanted to reach sweet fruits growing across a wide river. The river was full of hungry crocodiles. Pilandok called out that the sultan had ordered a feast and needed to know how many crocodiles lived in the river, so he had been sent to count them. Proud and greedy, the crocodiles lined up from one bank to the other. Pilandok jumped from back to back, counting aloud, one, two, three, until he reached the far shore. Then he thanked them and skipped away, leaving the crocodiles grumbling. Children across the Philippines and its neighbors still enjoy this tale of brains defeating brawn."],
g:[["Pilandok","The Filipino name for the Philippine mouse-deer"],["Chevrotain","A small, ancient type of hoofed mammal"],["Ruminant","A plant-eater that chews the cud"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"]],
f:["The pilandok is not a true deer.","It lives only on Balabac and nearby islands.","Male mouse-deer have tusk-like teeth instead of antlers."],
fun:"An adult Philippine mouse-deer is about as tall as a house cat."});

ADDART({id:"cloudrats",t:"Cloud Rats",c:"Mammals",i:"🐀",w:"Cloud rat",r:["rodents","phbio","banaue","mindanao","conservation"],
p:[["Giant tree rats","Cloud rats are large, furry rodents found only in the Philippines."],["Bushy tails","Some cloud rats have long, fluffy tails."],["A Philippine treasure","Luzon has one of the richest collections of unique mammals on Earth."]],
l:"Cloud rats are large, furry rodents that live only in the Philippines, mostly in the mountain forests of Luzon. The name refers to their home in high, misty cloud forests. They include the giant cloud rats of the genus Phloeomys, which can weigh around two kilograms and are among the largest rats in the world, and the bushy-tailed cloud rats of the genus Crateromys, found on Luzon, Panay, Dinagat and Ilin Island. Cloud rats are mostly nocturnal plant-eaters that climb trees and nest in hollows. They are part of a remarkable group of mammals that evolved only on Philippine islands. Several species are threatened by hunting and forest loss, and one may already be extinct.",
s:[["Giant cloud rats","The northern and southern giant cloud rats of Luzon have long, shaggy fur, often a mix of white, brown and black, small ears and long tails. They live in forests and even forest edges and plantations. They are hunted for meat in some upland areas, and are occasionally kept in zoos and parks."],["Bushy-tailed cloud rats","Bushy-tailed cloud rats have thick, fluffy tails that may help them balance in trees. The Luzon bushy-tailed cloud rat lives in the Cordillera mountains. The Panay cloud rat was described in 1996, the Dinagat bushy-tailed cloud rat was known for decades from a single specimen until it was photographed alive in 2012, and the Ilin Island cloud rat has not been seen since the 1950s."],["Life in the trees","Cloud rats climb with strong claws, feeding on leaves, buds, fruits and bark at night. They spend the day in tree hollows or dense vegetation. Unlike the common rats found in houses and rice fields, which were brought by people, cloud rats are native forest animals that do not invade homes."],["Island evolution","Luzon has been separated from mainland Asia for millions of years. Ancestors of cloud rats arrived long ago and evolved into many new forms. Separate mountain ranges on Luzon, like islands within an island, each developed their own unique rodent species."]],
k:[["Found in","The Philippines only"],["Largest","Giant cloud rats, about 2 kg"],["Main island","Luzon"],["Activity","Mostly nocturnal"],["Food","Leaves, fruits, bark"]],
d:[["1890s","Scientists describe the giant cloud rats of Luzon"],["1953","The only known Ilin Island cloud rat specimen is collected"],["1981","The Dinagat bushy-tailed cloud rat is described"],["1996","The Panay cloud rat is described"],["2012","The Dinagat bushy-tailed cloud rat is photographed alive"],["2016","Scientists report dozens of mammal species found only on Luzon"]],
s2:[["Luzon’s mammal treasures","A long-term survey of Luzon’s mountains led by researchers from the Field Museum in Chicago and the National Museum of the Philippines, including the late Filipino biologist Danilo Balete, found that the vast majority of the island’s native non-flying mammals live nowhere else. Many were new to science, including several species of shrew-rats and forest mice. Scientists have called Luzon one of the richest places on Earth for unique mammals."],["Rediscovery on Dinagat","The Dinagat bushy-tailed cloud rat was first described from a specimen collected in 1975. For decades no scientist saw another, and some feared it was extinct. In 2012 researchers working with local people photographed a living individual on Dinagat Island. The island’s forests are threatened by mining, which some communities depend on for income while others oppose it because of its environmental effects."],["Possibly extinct","The Ilin Island cloud rat is known only from a single specimen bought from local hunters in 1953 on Ilin Island, near Mindoro. Little forest remains on the island, and no further individuals have been found. It may already be extinct, a reminder of how quickly small-island species can disappear."],["Cloud rats and people","In some upland communities, cloud rats are traditionally hunted for food, and they sometimes eat crops near forests. Conservationists work with communities to reduce hunting of rare species and protect forests, noting that large cloud rats reproduce slowly, often having only one young at a time."],["Seeing them","Giant cloud rats are sometimes kept at wildlife centers in the Philippines, where visitors can see them. In the wild, they are hard to observe because they are active at night high in trees. Some birdwatchers and wildlife photographers in Luzon’s mountains have spotted them at night with flashlights."],["Not house rats","The rats commonly seen in Philippine cities and farms are introduced species, such as the black rat and the brown rat, which spread around the world with human trade. They can damage crops and spread disease. Cloud rats and most other native Philippine rodents are shy forest animals that rarely encounter people."]],
sp:["A rat photographed at last","For almost four decades, the Dinagat bushy-tailed cloud rat existed for science only as a single preserved specimen in a museum drawer. Biologists who searched Dinagat’s forests could not find it, and the species was feared lost. In 2012 a team of Filipino and foreign researchers, guided by local people who knew the forest, set up camera traps and searched at night. Finally they found and photographed a living cloud rat, with dark fur and a long, bushy tail, clinging to a tree. The rediscovery showed that the species survived and highlighted the importance of protecting Dinagat’s remaining forests."],
g:[["Cloud rat","A large tree-dwelling rodent of the Philippines"],["Cloud forest","A misty mountain forest"],["Rodent","A mammal with ever-growing front teeth"],["Specimen","A preserved animal kept for study"]],
f:["Cloud rats live only in the Philippines.","Giant cloud rats can weigh about 2 kg.","The Dinagat cloud rat was photographed alive in 2012."],
fun:"A giant cloud rat can weigh about as much as a small house cat."});

ADDART({id:"wartypig",t:"The Visayan Warty Pig",c:"Mammals",i:"🐗",w:"Visayan warty pig",r:["phbio","deer","conservation","deforestation","domestication"],
p:[["A rare wild pig","The Visayan warty pig lives only on Negros and Panay."],["Punk hairstyle","Males grow a spiky mane in the breeding season."],["Critically endangered","It is one of the most threatened pigs in the world."]],
l:"The Visayan warty pig, Sus cebifrons, is a small wild pig found only in the central Philippines. It once lived on Cebu, Negros, Panay, Masbate, Guimaras and other Visayan islands, but today survives mainly in the remaining forests of Negros and Panay, and it is listed as critically endangered. Males have three pairs of fleshy warts on their faces, which may protect them during fights, and in the breeding season they grow a long, spiky mane of hair on their heads and backs, giving them a striking appearance. The pig has declined because of forest loss, hunting and interbreeding with domestic pigs. Conservation breeding programs in the Philippines and in zoos abroad aim to save it. The Philippines has several other native wild pig species.",
s:[["Appearance","Visayan warty pigs are smaller than domestic pigs, with dark gray to black bristly fur. Adult males have three pairs of warts on the face and grow a mane of long hair, sometimes pale, that stands up like a crest when they are excited. Females are smaller and lack the long mane."],["Life in the forest","These pigs live in small family groups in forests and grasslands. They root in the soil for tubers, roots, fallen fruits and earthworms, helping turn over soil and spread seeds. They are mostly active in the early morning and evening."],["Disappearing from islands","Forests on Cebu, Guimaras and other Visayan islands were cleared long ago for farms and towns, and the warty pig vanished from many of them. On Negros and Panay, it survives in mountains such as the Central Panay Mountain Range and Mount Kanlaon, though remaining forests are small and scattered."],["Interbreeding","Where wild and domestic pigs meet, they can breed, producing hybrids. This mixing threatens the genetic identity of the Visayan warty pig. Wild pigs that raid crops are also sometimes killed by farmers."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Sus cebifrons"],["Range today","Negros and Panay"],["Status","Critically endangered"],["Male feature","Three pairs of facial warts"],["Main threats","Forest loss, hunting, interbreeding"]],
d:[["1888","The Visayan warty pig is described for science"],["1990s","Conservation breeding begins in the Philippines and Europe"],["1996","The species is listed as critically endangered"],["2000s","Breeding centers operate in Negros and Panay"],["2019","African swine fever reaches the Philippines, raising concern for wild pigs"],["2020s","Field surveys continue in Panay and Negros mountains"]],
s2:[["Philippine wild pigs","The Philippines has several native wild pigs. The Philippine warty pig lives on Luzon, Mindanao and other islands, and the Mindoro warty pig on Mindoro. The Palawan bearded pig lives on Palawan and nearby islands. All are hunted for meat, and some are threatened by forest loss."],["Saving the species","In the 1990s, Philippine conservation groups, local governments and European zoos began a breeding program for the Visayan warty pig. Pigs are bred at centers in Negros, such as in Bacolod, and in zoos abroad, creating backup populations. Researchers also survey wild populations and work with communities to protect forests."],["African swine fever","African swine fever, a deadly viral disease of pigs, reached the Philippines in 2019, devastating many backyard and commercial pig farms. It does not harm humans, but it can kill wild pigs. Conservationists worry it could spread to the small, rare populations of native wild pigs and have urged careful biosecurity."],["Hunting and culture","Wild pigs have long been hunted in the Philippines, and wild boar meat is valued in some communities. Hunting of threatened species like the Visayan warty pig is illegal under the Wildlife Act, but enforcement in remote mountains is difficult. Conservation groups emphasize education and alternative livelihoods for hunters."],["Forest gardeners","By rooting in the soil and eating fallen fruits, wild pigs mix the soil, bury seeds and help forests regenerate. Losing them can change how forests grow. Scientists consider native wild pigs an important part of healthy Philippine forest ecosystems."],["A pig with a hairstyle","The male warty pig’s breeding-season mane, which can be long and spiky like a punk hairstyle, has made it popular with zoo visitors abroad. Zoos use this attention to teach visitors about Philippine wildlife and to raise funds for conservation in the Visayas."]],
sp:["Searching the Panay mountains","High in the rugged Central Panay Mountain Range, a team of Filipino biologists and forest rangers hikes for days through steep, misty forest. They are looking for signs of the Visayan warty pig: patches of soil turned over by rooting, tracks in the mud and wallows where pigs have rolled. Camera traps strapped to trees capture photos of the pigs at dawn, revealing family groups with striped piglets. These surveys show that the species still survives in the wild, though in small numbers. The team shares their findings with local communities and officials, hoping to strengthen protection of one of the last strongholds of this unique Philippine animal."],
g:[["Warty pig","A wild pig with fleshy warts on its face"],["Hybrid","The offspring of two different species or types"],["Wallow","A muddy hollow where pigs roll"],["Critically endangered","At extremely high risk of extinction"]],
f:["The Visayan warty pig lives only in the Philippines.","Males grow a spiky mane in the breeding season.","It survives mainly on Negros and Panay."],
fun:"Male Visayan warty pigs grow a dramatic mane during the breeding season, like a punk hairstyle."});

