// Animal Kingdom: the Philippine cockatoo, swiftlets, kingfishers, pythons and cobras.
ADDART({id:"cockatoo",t:"The Philippine Cockatoo",c:"Birds",i:"🦜",w:"Red-vented cockatoo",r:["parrot","palawan","phbio","conservation","birds"],
p:[["The katala","The Philippine cockatoo is known locally as katala."],["Red underneath","It has bright red feathers under its tail."],["Fighting back","Community conservation has helped it recover on Rasa Island."]],
l:"The Philippine cockatoo, also called the red-vented cockatoo and known locally as katala, is a white cockatoo found only in the Philippines. It is named for the red feathers under its tail, and has a pale yellow crest it raises when excited. Once common across much of the country, it has become critically endangered, mainly because nestlings were taken for the pet trade and its lowland forests and mangroves were cleared. Today it survives mostly in Palawan, including Rasa Island and Dumaran, and in the Sulu archipelago, especially Tawi-Tawi. Conservation efforts involving local communities, former poachers turned wardens and protected areas have helped its numbers grow on some islands, making it a hopeful example for Philippine wildlife.",
s:[["Appearance","The katala is a medium-sized cockatoo, about 30 centimeters long, with white feathers, a short crest tinged yellow, and red and yellow under its tail. Like other cockatoos, it has a strong, curved beak for cracking seeds and nuts."],["Habitat and food","Philippine cockatoos live in lowland forests, mangroves and coastal areas. They feed on fruits, seeds, flowers and buds, flying between islands and the mainland to find food. They nest in hollows of large trees, often near the coast."],["Why it declined","For decades, poachers climbed nesting trees to steal chicks for sale as pets, since cockatoos are intelligent, can mimic speech and fetch high prices. At the same time, lowland forests and mangroves were cut for farms, fishponds and coconut plantations, removing the large trees cockatoos need."],["Cockatoos of the world","Cockatoos are a family of about 21 species of parrots, found mainly in Australia, New Guinea, Indonesia and the Philippines. They are known for their crests, loud calls and long lives; some cockatoos in captivity have lived more than 80 years. The Philippine cockatoo is the only cockatoo native to the Philippines."]],
k:[["Local name","Katala"],["Scientific name","Cacatua haematuropygia"],["Status","Critically endangered"],["Strongholds","Palawan and Tawi-Tawi"],["Main past threat","Nest poaching for pets"]],
d:[["1776","The Philippine cockatoo is described for science"],["1990s","Surveys show the species has disappeared from much of its range"],["1998","A conservation program begins on Rasa Island, Palawan"],["2006","Rasa Island is declared a wildlife sanctuary"],["2010s","Cockatoo numbers rise in protected areas of Palawan"],["2020s","Conservationists work to restore populations in other areas"]],
s2:[["Poachers turned protectors","One of the most successful conservation ideas for the katala was hiring former poachers as wardens. They knew exactly where cockatoos nested and how to climb the trees. Instead of stealing chicks, they now guard nests during the breeding season and are paid for their work. This approach, begun by the Katala Foundation in Palawan, has been praised as a model of community-based conservation."],["Rasa Island","Rasa Island, a small coral island off the town of Narra in southern Palawan, became the most important stronghold of the Philippine cockatoo. After protection began in 1998, the island’s cockatoo population grew several times over. In 2006 it was declared a wildlife sanctuary, and visitors can sometimes see flocks flying between the island and the mainland at dawn and dusk."],["Tawi-Tawi","The Sulu archipelago, especially Tawi-Tawi, still has some cockatoos, but forests there have been heavily cleared and security conditions have made fieldwork difficult. Local groups and the government have worked to survey and protect remaining birds."],["Pets and the law","Keeping, selling or transporting Philippine cockatoos without a permit is illegal under the Philippine Wildlife Act. Conservationists urge people not to buy wild-caught parrots. Some confiscated birds are cared for at rescue centers and may be used for breeding programs."],["A flagship species","The katala has become a flagship species for Palawan conservation, appearing on posters, festivals and school programs. Protecting its nesting trees and mangroves also helps many other species, including fish nurseries along the coast. Conservationists say the cockatoo’s recovery shows that extinction is not inevitable."],["Clever parrots","Cockatoos are highly intelligent. In studies abroad, cockatoos have learned to pick complex locks, use tools and even dance to music. In Australia, wild sulphur-crested cockatoos have learned from each other how to open household rubbish bins."]],
sp:["Dawn on Rasa Island","Just after sunrise, a loud, harsh screeching rises from the mangroves of Rasa Island. Dozens of white birds lift off together and fly across the channel toward mainland Palawan, their red undertail feathers flashing in the light. They are Philippine cockatoos, heading out to feed on fruits and seeds for the day. Watching them, a warden who once climbed these same trees to steal chicks now counts them carefully and records the numbers in a notebook. In the evening, the flock returns to roost on the island. Scenes like this were rare in the 1990s, when the katala seemed close to vanishing; today they offer hope."],
g:[["Cockatoo","A crested parrot"],["Poaching","Illegally taking wild animals"],["Warden","A person who guards wildlife"],["Flagship species","A species used to rally support for conservation"]],
f:["The katala is found only in the Philippines.","It has red feathers under its tail.","Former poachers now guard its nests."],
fun:"Some cockatoos can live for more than 80 years in captivity."});

ADDART({id:"swiftlets",t:"Swiftlets and Edible Nests",c:"Birds",i:"🐦",w:"Edible bird's nest",r:["birds","palawan","phfood","bat","hummingbird","echolocation"],
p:[["Nests of saliva","Some swiftlets build nests made almost entirely of their own saliva."],["A costly delicacy","These nests are used to make bird’s nest soup."],["El Nido","El Nido in Palawan is named after swiftlet nests."]],
l:"Swiftlets are small birds of the swift family that live in tropical Asia and the Pacific, including the Philippines. Some species, especially the edible-nest swiftlet, build cup-shaped nests on cave walls made almost entirely of their own hardened saliva. These nests are harvested to make bird’s nest soup, a delicacy in Chinese cuisine for centuries and one of the most expensive animal products in the world. Many swiftlets can navigate in the dark caves where they nest using echolocation, producing clicking sounds and listening for echoes, an ability shared with only a few birds. The town of El Nido in Palawan takes its name from the Spanish word for nest, referring to the swiftlet nests collected from its limestone cliffs.",
s:[["Saliva nests","During the breeding season, edible-nest swiftlets produce large amounts of sticky saliva from glands under their tongues. They lay it down in strands on cave walls, and it hardens into a white, cup-shaped nest. Building one can take about a month or more. Other swiftlets mix their saliva with feathers or plant material, making black or mixed nests of lower value."],["Echolocation","Swiftlets that roost deep in dark caves produce rapid clicking calls and listen to the echoes to avoid walls and find their nests, much like bats. Unlike bats, they use clicks we can hear and do not use echolocation to catch food; they hunt flying insects outside in daylight."],["Master fliers","Swiftlets and swifts spend most of their lives in the air, catching insects on the wing. Their legs are tiny, and they cannot perch on branches like most birds; instead, they cling to vertical surfaces. Some swifts stay airborne for months without landing."],["Bird’s nest soup","Edible bird’s nests are cleaned of feathers, then soaked and cooked into a soft, gelatinous soup, often sweetened. In Chinese tradition, they are believed to benefit health and skin. Scientists have found that the nests contain proteins and other nutrients, but many of the health claims have not been confirmed by strong evidence."]],
k:[["Bird family","Swifts"],["Famous species","Edible-nest swiftlet"],["Nest material","Hardened saliva"],["Navigation in caves","Echolocation"],["Philippine place name","El Nido, Palawan"]],
d:[["1300s–1600s","Bird’s nest soup becomes a luxury food in China"],["1500s","Chinese traders buy swiftlet nests from Southeast Asian islands"],["1990s","Swiftlet houses spread in Malaysia and Indonesia"],["2000s","Palawan regulates the collection of nests in El Nido"],["2016","Scientists report swifts staying airborne for ten months"],["2020s","Southeast Asia supplies most edible nests to China"]],
s2:[["Collectors of El Nido","In the limestone caves of El Nido and other parts of Palawan, licensed collectors climb tall bamboo poles and ladders to reach nests on cave ceilings and walls, a dangerous job passed down in families. Harvesting is allowed only at certain times so that swiftlets can raise their chicks. Local government regulations aim to keep harvesting sustainable."],["Swiftlet houses","In Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand and Vietnam, many people now farm swiftlets in specially built concrete buildings called swiftlet houses. Loudspeakers play swiftlet calls to attract birds, which nest inside, and owners harvest the nests. The industry has grown enormously, though neighbors sometimes complain about the noise, and its spread has raised questions about effects on wild populations."],["Overharvesting","Because the nests are so valuable, some caves have been overharvested, with nests taken before eggs hatched. In places where this happened, swiftlet populations declined sharply. Conservationists support rules that allow harvesting only after young birds have left the nests, and protecting some caves from harvesting entirely."],["Fake nests","The high price of edible nests has led to fraud, including nests bleached, glued or mixed with other materials to look whiter and weigh more. Authorities in several countries have tested nests for additives and banned unsafe products."],["Swifts versus swallows","Swifts and swallows look similar and both catch insects in flight, but they are not closely related. Swifts are closer relatives of hummingbirds. Their similar shapes are an example of convergent evolution, when unrelated animals evolve similar features for a similar way of life."],["Birds of El Nido","El Nido, now one of the Philippines’ most popular tourist destinations, was named for the swiftlet nests by Spanish-era settlers. Its towering limestone cliffs and islands provide nesting sites for swiftlets as well as habitat for other birds and marine life. Tourism and environmental protection have become central issues for the town."]],
sp:["Climbing for nests","In the dim light of a sea cave in northern Palawan, a nest collector checks his bamboo pole, lashed together with rattan, and begins to climb. Far above, near the cave ceiling, rows of small white nests cling to the rock. With a long-handled scraper and a flashlight, he carefully pries the nests loose, dropping them into a basket at his waist. Swiftlets dart past his head, clicking as they navigate in the darkness. The work is dangerous, and a fall can be fatal. The nests he collects will be cleaned, sorted and sold, many eventually ending up in expensive soups in Hong Kong, Taipei or mainland China."],
g:[["Swiftlet","A small bird of the swift family"],["Echolocation","Finding the way using echoes"],["Edible nest","A nest made of saliva used in soup"],["Convergent evolution","Unrelated animals evolving similar features"]],
f:["Edible nests are made of swiftlet saliva.","Swiftlets can echolocate in caves.","El Nido is named after swiftlet nests."],
fun:"A kilogram of the best white edible nests can cost thousands of dollars."});

ADDART({id:"kingfishers",t:"Kingfishers",c:"Birds",i:"🐦",w:"Kingfisher",r:["birds","fishes","wetlands","phbio","eagle"],
p:[["Diving hunters","Many kingfishers plunge into water to catch fish."],["Jewel colors","Kingfishers are known for bright blue, orange and green feathers."],["Philippine kingfishers","The Philippines has many kingfisher species, some found nowhere else."]],
l:"Kingfishers are a family of brightly colored birds with large heads, long, sharp bills and short legs, found on every continent except Antarctica. There are over 100 species, from the tiny African dwarf kingfisher to the laughing kookaburra of Australia. Many kingfishers hunt fish by perching above water and diving in headfirst, but many others, especially in forests, catch insects, lizards and frogs on land. Kingfishers usually nest in burrows dug into riverbanks or termite mounds, or in tree holes. The Philippines is a hotspot for kingfishers, with numerous species, including several found only in the country, such as the spotted wood kingfisher and the Philippine dwarf kingfisher. The kingfisher’s beak inspired the design of a Japanese bullet train.",
s:[["Diving for fish","A fishing kingfisher perches still above water, judging the depth and position of a fish while adjusting for the way water bends light. It then dives, closing a protective membrane over its eyes, grabs the fish in its bill and flies back to its perch. It often beats the fish against a branch before swallowing it headfirst."],["Forest kingfishers","Many kingfishers rarely fish at all. Forest species, such as the Philippine spotted wood kingfisher, wait quietly in the shade and pounce on insects, worms and small lizards on the ground. The kookaburra of Australia even eats snakes."],["Colors from structure","The brilliant blue of many kingfishers does not come from blue pigment. Instead, tiny structures in their feathers scatter light to produce blue, a phenomenon called structural color. This is why a kingfisher’s color can seem to change with the angle of light."],["Burrow nests","Most kingfishers dig nesting tunnels by repeatedly flying at a bank and striking it with their bills, then scraping out a tunnel with their feet. Some tunnels are a meter long, ending in a chamber where eggs are laid. Others nest in tree cavities or in termite nests."]],
k:[["Family","Alcedinidae"],["Species","Over 100"],["Largest","Laughing kookaburra and giant kingfisher"],["Nest","Burrows or tree holes"],["Philippine endemic example","Spotted wood kingfisher"]],
d:[["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the common kingfisher"],["1800s","Kingfisher feathers are used in Chinese jewelry"],["1997","A Japanese bullet train with a kingfisher-inspired nose enters service"],["2000s","DNA studies reveal new kingfisher species in the Philippines"],["2010s","Birdwatching tourism grows in Philippine forests"],["2020s","Conservationists warn of threats to forest kingfishers from logging"]],
s2:[["Kingfishers of the Philippines","The Philippines has a large variety of kingfishers. Common species include the collared kingfisher, often seen in mangroves and coastal areas, and the white-throated kingfisher of open country. Forest endemics include the spotted wood kingfisher of Luzon and Mindanao, the Philippine dwarf kingfisher and other rare island species. DNA studies have shown that some birds once thought to be one species on different islands are actually separate species."],["The bullet train","In the 1990s, Japanese engineers had a problem: when their fast Shinkansen trains exited tunnels, they produced loud booms that disturbed residents. Engineer Eiji Nakatsu, a birdwatcher, noticed that kingfishers dive into water with barely a splash thanks to their long, tapered beaks. A new train nose modeled on the kingfisher’s beak, introduced in 1997, was quieter, faster and used less electricity, a famous example of biomimicry."],["Kingfisher feathers in art","In imperial China, artisans glued iridescent kingfisher feathers onto metal to make jewelry, headdresses and decorations, a technique called tian-tsui. The craft required huge numbers of birds and was eventually abandoned in the 20th century. Today such antiques are prized in museums."],["Kingfishers in myth","In Greek mythology, Alcyone was turned into a kingfisher, and the gods calmed the seas so she could nest; this gave rise to the expression halcyon days, meaning calm, happy times. In some Pacific and Southeast Asian traditions, kingfishers are omens or messengers."],["Threats","Forest kingfishers depend on intact forests and are threatened by logging and land clearing in the Philippines and elsewhere. River kingfishers suffer from water pollution, which reduces fish, and from riverbank changes that destroy nest sites. Healthy kingfisher populations are often a sign of clean waterways."],["Birdwatching","Kingfishers are favorites of birdwatchers and photographers because of their colors and dramatic dives. Birdwatching tours in places such as Mount Kitanglad, Palawan and the Subic forests attract visitors hoping to see rare Philippine kingfishers, providing income for local guides."]],
sp:["The train that copied a bird","In the late 1980s and 1990s, Japan’s high-speed trains had grown so fast that they created a problem. As a train rushed into a narrow tunnel, it pushed a wave of air ahead of it, which burst out of the other end with a boom that could be heard hundreds of meters away. Eiji Nakatsu, an engineer and member of a wild bird society, wondered how a kingfisher could plunge from air into water without a splash. He tested train noses of different shapes and found that one closely resembling a kingfisher’s beak greatly reduced the tunnel boom. The 500 Series Shinkansen, introduced in 1997, used this design and became a celebrated example of engineers learning from nature."],
g:[["Kingfisher","A bird with a large head and sharp bill"],["Structural color","Color produced by tiny structures rather than pigment"],["Biomimicry","Designing technology inspired by nature"],["Halcyon","A word linked to the kingfisher, meaning calm"]],
f:["Not all kingfishers eat fish.","A kingfisher’s beak inspired a bullet train.","Kingfisher blue comes from feather structure."],
fun:"The laughing kookaburra of Australia, famous for its loud call, is a kingfisher."});

ADDART({id:"pythons",t:"Pythons",c:"Reptiles",i:"🐍",w:"Pythonidae",r:["snake","lizard","croc","phbio","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Giant snakes","Pythons include some of the longest snakes in the world."],["Squeezing hunters","They kill prey by coiling around it and squeezing."],["A Philippine giant","The reticulated python, or sawa, lives in the Philippines."]],
l:"Pythons are a family of large, non-venomous snakes found in Africa, Asia and Australia. There are about 40 species, ranging from small pythons under a meter long to the reticulated python of Southeast Asia, the longest snake in the world, which can exceed 6 meters. Pythons are constrictors: they seize prey with backward-pointing teeth, wrap their coils around it and squeeze, stopping blood flow and killing it quickly. They can swallow animals much wider than their heads, including pigs, deer and occasionally even people, though attacks on humans are rare. Many pythons can sense the body heat of prey using special pits along their lips. The reticulated python, called sawa in the Philippines, is found across the country.",
s:[["How constriction works","When a python strikes, it grips its prey with many sharp, curved teeth, then rapidly wraps coils around the animal. Each time the prey breathes out, the python tightens. Studies in 2015 showed that constriction kills mainly by stopping blood circulation, which happens within minutes."],["Swallowing huge meals","Python jaws are connected by stretchy ligaments, and the two halves of the lower jaw can spread apart. This allows a python to swallow prey much wider than its head. After a big meal, a python may not eat again for weeks or months, and its heart and other organs temporarily grow to help digest."],["Heat-sensing pits","Many pythons have rows of pits along their lips that detect infrared radiation, or heat, from warm-blooded animals. This lets them sense prey in complete darkness. Combined with smell from their flicking tongues, it makes them effective night hunters."],["Mother pythons","Most snakes leave their eggs after laying them, but female pythons coil around their eggs. Some species shiver their muscles to raise the temperature of the eggs, a rare example of a reptile producing heat. They stay with the eggs until they hatch."]],
k:[["Family","Pythonidae"],["Species","About 40"],["Longest species","Reticulated python"],["Venomous","No"],["Filipino name","Sawa"]],
d:[["1801","The reticulated python is described for science"],["1979","A Burmese python is first recorded in Florida’s Everglades"],["2000","Scientists confirm Burmese pythons are breeding in the Everglades"],["2012","The United States bans imports of Burmese pythons"],["2015","Scientists show how constriction kills prey"],["2023","A record-length Burmese python is captured in Florida"]],
s2:[["The sawa","The reticulated python, or sawa, lives in forests, grasslands, farms and even towns across the Philippines and Southeast Asia. Its intricate net-like pattern gives it its name, from the Latin for net. Sawa sometimes enter homes and farms to catch chickens, dogs and rats, and they are often captured and relocated by rescue teams. Reticulated pythons are excellent swimmers and have colonized many islands."],["Pythons and people","Most pythons avoid people, but very large reticulated pythons have occasionally killed and swallowed humans, with a few documented cases in Indonesia in recent years. A study among the Agta people of Luzon, hunter-gatherers who lived in forests, found in the 1970s and 1980s that a significant number had survived python attacks. Such events remain rare."],["Invasion of the Everglades","Burmese pythons, released or escaped from the pet trade, have established a large population in Florida’s Everglades. Studies have linked them to steep declines of raccoons, opossums, rabbits and other mammals there. Florida pays hunters to remove pythons and holds python-catching challenges, but eradicating them has proved extremely difficult."],["The skin trade","Python skins are used to make luxury handbags, shoes and belts, and large numbers are exported from Southeast Asia. Some trade is legal and regulated, while some is illegal or involves cruelty. Fashion companies and conservation groups debate whether the trade can be made sustainable or should be reduced."],["Pythons as pets","Ball pythons and other small pythons are popular pets because they are calm and easy to handle. Experts warn that large species such as reticulated and Burmese pythons can become dangerous and difficult to care for, and that releasing pets into the wild can harm ecosystems, as in Florida."],["Pythons and boas","Pythons are often confused with boas, another family of constricting snakes. Boas mostly live in the Americas and usually give birth to live young, while pythons live in the Old World and lay eggs. The green anaconda of South America, a boa, is the heaviest snake, while the reticulated python is the longest."]],
sp:["The Everglades python hunters","Every summer, people from across the United States travel to the swamps of southern Florida for a contest unlike any other: the Florida Python Challenge. Armed with flashlights, snake hooks and determination, they search levees and roads at night for invasive Burmese pythons. Some catch none; others catch dozens. The pythons are humanely killed, measured and studied. In 2023, hunters and scientists reported a Burmese python measuring about 5.8 meters, a record for the state. Despite the efforts of hunters, trained dogs and researchers, tens of thousands of pythons may still be hiding in the vast wetlands, showing how hard it is to remove an invasive species once it has spread."],
g:[["Constrictor","A snake that kills by squeezing"],["Heat pit","An organ that senses body heat"],["Invasive species","A non-native species that spreads harmfully"],["Sawa","The Filipino word for python"]],
f:["The reticulated python is the longest snake.","Pythons are not venomous.","Female pythons guard their eggs."],
fun:"After a very large meal, a python’s heart can grow noticeably bigger for a while."});

ADDART({id:"cobras",t:"Cobras",c:"Reptiles",i:"🐍",w:"Cobra",r:["snake","seasnakes","firstaid","phbio","egypt"],
p:[["Spreading hoods","Cobras spread a hood when threatened."],["Spitting venom","Some cobras, including the Philippine cobra, spit venom at attackers’ eyes."],["King of snakes","The king cobra is the longest venomous snake."]],
l:"Cobras are venomous snakes known for spreading their necks into a hood when threatened. Most belong to the genus Naja and live in Africa and Asia, including the Philippines. Cobras have short, fixed front fangs and powerful venom that attacks the nervous system and can cause paralysis and death if untreated. Several species, including the Philippine cobra, are spitting cobras that can spray venom into the eyes of a threat several meters away. The king cobra, which belongs to a separate genus, is the longest venomous snake in the world and feeds mostly on other snakes; in 2024 scientists proposed that it is actually four species, including one in Luzon. Cobras play important roles in controlling rodents and are prominent in myths and religions.",
s:[["The hood","When threatened, a cobra raises the front of its body and spreads loose skin over long, movable ribs in its neck, forming a hood. This makes it look larger and warns enemies to keep away. Some cobras have markings on the back of the hood, such as the spectacle pattern of the Indian cobra."],["Venom","Cobra venom mainly contains neurotoxins, which block signals between nerves and muscles, causing drooping eyelids, difficulty swallowing and breathing, and eventually paralysis. Some venoms also destroy tissue around the bite. Antivenom, made from antibodies, can neutralize venom if given quickly."],["Spitting cobras","Spitting cobras have fangs with small openings facing forward. By squeezing their venom glands, they spray venom in a stream toward the eyes of an attacker, sometimes two meters or more. Venom in the eyes causes intense pain and can lead to blindness if not washed out quickly with water."],["King cobras","The king cobra can grow longer than 5 meters. It hunts other snakes, including pythons and venomous species. It is the only snake that builds a nest for its eggs, gathering leaves into a mound and guarding it. Its venom yield is large enough to kill an elephant."]],
k:[["Main genus","Naja"],["Hood support","Elongated neck ribs"],["Venom type","Mainly neurotoxic"],["Philippine species","Philippine cobra, Samar cobra, king cobra"],["Treatment","Antivenom"]],
d:[["c. 1300 BC","Cobras appear on the crowns of Egyptian pharaohs"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the Indian cobra"],["1922","The Philippine cobra is described for science"],["1972","India’s Wildlife Protection Act restricts snake charming"],["2017","The World Health Organization lists snakebite as a neglected tropical disease"],["2024","Scientists propose splitting the king cobra into four species"]],
s2:[["The Philippine cobra","The Philippine cobra, Naja philippinensis, lives in northern islands such as Luzon, Mindoro and Masbate. It is a spitting cobra, and its venom is considered one of the most potent of any cobra. The Samar cobra lives in the southern and eastern islands, including Samar, Leyte and Mindanao. Cobra bites are a medical emergency, and victims should be taken immediately to a hospital that has antivenom."],["The Luzon king cobra","King cobras live from India to Southeast Asia, including the Philippines. In 2024 a study of their DNA and body features proposed dividing them into four species. One of these, Ophiophagus salvatana, lives in Luzon. The research suggests that each king cobra population may need its own conservation planning, though some scientists continue to examine the proposal."],["Snakebite","Snakebites kill tens of thousands of people each year, mostly in rural South Asia, Africa and Southeast Asia, and leave many more with disabilities. Farmers working in fields are at high risk. In 2017 the World Health Organization recognized snakebite as a neglected tropical disease and set goals to halve deaths and disabilities by 2030 by improving access to antivenom and treatment."],["First aid for snakebite","Health experts advise keeping a snakebite victim calm and still, immobilizing the bitten limb and getting to a hospital as quickly as possible. Traditional remedies such as cutting the wound, sucking out venom, applying tight tourniquets or using herbs are not recommended and can cause harm. For venom in the eyes, rinse with plenty of clean water."],["Cobras in culture","In ancient Egypt, the rearing cobra, or uraeus, was a symbol of royalty and protection worn on pharaohs’ crowns. In Hinduism, cobras called nagas are revered, and the festival of Nag Panchami honors them. In Buddhism, a cobra king named Mucalinda is said to have sheltered the Buddha from rain. Snake charmers in South Asia performed with cobras, though this is now restricted by animal protection laws."],["Mongooses and cobras","Mongooses are famous for fighting cobras. They are fast and agile, and have changes in their nerve receptors that make them partly resistant to cobra venom. Rudyard Kipling’s story Rikki-Tikki-Tavi, about a mongoose that defends a family from cobras, made this rivalry famous."]],
sp:["Four kings instead of one","For a long time, scientists thought the king cobra was a single species stretching from India to the Philippines. Researchers led by Indian herpetologist P. Gowri Shankar spent more than a decade examining museum specimens and DNA from snakes across Asia. They noticed differences in the bands on the snakes’ bodies, in their scales and in their genes. In 2024 they published a study proposing four species: one in India’s Western Ghats, one in northern South and Southeast Asia, one in the Sunda region, and one in Luzon. The Luzon king cobra was named Ophiophagus salvatana. The study showed that even famous animals can still hold surprises."],
g:[["Hood","The flared neck of a cobra"],["Neurotoxin","A poison that affects nerves"],["Antivenom","A medicine that neutralizes venom"],["Uraeus","The cobra symbol of Egyptian royalty"]],
f:["Some cobras spit venom.","King cobras eat other snakes.","The Philippine cobra is a spitting cobra."],
fun:"The king cobra is the only snake known to build a nest for its eggs."});

