// Animal Kingdom: monitor lizards, squid, tuna, scorpions and wasps.
ADDART({id:"monitorlizards",t:"Monitor Lizards",c:"Reptiles",i:"🦎",w:"Monitor lizard",r:["lizard","komodo","phbio","geckos","croc"],
p:[["Big, smart lizards","Monitor lizards are large, active and intelligent reptiles."],["A common sight","The water monitor, or bayawak, is common in the Philippines."],["Fruit-eating monitors","The Philippines has rare monitors that eat fruit."]],
l:"Monitor lizards are a group of large lizards of the genus Varanus, found in Africa, Asia and Australia. There are around 80 species, ranging from small monitors about 20 centimeters long to the Komodo dragon of Indonesia, the largest living lizard. Monitors have long necks, powerful tails and claws, and forked tongues that they flick constantly to smell their surroundings. Most are active hunters and scavengers, eating insects, crabs, fish, birds, eggs and carrion, and they are considered among the most intelligent reptiles. The Philippines has several monitor species, including the widespread water monitor, known as bayawak, and three remarkable fruit-eating monitors found only in the country, among them the Northern Sierra Madre forest monitor, described in 2010.",
s:[["Forked tongues","Like snakes, monitor lizards flick their long, forked tongues in and out, picking up scent particles and delivering them to a special smell organ in the roof of the mouth. The two tips help them tell which direction a smell comes from, so they can track prey or carrion over long distances."],["Active lifestyle","Monitors have more efficient lungs and hearts than most lizards, allowing them to stay active longer. Many are strong swimmers, climbers or diggers. Water monitors can stay underwater for many minutes and are often seen swimming in rivers, mangroves and even city canals in Southeast Asia."],["Intelligence","In zoos, monitors have learned to recognize their keepers, solve simple puzzles to get food and count small numbers of prey. Some can tell how many snails they have been offered. Their curiosity and problem-solving have surprised scientists who once assumed reptiles were simple creatures."],["Venom glands","Scientists have found venom glands in the mouths of some monitors, including the Komodo dragon. Their saliva may contain substances that interfere with blood clotting. However, how important venom is in their hunting is still debated."]],
k:[["Genus","Varanus"],["Species","About 80"],["Largest","Komodo dragon"],["Filipino name","Bayawak"],["Philippine fruit-eaters","Gray’s monitor, Panay monitor, Sierra Madre monitor"]],
d:[["1845","Gray’s monitor of Luzon is described"],["1912","The Komodo dragon is described for science"],["2001","The fruit-eating Panay monitor is described"],["2010","The Northern Sierra Madre forest monitor is described"],["2010s","Studies reveal several new monitor species in the Philippines"],["2020s","Conservationists work to protect fruit-eating monitors"]],
s2:[["The bayawak","The water monitor and its close Philippine relatives are known as bayawak. They live near rivers, mangroves, farms and towns across the country, feeding on fish, crabs, frogs, birds and chicken eggs, which sometimes makes them unpopular with farmers. In some areas they are hunted for meat and skins, and their meat is eaten in traditional dishes."],["Butaan","Gray’s monitor, called butaan, lives in the forests of southern Luzon and nearby islands. It is unusual because it eats mainly fruit, especially pandanus fruits, as well as snails. It spends much of its time in trees and is rarely seen. Butaan are hunted for food and threatened by forest loss."],["A new giant lizard","In 2010 scientists announced a new species of large monitor lizard from the forests of the Northern Sierra Madre in Luzon, Varanus bitatawa. Growing about 2 meters long, it eats fruit and lives in trees. The Agta people and other local communities had long known and hunted the lizard, but scientists had overlooked it. Its discovery showed how much remains unknown about Philippine wildlife."],["The Panay monitor","The Panay monitor, described in 2001, lives in the remaining forests of Panay. It is dark with a bluish tinge and eats mainly fruit. It is endangered because the island’s forests have been heavily cleared. Its relatives in Luzon suggest that fruit-eating monitors evolved in the Philippines."],["Monitors in Asia","In countries such as Thailand, Malaysia and Indonesia, large water monitors are common in cities, swimming in canals and lounging on riverbanks. In Bangkok’s Lumpini Park, many monitors live among joggers and visitors. They help clean up waste by eating dead animals and leftovers."],["Leather and trade","Monitor lizard skins are used to make leather goods, and large numbers are exported from parts of Asia and Africa, under regulations meant to keep harvests sustainable. Some species are also traded as pets. Conservationists monitor the trade to prevent overexploitation, especially of rare species."]],
sp:["The lizard scientists missed","For years, biologists working in northern Luzon heard local hunters describe a huge, colorful lizard that lived high in the forest trees and ate fruit. In 2009 researchers obtained a specimen from Agta hunters and, after comparing it with other monitors and studying its DNA, realized it was a new species. Announced in 2010, Varanus bitatawa was nearly two meters long, with black and gold patterns and a bright blue tinge. It was surprising that such a large animal had escaped scientific notice, especially on Luzon, one of the most-studied islands in the Philippines. The discovery highlighted the knowledge of indigenous communities and the importance of protecting the Sierra Madre forests."],
g:[["Monitor lizard","A large lizard of the genus Varanus"],["Forked tongue","A tongue split at the tip used for smelling"],["Carrion","The flesh of dead animals"],["Bayawak","The Filipino name for monitor lizards"]],
f:["The Komodo dragon is a monitor lizard.","Some Philippine monitors eat fruit.","A new monitor species was described in 2010."],
fun:"Some monitor lizards in zoos have learned to count how many snails they were given."});

ADDART({id:"squid",t:"Squid",c:"Sea Life",i:"🦑",w:"Squid",r:["octopus","mollusc","sea","trench","phfood"],
p:[["Jet-powered","Squid swim by shooting water out of a tube."],["Giants of the deep","The giant and colossal squid are among the largest invertebrates."],["Pusit","Squid, or pusit, is a favorite food in the Philippines."]],
l:"Squid are fast-swimming marine animals related to octopuses and cuttlefish, belonging to a group of mollusks called cephalopods. There are around 300 species, from tiny bobtail squid a few centimeters long to the giant squid and colossal squid of the deep sea, among the largest invertebrates on Earth. Squid have eight arms and two longer feeding tentacles with suckers, large eyes, three hearts and blue blood. They move by jet propulsion, forcing water out of a funnel, and can change color in an instant using special skin cells. Many squeeze out clouds of ink to escape predators. Squid are important prey for whales, seals and fish, and a major seafood worldwide, including in the Philippines, where pusit is cooked in many dishes.",
s:[["Body plan","A squid’s long, streamlined body, called the mantle, contains its organs. Fins on the mantle help steer. Inside is a thin, feather-shaped internal shell called the pen, a remnant of the shells of its ancestors. Its arms surround a sharp, parrot-like beak used to tear prey."],["Jet propulsion","Squid draw water into the mantle and then squeeze it out through a movable funnel, shooting themselves backward or forward. Some squid can move so fast that they leap out of the water and glide through the air for short distances."],["Changing colors","Squid skin contains thousands of color cells called chromatophores, each controlled by muscles and nerves. By expanding or shrinking them, a squid can change color and pattern in a fraction of a second, to camouflage itself, communicate or startle predators."],["Light and ink","Many deep-sea squid produce light through bioluminescence, using it to camouflage their silhouettes or signal. The Hawaiian bobtail squid has a special organ filled with glowing bacteria. Squid also release ink, which can form a dark cloud or a blob shaped like a squid to confuse predators."]],
k:[["Group","Cephalopods"],["Species","About 300"],["Hearts","Three"],["Blood color","Blue"],["Heaviest","Colossal squid"]],
d:[["1857","The giant squid genus Architeuthis is named"],["1952","Studies of squid nerves explain how nerve signals work"],["2004","The first photographs of a live giant squid in the wild are taken"],["2007","A colossal squid of about 495 kg is caught near Antarctica"],["2012","A giant squid is filmed alive in its deep-sea habitat"],["2025","A colossal squid is filmed alive in the deep sea for the first time"]],
s2:[["The giant squid","The giant squid can reach perhaps 12 meters or more including its tentacles, and has the largest eyes of any animal, about the size of dinner plates. For centuries, it was known only from carcasses washed ashore or found in whale stomachs, and it may have inspired legends of the kraken. In 2004 Japanese scientists photographed one alive for the first time, and in 2012 a team filmed one in its deep-sea home."],["The colossal squid","The colossal squid of the Southern Ocean is shorter than the giant squid but much heavier, with hooks as well as suckers on its arms. A specimen caught in 2007 weighed about 495 kilograms. In 2025 scientists aboard an ocean research vessel filmed a young colossal squid alive in the deep sea for the first time."],["Squid and science","Some squid have giant nerve fibers, up to a millimeter thick, that make them escape quickly. In the 1940s and 1950s, British scientists Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley used these giant nerves to discover how electrical signals travel along nerves. They shared the 1963 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Squid remain important animals in neuroscience research."],["Squid in Filipino food","Squid, or pusit, is popular in Filipino cooking, prepared as adobong pusit cooked in its own ink, grilled inihaw na pusit, stuffed squid and fried calamares, a favorite street food. Dried squid is also widely sold. Small squid called lukot and baby squid are eaten in many coastal areas."],["Squid fishing","Many squid are caught at night by boats using bright lights to attract them to the surface, a method used in the Philippines, Japan, Peru and elsewhere. Large lighted fishing fleets can be seen from space. Scientists and governments monitor squid catches because their populations can rise and fall quickly with ocean conditions."],["Humboldt squid","The Humboldt squid of the eastern Pacific grows up to about 2 meters and hunts in large groups. Fishermen call it the red devil because it flashes red and white and can be aggressive. It is one of the most heavily fished squid in the world, especially off Peru and Mexico."]],
sp:["Photographing a legend","For years, Japanese zoologist Tsunemi Kubodera and whale expert Kyoichi Mori searched for a way to see a living giant squid. They knew sperm whales hunted squid near the Ogasawara Islands south of Tokyo, so they lowered a camera and bait on a long line to about 900 meters deep. On September 30, 2004, a giant squid attacked the bait, and the camera snapped hundreds of photographs as the animal struggled for hours before breaking free, leaving one of its tentacles behind. The images, published in 2005, were the first of a giant squid alive in its natural habitat. In 2012 Kubodera and colleagues filmed one from a submersible, revealing a shimmering silver and gold animal."],
g:[["Cephalopod","A mollusk with arms, such as a squid or octopus"],["Mantle","The main body of a squid"],["Chromatophore","A color-changing skin cell"],["Pusit","The Filipino word for squid"]],
f:["Squid have three hearts.","Giant squid have the largest eyes of any animal.","Squid swim by jet propulsion."],
fun:"Some squid can leap out of the water and glide through the air."});

ADDART({id:"tuna",t:"Tuna",c:"Sea Life",i:"🐟",w:"Tuna",r:["fishes","sea","pacific","phfood","mindanao"],
p:[["Ocean racers","Tuna are among the fastest fish in the sea."],["Warm-blooded fish","Some tuna keep parts of their bodies warmer than the water."],["General Santos","General Santos City is known as the tuna capital of the Philippines."]],
l:"Tuna are large, fast-swimming fish of the mackerel family found in warm and temperate oceans around the world. There are about 15 species, including skipjack, yellowfin, bigeye, albacore and the giant bluefin tunas, which can grow over 3 meters long and weigh more than 600 kilograms. Tuna have torpedo-shaped bodies and can swim at high speeds, crossing entire oceans on migrations. Unlike most fish, some tuna can keep their muscles, eyes and brains warmer than the surrounding water. Tuna are among the most valuable fish in the world, eaten fresh as sashimi and sushi, grilled or canned. The Philippines is an important tuna-fishing country, and General Santos City in Mindanao is famous as its tuna capital.",
s:[["Built for speed","Tuna have smooth, streamlined bodies, stiff crescent-shaped tails and fins that fold into grooves to reduce drag. Small finlets near the tail help control water flow. Some species can reach bursts of around 70 kilometers per hour."],["Warm muscles","Bluefin tuna and some relatives have a system of blood vessels called a countercurrent heat exchanger, which keeps heat produced by their muscles from escaping through the gills. This lets them hunt in cold waters and dive deep while staying active, a rare ability among fish."],["Never stopping","Many tuna must keep swimming to breathe, because water must flow over their gills as they move, a method called ram ventilation. They swim continuously throughout their lives, even while resting."],["Species","Skipjack tuna, the smallest of the commercial species, are the most commonly canned. Yellowfin and bigeye are important for fresh and frozen markets. Atlantic, Pacific and southern bluefin tunas are the largest and most prized for sushi and sashimi."]],
k:[["Family","Mackerels and tunas"],["Species","About 15"],["Largest","Atlantic bluefin tuna"],["Most canned","Skipjack"],["Philippine tuna capital","General Santos City"]],
d:[["1969","An international commission to manage Atlantic tuna is founded"],["1998","The General Santos fish port complex opens"],["2004","A commission to manage western and central Pacific tuna is created"],["2019","A bluefin tuna sells for a record price at a Tokyo auction"],["2021","Some bluefin tuna are downgraded from endangered as stocks recover"],["2020s","General Santos continues to celebrate its yearly Tuna Festival"]],
s2:[["General Santos City","General Santos City on Sarangani Bay in southern Mindanao is home to one of the largest tuna landing ports in Asia. Each day, boats unload yellowfin, skipjack and other tuna, which are sold at auction, processed in canneries or exported fresh to Japan and other countries. Tuna fishing and processing provide many jobs, and the city celebrates its Tuna Festival every September."],["Handline fishing","Many Filipino fishers catch large yellowfin tuna using handlines, a traditional method in which a single baited hook on a long line is lowered from a small boat. Handline fishing is considered more selective and sustainable than large nets. Some Philippine handline-caught tuna has received sustainability certification."],["Overfishing","Demand for tuna has led to heavy fishing pressure. Atlantic and Pacific bluefin tuna were badly overfished in the late 20th century. International commissions set catch limits, and enforcement has improved. In 2021 scientists reported that Atlantic bluefin had recovered enough to be listed as of least concern, although some stocks and other tuna species remain under pressure."],["Record prices","At the first auction of the year at Tokyo’s fish market, restaurant owners compete to buy the best bluefin tuna, sometimes paying enormous sums for publicity. In 2019 a 278-kilogram bluefin sold for about 333 million yen, a record. Such prices are unusual, but they show how valuable bluefin are."],["Tuna and mercury","Large, long-lived tuna can accumulate mercury from their food. Health agencies advise that most people can safely eat tuna as part of a balanced diet, but recommend that pregnant women and young children limit their intake of large tuna species such as bigeye and bluefin, choosing lighter, smaller species such as skipjack instead."],["Dolphin-safe debate","In parts of the Pacific, tuna often swim beneath dolphins, and fishing nets once killed many dolphins. Campaigns in the 1980s and 1990s led to dolphin-safe labels and changes in fishing methods. Debate continues over how well different labels protect dolphins, sharks, turtles and other animals caught accidentally."]],
sp:["Dawn at the fish port","Before dawn at the fish port in General Santos City, boats crowd the docks, their decks loaded with tuna caught days earlier in the waters of Mindanao and beyond. Workers in rubber boots haul huge yellowfin, some taller than a person, onto the concrete floor. Inspectors check each fish, cutting a small sample from the tail to judge its color and fat. Buyers move between rows of fish, bidding for the best ones to export fresh to Japan for sashimi, while others are sent to canneries. By midmorning, the port is washed down, ready for the next catch. For the city, tuna is not only food but the heart of its economy and identity."],
g:[["Tuna","A large, fast ocean fish"],["Ram ventilation","Breathing by swimming with the mouth open"],["Handline","A single line and hook used for fishing"],["Sashimi","Thinly sliced raw fish"]],
f:["Some tuna keep their muscles warm.","General Santos is the Philippine tuna capital.","Skipjack is the most commonly canned tuna."],
fun:"A single bluefin tuna once sold for more than 300 million yen at a Tokyo auction."});

ADDART({id:"scorpions",t:"Scorpions",c:"Insects",i:"🦂",w:"Scorpion",r:["spider","insect","cactus","constellations","firstaid","centipedes"],
p:[["Ancient arachnids","Scorpions are relatives of spiders that have existed for over 400 million years."],["Glow in the dark","Scorpions glow under ultraviolet light."],["Stinging tails","They have a venomous stinger at the tip of their tails."]],
l:"Scorpions are arachnids, relatives of spiders, ticks and mites, with eight legs, a pair of grasping pincers and a segmented tail that curves over the back and ends in a venomous stinger. There are over 2,500 species, found on every continent except Antarctica, especially in deserts and tropical forests. Scorpions are mostly nocturnal predators of insects, spiders and other small animals. They are among the oldest land animals, with fossils more than 400 million years old. Most scorpion stings are painful but not dangerous to people, though a few dozen species have venom that can be deadly, especially to children. Under ultraviolet light, scorpions glow a vivid blue-green. Mother scorpions give birth to live young and carry them on their backs.",
s:[["Body","A scorpion has a front section with its eyes, mouthparts, pincers and legs, and a long tail made of segments ending in a bulb with venom glands and a stinger. It has two eyes on top and several smaller ones on the sides, but sees poorly and relies mainly on touch and vibrations."],["Hunting","Scorpions wait in burrows or under rocks and sense prey through tiny hairs and comb-like organs underneath their bodies, which detect vibrations and textures. They seize prey with their pincers and use the stinger when needed. Species with large pincers often rely less on venom."],["Glowing scorpions","A substance in the outer layer of a scorpion’s exoskeleton makes it glow blue-green under ultraviolet light, such as moonlight or a black light. Scientists are not sure why. One idea is that it helps scorpions sense light and decide when to hide. Researchers use UV lamps to find scorpions at night."],["Babies on board","Scorpions give birth to live young, which climb onto their mother’s back and ride there until after their first molt. The mother protects them during this vulnerable time. Some scorpions can live for several years, a long time for an invertebrate."]],
k:[["Group","Arachnids"],["Species","Over 2,500"],["Oldest fossils","More than 400 million years old"],["Dangerous species","A few dozen"],["Special feature","Glows under ultraviolet light"]],
d:[["c. 435 million years ago","Early scorpions live in ancient seas and shores"],["Ancient times","Babylonians and Greeks name the constellation Scorpius"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names several scorpion species"],["1993","Scientists isolate chlorotoxin from scorpion venom"],["2010s","Scorpion-based tumor paint is tested to help surgeons see cancer"],["2020s","Scorpion stings rise in parts of Brazil as cities grow"]],
s2:[["Dangerous scorpions","Most deadly scorpions belong to the family Buthidae, such as the deathstalker of North Africa and the Middle East and the Brazilian yellow scorpion. In countries such as Mexico, Brazil, India and parts of North Africa, scorpion stings cause many emergency cases and some deaths each year, mostly among children. Antivenoms and better medical care have reduced deaths."],["Scorpions in the Philippines","The Philippines has several scorpion species, including large dark forest scorpions. Their stings are painful but generally not life-threatening for healthy adults. People who are stung should clean the wound, apply a cold pack and seek medical care, especially for children or anyone with severe symptoms."],["Medicines from venom","Scientists are studying scorpion venom for medical uses. Chlorotoxin, a substance from the deathstalker scorpion, sticks to certain cancer cells. Researchers attached glowing dyes to a version of it to create tumor paint, which helps surgeons see tumors during operations. Other venom compounds are being studied for pain and immune disorders."],["Scorpions in culture","The constellation Scorpius has been seen as a scorpion since ancient times. In Greek myth, a scorpion killed the hunter Orion, and the two were placed on opposite sides of the sky so they never appear together. In ancient Egypt, the goddess Serket was associated with scorpions and protection against venom."],["Desert survivors","Scorpions can survive extreme conditions. Some can go a year without eating by slowing their metabolism. Others survive freezing and thawing. Their waxy exoskeletons reduce water loss in hot deserts. Scientists have even found that some scorpions can survive being submerged in water for many hours."],["Eating scorpions","In parts of China and Southeast Asia, fried scorpions are sold as snacks, and scorpions are used in some traditional medicines. Their stingers are usually removed or the venom destroyed by cooking. Eating insects and arachnids, called entomophagy, is promoted by some as a sustainable source of protein."]],
sp:["Scorpion versus Orion","In Greek mythology, Orion was a giant hunter who boasted that he could kill every animal on Earth. To stop him, the Earth goddess Gaia, or in some versions the goddess Artemis, sent a giant scorpion, which stung Orion and killed him. Zeus placed both in the sky as constellations. Because they were enemies, they were placed on opposite sides of the heavens: when Scorpius rises in the east, Orion sets in the west, as if fleeing. From the Philippines, Scorpius, with its bright red star Antares, can be seen in the evening sky from about May to August, while Orion dominates the sky from December to March."],
g:[["Arachnid","An eight-legged animal such as a spider or scorpion"],["Pincers","Grasping claws"],["Stinger","A sharp organ that injects venom"],["Exoskeleton","A hard outer skeleton"]],
f:["Scorpions glow under ultraviolet light.","Most scorpion stings are not deadly.","Scorpion mothers carry their babies on their backs."],
fun:"Some scorpions can survive a year without eating."});

ADDART({id:"wasps",t:"Wasps and Hornets",c:"Insects",i:"🐝",w:"Wasp",r:["bee","ants","insect","flower","agri"],
p:[["Not just stingers","Most wasps are harmless to people and help control pests."],["Paper makers","Paper wasps and hornets build nests from chewed wood."],["Tiny helpers","Many tiny wasps are used to protect crops naturally."]],
l:"Wasps are a huge and varied group of insects related to bees and ants. Scientists have described over 100,000 species, and many more remain undiscovered, making wasps one of the most diverse animal groups on Earth. The familiar social wasps, such as paper wasps, yellowjackets and hornets, live in colonies with a queen and workers and can sting to defend their nests. But most wasps are solitary, and many are parasitoids that lay eggs in or on other insects, eventually killing them. This makes wasps important natural controllers of pests, and farmers use them in biological pest control. Wasps also pollinate many plants, including figs, which depend entirely on tiny fig wasps. In the Philippines, wasps are called putakti.",
s:[["Social wasps","Social wasps live in nests with a queen that lays eggs and workers that build, gather food and defend the colony. In temperate countries, colonies die off in winter, and only new queens survive. In tropical places such as the Philippines, some colonies can last much longer."],["Paper nests","Paper wasps and hornets chew wood fibers from dead trees or fences and mix them with saliva to make a papery material. They build nests of six-sided cells, some hanging under roofs or branches and others hidden in the ground or in hollow trees. In 1719 the French scientist René-Antoine de Réaumur suggested that people could make paper from wood, as wasps do."],["Parasitoid wasps","Most wasp species are parasitoids. A female lays her eggs on or inside a caterpillar, beetle larva, aphid or spider, and the young wasps eat the host from within. Some are so tiny they lay eggs inside the eggs of other insects. Although gruesome, these wasps keep pest populations in check."],["Stings","Only female wasps can sting, because the stinger is a modified egg-laying tube. Unlike honeybees, most wasps can sting more than once. Stings are painful, and for people with severe allergies they can be dangerous. Large numbers of stings from disturbed nests can also be serious."]],
k:[["Order","Hymenoptera, with bees and ants"],["Described species","Over 100,000"],["Most species","Solitary parasitoids"],["Nest material","Chewed wood paper"],["Filipino name","Putakti"]],
d:[["c. 200 million years ago","Early wasps appear"],["1719","Réaumur suggests making paper from wood, inspired by wasps"],["Late 1800s","Natural enemies of pests begin to be used deliberately to protect crops"],["2019","Asian giant hornets are found in North America"],["2020","The first Asian giant hornet nest in the United States is destroyed"],["2024","Asian giant hornets are declared eradicated from the United States"]],
s2:[["Fig wasps","Every species of fig tree depends on its own species of tiny fig wasp for pollination. Female wasps squeeze into a fig, which is actually a hollow cluster of flowers, pollinating it and laying eggs inside. Many fig species are important food for birds, bats and monkeys in tropical forests, including the balete and other figs of the Philippines."],["Protecting crops","Farmers and scientists release parasitoid wasps to control pests such as corn borers, whiteflies and caterpillars instead of, or alongside, chemical pesticides. In the Philippines, the Department of Agriculture has used Trichogramma wasps, which lay eggs inside pest moth eggs, to protect corn and sugarcane. This biological control can reduce pesticide use."],["Asian giant hornets","The Asian giant hornet, the world’s largest hornet, can attack and destroy honeybee hives. In 2019 it was found in British Columbia in Canada and Washington State in the United States, probably accidentally brought by ships. Scientists and volunteers tracked hornets with tiny radio tags to find and destroy their nests, and in 2024 officials declared the species eradicated from the United States."],["Wasps and people","Many people fear wasps because of their stings, especially when wasps gather around food and drinks. Experts advise staying calm, not swatting wasps, covering sweet foods and calling professionals to remove nests near homes. They note that wasps are valuable in gardens because they eat many pest insects."],["Strange wasps","Some wasps have remarkable behaviors. The emerald cockroach wasp stings a cockroach in the brain, then leads it to a burrow like a dog on a leash to feed her young. Tarantula hawk wasps hunt huge spiders, and their sting is considered one of the most painful insect stings."],["Wasps in the Philippines","Putakti and other wasps are common in Philippine gardens, farms and forests. Large hornets and paper wasps sometimes build nests under roofs and in trees. Like elsewhere, many tiny wasps in the Philippines quietly help control crop pests."]],
sp:["Tracking the giant hornets","In late 2020, scientists in Washington State faced a challenge: find the nest of Asian giant hornets before new queens could escape and spread. Entomologists captured live hornets using traps baited with sweet liquids. Then, using dental floss, they carefully tied tiny radio transmitters to the hornets’ waists and released them. Following the signals through forests with antennas, they tracked one hornet to a hollow tree near the town of Blaine. Wearing thick protective suits, they vacuumed out the hornets and removed the nest, which contained hundreds of hornets and developing queens. Similar efforts continued until 2024, when the hornet was declared eradicated from the United States."],
g:[["Parasitoid","An insect that develops inside or on a host, killing it"],["Colony","A group of insects living together"],["Biological control","Using living things to control pests"],["Putakti","The Filipino word for wasp"]],
f:["Only female wasps can sting.","Fig trees depend on fig wasps.","Paper wasps make nests from chewed wood."],
fun:"Some parasitoid wasps are so small they lay their eggs inside the eggs of other insects."});

