// Plants: sunflowers, palms, conifers, climbing plants and poisonous plants.
ADDART({id:"sunflowers",t:"Sunflowers",c:"Plants",i:"🌻",w:"Common sunflower",r:["flower","seeds","fib","agri","fruits"],
p:[["Following the Sun","Young sunflowers turn to follow the Sun across the sky."],["Thousands of flowers","A sunflower head is made of many tiny flowers."],["Seeds and oil","Sunflowers are grown for seeds and cooking oil."]],
l:"The common sunflower, Helianthus annuus, is a tall flowering plant native to North America, famous for its large yellow flower heads. It was domesticated by Native Americans thousands of years ago and later spread worldwide. What looks like a single sunflower is actually a head made of hundreds to over a thousand tiny flowers, arranged in spirals that follow a mathematical pattern linked to the Fibonacci sequence. Young sunflowers turn to follow the Sun during the day, while mature ones face east. Sunflowers are grown for their edible seeds and for sunflower oil, one of the world’s most important cooking oils, with Russia and Ukraine as top producers. They have also been used to clean polluted soil and have inspired famous paintings.",
s:[["One head, many flowers","A sunflower is a composite flower. The yellow petals around the edge belong to ray flowers, which attract insects. The center is packed with disk flowers, each able to produce a seed. Bees and other insects pollinate them, moving from the outer disk flowers inward over several days."],["Spirals in the seeds","Sunflower seeds are arranged in two sets of interlocking spirals, one curving clockwise and the other counterclockwise. The numbers of spirals are often consecutive Fibonacci numbers, such as 34 and 55. This arrangement packs the seeds efficiently, leaving almost no gaps."],["Tracking the Sun","Young sunflower plants turn from east to west during the day following the Sun and swing back to face east at night, a behavior called heliotropism. A 2016 study showed this is controlled by an internal clock. When mature, most stop moving and face east, which warms the flowers in the morning and attracts more bees."],["Sunflower farming","Sunflowers grow quickly and tolerate dry conditions. Farmers grow varieties for oil, whose seeds are small and black, and for snacks, whose seeds are larger and striped. Sunflower meal left after pressing oil is fed to livestock. In the Philippines, sunflowers are grown in gardens and on some farms, and sunflower fields have become popular tourist spots."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Helianthus annuus"],["Native to","North America"],["Flower type","Composite of many small flowers"],["Top oil producers","Russia and Ukraine"],["Tallest recorded","Over 9 meters"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Native Americans domesticate sunflowers"],["1500s","Spanish explorers bring sunflowers to Europe"],["1716","An English patent describes pressing oil from sunflower seeds"],["1830s","Large-scale sunflower oil production begins in Russia"],["1888","Vincent van Gogh paints his famous Sunflowers series"],["2016","Scientists explain how sunflowers track the Sun"]],
s2:[["Van Gogh’s sunflowers","In 1888 and 1889, the Dutch painter Vincent van Gogh painted a series of still lifes of sunflowers in vases in Arles, France, to decorate the room of his friend Paul Gauguin. With their bold yellows, they became some of the most famous paintings in the world. One sold in 1987 for a then-record price."],["Ukraine’s flower","The sunflower is a national symbol of Ukraine, where vast fields cover the countryside and sunflower oil is a major export. After Russia’s full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, the sunflower became a worldwide symbol of support for Ukraine. The war disrupted exports and caused sunflower oil prices to rise in many countries."],["Cleaning polluted soil","Sunflowers can absorb some heavy metals and radioactive substances from soil and water through their roots, a process called phytoremediation. After the 1986 Chernobyl nuclear disaster, sunflowers were tested on floating rafts to remove radioactive materials from a pond. Sunflowers were also planted in Japan after the 2011 Fukushima disaster, though their effectiveness there was limited."],["A native American crop","Archaeological evidence shows that Native Americans grew sunflowers in what is now the eastern United States and Mexico thousands of years ago, using the seeds for food, oil, dye and medicine. Sunflowers are one of the few major crops domesticated in North America."],["Sunflowers and bees","Sunflowers produce plenty of nectar and pollen, making them valuable for honeybees and wild bees. Farmers sometimes place beehives near sunflower fields to improve seed production, while beekeepers get sunflower honey, which is golden and crystallizes quickly."],["Giant sunflowers","Some sunflower varieties can grow extremely tall. In 2014 a German gardener grew a sunflower over 9 meters tall, a world record. Sunflower-growing contests are popular in schools and communities, teaching children about plants."]],
sp:["The flower that follows the Sun","Scientists had long noticed that young sunflowers seem to watch the Sun, but they did not know how. In 2016 researchers at the University of California, Davis, studied sunflowers in fields and in growth chambers. They found that during the day, cells on the east side of the stem grow faster, bending the plant westward to follow the Sun. At night, the west side grows faster, swinging the head back to face east. The plants used an internal clock, much like the body clock in humans. The team also found that east-facing mature flowers warm up faster in the morning and receive several times more visits from bees than flowers turned west."],
g:[["Composite flower","A head made of many small flowers"],["Heliotropism","Turning to follow the Sun"],["Phytoremediation","Using plants to clean polluted land"],["Disk flower","A tiny flower in the center of a sunflower"]],
f:["A sunflower head contains many tiny flowers.","Young sunflowers follow the Sun.","Sunflower seeds form Fibonacci spirals."],
fun:"The record sunflower grew taller than a three-story building."});

ADDART({id:"palms",t:"Palm Trees",c:"Plants",i:"🌴",w:"Arecaceae",r:["coconut","trees","deforestation","phfood","weaving","orangutans"],
p:[["Not true trees","Palms are more closely related to grasses than to oaks."],["Useful plants","Palms give us coconuts, dates, palm oil and rattan."],["Philippine palms","Nipa, buri and anahaw are important palms in the Philippines."]],
l:"Palms are flowering plants of the family Arecaceae, with about 2,600 species found mostly in tropical and subtropical regions. Most have a single unbranched trunk topped by a crown of large leaves, either feather-shaped or fan-shaped. Botanically, palms are monocots, related to grasses and lilies, and their trunks lack true wood and growth rings. Palms are among the most useful plants to humans, providing coconuts, dates, palm oil, sago starch, betel nuts, rattan for furniture, and leaves for roofing and weaving. The Philippines has many native palms, including nipa, used for thatch, vinegar and drinks; buri, the country’s largest palm; and anahaw. The rapid spread of oil palm plantations in Southeast Asia has become a major environmental issue.",
s:[["How palms grow","Palms grow from a single bud at the top of the trunk. If this bud is damaged, the palm usually dies, because it cannot branch. Their trunks do not thicken much over time like other trees; instead, they reach their full width before growing tall. Bundles of tough fibers scattered through the trunk give them strength and flexibility in storms."],["Palm leaves","Palm leaves, called fronds, are either pinnate, like feathers, as in coconut and nipa, or palmate, like fans, as in anahaw. Some palm leaves are among the largest of any plant. People weave them into roofs, mats, baskets, hats and decorations."],["Fruits and seeds","Palms produce fruits ranging from dates and coconuts to the giant coco de mer, whose seed is the largest in the plant world. Many palm fruits are eaten by animals, which spread the seeds. Betel nut, from the areca palm, is chewed as a stimulant in parts of Asia, though it is linked to mouth cancer."],["Climbing palms","Rattans are climbing palms with long, thin, flexible stems covered in spines. They climb through forests using hooked whips. Their stems are harvested and made into furniture, baskets and canes. The Philippines, especially Cebu, is known for rattan furniture."]],
k:[["Family","Arecaceae"],["Species","About 2,600"],["Plant group","Monocots"],["Largest Philippine palm","Buri"],["Major crop palms","Coconut, oil palm, date palm"]],
d:[["c. 4000 BC","Date palms are cultivated in Mesopotamia"],["1848","Oil palms from Africa are planted in Java"],["1910s","Commercial oil palm plantations begin in Malaysia"],["2004","The Roundtable on Sustainable Palm Oil is founded"],["2010s","Palm oil becomes the world’s most produced vegetable oil"],["2020s","The European Union adopts rules on deforestation-free products, including palm oil"]],
s2:[["Nipa","Nipa palms grow in brackish water along rivers and mangroves. Their leaves are used for traditional nipa hut roofs, called bahay kubo. Sap from their flower stalks is fermented into tuba, a palm wine, and made into vinegar called sukang nipa, and distilled into a liquor called laksoy. Nipa swamps also protect riverbanks and coasts."],["Buri","The buri palm, also called the talipot or gebang palm, is the largest palm in the Philippines. It grows for decades, then produces one enormous flower cluster with millions of flowers and dies after fruiting. Its leaves are woven into mats, hats and bags, its fibers make raffia and its trunk yields starch. Buri weaving is an important craft in provinces such as Bohol and Quezon."],["Palm oil","Palm oil, from the fruit of the African oil palm, is used in many foods, soaps, cosmetics and biofuels. It is very productive, giving more oil per hectare than other oil crops. But the expansion of plantations in Indonesia and Malaysia has destroyed large areas of rainforest and peatland, threatening orangutans and releasing greenhouse gases. Producers argue that palm oil supports millions of small farmers and that switching to other oils would require even more land. Certification schemes aim to make production more sustainable, though critics say they have weaknesses."],["Palm Sunday","On Palm Sunday, which begins Holy Week, Filipino Catholics bring woven palm leaves, called palaspas, to church to be blessed, recalling the crowds who waved palm branches as Jesus entered Jerusalem. Many palaspas are skillfully woven from young coconut leaves into intricate shapes."],["Dates","The date palm has been cultivated in the Middle East and North Africa for thousands of years and is central to life in desert oases. Dates are rich in sugar and are traditionally eaten to break the fast during Ramadan. A 2,000-year-old date palm seed from Masada has even been sprouted into a living tree."],["Palms in cities","Palms are planted along streets and beaches in many tropical cities, including in the Philippines, for their decorative look. Some urban planners note that palms provide less shade and absorb less carbon than broad-leaved trees, and recommend mixing them with native shade trees."]],
sp:["The palm that flowers once","For 30 to 80 years, a buri palm grows quietly in a Philippine field, its huge fan-shaped leaves spreading over the land. Farmers harvest its leaves for weaving and collect sap. Then, one year, the palm begins its grand finale. From the top of its trunk rises a massive, branching flower cluster several meters tall, one of the largest of any plant, carrying millions of tiny cream-colored flowers. Bees and other insects swarm around it. Over the following months, the flowers become countless round fruits. Having used all its stored energy, the palm then slowly dies, leaving behind seeds that may grow into a new generation."],
g:[["Palm","A plant of the family Arecaceae"],["Frond","A large palm leaf"],["Monocot","A flowering plant group that includes grasses and palms"],["Rattan","A climbing palm used for furniture"]],
f:["Palms are monocots, not true trees.","The buri palm flowers once and dies.","Rattan is a climbing palm."],
fun:"A buri palm may wait decades to flower, then produce millions of flowers at once before dying."});

ADDART({id:"conifers",t:"Conifers",c:"Plants",i:"🌲",w:"Conifer",r:["trees","giantrees","seeds","bohol","deforestation","ginkgo"],
p:[["Cone bearers","Conifers carry their seeds in cones."],["Needles and scales","Most have needle-like or scale-like leaves."],["City of Pines","Baguio is famous for its Benguet pines."]],
l:"Conifers are cone-bearing seed plants, including pines, spruces, firs, cedars, cypresses, junipers, redwoods and yews. There are about 600 species. Unlike flowering plants, they do not produce flowers or fruits; their seeds develop on the scales of cones. Most conifers are evergreen trees with needle-like or scale-like leaves that stay on the tree year-round. They dominate vast forests across the cold northern parts of North America, Europe and Asia and grow on mountains worldwide. Conifers include the tallest, largest and oldest trees on Earth. They provide most of the world’s timber and paper pulp. In the Philippines, native conifers include the Benguet pine of the Cordillera, which gave Baguio its nickname, the City of Pines, and the almaciga of Palawan.",
s:[["Cones and seeds","Most conifers have separate male and female cones. Small male cones release clouds of pollen carried by the wind. Larger female cones have scales that hold ovules, which become seeds after pollination. Some cones take two years to mature, then open to release winged seeds, while others need fire to open."],["Needles","Conifer needles are narrow, tough and coated in wax, which reduces water loss and helps them survive cold, dry winters and snow. Because needles stay on the tree for several years, conifers can begin making food as soon as spring arrives. A few conifers, such as larches, drop their needles in autumn."],["Resin","Conifers produce sticky resin that seals wounds and defends against insects and fungi. Resin from pines is used to make turpentine and rosin, used by violinists and athletes. Fossilized resin is amber, which sometimes preserves ancient insects."],["The boreal forest","The boreal forest, or taiga, is a huge belt of mostly conifer forest stretching across Canada, Alaska, Scandinavia and Russia. It is the largest land habitat on Earth and stores enormous amounts of carbon in trees and soils. Wildfires and warming temperatures are changing it rapidly."]],
k:[["Group","Gymnosperms"],["Species","About 600"],["Seeds held in","Cones"],["Philippine pine","Benguet pine"],["Tallest conifer","Coast redwood"]],
d:[["c. 300 million years ago","The first conifers appear"],["1600s","European navies prize tall pines for ship masts"],["1909","Baguio, the City of Pines, becomes a chartered city"],["1994","The Wollemi pine is discovered in Australia"],["2020","Firefighters save the wild Wollemi pines from bushfires"],["2020s","Bark beetles and fires damage conifer forests in many countries"]],
s2:[["Benguet pine and Baguio","The Benguet pine grows in the mountains of northern Luzon, especially the Cordillera Central. Its fragrant forests give Baguio City its cool, pine-scented atmosphere and its nickname, City of Pines. Pine cones are sold as souvenirs. The pines are threatened by urban growth, forest fires and pests, and Baguio has run tree-planting and protection programs."],["Almaciga","The almaciga, a native conifer of the genus Agathis, grows in the forests of Palawan and other islands. Indigenous peoples such as the Tagbanua and Batak tap its trunks for a resin known as Manila copal, used in varnishes, paints and incense. Resin collection provides income, though overtapping can harm trees, and communities have worked to manage it sustainably."],["The Wollemi pine","In 1994 a park ranger exploring a remote canyon in the Blue Mountains near Sydney, Australia, discovered a strange conifer known only from fossils. The Wollemi pine had survived as fewer than 100 wild trees. During the 2019 to 2020 bushfires, firefighters were flown in to protect the secret grove with water bombers and sprinklers, saving the trees."],["Christmas trees","In many countries, conifers such as firs, spruces and pines are used as Christmas trees, a custom that spread from Germany. In the Philippines, where real conifers are uncommon in lowlands, artificial trees and native alternatives are popular. Some groups promote reusable or locally made trees to reduce waste."],["Timber and paper","Conifers grow straight and produce softwood timber used for houses, furniture and paper. Pine plantations are grown around the world. Supporters say well-managed plantations reduce pressure on natural forests, while critics note that large monocultures support less wildlife than natural forests."],["Mindoro pine","Another native pine, the Mindoro pine, grows in scattered areas of Mindoro, Zambales and other places. It is adapted to fire and poor soils. Botanists consider the Philippines the meeting point of conifers from northern Asia and the southern hemisphere."]],
sp:["The dinosaur tree","In September 1994, David Noble, a field officer with the New South Wales parks service, was abseiling and exploring a narrow, deep canyon in Wollemi National Park when he noticed some trees with unusual bark that looked like bubbling chocolate. He took a branch to experts, who were astonished: the tree belonged to a family thought to have disappeared from the fossil record millions of years ago. Nicknamed the dinosaur tree, the Wollemi pine became a sensation. To protect the tiny wild population, its location was kept secret, and young trees grown from cuttings were sold worldwide to reduce the temptation to collect wild ones."],
g:[["Conifer","A cone-bearing seed plant"],["Gymnosperm","A plant with seeds not enclosed in fruits"],["Resin","A sticky substance produced by conifers"],["Taiga","The northern conifer forest"]],
f:["Conifers produce cones instead of flowers.","Baguio is called the City of Pines.","Amber is fossilized resin."],
fun:"The Wollemi pine was known only from fossils until it was found alive in 1994."});

ADDART({id:"vines",t:"Climbing Plants",c:"Plants",i:"🌿",w:"Vine",r:["trees","amazon","flower","invasiveplants","palms"],
p:[["Reaching for light","Vines climb other plants and structures to reach sunlight."],["Clever climbers","They twine, use tendrils, hooks or sticky roots."],["A turquoise treasure","The turquoise jade vine is native to the Philippines."]],
l:"Climbing plants, often called vines or creepers, are plants that grow upward by attaching to other plants, rocks or structures rather than supporting themselves with thick trunks. This allows them to reach sunlight with less investment in strong stems. They climb by twining around supports, grasping with tendrils, hooking with thorns or clinging with sticky roots or pads. Woody climbers called lianas are common in tropical rainforests, where they can stretch hundreds of meters through the canopy. Many important crops are climbers, including grapes, beans, peas, squash, passion fruit and ampalaya. The Philippines is home to the jade vine, with its striking turquoise flowers, and to many rattans. Some vines, such as kudzu, have become invasive pests.",
s:[["Twiners","Twining plants, such as morning glories, beans and sweet potatoes, wind their stems around a support as they grow. The growing tip slowly circles in the air until it touches something, then wraps around it. Each species usually twines in a particular direction."],["Tendrils","Plants such as peas, grapes, cucumbers and ampalaya produce thin, sensitive tendrils. When a tendril touches a support, it curls around it within minutes to hours, then coils like a spring, pulling the plant closer and absorbing movement in the wind."],["Hooks and roots","Rattans and roses use hooks and thorns to hold onto other plants. Ivy and some figs grow small roots or sticky pads that cling to walls and bark. Boston ivy can climb smooth walls using tiny adhesive discs."],["Lianas","In tropical forests, lianas start on the ground and climb into the canopy, sometimes connecting many trees. They provide food and pathways for animals such as monkeys and squirrels. Lianas compete with trees for light, and in some forests they have become more common as climates change."]],
k:[["Main climbing methods","Twining, tendrils, hooks, roots"],["Woody tropical climbers","Lianas"],["Philippine endemic","Jade vine"],["Famous invasive","Kudzu"],["Crop examples","Grapes, beans, ampalaya"]],
d:[["c. 6000 BC","Grapevines are cultivated in the Caucasus and Middle East"],["1841","A US expedition collects the jade vine in the Philippines"],["1865","Charles Darwin publishes a study of climbing plants"],["1876","Kudzu is introduced to the United States"],["1930s","The US government promotes kudzu to control soil erosion"],["2020s","Scientists study why lianas are increasing in tropical forests"]],
s2:[["The jade vine","The jade vine, Strongylodon macrobotrys, grows in the rainforests of the Philippines and is found nowhere else in the wild. It produces hanging clusters of claw-shaped flowers in an unusual blue-green color. In the wild, the flowers are pollinated by bats, which hang upside down to drink nectar. It is grown in botanical gardens worldwide and is threatened by forest loss in the Philippines."],["Rattan","Rattans are climbing palms with spiny stems that can grow over 100 meters long. The Philippines has many native rattan species. For generations, rattan has been harvested for furniture, baskets and handicrafts, and Cebu became famous for rattan furniture exports. Overharvesting has reduced wild rattan, and the government has regulated its collection and encouraged plantations."],["Kudzu, the vine that ate the South","Kudzu, a fast-growing vine from East Asia, was introduced to the United States in 1876 as an ornamental plant and was later planted widely to control soil erosion. In the warm, humid American South, it spread rapidly, smothering trees, fields and buildings and earning the nickname the vine that ate the South. It is now considered an invasive weed."],["Darwin and climbing plants","Charles Darwin was fascinated by how climbing plants move. He grew them in his greenhouse and watched their tips trace slow circles in the air. In 1865 he published a study describing how plants find and grasp supports. His work showed that plants are capable of complex, purposeful-looking movements."],["Vegetables on vines","Many Filipino vegetables grow on vines trained up bamboo trellises, including ampalaya, upo, patola, sitaw and chayote, known as sayote. Trellising saves space, keeps fruits off the ground and makes harvesting easier, an important practice in backyard gardens and small farms."],["Strangler figs","Some figs start as seeds dropped by birds high in a tree. They send roots down to the ground and gradually wrap around the host tree, sometimes killing it and leaving a hollow lattice of fig roots. In the Philippines, these balete trees are often linked to folklore about spirits and supernatural beings."]],
sp:["The bat-pollinated jewel","Deep in a Philippine rainforest, a vine twists high into the trees. At certain times of the year, it produces long clusters of flowers that hang down like chandeliers, each flower shaped like a claw and colored an astonishing shade of turquoise. Few flowers in the world are this color. At night, a bat arrives, hangs upside down from the flower cluster and pushes its head into a flower to lap the sweet nectar. As it does so, the flower’s structure presses pollen onto the bat’s head. When the bat visits another jade vine, it delivers the pollen. This is the jade vine, one of the Philippines’ most beautiful native plants."],
g:[["Vine","A plant that climbs or trails"],["Tendril","A thin, coiling plant part that grasps supports"],["Liana","A woody climbing plant of tropical forests"],["Trellis","A frame that supports climbing plants"]],
f:["The jade vine is native to the Philippines.","Rattans are climbing palms.","Kudzu is invasive in the United States."],
fun:"A climbing plant’s tendril can curl around a support within minutes of touching it."});

ADDART({id:"poisonousplants",t:"Poisonous Plants",c:"Plants",i:"☠️",w:"List of poisonous plants",r:["firstaid","medicinalplants","allergy","flower","agri","plantdefenses"],
p:[["Chemical defenses","Many plants make poisons to protect themselves."],["Common dangers","Some poisonous plants grow in homes and gardens."],["Healing in small doses","Some plant poisons are also used as medicines."]],
l:"Poisonous plants contain substances that can harm or kill people or animals if eaten, touched or inhaled. Plants cannot run from animals that eat them, so many produce chemicals that make them bitter, irritating or toxic. Some of the world’s most poisonous plants are common ornamental and garden plants, such as oleander, while others are food crops that must be prepared carefully, such as cassava. In the Philippines, the seeds of the tubang-bakod, or physic nut, are a common cause of accidental poisoning in children. Many plant poisons are also used as medicines in small, controlled doses. Learning to recognize poisonous plants, keeping them away from children and pets, and seeking help quickly when poisoning is suspected can prevent serious harm.",
s:[["Why plants make poisons","Plants produce a huge variety of chemicals that discourage insects, mammals and fungi from eating them. These include alkaloids such as nicotine and atropine, cyanide-producing compounds, sharp crystals and substances that affect the heart. Some animals have evolved resistance and can eat plants that are poisonous to others."],["Household and garden plants","Common ornamental plants can be dangerous. Oleander, widely planted along roads and in gardens, contains heart toxins in all its parts. Dieffenbachia, or dumb cane, contains needle-like crystals that cause painful swelling of the mouth and throat if chewed. Lilies are especially dangerous to cats."],["Food plants","Some foods are safe only when properly prepared. Raw cassava contains compounds that release cyanide and must be peeled, soaked and cooked. Raw kidney beans contain a toxin destroyed by thorough boiling. Potato sprouts and green potato skins contain solanine. Bitter almonds and some fruit seeds also contain cyanide-producing compounds."],["Touching plants","Some plants harm through contact. Poison ivy and its relatives produce an oily substance called urushiol that causes itchy rashes; mango sap contains related chemicals that can irritate sensitive skin. The Australian stinging tree, gympie-gympie, has tiny hairs that cause pain lasting weeks."]],
k:[["Common Philippine danger","Tubang-bakod seeds"],["Heart toxins in","Oleander and foxglove"],["Cyanide risk","Raw cassava"],["Rash-causing substance","Urushiol"],["Famous poison","Hemlock"]],
d:[["399 BC","Socrates is executed with a drink of poison hemlock"],["1785","William Withering describes foxglove as a heart medicine"],["1888","Ricin is identified in castor beans"],["1978","A ricin pellet is used in the assassination of Georgi Markov in London"],["2000s","Physic nut is promoted as a biofuel crop in the Philippines"],["2020s","Poison control centers continue to warn about plant poisonings in children"]],
s2:[["Tubang-bakod","The physic nut, known in the Philippines as tubang-bakod, is a shrub often grown as a living fence and was promoted as a biofuel crop in the 2000s. Its seeds look and taste somewhat like nuts, so children sometimes eat them, causing vomiting, diarrhea and dehydration. Clusters of poisoning cases among schoolchildren have been reported. Doctors advise seeking medical care and keeping the seeds away from children."],["Saga and castor beans","The bright red and black seeds of the rosary pea, called saga in the Philippines, are used in jewelry and crafts but contain abrin, a powerful toxin if the seeds are chewed or broken. Castor beans contain ricin, one of the most toxic natural substances. Castor oil, extracted from the seeds, is safe because the toxin does not pass into the oil."],["Poison and medicine","The difference between a poison and a medicine is often the dose. Digoxin from foxglove treats heart failure, atropine from deadly nightshade is used in surgery and to treat some poisonings, and vincristine from the periwinkle treats cancer. In 1785 the English doctor William Withering described how carefully measured foxglove doses could treat heart problems."],["Poisons in history","Plant poisons have been used for hunting, warfare and executions. In 399 BC the Greek philosopher Socrates was put to death by drinking poison hemlock. Indigenous peoples in South America used curare from plants on blow darts, and peoples of Southeast Asia, including some in the Philippines, used plant poisons on arrows and darts for hunting."],["If poisoning happens","If someone may have eaten a poisonous plant, health experts advise removing any remaining plant material from the mouth, keeping a sample of the plant for identification, and calling a poison control center or going to the nearest hospital immediately. They warn against inducing vomiting unless instructed by a medical professional. In the Philippines, the National Poison Management and Control Center at the Philippine General Hospital provides advice."],["Animals and plants","Pets and livestock can also be poisoned by plants. Dogs and cats can be harmed by lilies, sago palms and some houseplants. Farmers watch for toxic weeds in pastures. Some animals, such as koalas eating eucalyptus and monarch caterpillars eating milkweed, are specially adapted to toxic plants and even use the toxins for defense."]],
sp:["The doctor and the foxglove","In the 1770s, the English doctor William Withering heard of an old folk remedy used by a woman in Shropshire to treat dropsy, a swelling caused by heart failure. The recipe contained about 20 herbs. Withering realized that the active ingredient was likely foxglove, a common but poisonous garden plant. Over nearly ten years, he carefully tested different doses on patients, noting how much helped and how much caused harmful side effects. In 1785 he published his results, one of the earliest careful studies of a medicine’s dose. Medicines derived from foxglove are still used for some heart conditions today."],
g:[["Toxin","A poisonous substance produced by a living thing"],["Alkaloid","A type of plant chemical that often affects the body"],["Urushiol","An oily substance that causes rashes"],["Dose","The amount of a substance taken"]],
f:["Oleander is poisonous in all its parts.","Raw cassava must be cooked to be safe.","Foxglove gave us a heart medicine."],
fun:"Castor oil is safe to use even though castor beans contain one of the most toxic natural substances."});

