ADDART({id:"invasiveplants",t:"Invasive Plants",c:"Plants",i:"🌱",w:"Invasive species",r:["aquaticplants","grasses","vines","conservation","phbio","pigs","dandelions","mimosa"],
p:[["Out of place","Invasive plants spread harmfully outside their native lands."],["Choking waterways","Water hyacinth clogs rivers and lakes."],["Hard to stop","Once established, invasive plants are difficult to remove."]],
l:"Invasive plants are species introduced by people to areas outside their native range that spread aggressively and harm ecosystems, farms or human activities. Many were brought deliberately as ornamental plants, crops, livestock feed or for erosion control, while others arrived accidentally as seeds in cargo or soil. Freed from the insects, diseases and competitors that kept them in check at home, they can grow rapidly and crowd out native plants. Invasive species are considered one of the main causes of biodiversity loss worldwide. Well-known examples include water hyacinth in rivers and lakes, kudzu in the United States, and lantana and Chromolaena in tropical countries. The Philippines faces many invasive plants, and controlling them requires prevention, early action and long-term management.",
s:[["How plants become invasive","Most introduced plants never become a problem. Those that do often grow and reproduce quickly, spread seeds widely, tolerate many conditions and have no natural enemies in their new home. Disturbed land, such as roadsides, cleared forests and farms, gives them an opening."],["Harm to ecosystems","Invasive plants can smother native plants, change soil chemistry, use up water and alter how often fires occur. When native plants disappear, the insects, birds and other animals that depend on them may decline. Some invaders also reduce crop yields and grazing land."],["Harm to people","Water hyacinth blocks boats, irrigation canals and drains, and can worsen flooding. Some invasive grasses increase wildfire risk. Others, such as parthenium weed, cause allergies. The cost of controlling invasive species worldwide is enormous."],["Prevention and control","Countries inspect imported plants and soil, ban the sale of known invaders and encourage gardeners to choose native species. Control methods include pulling and cutting, herbicides, controlled burns and biological control, in which natural enemies from the plant’s native range are carefully tested and released."]],
k:[["Main causes of spread","Ornamental, farming and accidental introductions"],["Philippine waterway invader","Water hyacinth"],["US example","Kudzu"],["Famous biological control","Cactus moth in Australia"],["Global concern","A major cause of biodiversity loss"]],
d:[["1876","Kudzu is introduced to the United States"],["c. 1912","Water hyacinth is introduced to the Philippines"],["1926","Cactus moths are released in Australia to control prickly pear"],["1960s","Large mahogany plantations are planted in Bohol"],["1993","An international biodiversity treaty addressing invasive species takes effect"],["2023","A UN-backed report warns of the rising cost of invasive species"]],
s2:[["Invaders in the Philippines","Common invasive plants in the Philippines include water hyacinth in waterways, Chromolaena odorata, known locally as hagonoy, which spreads over cleared land, lantana, mile-a-minute vine and cogon grass on degraded hillsides. In some protected areas, rangers and volunteers work to remove invaders and replant native species."],["The mahogany debate","Big-leaf mahogany from Central and South America was widely planted in the Philippines for reforestation and timber, including the famous man-made forest in Bilar, Bohol. Supporters value its timber, shade and beauty. Some ecologists argue that mahogany plantations support fewer native animals, may hinder native tree growth and spread into natural forests, and recommend planting native trees instead. Others say mahogany can play a role in mixed plantings."],["The prickly pear victory","Prickly pear cacti brought to Australia in the 1800s spread over millions of hectares of farmland. In 1926 scientists released the cactus moth from South America, whose caterpillars eat prickly pear. Within a few years, the cactus was largely brought under control, and farmers even built a hall in Queensland honoring the moth. It remains one of the most successful examples of biological control."],["When control backfires","Biological control must be carefully tested. Some introduced animals meant to control pests, such as the cane toad in Australia and the golden apple snail in the Philippines, which was brought in for food, became serious invaders themselves. Today scientists test potential control agents for years to make sure they will not attack native species."],["Gardeners can help","Many invasive plants escaped from gardens. Experts encourage gardeners to avoid planting known invaders, dispose of plant waste carefully, and choose native species that support local wildlife. In the Philippines, groups promote native trees such as narra, molave and balete for gardens and reforestation."],["Turning weeds into products","Some communities harvest invasive plants for useful products. Water hyacinth stems are woven into bags and furniture, and some invasive shrubs are used for compost, animal bedding or biofuel. While this does not eliminate the plants, it can help reduce them while providing income."]],
sp:["A hall for a moth","In the early 1900s, prickly pear cacti covered vast areas of Queensland and New South Wales in Australia, forming dense, spiny thickets that made farmland useless. Farmers tried poison and fire with little success. Scientists searched the Americas for natural enemies and found a small moth from Argentina whose caterpillars tunnel into prickly pear pads. Beginning in 1926, billions of moth eggs were distributed across the infested land. Within about a decade, the cactus had collapsed across huge areas, and the land was returned to farming. Grateful residents of the town of Boonarga built the Cactoblastis Memorial Hall in honor of the insect, perhaps the only building in the world dedicated to a moth."],
g:[["Invasive species","A non-native species that spreads harmfully"],["Native species","A species that occurs naturally in an area"],["Biological control","Using natural enemies to control pests"],["Hagonoy","A Filipino name for an invasive shrub"]],
f:["Water hyacinth is invasive in the Philippines.","Most introduced plants do not become invasive.","A moth controlled prickly pear in Australia."],
fun:"An Australian town built a memorial hall to honor a moth that destroyed invasive cacti."});

ADDART({id:"narra",t:"Narra",c:"Plants",i:"🌳",w:"Pterocarpus indicus",r:["trees","sampaguita","deforestation","giantrees","conservation"],
p:[["National tree","The narra is the national tree of the Philippines."],["Prized wood","Its beautiful, hard wood is valued for furniture."],["Golden blooms","It produces fragrant yellow flowers for a few days a year."]],
l:"The narra, Pterocarpus indicus, is a large tree native to Southeast Asia and the western Pacific, including the Philippines, where it was declared the national tree in 1934. It can grow over 30 meters tall with a broad, spreading crown and buttressed trunk. Narra wood is hard, durable, fragrant and beautifully colored in shades of red and gold, making it one of the most prized timbers for furniture, flooring and carving. Its sap is red and turns dark when exposed to air. For a few days each year, narra trees burst into clusters of fragrant yellow flowers. Because of heavy logging, wild narra trees have declined, and the species is considered threatened. It is widely planted along roads and in parks, schools and campuses.",
s:[["Appearance","Narra trees have gray bark, glossy compound leaves and a wide crown that casts deep shade. Older trees develop large buttress roots. The tree may lose some of its leaves in the dry season and then flower as new leaves appear."],["Flowers and seeds","Narra trees flower briefly, often for only a day or a few days, covering the tree in small, bright yellow, fragrant flowers that then fall like golden rain. The seeds develop inside round, flat, winged pods, which spin as they fall and are carried by wind and water."],["The wood","Narra heartwood ranges from golden yellow to deep red, with attractive patterns, and polishes to a fine shine. It resists insects and decay. Traditional Filipino furniture, such as heavy carved beds, cabinets and dining sets made of narra, is often handed down through families."],["Red sap","When the bark is cut, narra releases a red sap, sometimes called kino, which hardens into a dark resin. It has been used in traditional medicine and as a dye. Its blood-like color has given rise to folk beliefs in some regions."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Pterocarpus indicus"],["National tree since","1934"],["Height","Over 30 meters"],["Wood colors","Gold to deep red"],["Status","Threatened"]],
d:[["1700s","Narra wood is used in colonial churches and furniture"],["1934","Narra is proclaimed the Philippine national tree"],["1970s","Logging greatly reduces natural forests in the Philippines"],["1980s","The government restricts the cutting of narra and other premium trees"],["2011","A moratorium on logging in natural forests is ordered"],["2020s","Narra is widely planted in reforestation and greening programs"]],
s2:[["A national symbol","In 1934 Governor-General Frank Murphy issued a proclamation naming the narra as the national tree and the sampaguita as the national flower of the Philippine Islands. The narra was chosen for its strength, beauty and usefulness. It appears in school lessons and is planted on campuses and at government buildings."],["The cost of popularity","Because narra wood is so valuable, the trees were heavily logged during the 20th century, both legally and illegally. The government restricted cutting of narra, and a 2011 order banned logging in natural forests. Cutting narra without permits remains illegal, but illegal logging and the sale of old-growth narra furniture continue to be enforcement challenges."],["Planting narra","Narra grows relatively quickly for a hardwood and can be propagated from seeds or cuttings. It is a common choice in Philippine tree-planting programs, school greening projects and along streets. Like other legumes, its roots host bacteria that add nitrogen to the soil."],["Narra in Southeast Asia","Narra is native across Southeast Asia and the western Pacific. It is known as angsana in Malaysia and Singapore, where it was widely planted as a shade tree along streets, and as sonokembang in Indonesia. A disease called angsana wilt has killed many street trees in Singapore and Malaysia."],["Glowing wood water","Shavings of narra wood soaked in water produce a liquid that glows blue under certain light, a property known for centuries. In the 1500s and 1600s, Europeans prized similar fluorescent woods from the Americas and Asia as medicine, and scientists studying them contributed to early understanding of fluorescence."],["Narra in place names","Several places in the Philippines are named after the narra, including the municipality of Narra in southern Palawan, near Rasa Island. Narra Street and Narra Hall are common names, reflecting the tree’s place in national identity."]],
sp:["Golden rain","One morning in the dry season, residents of a Philippine university campus notice that the tall narra trees lining the main road have changed overnight. Their crowns are covered in tiny yellow flowers, and a sweet fragrance fills the air. Bees hum among the branches. Students stop to take photos. By the next day, the flowers begin to fall, covering the road and sidewalks in a golden carpet. Within a few days, the show is over, and the trees return to green. Because the blooming is so brief and spectacular, many Filipinos remember the first time they saw the national tree in full flower."],
g:[["National tree","A tree chosen as a symbol of a country"],["Heartwood","The dense inner wood of a tree"],["Buttress root","A wide root that supports a large tree"],["Moratorium","A temporary ban"]],
f:["Narra is the national tree of the Philippines.","Its wood ranges from gold to red.","It blooms for only a few days at a time."],
fun:"Water soaked with narra wood shavings can glow blue under certain light."});

ADDART({id:"sampaguita",t:"Sampaguita",c:"Plants",i:"🌼",w:"Jasminum sambac",r:["flower","narra","phfest","orchids","relig"],
p:[["National flower","The sampaguita is the national flower of the Philippines."],["Sweet fragrance","Its small white flowers have a strong, sweet scent."],["Strings of flowers","Sampaguita garlands are sold outside churches and offered to guests."]],
l:"The sampaguita, Jasminum sambac, also called Arabian jasmine, is a climbing shrub with small, white, star-shaped flowers famous for their sweet fragrance. It was declared the national flower of the Philippines in 1934 and is also the national flower of Indonesia, where it is called melati putih. Native to South Asia, it spread across Asia long ago and has been grown in the Philippines for centuries. Filipinos string sampaguita flowers into garlands, called kwintas or leis, which are offered to honored guests, placed on religious images and sold by vendors outside churches and on city streets. The flowers bloom in the evening and release their scent at night. Sampaguita is also used to scent tea and perfumes.",
s:[["The plant","Sampaguita is an evergreen shrub or vine with glossy, oval leaves. It grows well in warm, sunny places and is common in Philippine gardens and farms. The flowers usually open in the evening and last about a day, turning pinkish as they fade."],["Fragrance","The scent of sampaguita comes from natural oils that include compounds also found in other jasmines. Flowers are often picked in the late afternoon, before they fully open, so that they are fresh and fragrant when sold in the evening or next morning."],["Garlands","Sampaguita garlands are a familiar sight in the Philippines. They are given to welcome visitors, worn at graduations and celebrations, and offered at shrines and church altars. Vendors, including children, sell garlands at traffic stops and outside churches such as Quiapo Church in Manila."],["Farming","Sampaguita is grown commercially in provinces such as Laguna, Bulacan and Pangasinan, where many small farmers pick flowers daily and sell them to garland makers. Some is processed for essential oils used in perfumes and soaps."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Jasminum sambac"],["Other name","Arabian jasmine"],["National flower since","1934"],["Also national flower of","Indonesia"],["Blooms","Evening"]],
d:[["1600s","Sampaguita is already grown in the Philippines"],["1879","Dolores Paterno composes the song La Flor de Manila"],["1934","Sampaguita is proclaimed the Philippine national flower"],["1990","Indonesia names white jasmine one of its national flowers"],["2010s","Lawmakers propose adding the waling-waling as a national flower"],["2020s","Sampaguita farming continues in Laguna and other provinces"]],
s2:[["Name and legend","A popular Filipino legend says the name sampaguita comes from the words sumpa kita, meaning I promise you, spoken by lovers. In one story, a young woman promised to wait for her beloved, and after she died of sorrow, a fragrant white flower grew on her grave. Linguists suggest the name may actually come from older words for jasmine, but the romantic legend remains well loved."],["A national flower","In 1934 Governor-General Frank Murphy proclaimed the sampaguita the national flower, along with the narra as national tree. It was chosen for its purity, simplicity and fragrance, qualities associated with Filipino ideals. Some lawmakers have proposed also recognizing the native waling-waling orchid, while others argue the sampaguita’s long cultural role should remain unique; the debate has continued for years."],["La Flor de Manila","In 1879 the young Filipino composer Dolores Paterno wrote La Flor de Manila, a romantic song inspired by the sampaguita. It became one of the most beloved Filipino songs of the Spanish era and is still performed today. The sampaguita has also appeared in poems, films and the names of schools and organizations."],["Sampaguita vendors","For many families, especially in Metro Manila, selling sampaguita garlands is a source of income. Children selling garlands in traffic face dangers, and social welfare groups work to protect them and support their education. Some programs help vendors sell in safer places."],["Jasmine tea","In China, jasmine tea is made by layering tea leaves with fresh sampaguita or other jasmine blossoms overnight so the leaves absorb their scent. This process may be repeated several times for high-quality tea. Jasmine tea has been made in Fujian province for centuries."],["Religious offerings","Across Asia, jasmine flowers are offered at temples, shrines and altars. In India, jasmine garlands are worn by women in their hair and offered to Hindu deities. In the Philippines, sampaguita garlands are placed on statues of the Santo Niño, the Virgin Mary and other saints."]],
sp:["The flower vendor’s evening","As the sun sets over Manila, a woman sits near the steps of a church with a basket of fresh sampaguita picked that afternoon on a farm in Laguna. Working quickly, she threads the tiny white buds onto strings, adding a red ribbon and a small rosebud or ilang-ilang flower to finish each garland. As churchgoers arrive for evening Mass, she calls out softly, offering garlands for a few pesos. Some buyers hang the garlands on the rearview mirrors of their cars, filling them with fragrance; others place them before statues of saints. By night, the buds open, and the sweet scent of the national flower drifts through the streets."],
g:[["Sampaguita","The Philippine national flower, a type of jasmine"],["Garland","A string of flowers"],["Kwintas","A Filipino word for necklace or garland"],["Essential oil","A fragrant oil extracted from plants"]],
f:["Sampaguita is the Philippine national flower.","It is also a national flower of Indonesia.","Its flowers bloom in the evening."],
fun:"A popular legend says the name sampaguita comes from sumpa kita, meaning I promise you."});

ADDART({id:"abaca",t:"Abaca",c:"Plants",i:"🧵",w:"Abacá",r:["weaving","coconut","agri","clothing","phfood"],
p:[["Manila hemp","Abaca fiber is known worldwide as Manila hemp."],["A banana relative","Abaca looks like a banana plant."],["Mostly Philippine","The Philippines produces most of the world’s abaca."]],
l:"Abaca, Musa textilis, is a plant native to the Philippines that looks like a banana plant but is grown for the strong fiber in its leaf stalks. Known internationally as Manila hemp, abaca fiber is one of the strongest natural fibers in the world and resists damage from saltwater, which made it ideal for ship ropes in the age of sail. Today it is used to make specialty paper for tea bags, coffee filters, banknotes and cigarette paper, as well as handicrafts, textiles and parts for cars. The Philippines produces most of the world’s abaca, with major plantations in the Bicol region, Eastern Visayas and Mindanao. Abaca is also the source of traditional fabrics such as t’nalak of the T’boli people.",
s:[["The plant","Abaca grows 4 to 6 meters tall, with large leaves and a false trunk made of overlapping leaf sheaths, like a banana. Its fruits are small and full of seeds, not good to eat. It thrives in warm, humid places with rich soil and plenty of rain, often under the shade of other trees."],["Extracting fiber","When the plant is about 18 months to two years old, farmers cut the stalks and strip the outer layers into long strips called tuxies. The strips are pulled through a knife to scrape away the pulp, leaving long, shiny fibers, which are then dried in the sun. This stripping can be done by hand or with machines."],["Strength","Abaca fibers can be up to 3 meters long and are very strong, flexible and resistant to rot and saltwater. This made Manila rope the choice for ships’ rigging, fishing nets and towing lines in the 1800s and early 1900s. Synthetic fibers such as nylon later replaced much of this use."],["Modern uses","Today most abaca is pulped to make strong, porous specialty papers, such as tea bags, coffee filters, sausage casings and some banknote papers. It is also used in fiber-reinforced car parts, furniture, handbags, hats, placemats and textiles. Demand for natural, biodegradable materials has renewed interest in abaca."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Musa textilis"],["Other name","Manila hemp"],["Main producer","The Philippines"],["Major regions","Bicol, Eastern Visayas, Mindanao"],["Traditional fabric","T’nalak"]],
d:[["1500s","Spanish chroniclers describe abaca cloth in the Philippines"],["1820s","Abaca exports from Manila to America and Europe grow"],["1900s","Japanese settlers develop large abaca plantations in Davao"],["1930s","Abaca becomes one of the Philippines’ top exports"],["2004","A carmaker uses abaca fibers in vehicle parts"],["2020s","The Philippines remains the world’s leading abaca producer"]],
s2:[["Abaca and history","In the 19th century, demand for Manila hemp rope from American and British shipping companies made abaca one of the Philippines’ most important exports, bringing wealth to provinces like Albay and Sorsogon. In the early 1900s, Japanese settlers developed large abaca plantations in Davao, which grew into a major Japanese community before World War II."],["T’nalak","The T’boli people of Lake Sebu in South Cotabato weave t’nalak, a sacred cloth made from abaca fibers dyed in deep reds, blacks and browns. According to tradition, the designs come to women weavers in their dreams from the spirit Fu Dalu. T’nalak weavers have been recognized as National Living Treasures, and the T’nalak Festival celebrates the craft."],["Other abaca crafts","Abaca is woven into sinamay, a stiff, open fabric used for hats, bags and fascinators, and into pinukpok, a soft fabric made by pounding fibers, from Kalinga and Bicol. Abaca slippers, rugs, placemats and baskets are popular products sold in markets and exported."],["Diseases","Abaca plantations are threatened by viral diseases, especially abaca bunchy top and abaca mosaic, spread by aphids. Infected plants become stunted and produce little fiber. Researchers in the Philippines are developing disease-resistant varieties and teaching farmers to remove infected plants."],["Farmers’ livelihoods","Many abaca farmers in the Philippines are smallholders in upland areas who earn modest incomes, and fiber stripping is physically demanding work. Government agencies and private companies run programs to improve stripping machines, prices and quality, while sustainability certifications help some farmers reach international buyers."],["Abaca in money","Because abaca paper is strong and durable, it has been used in banknote paper in some countries, including Japan’s yen banknotes, which use abaca alongside other fibers. Philippine peso paper banknotes have also contained abaca fiber. Its strength helps notes survive folding and handling."]],
sp:["The dream weavers of Lake Sebu","On the shores of Lake Sebu in the mountains of South Cotabato, a T’boli woman sits at a backstrap loom, her body leaning back to keep the threads tight. The warp threads are abaca fibers that have been tied in intricate patterns and dyed using roots and leaves, a process that can take weeks. She weaves slowly, the dark cloth growing a few centimeters at a time, revealing patterns of crocodiles, frogs and human figures. In T’boli belief, these patterns come to weavers in dreams, and a dream weaver may not copy designs from others. When finished, the t’nalak may be used in ceremonies, given as a gift or sold, carrying the weaver’s vision to the world."],
g:[["Abaca","A Philippine plant grown for its strong fiber"],["Manila hemp","The international name for abaca fiber"],["T’nalak","A sacred abaca cloth of the T’boli"],["Tuxy","A strip of abaca leaf sheath before stripping"]],
f:["Abaca is related to the banana.","The Philippines produces most of the world’s abaca.","Abaca is used in tea bags."],
fun:"Abaca fiber is used in tea bags and in some countries’ banknotes."});

ADDART({id:"lichens",t:"Lichens",c:"Plants",i:"🪨",w:"Lichen",r:["fungi","ferns","pollution","soil","arctic"],
p:[["A partnership","A lichen is a fungus living together with algae or bacteria."],["Pioneers","Lichens can grow on bare rock and help make soil."],["Clean air signs","Many lichens are sensitive to air pollution."]],
l:"Lichens are remarkable living things formed by a partnership between a fungus and one or more photosynthetic partners, either green algae or cyanobacteria. The fungus provides structure and protection, while its partners make food from sunlight. Together they form a new organism that looks like neither partner alone, growing as crusts, leafy patches or shrubby tufts on rocks, tree bark, soil, walls and gravestones. There are around 20,000 known kinds of lichens, found from tropical rainforests to Antarctica, where they are among the few living things. Lichens grow very slowly and can live for centuries. They help form soil, provide food for animals such as reindeer, and are used to monitor air quality, because many cannot survive in polluted air.",
s:[["Partners in life","In a lichen, fungal threads form a protective body that holds water and anchors to surfaces. Inside, a layer of algae or cyanobacteria makes sugars through photosynthesis, feeding the fungus. Some cyanobacteria also capture nitrogen. In 2016 scientists discovered that many lichens also contain a third partner, a type of yeast."],["Growth forms","Crustose lichens form flat crusts tightly attached to rock or bark. Foliose lichens look like leafy patches with loose edges. Fruticose lichens grow as tiny shrubs or hanging strands, such as old man’s beard, or Usnea, which hangs from branches in misty mountain forests, including in the Philippines."],["Survivors","Lichens can dry out completely and become dormant, then revive within minutes when wet. They survive extreme heat, cold and drought. In 2005 European scientists exposed lichens to open space on a satellite for over two weeks, and they survived and continued to grow afterward."],["Making soil","Lichens are often the first living things to colonize bare rock, such as new lava flows or rocks left by melting glaciers. They release acids that slowly break down rock, and as they die and decay, they add organic matter. This helps form the first thin soils in which mosses and plants can later grow."]],
k:[["Partners","Fungus plus algae or cyanobacteria"],["Known kinds","About 20,000"],["Growth forms","Crustose, foliose, fruticose"],["Use in science","Air quality indicators"],["Famous food lichen","Reindeer lichen"]],
d:[["1866","Lichens are noted as indicators of air pollution in Paris"],["1867","Simon Schwendener proposes that lichens are partnerships"],["1897","Beatrix Potter’s paper on spore germination is presented in London"],["2005","Lichens survive exposure to outer space"],["2016","Scientists discover yeasts as a third lichen partner"],["2020s","Lichens are used to monitor air pollution in cities"]],
s2:[["Discovering the partnership","For centuries, lichens were thought to be single plants. In 1867 the Swiss botanist Simon Schwendener proposed that each lichen was a fungus living with algae. Many lichen experts rejected his idea strongly, but it was eventually proven correct. The word symbiosis, meaning living together, was coined partly to describe lichens."],["Beatrix Potter, lichen scientist","Before she became famous for Peter Rabbit, Beatrix Potter was a skilled naturalist who studied fungi and lichens and painted them in detail. She supported the dual nature of lichens and studied how fungal spores germinate. In 1897 her paper was presented at the Linnean Society in London by a male colleague, because women were not allowed to attend meetings at the time. In 1997 the Society formally apologized for how she had been treated."],["Air quality monitors","Many lichens absorb water and nutrients directly from the air, along with pollutants such as sulfur dioxide. In polluted cities, sensitive lichens disappear. Scientists survey lichens to map air quality, and some cities have seen lichens return as air pollution declined. In the Philippines, researchers have studied lichens on trees as indicators of air quality in cities and forests."],["Food and dyes","Reindeer and caribou depend on reindeer lichen as winter food in the Arctic. Some lichens have been eaten by people in famine times. Lichens were traditionally used to make dyes, including the purple dye used in Harris tweed, and litmus, the indicator that turns red in acids and blue in bases, comes from lichens."],["Medicines and perfumes","Some lichens contain chemicals with antibacterial properties and have been used in traditional medicine. Oakmoss, a lichen, is used in perfumes. Usnic acid from Usnea lichens is used in some creams, though in high doses it can harm the liver, so its use in supplements is regulated in some countries."],["Very old lichens","Lichens grow extremely slowly, sometimes only a millimeter a year or less. Some lichen patches in the Arctic and Antarctica are estimated to be thousands of years old. Geologists use the size of certain lichens on rocks to estimate how long ago glaciers retreated, a method called lichenometry."]],
sp:["Lichens in space","In 2005 scientists from Spain and other European countries placed samples of two lichen species inside a small container on the outside of a Russian satellite. Once in orbit, the container opened, exposing the lichens to the vacuum of space, extreme temperatures and strong ultraviolet and cosmic radiation for about 15 days. When the capsule returned to Earth, the scientists rehydrated the lichens and found that they were still alive and able to carry out photosynthesis normally. The experiment suggested that some life forms might survive short journeys through space and helped scientists think about the limits of life."],
g:[["Lichen","A partnership of a fungus and algae or bacteria"],["Symbiosis","Two different organisms living closely together"],["Cyanobacteria","Bacteria that can make food from sunlight"],["Lichenometry","Using lichen size to date rock surfaces"]],
f:["A lichen is a partnership between organisms.","Lichens can survive in outer space.","Litmus comes from lichens."],
fun:"Lichens have survived more than two weeks exposed to outer space."});

;
